g>\n|<big>276.5</big>\n|<big>11</big>\n|-\n|<big>LTR 4 RNG CNTL</big>\n|<big>247.8</big>\n|<big>12</big>\n|-\n|<big>LCR 6 RNG CNTL</big>\n|<big>280.175</big>\n|<big>13</big>\n|-\n|<big>AS RQRD</big>\n|<big>N/A</big>\n|<big>14</big>\n|-\n|<big>CVN-75 APP #1</big>\n|<big>339.500</big>\n|<big>15</big>\n|-\n|<big>CVN-75 Marshal</big>\n|<big>377.200</big>\n|<big>16</big>\n|-\n|<big>CVN-75 APP #2</big>\n|<big>339.600</big>\n|<big>17</big>\n|-\n|<big>SHELL 2-1</big>\n|<big>254.000</big>\n|<big>18</big>\n|-\n|<big>Mauler 6-1</big>\n|<big>311.500</big>\n|<big>19</big>\n|-\n|<big>AS RQRD</big>\n|<big>N/A</big>\n|<big>20</big>\n|}\n-----\n=== WAYPOINTS ===','utf-8'),
(547,'{{DISPLAYTITLE:Squadrons/41st Black Aces}} {{DISPLAYTITLE:41st VSFS Black Aces}} {{DISPLAYTITLE:41st VSFS Black Aces}}\n\n= 41st VSFS special operating procedures =\n\n== Overview ==\n[[File:Screen_260330_155302_4.png|thumb|387x387px]]\nThe 41st Virtual Strike Fighter Squadron (VSFS) \"Black Aces\" is a carrier-based F-14B(U) strike fighter squadron deployed aboard the USS Harry S. Truman (CVN-75). The squadron takes its name, identity, and doctrine from VFA-41 Black Aces: A distinguished US Navy strike fighter squadron established in 1950 and renowned for its combat record, operational excellence, and carrier-based fighter tradition.\n\n41st VSFS strives to honour that legacy by embracing the professionalism, discipline, and combat effectiveness of the real Black Aces, integrating authentic US Navy carrier procedures, tactical doctrine, and mission planning into every virtual operation.\n\n== Carrier Operations and Procedures ==\n[[File:Screen_260405_152707.png|left|thumb|429x429px]]\nThe USS Harry S. Truman is the beating heart of our operations. All mission-ready pilots in our squadron are expected to be comfortable operating to, on, and from the carrier. Below you will find useful guides and resources to conduct safe and efficient carrier operations.\n\n[[41st VSFS/Case I Procedures|Case I Procedures (work in progress)]]\n\n[[41st VSFS/Case III Procedures|Case III Procedures (work in progress)]]\n\n[[General/CATC Communications Guide|Carrier Communications and Phraseology (CATCC)]]\n\n[[Landing Signal Officer (LSO) Guide]]\n\n== Tactical Procedures (WIP) ==\n[[File:Screen_260330_155406.png|thumb|510x510px]]\n\'\'\'<u>Tactical Procedures will cover how we fight together.</u>\'\'\'\n\nAir to Ground Tactics\n\n* [[14th VSFS/Close Air Support (CAS)|Close Air Support (CAS)]]\n* [[Strike/Air Interdiction]]\n* [[SEAD/DEAD]]\n* [[Anti-Ship]]\n\nAir to Air Tactics\n\n* [[Tactical Turns]]\n* [[Beyond Visual Range (BVR)]]\n* [[Basic Fighter Maneuvers (BFM)]]\n* [[Combat Air Patrol (CAP)]]\n\n== General Flight Procedures (WIP) ==\n[[File:Screen_260327_220735.png|left|thumb|464x464px]]\n\'\'\'<u>General Flight Procedures will cover how we fly together.</u>\'\'\'\n\n[[Airfield operations (Shore-based)]]\n\n[[Formation Flying]]\n\n[[14th VSFS/Air to Air Refueling|Air to Air Refueling]]\n\n[[Advanced Fuel Management]]\n\n[[Night Flying]]\n\n== Resources and Files ==\nTIP: CTRL+CLICK on any link to open it in a new tab instead of replacing this one\n\n[Link to livery, when livery]\n\n[https://polybox.ethz.ch/index.php/s/S8Es6KCzBao5WYm/download?path=%2FKneeboards&files=FA-18C_hornet.rar <u>14th VSFS Kneeboard (Checklists + Quick Reference Guides + LTAG Aerodrome and Tacan Approach Charts)</u>]\n\n[https://info.publicintelligence.net/F14AAP-1B.pdf F-14B Emergency Procedures]\n\n[https://chucksguides.com/aircraft/dcs/f-14b/#&#x5B;1,%22XYZ%22,0,540,1&#x5D; Chucks Guides - F-14B] \n\n[https://server.3rd-wing.net/public/Bureau_VF31/Docs%20F-14%20r%C3%A9el/NAVAIR%2001-F14AAP-1%20-%20NATOPS%20Flight%20Manual%20-%20F-14B.pdf NATOPS FLIGHT MANUAL 01-F14AAP-1] (Aug, 2001)\n\n[https://f14.manuals.heatblur.se/ Heatblur F-14 Manual] (June, 2026)','utf-8'),
(548,'= Available tankers (Source Training Theatre) =\n[[File:US Navy 030125-N-0905V-002 S-3B \'Viking assigned to the Screwbirds of Sea Control Squadron 33 (VS-33) .jpg|thumb|383x383px|Lockheed S-3B Viking]]\n[[File:Screen 260212 152743.png|thumb|380x380px|Boeing KC-135 Stratotanker (with MPRS Pods)]]\n{| class=\"wikitable\"\n|+TANKER INFORMATION\n|\n|CALLSIGN \n|FREQUENCY\n|TACAN\n|ALTITUDE\n|NOTES\n|-\n|S-3B VIKING\n|MAULER 6-1\n|311.5 MHz\n|25Y\n|6,000ft\n|CARRIER RECOVERY TANKER\n|-\n|KC-135 MPRS\n|SHELL 2-1\n|254.0 MHz\n|38X\n|24,000ft\n|TRA ADANA\n|}\n\'\'\'S-3B\'\'\' is a small, carrier capable tanker that has one drogue capable of refueling only one aircraft at a time. It is established in a left hand racetrack pattern around the carrier on the BRC.\n\n\'\'\'KC-135 MPRS\'\'\' is a large purpose built tanker with the Multi-Point Refueling System (MPRS) pods installed on each wingtip, which allows for two probe-style aircraft to connect and receive fuel at the same time. It is established in a left hand racetrack pattern within TRA ADANA.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n= F/A-18 Hornet — Air-to-Air Refueling (AAR) Standard Procedure =\n\'\'Note: This guide blends standard Navy probe-and-drogue technique with sim-community best practices. Lines marked (IRL) reflect real-world reasoning; lines marked (SIM) are conventions common in DCS/sim training rather than confirmed NATOPS text.\'\'\n----\n\n== 1. Inbound and Join-Up on Port (Left) Observation ==\n\'\'\'Tanker Intercept\'\'\'\n\n* Proceed to the tanker\'s position via the assigned rendezvous point, altitude, and heading (as briefed or per controlling agency instructions).\n* Call \"[Callsign], [position/bearing/range], for join-up\" on the tanker\'s frequency once within radio range.\n* Close on the tanker from astern, offsetting to the left side, and match the tanker\'s airspeed as you arrive.\n\n=== Closing on the Tanker — Detailed Technique ===\nThis is where most join-up errors happen. Overshoot, being too slow/cautious, and wrong intercept geometry are really the same root issue seen three different ways: \'\'\'poor closure-rate management relative to intercept geometry.\'\'\' Below is how to read and fix each one.\n\n==== A. Set Up the Geometry Before You Start Closing ====\n\n* Don\'t point straight at the tanker. Flying directly at it collapses your ability to judge closure — everything looks \"just get bigger\" until it\'s suddenly too big.\n* Instead, fly an \'\'\'offset intercept\'\'\': approach on a shallow angle (roughly 20-30° off the tanker\'s track), aiming for a point \'\'behind and to the side\'\' of the tanker rather than at the tanker itself. This gives you a stable visual reference (the \"bearing line\") to judge closure against, and room to convert excess speed into a smooth turn onto the tanker\'s heading instead of a straight-line stop.\n* \'\'\'Bearing line check\'\'\': pick a fixed point on the tanker (e.g., a wingtip or the refueling store) and watch how it moves against the horizon/tanker fuselage as you close.\n** If the reference point \'\'\'holds still relative to the background\'\'\' — you\'re on a collision course; you need to be actively flying an angle-off arrival to convert into position, not just closing straight in.\n** If it\'s \'\'\'moving toward your nose\'\'\' (line-of-sight drifting forward) — you\'re going to pass in front (overshoot risk). Reduce power and/or increase angle-off.\n** If it\'s \'\'\'moving toward your tail\'\'\' (line-of-sight drifting aft) — you\'re going to fall behind/underneath. Add power or tighten your angle-off.\n\n==== B. Fixing \"Overshoot\" ====\nOvershoot almost always means arriving with too much excess closure late in the join, then having to make a big, abrupt correction that carries you through the desired position.\n\n* \'\'\'Diagnose early, not late.\'\'\' Excess closure is easy to fix at 1000 ft and hard to fix at 100 ft. Cross-check closure rate continually from first visual contact — don\'t wait until you\'re \"close\" to start paying attention.\n* \'\'\'Bleed energy progressively.\'\'\' Reduce power in stages as range decreases, rather than carrying high closure all the way in and chopping power at the last second. A late, large power reduction is exactly what produces the \"rocket in, blow through\" overshoot.\n* \'\'\'Use lag, not lead.\'\'\' If arriving from an offset (per A above), flying a \'\'\'lag pursuit\'\'\' curve (aiming slightly behind the tanker\'s current position rather than ahead of it) naturally bleeds closure as range decreases — the geometry itself does some of the speed control for you.\n* \'\'\'If an overshoot starts developing anyway:\'\'\' don\'t try to salvage it by cutting power hard and holding position — go high and outside (or low and outside, per brief), let the closure dissipate, and rejoin cleanly rather than fighting a correction that\'s already in motion. A reset costs a few seconds; a fought overshoot risks an unsafe pass down the tanker\'s side.\n\n==== C. Fixing \"Too Slow / Too Cautious\" ====\nThis is the opposite failure — arriving with insufficient closure, which makes the join take forever and can look like the aircraft is \"parked\" well behind position.\n\n* \'\'\'Establish real closure early and hold it.\'\'\' A common cause of the overly cautious join is decelerating too early, trying to arrive at the final position already at the final speed. Instead, hold a deliberate overtake (commonly 20-30 KIAS faster than the tanker, adjusted to briefed technique) through the middle portion of the join, and only bleed it off in the final few hundred feet.\n* \'\'\'Don\'t chase small closure-rate changes with the throttle the whole way in.\'\'\' Constant micro-adjustments early in the join waste time and attention that should be spent on geometry. Set a power setting that gives a healthy, stable overtake and leave it alone until close enough that fine correction actually matters.\n* \'\'\'Trust the geometry, not just the picture.\'\'\' A cautious wingman often slows down because the tanker \"looks big\" or \"looks close,\" when range and closure are still perfectly safe. Reference the bearing-line technique in A — it\'s a more reliable cue than raw visual size, which is easy to misjudge.\n\n==== D. Fixing \"Wrong Intercept Geometry\" ====\nThis is usually the root cause behind both B and C above, and shows up as approaching from the wrong angle, cutting the corner too early, or arriving on the wrong side/line.\n\n* \'\'\'Fly the assigned join-up side deliberately from the start\'\'\' — don\'t drift onto whichever side is easier in the moment. If assigned Port Observation, set up the intercept to arrive on the left side from the outset, rather than crossing behind the tanker and correcting late.\n* \'\'\'Respect the offset, don\'t cut it.\'\'\' A wingman in a hurry will often try to shortcut the intercept by turning inbound too early, which collapses the offset angle and turns a manageable curved join into a straight-line, high line-of-sight-rate closure — exactly the setup for an overshoot. Hold the offset heading longer than feels natural, then commit to the turn inbound once the geometry (range/bearing) actually supports it.\n* \'\'\'Watch track-crossing angle, not just range.\'\'\' Two aircraft can be at the same range with completely different closure problems depending on the angle between their flight paths. Closing nearly parallel to the tanker\'s track is a much more controllable profile than closing on a steep cutoff angle — steer toward parallel/shallow angles as range decreases, not toward pointing straight at the tanker.\n\n\'\'\'Port Observation\'\'\'\n\n* Port Observation is the holding area established in left echelon off the tanker, slightly aft and level with (or slightly below) the tanker\'s wing line.\n* This is where flight lead sorts jets in refueling order — lowest fuel state moves toward the basket first.\n* Maintain loose formation here; this is a \"hold and wait your turn\" position, not a precision station-keeping position yet.\n\n----\n\n== 2. Transition to Pre-Contact and Connect ==\nWhen cleared and ready to move from Port Observation to Pre-Contact, complete the following memory items:\n\n* \'\'\'Refueling probe\'\'\' — EXTEND\n* \'\'\'Radar\'\'\' — SIL or STBY\n* \'\'\'Engine Bleed Air\'\'\' — RIGHT OFF \'\'(optional; done IRL to prevent drogue fuel leaks from entering the cabin via the right engine intake)\'\'\n\n\'\'\'Throttle technique:\'\'\' Avoid relying on Automatic Throttle Control (ATC) whenever possible. While convenient, leaning on ATC keeps you from developing and maintaining the fine throttle control skills that are important throughout many other phases of flying the Hornet.\n\n\'\'\'Closing on the basket:\'\'\'\n\n* Move from Port Observation, cross behind the tanker, and stabilize in the Pre-Contact position — a few feet aft and slightly below the drogue, matching speed with zero rate of closure.\n* On clearance, close smoothly and steadily into the basket. Make small, early corrections rather than large, late ones.\n* Reference a fixed point on the tanker (e.g., the refueling pod itself) rather than staring at the moving basket, which tends to cause overcorrection.\n* Confirm a good connection (contact light/indication, fuel flow) before settling into position and monitoring fuel transfer.\n\n----\n\n== 3. Disconnect and Join Up on Starboard (Right) Observation ==\n\n* Once refueling is complete (full load or as briefed), smoothly reduce power slightly to back straight out of the basket — avoid abrupt throttle changes or lateral drift during disconnect.\n* Confirm disconnect indication and retract the refueling probe.\n* \'\'\'Engine Bleed Air\'\'\' — restore to NORM (if previously turned off for the right engine).\n* Clear from behind the tanker to the \'\'\'Starboard (Right) Observation\'\'\' position — the mirror of Port Observation, held in right echelon off the tanker.\n* This clears the pre-contact/contact area for the next aircraft in the flight and positions you to either re-enter the queue (if more fuel is needed later) or depart the tanker formation once the flight is complete.\n\n----\n\n== Closure Rate vs. Range — Speed Management Table ==\n\'\'Use this as a decelerating \"ladder\" during the intercept: bleed closure down a step as each range gate is crossed, rather than holding one speed the whole way and cutting power hard at the end. This schedule was checked against expected join-up time (see note below the table) to make sure it\'s neither dangerously fast nor unrealistically slow — adjust to your actual briefed technique/squadron SOP if one exists.\'\'\n{| class=\"wikitable\"\n!Range\n!Target Closure\n|-\n|5 NM\n|≤ 200 KIAS overtake\n|-\n|1 NM\n|≤ 100 KIAS overtake\n|-\n|0.5 NM\n|≤ 50 KIAS overtake\n|-\n|0.4 NM\n|≤ 40 KIAS overtake\n|-\n|0.3 NM\n|≤ 30 KIAS overtake\n|-\n|0.2 NM\n|≤ 20 KIAS overtake\n|-\n|0.1 NM\n|≤ 10 KIAS overtake\n|}\n\'\'\'How to use this table in practice:\'\'\'\n\n* Treat each range as a \'\'\'gate\'\'\', not a hard switch — start the reduction slightly before the gate so you arrive at the target closure right as you cross it, rather than reacting late.\n* If you arrive at a gate with \'\'\'more\'\'\' closure than the table calls for, that\'s your overshoot warning sign — reduce power now, don\'t wait for the next gate.\n* If you arrive at a gate with \'\'\'less\'\'\' closure than the table calls for, that\'s your \"too slow/cautious\" warning sign — add power now rather than letting the join stretch out further.\n* Notice the pace: big steps at long range (5nm → 1nm), then progressively smaller, finer steps as range decreases (every 0.1nm inside 0.5nm). That mirrors how the join should actually feel — free to move quickly early, increasingly deliberate and fine-tuned as you get close.\n\n\'\'\'Sanity-checked timing:\'\'\' following this schedule (using average closure across each segment), the total time from 5nm to 0.1nm works out to about \'\'\'3 minutes\'\'\' — fast enough to be a normal, efficient join-up, not so fast that it\'s reckless, and without the \"crawl in over 15+ minutes\" problem of overly conservative schedules.\n\n== Quick-Reference Memory Items (Pre-Contact) ==\n{| class=\"wikitable\"\n!Item\n!Setting\n|-\n|Refueling probe\n|EXTEND\n|-\n|Radar\n|SIL / STBY\n|-\n|Engine Bleed Air\n|RIGHT OFF (optional)\n|-\n|Throttle\n|Manual preferred; avoid ATC\n|}','utf-8'),
