(570,'== Formation ==\nA formation is defined as more than one aircraft that, by prior arrangement between the pilots, operates as a single aircraft with regard to navigation and position reporting. Separation between aircraft within the formation is the responsibility of the flight leader and the pilots of the other aircraft in the flight. This includes transition periods when aircraft within the formation are maneuvering to attain separation from each other to effect individual control and during join up or breakaway.\n\n1. Standard Formation. A formation in which a proximity of no more than 1 mile laterally or longitudinally and within 100 feet vertically from the flight leader is maintained by each wingman.\n\n2. Nonstandard Formation. A formation operating under any of the following conditions:\n\n1. When the flight leader has requested and air traffic control has approved other than standard formation dimensions.\n\n2. When operating within an authorized Altitude reservation or under the provisions of a letter of agreement.\n\n3. When the operations are conducted in airspace specifically designed for a special activity.\n\n== Formation Departure ==\nA formation departure consists of more than one aircraft at intervals of 1 minute or less which, by prior arrangement between the pilots, operate as a single aircraft with regard to navigation and position reporting. The departure portion of the flight may terminate at a preplanned breakup point which may be located up to, but not beyond, the planned initial level-off at cruise altitude.\n\n== Taxi Operations ==\n• Keep use of power to a minimum\n\n• Taxi with 20 – 25 knots at straight taxiway sections and turn with 10 – 15 knots\n\n• Avoid jet blast damage\n\n• Check nose wheel steering and brakes as you taxi out of the spot\n\n• Check the flight instrument in the turn onto the taxiway\n\n• Taxi only in authorized areas\n\n• Slow down and taxi on the centerline in congested areas\n\n• Do not taxi within 10 feet of any obstacle\n\n• Do not taxi within 25 feet without a wingwalker\n\n• If taxiing behind an aircraft, a minimum spacing of four ship lengths is necessary\n\n== Before Departure (procedure at LCC, EOR, BTI) ==\n\n=== Aircraft checks ===\n• Aircraft are checked, armed and dearmed on Arming / Dearming Pads (of LCC/EOR Pads)\n\n• Before departure, aircraft are checked and armed at the departure end EOR pad\n\n• After Arrival, aircraft are disarmed at the arrival end EOR pad\n\n\nLCC = Last Chance Check\n\nEOR = End of Runway\n\nBTI = Before Takeoff Inspection\n\n=== End of Runway (EOR) ===\n• After stopping EOR, confirm all weapons switches safe\n\n• Keep hands in full view during arming\n\n• Aircraft should not taxi in front of aircraft with forward firing ordnance being armed / dearmed\n\n\n• As soon as you stop in the arming area, check your GS (reads zero)\n\n• If GS is zero or one knot, the INS platform is good\n\n• If GS is two or more, accomplish another alignment if possible\n\n\n• Ensure Speed Brake Stowed\n\n• Review takeoff procedure and details as EP immediately after takeoff\n\n• Check other aircraft for leaks, loose panels, proper configuration, etc\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_21_05_47.png|alt=Example of EOR Lineup|border|frameless|691x691px]]\n\n== Departure Briefing ==\nIn the so-called “Departure Briefing”, which takes place on the EOR / LCC Pad prior to the lineup, the Flight Lead informs his Flight about all relevant information regarding departure. A Departure Briefing usually contains the following information:\n\n1. Which type of Lineup\n\n2. Type of Takeoff (Two Ship, Interval or Trail)\n\n3. Speeds\n\n4. Lost Wingmen Procedure\n\n5. Clearence for visual join-up in case of interval and trail departure\n\n6. Further / Additional information / structure\n\n\nExample of a Departure Briefing:\n\nWe´ll make a 3 ship echelon gear doors allignment for interval departure 5 seconds, (max Brake 140, min Go 150, Go 160 (are V speeds currently not used in SK), rotation speed 200, AB off at 300, contract speed 350, 5 nose up, in case of lost Wingmen turn right for 30 seconds unsing 30 AOB, inform Lead, transition to instruments, obtain seperate ATC clearence and attempt re-join, say when airborne, you´re cleared to join on the right, say when in position.\n\n== Runway Lineup ==\nGeneral:\n\n• Leader will direct the appropriate runway line up based on winds and runway width\n\n• Wingmen should line up ensuring wingtip clearance\n\nLineup Types:\n\n• Two Ship Lineup\n\n• Three Ship Echelon\n\n• Four Ship Echelon\n\n• Four Ship three in The Slot\n\n• Four Ship four in The Slot\n\n• Three Ship three in The Slot\n\nLineup Standards:\n\n• Runway lineup is determined by taking into account several factors:\n\no Wind direction and speed\n\no Direction of turn out of traffic\n\no Weather\n\n• Minimum wingtip spacing is wingtip clearance but may be wider as desired or required\n\n=== Two Ship Lineup ===\n• Lead takes the center of it´s half of the runway\n\n• The wingmen will line up lead´s main gear doors and take wingtip spacing as briefed\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_19_39_19.png|alt=Example Two Ship Lineup|frameless|692x692px]]\n\n=== Three ship echelon ===\n• Three ship formations may line up in echelon, if the runway is wide enough\n\n• “2” and “3” will line up gear doors and take wingtip spacing as briefed\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_19_46_25.png|frameless|753x753px]]\n\n=== Four Ship Echelon ===\n• Four ship may line up in echelon, if the runway is wide enough\n\n• “2” will line up gear doors and take wingtip spacing as briefed\n\n• “3” and “4” should align helmets of preceding flight members\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_19_51_04.png|frameless|628x628px]]\n\n=== Four Ship Three in The Slot ===\n• Lead should line up as far to the side of the runway as practical\n\n• “2” will place the wingtip closest to lead on the centerline an line up gear doors\n\n• “3” will line up between lead and “2”, maintaining nose-tail clearance out to 500 ft\n\n• “4” will line up offset from “2”s jet blast while aligning gear doors of “3\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_19_59_31.png|frameless|713x713px]]\n\n=== Four Ship Four in The Slot ===\n• Lead should line up as far to the side of the runway as practical\n\n• “2” will place the wingtip closest to lead on the center line and line up gear doors\n\n• “4” will pull in between lead and “2” while aligning gear doors\n\n• “3” will line up with wingtip clearance on “2” while aligning gear doors\n\n• “4” will pull in further to align tail fin or gear doors of “3”\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_20_06_54.png|frameless|651x651px]]\n\n=== Three Ship Three in The Slot ===\n• Lead should line up as far to the side of the runway as practical\n\n• “2” will place the wingtip closest to lead on the center line and line up gear doors\n\n• “3” will line up between lead and “2”, maintaining nose-tail clearance out to 500 ft\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_20_10_03.png|frameless|696x696px]]\n\n== Takeoff ==\n\n=== Takeoff Types: ===\n• Two Ship Takeoff\n\no Formation Takeoff\n\no Usable for two aircraft only\n\no Not usable during periods of high crosswind, low visibility, wet runway\n\n• Interval Departure\n\no Used whenever a two ship takeoff is not possible\n\no Separation assured by timing\n\no Rejoin required after departure\n\n• (Radar assisted) Trail Departure\n\no Used whenever a two ship takeoff is not possible\n\no A type of interval departure\n\no Separation assured by on board radar\n\n=== Two Ship Takeoff ===\n• Signal for brake release is a head nod or radio call\n\n• Leader will make a normal takeoff with throttle slightly retarded (power advantage\n\nfor Wingman)\n\n• Leader will pass a gear up and AB out signal, if appropriate\n\n• Wingman will maintain wingtip clearance throughout takeoff roll\n\n• If overrunning the leader, wingman will be directed to assume the lead while\n\ncontinuing takeoff\n\n• If either member or the element must abort, the other member should continue the\n\ntakeoff\n\n• In either case, stay on your side of the runway!\n\n=== Trail Departure ===\n• Used when conditions won´t permit a formation takeoff\n\n• Examples: Wet runways, crosswind limits, weapon loads, configuration differences, visibility\n\n\n• Set up radar for easy acquisition\n\no Select RWS Mode\n\n\n• Priority is to fly the aircraft, not operating the radar\n\n• Ignore the radar until all high priority tasks are completed\n\n\n• During climbout, establish and maintain briefed power setting and airspeed\n\n\n• Unless restricted by procedure or ATC, begin all climbs at the same time as the leader\n\n\n• Lead should call passing every 5000 ft increment until all flight members are tied/visual\n\n\n• Rule of thumb: For 30 AOB turns, allow preceding aircraft to drift 5 deg laterally for each mile of separation between aircraft, Example: to maintain a 2 NM trail position, allow the preceding aircraft to drift 10 deg before initiating your turn\n\n• Once a tied or visual call ha been made, advise the leader if either radar or visual contact is subsequently lost.\n\n=== Lost Wingman ===\nGeneral:\n\n• Transition to instruments\n\n• Notify Lead (“Lost Wingman”)\n\n• Execute lost wingman procedures\n\n• Obtain separate ATC clearance\n\n• Attempt rejoin\n\n• Flight lead: Acknowledge lost wingman, transmit altitude, speed, post, etc.\n\n\nWings level after departure:\n\n• Turn away using 15 deg angle of bank (AOB) for 15 seconds, ensure operation and obtain separate clearance\n\n\nWingman outside of the turn after departure:\n\n• Reverse the direction of the turn using 15 deg AOB for 15 seconds, ensure separation and obtain separate clearance\n\n\nWingman inside of the turn after departure:\n\n• Momentarily reduce power to ensure separation and inform lead to roll out, maintain bank to ensure lateral separation and obtain separate clearance\n\n\nFinal approach:\n\n• Momentarily turn away to ensure separation, commence the published missed approach procedure and obtain separate clearance\n\n\nMissed approach:\n\n• Momentarily turn away to ensure separation, continue the missed approach procedure while climbing 500 ft above missed approach altitude and get a separate clearance\n\n\nLocal procedures:\n\n• Sometimes local conditions require different lost wingman procedures\n\n== Rejoin ==\nPurpose:\n\n• The purpose of a rejoin is to get the flight back together safely and efficiently\n\n• Necessary after an interval / trail departure or lost wingman procedure\n\nBasics:\n\n• Lead should monitor wingmen during all rejoins\n\n• Airspeeds and bank angles are prebriefed or unit standard\n\n• Lead should make a radio call if flying different speed or bank angle\n\nRejoin Techniques\n\n• A radar lock on may be used during the rejoin to provide range and overtake info\n\n• Maintaining 50 – 100 Knots of true overtake will provide a controlled, expeditious\n\n\nrejoin\n\n• “3” and “4” should be locked to the leader\n\n• At 3000 ft reduce power smoothly to control overtake\n\n• At 1500 ft, true overtake should be 50 knots\n\n• Use speed brakes if overtake is excessive\n\n• If overtake is excessive approaching the extended fingertip position, initiate a controlled overshoot\n\n== Crossunder ==\nGeneral:\n\n• Used to move wingman to the other side\n\n\nTwo Ship Crossunder:\n\n• On lead´s signal wingman will reduce power\n\n• The wingman will move back and down below lead´s Plane of Motion (POM) and add power again to avoid falling further behind\n\n• Next, wingman moves across and behind lead with a minimum of nose-tail clearance\n\n• Add power as required to avoid falling further behind\n\n• Once on the opposite side and with wingtip clearance, wingman will add power to move up and forward into position\n\n\nFour Ship Fingertip Crossunder\n\n• If “2” is crossing to the side that “3” and “4” are on, “3” should move out to create room for “2”\n\n• “2” begins a normal crossunder (ensuring adequate spacing before crossing lead´s six o´clock)\n\n• If “3” and “4” are crossing simultaneously, “3” performs a normal crossunder on “2” and “1”\n\n• As “3” begins the crossunder, “4” performs a crossunder on “3”\n\n\nFour Ship Echelon Crossunder:\n\n• As “2” begins the crossunder, “3” performs a crossunder on “2”, “4” performs a crossunder on “3”\n\n\nVisual Signals\n\n• Crossunder may be initiated using a wing dip to the side the wingman is requested to move or after instruction on radio\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 10.10.2025 13 59 01.png|frameless|663x663px]]\n\n== Lead Changes ==\n• Lead changes require an unmistakable transfer of responsibilities from one flight member to another\n\n• The flight member assuming lead will be no further aft than normal route/fingertip\n\n• Lead changes only change “Nav Lead” responsibilities\n\n• Flight lead responsibilities cannot be transferred\n\n== Formation Turns ==\n\n=== Fingertip Turns ===\n• Wingman will maintain fingertip position using fingertip spacing reference\n\n• Always stay slightly below preceding aircraft\n\n• Fingertip turns are limited to approximately 30 deg AOB\n\n• Lead will avoid sudden or abrupt changes in AOB or back stick pressure\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 10.10.2025 14 25 57.png|frameless|663x663px]]\n\n=== Echelon Turn Into the Echelon ===\n• Turns into the echelon will be avoided if at all possible\n\n• Wingman will maintain fingertip position using fingertip spacing references\n\n• Turns are limit to very shallow turns\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_10.10.2025_14_34_38.png|frameless|656x656px]]\n\n=== Echelon Turns Away From the Echelon ===\n• Plan to limit AOB to 60 degrees maximum\n\n• All aircraft will match lead´s roll rates while maintaining the same horizontal plane\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 10.10.2025 14 38 58.png|frameless|656x656px]]\n\n=== Route Turns ===\n• When turned away from the wingman, will stack level with the leader\n\n• When turned into, the wingman will only stack down enough to keep lead in sightand avoid his maneuvering plane\n\n=== Route Echelon Turns ===\n• If a turn is made into the echelon, each aircraft will only stack down enough to keep rest of the formation in sight and avoid their maneuvering plane\n\n• On turns away from the echelon, all aircrafts will maintain the same horizontal plane\n\n== Channel Changes ==\n• When a channel / frequency change is directed, wingman will normally move out from fingertip to route or from echelon to route echelon\n\n== Leaving Formation ==\nLeave Formation:\n\n• When directed to do so\n\n• When you loose sight of the aircraft ahead (lost wingman, blind)\n\n• When you are unable to join up or to stay in formation without crossing over, under or in front of the aircraft ahead\n\n• At any other time you feel that your presence in the formation constitutes a hazard\n\n• When leaving a formation, clear yourself in the direction of your turn and notify\n\n\nleader\n\n• If you have lost sight, comply with the appropriate lost wingman procedure\n\n• Rejoin only by directed to do so by the flight lead\n\n== Formation Landing ==\nLead:\n\n• Approach speed should be consistent with heavier aircraft\n\n• Wingmen should be positioned upwind if crosswind component exceeds 5 knots\n\n• Plan to land near the center of your half of the runway\n\n== • Do not touchdown long and fast ==\n\n\nWingman:\n\n• Maintain a minimum of 10 feet lateral wingtip spacing\n\n• Stack level with lead as briefed when configured on final approach\n\n• Cross check the runway to ensure sufficient runway is available\n\n== Overhead Break ==\n• Used to split a formation in an orderly and expeditious manner to prepare for landing\n\n• Also called “360 overhead\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 10.10.2025 15 05 58.png|frameless|936x936px]]\n\n== Standard Formations ==\n\n=== Fingertip Formations ===\nUsage:\n\n• Weather penetration\n\n• Arfield arrivals / departures\n\n• Shoe formations\n\n\nAdvantages:\n\n• Lead aircraft is easy to see in bad weather\n\n• Close formation is used for formation takeoff / landing\n\n• Preferred before overhead break (360 overhead)\n\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n• Does not allow for much head down time\n\n• Limited maneuvering\n\n• Shallow turns (up to 30 deg AOB) only\n\n• Both aircraft are easily detected as one group by an adversary\n\n\nParameters:\n\n• Use fingertip reference points','utf-8'),
