(591,'== Formation ==\nA formation is defined as more than one aircraft that, by prior arrangement between the pilots, operates as a single aircraft with regard to navigation and position reporting. Separation between aircraft within the formation is the responsibility of the flight leader and the pilots of the other aircraft in the flight. This includes transition periods when aircraft within the formation are maneuvering to attain separation from each other to effect individual control and during join up or breakaway.\n\n1. Standard Formation. A formation in which a proximity of no more than 1 mile laterally or longitudinally and within 100 feet vertically from the flight leader is maintained by each wingman.\n\n2. Nonstandard Formation. A formation operating under any of the following conditions:\n\n1. When the flight leader has requested and air traffic control has approved other than standard formation dimensions.\n\n2. When operating within an authorized Altitude reservation or under the provisions of a letter of agreement.\n\n3. When the operations are conducted in airspace specifically designed for a special activity.\n\n=== Formation Responsibilities ===\nThe flight lead assigns responsibilities for each flight member. Dividing responsibilities ensures each pilot has a manageable number of tasks to perform. Flight member normal responsibilities are:\n\n• Number One: Primary planner and decision maker, primary navigation and radar lookout, visual lookout for mutual support of #2, and primary engaged fighter, if practical.\n\n• Number Two: Maintain formation position, visual lookout, mutual support of number one. Navigation position awareness, and radar awareness as other responsibilities allow.\n\n• Number Three: Support number one. Secondary planner and alternate decision maker, maintain support position for lead element, secondary navigation and radar monitor, visual lookout for number four, mutual support of the entire flight, and secondary engaged fighter, if practical.\n\n• Number Four: Maintain formation position, visual lookout for the flight, mutual support of number three. Navigation position awareness, and radar awareness as other responsibilities allow\n\n=== Time Sharing ===\nThe goal on every low level training mission should be to improve the development of this cross-check. Practice and discipline are essential to maximizing visual mutual support. On each mission, pilots must start with the basic NEAR ROCKS, FAR ROCKS, CHECK SIX pattern and build up the visual search arena as allowed by task saturation, threat and flight conditions. When encountering extremely rough terrain, defensive reactions, navigation turns, etc., drop the lowest priority sectors in order. There will be times, such as hard turns, when only NEAR ROCKS can be cross-checked. The key is to quickly re-establish the cross-check one sector at a time as tasks permit\n\n\n\n[[File:16V5.DOC - 16v5.pdf — Mozilla Firefox 08.07.2026 04 34 19.png|frameless|737x737px]]\n\n== Formation Departure ==\nA formation departure consists of more than one aircraft at intervals of 1 minute or less which, by prior arrangement between the pilots, operate as a single aircraft with regard to navigation and position reporting. The departure portion of the flight may terminate at a preplanned breakup point which may be located up to, but not beyond, the planned initial level-off at cruise altitude.\n\n== Taxi Operations ==\n• Keep use of power to a minimum\n\n• Taxi with 20 – 25 knots at straight taxiway sections and turn with 10 – 15 knots\n\n• Avoid jet blast damage\n\n• Check nose wheel steering and brakes as you taxi out of the spot\n\n• Check the flight instrument in the turn onto the taxiway\n\n• Taxi only in authorized areas\n\n• Slow down and taxi on the centerline in congested areas\n\n• Do not taxi within 10 feet of any obstacle\n\n• Do not taxi within 25 feet without a wingwalker\n\n• If taxiing behind an aircraft, a minimum spacing of four ship lengths is necessary\n\n== Before Departure (procedure at LCC, EOR, BTI) ==\n\n=== Aircraft checks ===\n• Aircraft are checked, armed and dearmed on Arming / Dearming Pads (of LCC/EOR Pads)\n\n• Before departure, aircraft are checked and armed at the departure end EOR pad\n\n• After Arrival, aircraft are disarmed at the arrival end EOR pad\n\n\nLCC = Last Chance Check\n\nEOR = End of Runway\n\nBTI = Before Takeoff Inspection\n\n=== End of Runway (EOR) ===\n• After stopping EOR, confirm all weapons switches safe\n\n• Keep hands in full view during arming\n\n• Aircraft should not taxi in front of aircraft with forward firing ordnance being armed / dearmed\n\n\n• As soon as you stop in the arming area, check your GS (reads zero)\n\n• If GS is zero or one knot, the INS platform is good\n\n• If GS is two or more, accomplish another alignment if possible\n\n\n• Ensure Speed Brake Stowed\n\n• Review takeoff procedure and details as EP immediately after takeoff\n\n• Check other aircraft for leaks, loose panels, proper configuration, etc\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_21_05_47.png|alt=Example of EOR Lineup|border|frameless|691x691px]]\n\n== Departure Briefing ==\nIn the so-called “Departure Briefing”, which takes place on the EOR / LCC Pad prior to the lineup, the Flight Lead informs his Flight about all relevant information regarding departure. A Departure Briefing usually contains the following information:\n\n1. Which type of Lineup\n\n2. Type of Takeoff (Two Ship, Interval or Trail)\n\n3. Speeds\n\n4. Lost Wingmen Procedure\n\n5. Clearence for visual join-up in case of interval and trail departure\n\n6. Further / Additional information / structure\n\n\nExample of a Departure Briefing:\n\nWe´ll make a 3 ship echelon gear doors allignment for interval departure 5 seconds, (max Brake 140, min Go 150, Go 160 (are V speeds currently not used in SK), rotation speed 200, AB off at 300, contract speed 350, 5 nose up, in case of lost Wingmen turn right for 30 seconds unsing 30 AOB, inform Lead, transition to instruments, obtain seperate ATC clearence and attempt re-join, say when airborne, you´re cleared to join on the right, say when in position.\n\n== Runway Lineup ==\nGeneral:\n\n• Leader will direct the appropriate runway line up based on winds and runway width\n\n• Wingmen should line up ensuring wingtip clearance\n\nLineup Types:\n\n• Two Ship Lineup\n\n• Three Ship Echelon\n\n• Four Ship Echelon\n\n• Four Ship three in The Slot\n\n• Four Ship four in The Slot\n\n• Three Ship three in The Slot\n\nLineup Standards:\n\n• Runway lineup is determined by taking into account several factors:\n\no Wind direction and speed\n\no Direction of turn out of traffic\n\no Weather\n\n• Minimum wingtip spacing is wingtip clearance but may be wider as desired or required\n\n=== Two Ship Lineup ===\n• Lead takes the center of it´s half of the runway\n\n• The wingmen will line up lead´s main gear doors and take wingtip spacing as briefed\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_19_39_19.png|alt=Example Two Ship Lineup|frameless|692x692px]]\n\n=== Three ship echelon ===\n• Three ship formations may line up in echelon, if the runway is wide enough\n\n• “2” and “3” will line up gear doors and take wingtip spacing as briefed\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_19_46_25.png|frameless|753x753px]]\n\n=== Four Ship Echelon ===\n• Four ship may line up in echelon, if the runway is wide enough\n\n• “2” will line up gear doors and take wingtip spacing as briefed\n\n• “3” and “4” should align helmets of preceding flight members\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_19_51_04.png|frameless|628x628px]]\n\n=== Four Ship Three in The Slot ===\n• Lead should line up as far to the side of the runway as practical\n\n• “2” will place the wingtip closest to lead on the centerline an line up gear doors\n\n• “3” will line up between lead and “2”, maintaining nose-tail clearance out to 500 ft\n\n• “4” will line up offset from “2”s jet blast while aligning gear doors of “3\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_19_59_31.png|frameless|713x713px]]\n\n=== Four Ship Four in The Slot ===\n• Lead should line up as far to the side of the runway as practical\n\n• “2” will place the wingtip closest to lead on the center line and line up gear doors\n\n• “4” will pull in between lead and “2” while aligning gear doors\n\n• “3” will line up with wingtip clearance on “2” while aligning gear doors\n\n• “4” will pull in further to align tail fin or gear doors of “3”\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_20_06_54.png|frameless|651x651px]]\n\n=== Three Ship Three in The Slot ===\n• Lead should line up as far to the side of the runway as practical\n\n• “2” will place the wingtip closest to lead on the center line and line up gear doors\n\n• “3” will line up between lead and “2”, maintaining nose-tail clearance out to 500 ft\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_20_10_03.png|frameless|696x696px]]\n\n== Takeoff ==\n\n=== Takeoff Types: ===\n• Two Ship Takeoff\n\no Formation Takeoff\n\no Usable for two aircraft only\n\no Not usable during periods of high crosswind, low visibility, wet runway\n\n• Interval Departure\n\no Used whenever a two ship takeoff is not possible\n\no Separation assured by timing\n\no Rejoin required after departure\n\n• (Radar assisted) Trail Departure\n\no Used whenever a two ship takeoff is not possible\n\no A type of interval departure\n\no Separation assured by on board radar\n\n=== Two Ship Takeoff ===\n• Signal for brake release is a head nod or radio call\n\n• Leader will make a normal takeoff with throttle slightly retarded (power advantage\n\nfor Wingman)\n\n• Leader will pass a gear up and AB out signal, if appropriate\n\n• Wingman will maintain wingtip clearance throughout takeoff roll\n\n• If overrunning the leader, wingman will be directed to assume the lead while\n\ncontinuing takeoff\n\n• If either member or the element must abort, the other member should continue the\n\ntakeoff\n\n• In either case, stay on your side of the runway!\n\n=== Trail Departure ===\n• Used when conditions won´t permit a formation takeoff\n\n• Examples: Wet runways, crosswind limits, weapon loads, configuration differences, visibility\n\n\n• Set up radar for easy acquisition\n\no Select RWS Mode\n\n\n• Priority is to fly the aircraft, not operating the radar\n\n• Ignore the radar until all high priority tasks are completed\n\n\n• During climbout, establish and maintain briefed power setting and airspeed\n\n\n• Unless restricted by procedure or ATC, begin all climbs at the same time as the leader\n\n\n• Lead should call passing every 5000 ft increment until all flight members are tied/visual\n\n\n• Rule of thumb: For 30 AOB turns, allow preceding aircraft to drift 5 deg laterally for each mile of separation between aircraft, Example: to maintain a 2 NM trail position, allow the preceding aircraft to drift 10 deg before initiating your turn\n\n• Once a tied or visual call ha been made, advise the leader if either radar or visual contact is subsequently lost.\n\n=== Lost Wingman ===\nGeneral:\n\n• Transition to instruments\n\n• Notify Lead (“Lost Wingman”)\n\n• Execute lost wingman procedures\n\n• Obtain separate ATC clearance\n\n• Attempt rejoin\n\n• Flight lead: Acknowledge lost wingman, transmit altitude, speed, post, etc.\n\n\nWings level after departure:\n\n• Turn away using 15 deg angle of bank (AOB) for 15 seconds, ensure operation and obtain separate clearance\n\n\nWingman outside of the turn after departure:\n\n• Reverse the direction of the turn using 15 deg AOB for 15 seconds, ensure separation and obtain separate clearance\n\n\nWingman inside of the turn after departure:\n\n• Momentarily reduce power to ensure separation and inform lead to roll out, maintain bank to ensure lateral separation and obtain separate clearance\n\n\nFinal approach:\n\n• Momentarily turn away to ensure separation, commence the published missed approach procedure and obtain separate clearance\n\n\nMissed approach:\n\n• Momentarily turn away to ensure separation, continue the missed approach procedure while climbing 500 ft above missed approach altitude and get a separate clearance\n\n\nLocal procedures:\n\n• Sometimes local conditions require different lost wingman procedures\n\n== Rejoin ==\nPurpose:\n\n• The purpose of a rejoin is to get the flight back together safely and efficiently\n\n• Necessary after an interval / trail departure or lost wingman procedure\n\nBasics:\n\n• Lead should monitor wingmen during all rejoins\n\n• Airspeeds and bank angles are prebriefed or unit standard\n\n• Lead should make a radio call if flying different speed or bank angle\n\nRejoin Techniques\n\n• A radar lock on may be used during the rejoin to provide range and overtake info\n\n• Maintaining 50 – 100 Knots of true overtake will provide a controlled, expeditious\n\n\nrejoin\n\n• “3” and “4” should be locked to the leader\n\n• At 3000 ft reduce power smoothly to control overtake\n\n• At 1500 ft, true overtake should be 50 knots\n\n• Use speed brakes if overtake is excessive\n\n• If overtake is excessive approaching the extended fingertip position, initiate a controlled overshoot\n\n== Crossunder ==\nGeneral:\n\n• Used to move wingman to the other side\n\n\nTwo Ship Crossunder:\n\n• On lead´s signal wingman will reduce power\n\n• The wingman will move back and down below lead´s Plane of Motion (POM) and add power again to avoid falling further behind\n\n• Next, wingman moves across and behind lead with a minimum of nose-tail clearance\n\n• Add power as required to avoid falling further behind\n\n• Once on the opposite side and with wingtip clearance, wingman will add power to move up and forward into position\n\n\nFour Ship Fingertip Crossunder\n\n• If “2” is crossing to the side that “3” and “4” are on, “3” should move out to create room for “2”\n\n• “2” begins a normal crossunder (ensuring adequate spacing before crossing lead´s six o´clock)\n\n• If “3” and “4” are crossing simultaneously, “3” performs a normal crossunder on “2” and “1”\n\n• As “3” begins the crossunder, “4” performs a crossunder on “3”\n\n\nFour Ship Echelon Crossunder:\n\n• As “2” begins the crossunder, “3” performs a crossunder on “2”, “4” performs a crossunder on “3”\n\n\nVisual Signals\n\n• Crossunder may be initiated using a wing dip to the side the wingman is requested to move or after instruction on radio\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 10.10.2025 13 59 01.png|frameless|663x663px]]\n\n== Lead Changes ==\n• Lead changes require an unmistakable transfer of responsibilities from one flight member to another\n\n• The flight member assuming lead will be no further aft than normal route/fingertip\n\n• Lead changes only change “Nav Lead” responsibilities\n\n• Flight lead responsibilities cannot be transferred\n\n== Formation Turns ==\n\n=== Fingertip Turns ===\n• Wingman will maintain fingertip position using fingertip spacing reference\n\n• Always stay slightly below preceding aircraft\n\n• Fingertip turns are limited to approximately 30 deg AOB\n\n• Lead will avoid sudden or abrupt changes in AOB or back stick pressure\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 10.10.2025 14 25 57.png|frameless|663x663px]]\n\n=== Echelon Turn Into the Echelon ===\n• Turns into the echelon will be avoided if at all possible\n\n• Wingman will maintain fingertip position using fingertip spacing references\n\n• Turns are limit to very shallow turns\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_10.10.2025_14_34_38.png|frameless|656x656px]]\n\n=== Echelon Turns Away From the Echelon ===\n• Plan to limit AOB to 60 degrees maximum\n\n• All aircraft will match lead´s roll rates while maintaining the same horizontal plane\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 10.10.2025 14 38 58.png|frameless|656x656px]]\n\n=== Route Turns ===\n• When turned away from the wingman, will stack level with the leader\n\n• When turned into, the wingman will only stack down enough to keep lead in sightand avoid his maneuvering plane\n\n=== Route Echelon Turns ===\n• If a turn is made into the echelon, each aircraft will only stack down enough to keep rest of the formation in sight and avoid their maneuvering plane\n\n• On turns away from the echelon, all aircrafts will maintain the same horizontal plane\n\n== Channel Changes ==\n• When a channel / frequency change is directed, wingman will normally move out from fingertip to route or from echelon to route echelon\n\n== Leaving Formation ==\nLeave Formation:\n\n• When directed to do so\n\n• When you loose sight of the aircraft ahead (lost wingman, blind)\n\n• When you are unable to join up or to stay in formation without crossing over, under or in front of the aircraft ahead\n\n• At any other time you feel that your presence in the formation constitutes a hazard\n\n• When leaving a formation, clear yourself in the direction of your turn and notify\n\n\nleader\n\n• If you have lost sight, comply with the appropriate lost wingman procedure\n\n• Rejoin only by directed to do so by the flight lead\n\n== Formation Landing ==\nLead:\n\n• Approach speed should be consistent with heavier aircraft\n\n• Wingmen should be positioned upwind if crosswind component exceeds 5 knots\n\n• Plan to land near the center of your half of the runway\n\n• Do not touchdown long and fast\n\n\n\nWingman:\n\n• Maintain a minimum of 10 feet lateral wingtip spacing\n\n• Stack level with lead as briefed when configured on final approach\n\n• Cross check the runway to ensure sufficient runway is available\n\n== Overhead Break ==\n• Used to split a formation in an orderly and expeditious manner to prepare for landing\n\n• Also called “360 overhead\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 10.10.2025 15 05 58.png|frameless|936x936px]]\n\n== Standard Formations ==\n\n=== Fingertip Formations ===\nUsage:\n\n• Weather penetration\n\n• Arfield arrivals / departures\n\n• Shoe formations\n\n\n\nAdvantages:\n\n• Lead aircraft is easy to see in bad weather\n\n• Close formation is used for formation takeoff / landing\n\n• Preferred before overhead break (360 overhead)\n\n\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n• Does not allow for much head down time\n\n• Limited maneuvering\n\n• Shallow turns (up to 30 deg AOB) only\n\n• Both aircraft are easily detected as one group by an adversary\n\n\n\nParameters:\n\n• Use fingertip reference points\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 10.10.2025 15 20 01.png|frameless|330x330px]]\n\n=== Finger four (Four Ship Fingertip) ===\nUsage:\n\n• Weather penetration\n\n• Show formations\n\n\nAdvantages:\n\n• Lead aircraft is easy to see in bad weather\n\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n• Does not allow for much heads down time\n\n• Limited maneuvering\n\n• Shallow turns (up to 30 deg AOB) only\n\n• All aircraft are easily detected as a group by an adversary\n\n\nParameters:\n\n• Same as fingertip\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 13 45 26.png|frameless|463x463px]]\n\n== Route Formations ==\nUsage:\n\n• Enroute\n\n\nAdvantages\n\n• Lead aircraft is easy to see in moderate weather\n\n• Easy to fly, allowing for a good amount of heads down time\n\n• Steeper turns up to 60 deg AOB possible\n\n• Easier to fly in a low altitude environment\n\n\nDisadvantages\n\n• All aircraft are easily detected as one group by an adversary\n\n• Limited engagement flexibility\n\n\nParameters:\n\n• Modified fingertip\n\n• Up to 500 feet between aircraft\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 13 54 38.png|frameless|583x583px]]\n\n=== Route Echelon ===\nUsage:\n\n• Enroute\n\n\nAdvantages:\n\n• Lead aircraft is easy to see in moderate weather\n\n• Easy to fly, allowing for a good amount of heads down time\n\n• Steeper turns up to 60 deg AOB possible\n\n• Easier to fly in a low altitude environment\n\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n• All aircraft are easily detected as one group by an adversary\n\n• Limited engagement flexibility\n\n• Turns into the echelon have to be shallow (max. 30 deg AOB)\n\n\nParameters:\n\n• Modified echelon\n\n• Up to 500 feet between aircraft\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 13 59 02.png|frameless|513x513px]]\n\n== Trail Formations ==\n\n=== Close Trail Formation ===\nUsage:\n\n• Tactical, Whenever necessary (e.g. Very low level)\n\n\nParameters:\n\n• One to two ship lengths behind lead aircraft\n\n• Below lead aircraft´s jetwash\n\n=== Trail Formations ===\nUsage:\n\n• Tactical, Whenever necessary (e.g. Very low level)\n\n\nParameters:\n\n• Flown in a cone 30 deg – 60 deg aft of lead\n\n• Range briefed by flight lead\n\n• Avoid flying at lead´s high six\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 14 06 03.png|frameless|503x503px]]\n\n== Non-Standard Formation ==\n\n=== Fighting Wings ===\nUsage:\n\n• Tactical\n\n\nAdvantages:\n\n• Helps to maintain flight integrity in bad weather or rough terrain\n\n• Allows for heads down time\n\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n• Poor 6 o´clock coverage\n\n• Easily detectable as bandit\n\n\nParameters:\n\n• 30 – 60 deg aft of 3/9 line\n\n• 30 – 60 deg above or below\n\n• 500 – 3000 ft spacing\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 14 11 11.png|frameless|666x666px]]\n\n=== Wedge ===\nUsage:\n\n• Tactical\n\n\nAdvantages:\n\n• Leader protected on 6 o´clock position\n\n• Leader free to maneuvering\n\n• Wingman may always switch sides\n\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n• No 6 o´clock protection for wingman\n\n• Lead changes difficult\n\n\nParameters:\n\n• 30 – 60 deg aft of 3/9 line\n\n• 4000 – 6000 ft spacing\n\n\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 14 18 22.png|frameless|417x417px]]\n\n=== Fluid Four ===\nUsage:\n\n• Tactical\n\n\nAdvantages:\n\n• Wingman kept close\n\n• Good maneuverability\n\n• Concentration of force\n\n• Easily converts to three ship if required\n\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n• Adversary can aquire all four aircraft\n\n• Defensive maneuvering confusing\n\n• Difficult in low altitude rough terrain\n\n\nParameters:\n\nTwo times fighting wing\n\n6000 – 9000 ft separated\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 14 58 09.png|frameless|620x620px]]\n\n=== Four Ship Wedge ===\nUsage:\n\n• Tactical\n\n\nAdvantages:\n\n• Very offensive for threats forward of 3/9 L\n\n• Easy to fly\n\n• Formation in bad weather / rough terrain\n\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n• Poor 6 o´clock lookout\n\n• Formation easily detected by bandit\n\n• Limited defensive maneuvering flexibility\n\n\nParameters:\n\n• Two times wedge\n\n• 1,5 – 3NM separated\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 15 04 22.png|frameless|419x419px]]\n\n=== Spread Four ===\nUsage:\n\n• Tactical\n\n\nAdvantages:\n\n• Difficult to aquire entire flight visually\n\n• Maximized fire power for BVR\n\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n• Maneuvering difficult\n\n• Very difficult to fly at low altitude\n\n\nParameters:\n\n• “1” and “2” 6000 – 9000 ft apart\n\n• “2” positioned 0 – 30 deg behind 3/9 of “1”\n\n• “1” and “3” 6000 – 9000 ft spread\n\n• “3” and “4” 6000 – 9000 ft apart\n\n• “4” positioned 0 – 30 deg behind 3/9 of “3”\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 15 13 03.png|frameless|804x804px]]\n\n=== Box Formation# ===\nUsage:\n\n• Tactical\n\n\nAdvantages:\n\n• Difficult to detect simultaneously by bandit\n\n• Easy support\n\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n• Difficult to maintain formation integrity\n\n\nParameters:\n\n• Two times spread\n\n• 1,5 – 3 NM separated\n\n• Variation: Four Ship Offset Box\n\n\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 15 18 55.png|frameless|397x397px]]\n\n=== Arrowhead Formation ===\nUsage:\n\n• Tactical\n\n\nAdvantages:\n\n• Same as box\n\n• Easier to fly as “2” allowing for more look out\n\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n• Same as box\n\n\nParameters:\n\n• Aircraft “3” and “4” fly spread 1,5 – 3 NM behind “1!”\n\n• Aircraft “2” 30 – 60 deg behind leader´s 3/9 line and 3000 ft spacing\n\n\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 16 14 37.png|frameless|589x589px]]\n\n=== Three Ship Vic ===\nUsage:\n\n• Tactical\n\n\nParameters:\n\n• Leader 1,5 – 3 NM in front of “2” and “3”\n\n• “2” and “3” 6000 – 9000 ft apart\n\n\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 16 18 26.png|frameless|419x419px]]\n\n== Line Abreast (Spread) ==\nUsage:\n\n• Tactical\n\n• Enroute\n\n\nAdvantages:\n\n• Minimizes the chances of simultaneous detection by bandit\n\n• Ordnance can be released quickly\n\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n• Hard to fly for unexperienced wingman\n\n\nParameters:\n\n• 0 – 20 deg aft\n\n• 4000 – 12000 ft spacing\n\n• Between 30 deg above and below\n\n• Optimum 0 deg and 6000 – 9000 ft\n\n• Or as briefed\n\n\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 14 25 43.png|frameless|616x616px]]\n\n\n== Line Abreast Turns ==\n\n=== Tac- and Hook Turns ===\nExecution TAC Turn:\n\nA TAC Turn is a delayed ninety 90 turn either to right or left. Depending on the direction of turn, the aircraft outside the turn turns first. The turn of the inner / second aircraft turning is delayed by a visual reference. Once the nose of the first turning aircraft is coming around to point on the second, the second aircraft starts its turn.\n\nThe TAC Turn is flown in full military Power with sustained altitude and speed.\n\n\nExecution Hook Turn:\n\nA Hook turn is a simultaneous 180 degree turn either to the left or rigt. It is flown in full military Power with sunstained altitude and speed.\n\n[[File:16V5.DOC - 16v5.pdf — Mozilla Firefox 08.07.2026 04 40 52.png|frameless|783x783px]]\n\n=== Delayed 45 Degree Turns, Crossturns and Check Turns ===\nExecution Delayed 45 Degree Turn:\n\nA Delayed 45 Degree turn is self explained by its name. It is flown in full military power with susatined speed and altitude.\n\nDepending on the direction of turn, the inner aircraft starts its turn when crossing the outer aircrafts flightpath.\n\n\nExecution Crossturn:\n\nIs a Hook turn with both aircrafts turning into each other. The same parameters as for a normal Hook turn are required.\n\n\nExecution Check Turn:\n\nCheck Turns are Turns for Heading adjustments that are smaller than 45 Degrees. They are flown with a desired power setting but with sustained altitude. A slight formation offset is required. The lead will adjust the heading by gently turning into the choosen direction.\n\n\n\n[[File:16V5.DOC - 16v5.pdf — Mozilla Firefox 08.07.2026 04 42 04.png|frameless|791x791px]]\nWho ever menages to come to this point, my congratulations. You are now a lot smarter and allowed to screenshot this and send it into \"genaral\"\nIceman','utf-8'),
(592,'== Formation ==\nA formation is defined as more than one aircraft that, by prior arrangement between the pilots, operates as a single aircraft with regard to navigation and position reporting. Separation between aircraft within the formation is the responsibility of the flight leader and the pilots of the other aircraft in the flight. This includes transition periods when aircraft within the formation are maneuvering to attain separation from each other to effect individual control and during join up or breakaway.\n\n1. Standard Formation. A formation in which a proximity of no more than 1 mile laterally or longitudinally and within 100 feet vertically from the flight leader is maintained by each wingman.\n\n2. Nonstandard Formation. A formation operating under any of the following conditions:\n\n1. When the flight leader has requested and air traffic control has approved other than standard formation dimensions.\n\n2. When operating within an authorized Altitude reservation or under the provisions of a letter of agreement.\n\n3. When the operations are conducted in airspace specifically designed for a special activity.\n\n=== Formation Responsibilities ===\nThe flight lead assigns responsibilities for each flight member. Dividing responsibilities ensures each pilot has a manageable number of tasks to perform. Flight member normal responsibilities are:\n\n• Number One: Primary planner and decision maker, primary navigation and radar lookout, visual lookout for mutual support of #2, and primary engaged fighter, if practical.\n\n• Number Two: Maintain formation position, visual lookout, mutual support of number one. Navigation position awareness, and radar awareness as other responsibilities allow.\n\n• Number Three: Support number one. Secondary planner and alternate decision maker, maintain support position for lead element, secondary navigation and radar monitor, visual lookout for number four, mutual support of the entire flight, and secondary engaged fighter, if practical.\n\n• Number Four: Maintain formation position, visual lookout for the flight, mutual support of number three. Navigation position awareness, and radar awareness as other responsibilities allow\n\n=== Time Sharing ===\nThe goal on every low level training mission should be to improve the development of this cross-check. Practice and discipline are essential to maximizing visual mutual support. On each mission, pilots must start with the basic NEAR ROCKS, FAR ROCKS, CHECK SIX pattern and build up the visual search arena as allowed by task saturation, threat and flight conditions. When encountering extremely rough terrain, defensive reactions, navigation turns, etc., drop the lowest priority sectors in order. There will be times, such as hard turns, when only NEAR ROCKS can be cross-checked. The key is to quickly re-establish the cross-check one sector at a time as tasks permit\n\n\n\n[[File:16V5.DOC - 16v5.pdf — Mozilla Firefox 08.07.2026 04 34 19.png|frameless|737x737px]]\n\n== Formation Departure ==\nA formation departure consists of more than one aircraft at intervals of 1 minute or less which, by prior arrangement between the pilots, operate as a single aircraft with regard to navigation and position reporting. The departure portion of the flight may terminate at a preplanned breakup point which may be located up to, but not beyond, the planned initial level-off at cruise altitude.\n\n== Taxi Operations ==\n• Keep use of power to a minimum\n\n• Taxi with 20 – 25 knots at straight taxiway sections and turn with 10 – 15 knots\n\n• Avoid jet blast damage\n\n• Check nose wheel steering and brakes as you taxi out of the spot\n\n• Check the flight instrument in the turn onto the taxiway\n\n• Taxi only in authorized areas\n\n• Slow down and taxi on the centerline in congested areas\n\n• Do not taxi within 10 feet of any obstacle\n\n• Do not taxi within 25 feet without a wingwalker\n\n• If taxiing behind an aircraft, a minimum spacing of four ship lengths is necessary\n\n== Before Departure (procedure at LCC, EOR, BTI) ==\n\n=== Aircraft checks ===\n• Aircraft are checked, armed and dearmed on Arming / Dearming Pads (of LCC/EOR Pads)\n\n• Before departure, aircraft are checked and armed at the departure end EOR pad\n\n• After Arrival, aircraft are disarmed at the arrival end EOR pad\n\n\nLCC = Last Chance Check\n\nEOR = End of Runway\n\nBTI = Before Takeoff Inspection\n\n=== End of Runway (EOR) ===\n• After stopping EOR, confirm all weapons switches safe\n\n• Keep hands in full view during arming\n\n• Aircraft should not taxi in front of aircraft with forward firing ordnance being armed / dearmed\n\n\n• As soon as you stop in the arming area, check your GS (reads zero)\n\n• If GS is zero or one knot, the INS platform is good\n\n• If GS is two or more, accomplish another alignment if possible\n\n\n• Ensure Speed Brake Stowed\n\n• Review takeoff procedure and details as EP immediately after takeoff\n\n• Check other aircraft for leaks, loose panels, proper configuration, etc\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_21_05_47.png|alt=Example of EOR Lineup|border|frameless|691x691px]]\n\n== Departure Briefing ==\nIn the so-called “Departure Briefing”, which takes place on the EOR / LCC Pad prior to the lineup, the Flight Lead informs his Flight about all relevant information regarding departure. A Departure Briefing usually contains the following information:\n\n1. Which type of Lineup\n\n2. Type of Takeoff (Two Ship, Interval or Trail)\n\n3. Speeds\n\n4. Lost Wingmen Procedure\n\n5. Clearence for visual join-up in case of interval and trail departure\n\n6. Further / Additional information / structure\n\n\nExample of a Departure Briefing:\n\nWe´ll make a 3 ship echelon gear doors allignment for interval departure 5 seconds, (max Brake 140, min Go 150, Go 160 (are V speeds currently not used in SK), rotation speed 200, AB off at 300, contract speed 350, 5 nose up, in case of lost Wingmen turn right for 30 seconds unsing 30 AOB, inform Lead, transition to instruments, obtain seperate ATC clearence and attempt re-join, say when airborne, you´re cleared to join on the right, say when in position.\n\n== Runway Lineup ==\nGeneral:\n\n• Leader will direct the appropriate runway line up based on winds and runway width\n\n• Wingmen should line up ensuring wingtip clearance\n\nLineup Types:\n\n• Two Ship Lineup\n\n• Three Ship Echelon\n\n• Four Ship Echelon\n\n• Four Ship three in The Slot\n\n• Four Ship four in The Slot\n\n• Three Ship three in The Slot\n\nLineup Standards:\n\n• Runway lineup is determined by taking into account several factors:\n\no Wind direction and speed\n\no Direction of turn out of traffic\n\no Weather\n\n• Minimum wingtip spacing is wingtip clearance but may be wider as desired or required\n\n=== Two Ship Lineup ===\n• Lead takes the center of it´s half of the runway\n\n• The wingmen will line up lead´s main gear doors and take wingtip spacing as briefed\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_19_39_19.png|alt=Example Two Ship Lineup|frameless|692x692px]]\n\n=== Three ship echelon ===\n• Three ship formations may line up in echelon, if the runway is wide enough\n\n• “2” and “3” will line up gear doors and take wingtip spacing as briefed\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_19_46_25.png|frameless|753x753px]]\n\n=== Four Ship Echelon ===\n• Four ship may line up in echelon, if the runway is wide enough\n\n• “2” will line up gear doors and take wingtip spacing as briefed\n\n• “3” and “4” should align helmets of preceding flight members\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_19_51_04.png|frameless|628x628px]]\n\n=== Four Ship Three in The Slot ===\n• Lead should line up as far to the side of the runway as practical\n\n• “2” will place the wingtip closest to lead on the centerline an line up gear doors\n\n• “3” will line up between lead and “2”, maintaining nose-tail clearance out to 500 ft\n\n• “4” will line up offset from “2”s jet blast while aligning gear doors of “3\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_19_59_31.png|frameless|713x713px]]\n\n=== Four Ship Four in The Slot ===\n• Lead should line up as far to the side of the runway as practical\n\n• “2” will place the wingtip closest to lead on the center line and line up gear doors\n\n• “4” will pull in between lead and “2” while aligning gear doors\n\n• “3” will line up with wingtip clearance on “2” while aligning gear doors\n\n• “4” will pull in further to align tail fin or gear doors of “3”\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_20_06_54.png|frameless|651x651px]]\n\n=== Three Ship Three in The Slot ===\n• Lead should line up as far to the side of the runway as practical\n\n• “2” will place the wingtip closest to lead on the center line and line up gear doors\n\n• “3” will line up between lead and “2”, maintaining nose-tail clearance out to 500 ft\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_20_10_03.png|frameless|696x696px]]\n\n== Takeoff ==\n\n=== Takeoff Types: ===\n• Two Ship Takeoff\n\no Formation Takeoff\n\no Usable for two aircraft only\n\no Not usable during periods of high crosswind, low visibility, wet runway\n\n• Interval Departure\n\no Used whenever a two ship takeoff is not possible\n\no Separation assured by timing\n\no Rejoin required after departure\n\n• (Radar assisted) Trail Departure\n\no Used whenever a two ship takeoff is not possible\n\no A type of interval departure\n\no Separation assured by on board radar\n\n=== Two Ship Takeoff ===\n• Signal for brake release is a head nod or radio call\n\n• Leader will make a normal takeoff with throttle slightly retarded (power advantage\n\nfor Wingman)\n\n• Leader will pass a gear up and AB out signal, if appropriate\n\n• Wingman will maintain wingtip clearance throughout takeoff roll\n\n• If overrunning the leader, wingman will be directed to assume the lead while\n\ncontinuing takeoff\n\n• If either member or the element must abort, the other member should continue the\n\ntakeoff\n\n• In either case, stay on your side of the runway!\n\n=== Trail Departure ===\n• Used when conditions won´t permit a formation takeoff\n\n• Examples: Wet runways, crosswind limits, weapon loads, configuration differences, visibility\n\n\n• Set up radar for easy acquisition\n\no Select RWS Mode\n\n\n• Priority is to fly the aircraft, not operating the radar\n\n• Ignore the radar until all high priority tasks are completed\n\n\n• During climbout, establish and maintain briefed power setting and airspeed\n\n\n• Unless restricted by procedure or ATC, begin all climbs at the same time as the leader\n\n\n• Lead should call passing every 5000 ft increment until all flight members are tied/visual\n\n\n• Rule of thumb: For 30 AOB turns, allow preceding aircraft to drift 5 deg laterally for each mile of separation between aircraft, Example: to maintain a 2 NM trail position, allow the preceding aircraft to drift 10 deg before initiating your turn\n\n• Once a tied or visual call ha been made, advise the leader if either radar or visual contact is subsequently lost.\n\n=== Lost Wingman ===\nGeneral:\n\n• Transition to instruments\n\n• Notify Lead (“Lost Wingman”)\n\n• Execute lost wingman procedures\n\n• Obtain separate ATC clearance\n\n• Attempt rejoin\n\n• Flight lead: Acknowledge lost wingman, transmit altitude, speed, post, etc.\n\n\nWings level after departure:\n\n• Turn away using 15 deg angle of bank (AOB) for 15 seconds, ensure operation and obtain separate clearance\n\n\nWingman outside of the turn after departure:\n\n• Reverse the direction of the turn using 15 deg AOB for 15 seconds, ensure separation and obtain separate clearance\n\n\nWingman inside of the turn after departure:\n\n• Momentarily reduce power to ensure separation and inform lead to roll out, maintain bank to ensure lateral separation and obtain separate clearance\n\n\nFinal approach:\n\n• Momentarily turn away to ensure separation, commence the published missed approach procedure and obtain separate clearance\n\n\nMissed approach:\n\n• Momentarily turn away to ensure separation, continue the missed approach procedure while climbing 500 ft above missed approach altitude and get a separate clearance\n\n\nLocal procedures:\n\n• Sometimes local conditions require different lost wingman procedures\n\n== Rejoin ==\nPurpose:\n\n• The purpose of a rejoin is to get the flight back together safely and efficiently\n\n• Necessary after an interval / trail departure or lost wingman procedure\n\nBasics:\n\n• Lead should monitor wingmen during all rejoins\n\n• Airspeeds and bank angles are prebriefed or unit standard\n\n• Lead should make a radio call if flying different speed or bank angle\n\nRejoin Techniques\n\n• A radar lock on may be used during the rejoin to provide range and overtake info\n\n• Maintaining 50 – 100 Knots of true overtake will provide a controlled, expeditious\n\n\nrejoin\n\n• “3” and “4” should be locked to the leader\n\n• At 3000 ft reduce power smoothly to control overtake\n\n• At 1500 ft, true overtake should be 50 knots\n\n• Use speed brakes if overtake is excessive\n\n• If overtake is excessive approaching the extended fingertip position, initiate a controlled overshoot\n\n== Crossunder ==\nGeneral:\n\n• Used to move wingman to the other side\n\n\nTwo Ship Crossunder:\n\n• On lead´s signal wingman will reduce power\n\n• The wingman will move back and down below lead´s Plane of Motion (POM) and add power again to avoid falling further behind\n\n• Next, wingman moves across and behind lead with a minimum of nose-tail clearance\n\n• Add power as required to avoid falling further behind\n\n• Once on the opposite side and with wingtip clearance, wingman will add power to move up and forward into position\n\n\nFour Ship Fingertip Crossunder\n\n• If “2” is crossing to the side that “3” and “4” are on, “3” should move out to create room for “2”\n\n• “2” begins a normal crossunder (ensuring adequate spacing before crossing lead´s six o´clock)\n\n• If “3” and “4” are crossing simultaneously, “3” performs a normal crossunder on “2” and “1”\n\n• As “3” begins the crossunder, “4” performs a crossunder on “3”\n\n\nFour Ship Echelon Crossunder:\n\n• As “2” begins the crossunder, “3” performs a crossunder on “2”, “4” performs a crossunder on “3”\n\n\nVisual Signals\n\n• Crossunder may be initiated using a wing dip to the side the wingman is requested to move or after instruction on radio\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 10.10.2025 13 59 01.png|frameless|663x663px]]\n\n== Lead Changes ==\n• Lead changes require an unmistakable transfer of responsibilities from one flight member to another\n\n• The flight member assuming lead will be no further aft than normal route/fingertip\n\n• Lead changes only change “Nav Lead” responsibilities\n\n• Flight lead responsibilities cannot be transferred\n\n== Formation Turns ==\n\n=== Fingertip Turns ===\n• Wingman will maintain fingertip position using fingertip spacing reference\n\n• Always stay slightly below preceding aircraft\n\n• Fingertip turns are limited to approximately 30 deg AOB\n\n• Lead will avoid sudden or abrupt changes in AOB or back stick pressure\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 10.10.2025 14 25 57.png|frameless|663x663px]]\n\n=== Echelon Turn Into the Echelon ===\n• Turns into the echelon will be avoided if at all possible\n\n• Wingman will maintain fingertip position using fingertip spacing references\n\n• Turns are limit to very shallow turns\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_10.10.2025_14_34_38.png|frameless|656x656px]]\n\n=== Echelon Turns Away From the Echelon ===\n• Plan to limit AOB to 60 degrees maximum\n\n• All aircraft will match lead´s roll rates while maintaining the same horizontal plane\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 10.10.2025 14 38 58.png|frameless|656x656px]]\n\n=== Route Turns ===\n• When turned away from the wingman, will stack level with the leader\n\n• When turned into, the wingman will only stack down enough to keep lead in sightand avoid his maneuvering plane\n\n=== Route Echelon Turns ===\n• If a turn is made into the echelon, each aircraft will only stack down enough to keep rest of the formation in sight and avoid their maneuvering plane\n\n• On turns away from the echelon, all aircrafts will maintain the same horizontal plane\n\n== Channel Changes ==\n• When a channel / frequency change is directed, wingman will normally move out from fingertip to route or from echelon to route echelon\n\n== Leaving Formation ==\nLeave Formation:\n\n• When directed to do so\n\n• When you loose sight of the aircraft ahead (lost wingman, blind)\n\n• When you are unable to join up or to stay in formation without crossing over, under or in front of the aircraft ahead\n\n• At any other time you feel that your presence in the formation constitutes a hazard\n\n• When leaving a formation, clear yourself in the direction of your turn and notify\n\n\nleader\n\n• If you have lost sight, comply with the appropriate lost wingman procedure\n\n• Rejoin only by directed to do so by the flight lead\n\n== Formation Landing ==\nLead:\n\n• Approach speed should be consistent with heavier aircraft\n\n• Wingmen should be positioned upwind if crosswind component exceeds 5 knots\n\n• Plan to land near the center of your half of the runway\n\n• Do not touchdown long and fast\n\n\n\nWingman:\n\n• Maintain a minimum of 10 feet lateral wingtip spacing\n\n• Stack level with lead as briefed when configured on final approach\n\n• Cross check the runway to ensure sufficient runway is available\n\n== Overhead Break ==\n• Used to split a formation in an orderly and expeditious manner to prepare for landing\n\n• Also called “360 overhead\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 10.10.2025 15 05 58.png|frameless|936x936px]]\n\n== Standard Formations ==\n\n=== Fingertip Formations ===\nUsage:\n\n• Weather penetration\n\n• Arfield arrivals / departures\n\n• Shoe formations\n\n\n\nAdvantages:\n\n• Lead aircraft is easy to see in bad weather\n\n• Close formation is used for formation takeoff / landing\n\n• Preferred before overhead break (360 overhead)\n\n\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n• Does not allow for much head down time\n\n• Limited maneuvering\n\n• Shallow turns (up to 30 deg AOB) only\n\n• Both aircraft are easily detected as one group by an adversary\n\n\n\nParameters:\n\n• Use fingertip reference points\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 10.10.2025 15 20 01.png|frameless|330x330px]]\n\n=== Finger four (Four Ship Fingertip) ===\nUsage:\n\n• Weather penetration\n\n• Show formations\n\n\nAdvantages:\n\n• Lead aircraft is easy to see in bad weather\n\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n• Does not allow for much heads down time\n\n• Limited maneuvering\n\n• Shallow turns (up to 30 deg AOB) only\n\n• All aircraft are easily detected as a group by an adversary\n\n\nParameters:\n\n• Same as fingertip\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 13 45 26.png|frameless|463x463px]]\n\n== Route Formations ==\nUsage:\n\n• Enroute\n\n\nAdvantages\n\n• Lead aircraft is easy to see in moderate weather\n\n• Easy to fly, allowing for a good amount of heads down time\n\n• Steeper turns up to 60 deg AOB possible\n\n• Easier to fly in a low altitude environment\n\n\nDisadvantages\n\n• All aircraft are easily detected as one group by an adversary\n\n• Limited engagement flexibility\n\n\nParameters:\n\n• Modified fingertip\n\n• Up to 500 feet between aircraft\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 13 54 38.png|frameless|583x583px]]\n\n=== Route Echelon ===\nUsage:\n\n• Enroute\n\n\nAdvantages:\n\n• Lead aircraft is easy to see in moderate weather\n\n• Easy to fly, allowing for a good amount of heads down time\n\n• Steeper turns up to 60 deg AOB possible\n\n• Easier to fly in a low altitude environment\n\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n• All aircraft are easily detected as one group by an adversary\n\n• Limited engagement flexibility\n\n• Turns into the echelon have to be shallow (max. 30 deg AOB)\n\n\nParameters:\n\n• Modified echelon\n\n• Up to 500 feet between aircraft\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 13 59 02.png|frameless|513x513px]]\n\n== Trail Formations ==\n\n=== Close Trail Formation ===\nUsage:\n\n• Tactical, Whenever necessary (e.g. Very low level)\n\n\nParameters:\n\n• One to two ship lengths behind lead aircraft\n\n• Below lead aircraft´s jetwash\n\n=== Trail Formations ===\nUsage:\n\n• Tactical, Whenever necessary (e.g. Very low level)\n\n\nParameters:\n\n• Flown in a cone 30 deg – 60 deg aft of lead\n\n• Range briefed by flight lead\n\n• Avoid flying at lead´s high six\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 14 06 03.png|frameless|503x503px]]\n\n== Non-Standard Formation ==\n\n=== Fighting Wings ===\nUsage:\n\n• Tactical\n\n\nAdvantages:\n\n• Helps to maintain flight integrity in bad weather or rough terrain\n\n• Allows for heads down time\n\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n• Poor 6 o´clock coverage\n\n• Easily detectable as bandit\n\n\nParameters:\n\n• 30 – 60 deg aft of 3/9 line\n\n• 30 – 60 deg above or below\n\n• 500 – 3000 ft spacing\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 14 11 11.png|frameless|666x666px]]\n\n=== Wedge ===\nUsage:\n\n• Tactical\n\n\nAdvantages:\n\n• Leader protected on 6 o´clock position\n\n• Leader free to maneuvering\n\n• Wingman may always switch sides\n\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n• No 6 o´clock protection for wingman\n\n• Lead changes difficult\n\n\nParameters:\n\n• 30 – 60 deg aft of 3/9 line\n\n• 4000 – 6000 ft spacing\n\n\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 14 18 22.png|frameless|417x417px]]\n\n=== Fluid Four ===\nUsage:\n\n• Tactical\n\n\nAdvantages:\n\n• Wingman kept close\n\n• Good maneuverability\n\n• Concentration of force\n\n• Easily converts to three ship if required\n\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n• Adversary can aquire all four aircraft\n\n• Defensive maneuvering confusing\n\n• Difficult in low altitude rough terrain\n\n\nParameters:\n\nTwo times fighting wing\n\n6000 – 9000 ft separated\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 14 58 09.png|frameless|620x620px]]\n\n=== Four Ship Wedge ===\nUsage:\n\n• Tactical\n\n\nAdvantages:\n\n• Very offensive for threats forward of 3/9 L\n\n• Easy to fly\n\n• Formation in bad weather / rough terrain\n\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n• Poor 6 o´clock lookout\n\n• Formation easily detected by bandit\n\n• Limited defensive maneuvering flexibility\n\n\nParameters:\n\n• Two times wedge\n\n• 1,5 – 3NM separated\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 15 04 22.png|frameless|419x419px]]\n\n=== Spread Four ===\nUsage:\n\n• Tactical\n\n\nAdvantages:\n\n• Difficult to aquire entire flight visually\n\n• Maximized fire power for BVR\n\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n• Maneuvering difficult\n\n• Very difficult to fly at low altitude\n\n\nParameters:\n\n• “1” and “2” 6000 – 9000 ft apart\n\n• “2” positioned 0 – 30 deg behind 3/9 of “1”\n\n• “1” and “3” 6000 – 9000 ft spread\n\n• “3” and “4” 6000 – 9000 ft apart\n\n• “4” positioned 0 – 30 deg behind 3/9 of “3”\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 15 13 03.png|frameless|804x804px]]\n\n=== Box Formation# ===\nUsage:\n\n• Tactical\n\n\nAdvantages:\n\n• Difficult to detect simultaneously by bandit\n\n• Easy support\n\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n• Difficult to maintain formation integrity\n\n\nParameters:\n\n• Two times spread\n\n• 1,5 – 3 NM separated\n\n• Variation: Four Ship Offset Box\n\n\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 15 18 55.png|frameless|397x397px]]\n\n=== Arrowhead Formation ===\nUsage:\n\n• Tactical\n\n\nAdvantages:\n\n• Same as box\n\n• Easier to fly as “2” allowing for more look out\n\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n• Same as box\n\n\nParameters:\n\n• Aircraft “3” and “4” fly spread 1,5 – 3 NM behind “1!”\n\n• Aircraft “2” 30 – 60 deg behind leader´s 3/9 line and 3000 ft spacing\n\n\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 16 14 37.png|frameless|589x589px]]\n\n=== Three Ship Vic ===\nUsage:\n\n• Tactical\n\n\nParameters:\n\n• Leader 1,5 – 3 NM in front of “2” and “3”\n\n• “2” and “3” 6000 – 9000 ft apart\n\n\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 16 18 26.png|frameless|419x419px]]\n\n== Line Abreast (Spread) ==\nUsage:\n\n• Tactical\n\n• Enroute\n\n\nAdvantages:\n\n• Minimizes the chances of simultaneous detection by bandit\n\n• Ordnance can be released quickly\n\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n• Hard to fly for unexperienced wingman\n\n\nParameters:\n\n• 0 – 20 deg aft\n\n• 4000 – 12000 ft spacing\n\n• Between 30 deg above and below\n\n• Optimum 0 deg and 6000 – 9000 ft\n\n• Or as briefed\n\n\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 14 25 43.png|frameless|616x616px]]\n\n\n== Line Abreast Turns ==\n\n=== Tac- and Hook Turns ===\nExecution TAC Turn:\n\nA TAC Turn is a delayed ninety 90 turn either to right or left. Depending on the direction of turn, the aircraft outside the turn turns first. The turn of the inner / second aircraft turning is delayed by a visual reference. Once the nose of the first turning aircraft is coming around to point on the second, the second aircraft starts its turn.\n\nThe TAC Turn is flown in full military Power with sustained altitude and speed.\n\n\nExecution Hook Turn:\n\nA Hook turn is a simultaneous 180 degree turn either to the left or rigt. It is flown in full military Power with sunstained altitude and speed.\n\n[[File:16V5.DOC - 16v5.pdf — Mozilla Firefox 08.07.2026 04 40 52.png|frameless|783x783px]]\n\n=== Delayed 45 Degree Turns, Crossturns and Check Turns ===\nExecution Delayed 45 Degree Turn:\n\nA Delayed 45 Degree turn is self explained by its name. It is flown in full military power with susatined speed and altitude.\n\nDepending on the direction of turn, the inner aircraft starts its turn when crossing the outer aircrafts flightpath.\n\n\nExecution Crossturn:\n\nIs a Hook turn with both aircrafts turning into each other. The same parameters as for a normal Hook turn are required.\n\n\nExecution Check Turn:\n\nCheck Turns are Turns for Heading adjustments that are smaller than 45 Degrees. They are flown with a desired power setting but with sustained altitude. A slight formation offset is required. The lead will adjust the heading by gently turning into the choosen direction.\n\n\n\n[[File:16V5.DOC - 16v5.pdf — Mozilla Firefox 08.07.2026 04 42 04.png|frameless|791x791px]]\n\n\n\nWho ever menages to come to this point, my congratulations. You are now a lot smarter and allowed to screenshot this and send it into \"genaral\"\nIceman','utf-8'),
(593,'== Formation ==\nA formation is defined as more than one aircraft that, by prior arrangement between the pilots, operates as a single aircraft with regard to navigation and position reporting. Separation between aircraft within the formation is the responsibility of the flight leader and the pilots of the other aircraft in the flight. This includes transition periods when aircraft within the formation are maneuvering to attain separation from each other to effect individual control and during join up or breakaway.\n\n1. Standard Formation. A formation in which a proximity of no more than 1 mile laterally or longitudinally and within 100 feet vertically from the flight leader is maintained by each wingman.\n\n2. Nonstandard Formation. A formation operating under any of the following conditions:\n\n1. When the flight leader has requested and air traffic control has approved other than standard formation dimensions.\n\n2. When operating within an authorized Altitude reservation or under the provisions of a letter of agreement.\n\n3. When the operations are conducted in airspace specifically designed for a special activity.\n\n=== Formation Responsibilities ===\nThe flight lead assigns responsibilities for each flight member. Dividing responsibilities ensures each pilot has a manageable number of tasks to perform. Flight member normal responsibilities are:\n\n• Number One: Primary planner and decision maker, primary navigation and radar lookout, visual lookout for mutual support of #2, and primary engaged fighter, if practical.\n\n• Number Two: Maintain formation position, visual lookout, mutual support of number one. Navigation position awareness, and radar awareness as other responsibilities allow.\n\n• Number Three: Support number one. Secondary planner and alternate decision maker, maintain support position for lead element, secondary navigation and radar monitor, visual lookout for number four, mutual support of the entire flight, and secondary engaged fighter, if practical.\n\n• Number Four: Maintain formation position, visual lookout for the flight, mutual support of number three. Navigation position awareness, and radar awareness as other responsibilities allow\n\n=== Time Sharing ===\nThe goal on every low level training mission should be to improve the development of this cross-check. Practice and discipline are essential to maximizing visual mutual support. On each mission, pilots must start with the basic NEAR ROCKS, FAR ROCKS, CHECK SIX pattern and build up the visual search arena as allowed by task saturation, threat and flight conditions. When encountering extremely rough terrain, defensive reactions, navigation turns, etc., drop the lowest priority sectors in order. There will be times, such as hard turns, when only NEAR ROCKS can be cross-checked. The key is to quickly re-establish the cross-check one sector at a time as tasks permit\n\n\n\n[[File:16V5.DOC - 16v5.pdf — Mozilla Firefox 08.07.2026 04 34 19.png|frameless|737x737px]]\n\n== Formation Departure ==\nA formation departure consists of more than one aircraft at intervals of 1 minute or less which, by prior arrangement between the pilots, operate as a single aircraft with regard to navigation and position reporting. The departure portion of the flight may terminate at a preplanned breakup point which may be located up to, but not beyond, the planned initial level-off at cruise altitude.\n\n== Taxi Operations ==\n• Keep use of power to a minimum\n\n• Taxi with 20 – 25 knots at straight taxiway sections and turn with 10 – 15 knots\n\n• Avoid jet blast damage\n\n• Check nose wheel steering and brakes as you taxi out of the spot\n\n• Check the flight instrument in the turn onto the taxiway\n\n• Taxi only in authorized areas\n\n• Slow down and taxi on the centerline in congested areas\n\n• Do not taxi within 10 feet of any obstacle\n\n• Do not taxi within 25 feet without a wingwalker\n\n• If taxiing behind an aircraft, a minimum spacing of four ship lengths is necessary\n\n== Before Departure (procedure at LCC, EOR, BTI) ==\n\n=== Aircraft checks ===\n• Aircraft are checked, armed and dearmed on Arming / Dearming Pads (of LCC/EOR Pads)\n\n• Before departure, aircraft are checked and armed at the departure end EOR pad\n\n• After Arrival, aircraft are disarmed at the arrival end EOR pad\n\n\nLCC = Last Chance Check\n\nEOR = End of Runway\n\nBTI = Before Takeoff Inspection\n\n=== End of Runway (EOR) ===\n• After stopping EOR, confirm all weapons switches safe\n\n• Keep hands in full view during arming\n\n• Aircraft should not taxi in front of aircraft with forward firing ordnance being armed / dearmed\n\n\n• As soon as you stop in the arming area, check your GS (reads zero)\n\n• If GS is zero or one knot, the INS platform is good\n\n• If GS is two or more, accomplish another alignment if possible\n\n\n• Ensure Speed Brake Stowed\n\n• Review takeoff procedure and details as EP immediately after takeoff\n\n• Check other aircraft for leaks, loose panels, proper configuration, etc\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_21_05_47.png|alt=Example of EOR Lineup|border|frameless|691x691px]]\n\n== Departure Briefing ==\nIn the so-called “Departure Briefing”, which takes place on the EOR / LCC Pad prior to the lineup, the Flight Lead informs his Flight about all relevant information regarding departure. A Departure Briefing usually contains the following information:\n\n1. Which type of Lineup\n\n2. Type of Takeoff (Two Ship, Interval or Trail)\n\n3. Speeds\n\n4. Lost Wingmen Procedure\n\n5. Clearence for visual join-up in case of interval and trail departure\n\n6. Further / Additional information / structure\n\n\nExample of a Departure Briefing:\n\nWe´ll make a 3 ship echelon gear doors allignment for interval departure 5 seconds, (max Brake 140, min Go 150, Go 160 (are V speeds currently not used in SK), rotation speed 200, AB off at 300, contract speed 350, 5 nose up, in case of lost Wingmen turn right for 30 seconds unsing 30 AOB, inform Lead, transition to instruments, obtain seperate ATC clearence and attempt re-join, say when airborne, you´re cleared to join on the right, say when in position.\n\n== Runway Lineup ==\nGeneral:\n\n• Leader will direct the appropriate runway line up based on winds and runway width\n\n• Wingmen should line up ensuring wingtip clearance\n\nLineup Types:\n\n• Two Ship Lineup\n\n• Three Ship Echelon\n\n• Four Ship Echelon\n\n• Four Ship three in The Slot\n\n• Four Ship four in The Slot\n\n• Three Ship three in The Slot\n\nLineup Standards:\n\n• Runway lineup is determined by taking into account several factors:\n\no Wind direction and speed\n\no Direction of turn out of traffic\n\no Weather\n\n• Minimum wingtip spacing is wingtip clearance but may be wider as desired or required\n\n=== Two Ship Lineup ===\n• Lead takes the center of it´s half of the runway\n\n• The wingmen will line up lead´s main gear doors and take wingtip spacing as briefed\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_19_39_19.png|alt=Example Two Ship Lineup|frameless|692x692px]]\n\n=== Three ship echelon ===\n• Three ship formations may line up in echelon, if the runway is wide enough\n\n• “2” and “3” will line up gear doors and take wingtip spacing as briefed\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_19_46_25.png|frameless|753x753px]]\n\n=== Four Ship Echelon ===\n• Four ship may line up in echelon, if the runway is wide enough\n\n• “2” will line up gear doors and take wingtip spacing as briefed\n\n• “3” and “4” should align helmets of preceding flight members\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_19_51_04.png|frameless|628x628px]]\n\n=== Four Ship Three in The Slot ===\n• Lead should line up as far to the side of the runway as practical\n\n• “2” will place the wingtip closest to lead on the centerline an line up gear doors\n\n• “3” will line up between lead and “2”, maintaining nose-tail clearance out to 500 ft\n\n• “4” will line up offset from “2”s jet blast while aligning gear doors of “3\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_19_59_31.png|frameless|713x713px]]\n\n=== Four Ship Four in The Slot ===\n• Lead should line up as far to the side of the runway as practical\n\n• “2” will place the wingtip closest to lead on the center line and line up gear doors\n\n• “4” will pull in between lead and “2” while aligning gear doors\n\n• “3” will line up with wingtip clearance on “2” while aligning gear doors\n\n• “4” will pull in further to align tail fin or gear doors of “3”\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_20_06_54.png|frameless|651x651px]]\n\n=== Three Ship Three in The Slot ===\n• Lead should line up as far to the side of the runway as practical\n\n• “2” will place the wingtip closest to lead on the center line and line up gear doors\n\n• “3” will line up between lead and “2”, maintaining nose-tail clearance out to 500 ft\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_20_10_03.png|frameless|696x696px]]\n\n== Takeoff ==\n\n=== Takeoff Types: ===\n• Two Ship Takeoff\n\no Formation Takeoff\n\no Usable for two aircraft only\n\no Not usable during periods of high crosswind, low visibility, wet runway\n\n• Interval Departure\n\no Used whenever a two ship takeoff is not possible\n\no Separation assured by timing\n\no Rejoin required after departure\n\n• (Radar assisted) Trail Departure\n\no Used whenever a two ship takeoff is not possible\n\no A type of interval departure\n\no Separation assured by on board radar\n\n=== Two Ship Takeoff ===\n• Signal for brake release is a head nod or radio call\n\n• Leader will make a normal takeoff with throttle slightly retarded (power advantage\n\nfor Wingman)\n\n• Leader will pass a gear up and AB out signal, if appropriate\n\n• Wingman will maintain wingtip clearance throughout takeoff roll\n\n• If overrunning the leader, wingman will be directed to assume the lead while\n\ncontinuing takeoff\n\n• If either member or the element must abort, the other member should continue the\n\ntakeoff\n\n• In either case, stay on your side of the runway!\n\n=== Trail Departure ===\n• Used when conditions won´t permit a formation takeoff\n\n• Examples: Wet runways, crosswind limits, weapon loads, configuration differences, visibility\n\n\n• Set up radar for easy acquisition\n\no Select RWS Mode\n\n\n• Priority is to fly the aircraft, not operating the radar\n\n• Ignore the radar until all high priority tasks are completed\n\n\n• During climbout, establish and maintain briefed power setting and airspeed\n\n\n• Unless restricted by procedure or ATC, begin all climbs at the same time as the leader\n\n\n• Lead should call passing every 5000 ft increment until all flight members are tied/visual\n\n\n• Rule of thumb: For 30 AOB turns, allow preceding aircraft to drift 5 deg laterally for each mile of separation between aircraft, Example: to maintain a 2 NM trail position, allow the preceding aircraft to drift 10 deg before initiating your turn\n\n• Once a tied or visual call ha been made, advise the leader if either radar or visual contact is subsequently lost.\n\n=== Lost Wingman ===\nGeneral:\n\n• Transition to instruments\n\n• Notify Lead (“Lost Wingman”)\n\n• Execute lost wingman procedures\n\n• Obtain separate ATC clearance\n\n• Attempt rejoin\n\n• Flight lead: Acknowledge lost wingman, transmit altitude, speed, post, etc.\n\n\nWings level after departure:\n\n• Turn away using 15 deg angle of bank (AOB) for 15 seconds, ensure operation and obtain separate clearance\n\n\nWingman outside of the turn after departure:\n\n• Reverse the direction of the turn using 15 deg AOB for 15 seconds, ensure separation and obtain separate clearance\n\n\nWingman inside of the turn after departure:\n\n• Momentarily reduce power to ensure separation and inform lead to roll out, maintain bank to ensure lateral separation and obtain separate clearance\n\n\nFinal approach:\n\n• Momentarily turn away to ensure separation, commence the published missed approach procedure and obtain separate clearance\n\n\nMissed approach:\n\n• Momentarily turn away to ensure separation, continue the missed approach procedure while climbing 500 ft above missed approach altitude and get a separate clearance\n\n\nLocal procedures:\n\n• Sometimes local conditions require different lost wingman procedures\n\n== Rejoin ==\nPurpose:\n\n• The purpose of a rejoin is to get the flight back together safely and efficiently\n\n• Necessary after an interval / trail departure or lost wingman procedure\n\nBasics:\n\n• Lead should monitor wingmen during all rejoins\n\n• Airspeeds and bank angles are prebriefed or unit standard\n\n• Lead should make a radio call if flying different speed or bank angle\n\nRejoin Techniques\n\n• A radar lock on may be used during the rejoin to provide range and overtake info\n\n• Maintaining 50 – 100 Knots of true overtake will provide a controlled, expeditious\n\n\nrejoin\n\n• “3” and “4” should be locked to the leader\n\n• At 3000 ft reduce power smoothly to control overtake\n\n• At 1500 ft, true overtake should be 50 knots\n\n• Use speed brakes if overtake is excessive\n\n• If overtake is excessive approaching the extended fingertip position, initiate a controlled overshoot\n\n== Crossunder ==\nGeneral:\n\n• Used to move wingman to the other side\n\n\nTwo Ship Crossunder:\n\n• On lead´s signal wingman will reduce power\n\n• The wingman will move back and down below lead´s Plane of Motion (POM) and add power again to avoid falling further behind\n\n• Next, wingman moves across and behind lead with a minimum of nose-tail clearance\n\n• Add power as required to avoid falling further behind\n\n• Once on the opposite side and with wingtip clearance, wingman will add power to move up and forward into position\n\n\nFour Ship Fingertip Crossunder\n\n• If “2” is crossing to the side that “3” and “4” are on, “3” should move out to create room for “2”\n\n• “2” begins a normal crossunder (ensuring adequate spacing before crossing lead´s six o´clock)\n\n• If “3” and “4” are crossing simultaneously, “3” performs a normal crossunder on “2” and “1”\n\n• As “3” begins the crossunder, “4” performs a crossunder on “3”\n\n\nFour Ship Echelon Crossunder:\n\n• As “2” begins the crossunder, “3” performs a crossunder on “2”, “4” performs a crossunder on “3”\n\n\nVisual Signals\n\n• Crossunder may be initiated using a wing dip to the side the wingman is requested to move or after instruction on radio\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 10.10.2025 13 59 01.png|frameless|663x663px]]\n\n== Lead Changes ==\n• Lead changes require an unmistakable transfer of responsibilities from one flight member to another\n\n• The flight member assuming lead will be no further aft than normal route/fingertip\n\n• Lead changes only change “Nav Lead” responsibilities\n\n• Flight lead responsibilities cannot be transferred\n\n== Formation Turns ==\n\n=== Fingertip Turns ===\n• Wingman will maintain fingertip position using fingertip spacing reference\n\n• Always stay slightly below preceding aircraft\n\n• Fingertip turns are limited to approximately 30 deg AOB\n\n• Lead will avoid sudden or abrupt changes in AOB or back stick pressure\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 10.10.2025 14 25 57.png|frameless|663x663px]]\n\n=== Echelon Turn Into the Echelon ===\n• Turns into the echelon will be avoided if at all possible\n\n• Wingman will maintain fingertip position using fingertip spacing references\n\n• Turns are limit to very shallow turns\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_10.10.2025_14_34_38.png|frameless|656x656px]]\n\n=== Echelon Turns Away From the Echelon ===\n• Plan to limit AOB to 60 degrees maximum\n\n• All aircraft will match lead´s roll rates while maintaining the same horizontal plane\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 10.10.2025 14 38 58.png|frameless|656x656px]]\n\n=== Route Turns ===\n• When turned away from the wingman, will stack level with the leader\n\n• When turned into, the wingman will only stack down enough to keep lead in sightand avoid his maneuvering plane\n\n=== Route Echelon Turns ===\n• If a turn is made into the echelon, each aircraft will only stack down enough to keep rest of the formation in sight and avoid their maneuvering plane\n\n• On turns away from the echelon, all aircrafts will maintain the same horizontal plane\n\n== Channel Changes ==\n• When a channel / frequency change is directed, wingman will normally move out from fingertip to route or from echelon to route echelon\n\n== Leaving Formation ==\nLeave Formation:\n\n• When directed to do so\n\n• When you loose sight of the aircraft ahead (lost wingman, blind)\n\n• When you are unable to join up or to stay in formation without crossing over, under or in front of the aircraft ahead\n\n• At any other time you feel that your presence in the formation constitutes a hazard\n\n• When leaving a formation, clear yourself in the direction of your turn and notify\n\n\nleader\n\n• If you have lost sight, comply with the appropriate lost wingman procedure\n\n• Rejoin only by directed to do so by the flight lead\n\n== Formation Landing ==\nLead:\n\n• Approach speed should be consistent with heavier aircraft\n\n• Wingmen should be positioned upwind if crosswind component exceeds 5 knots\n\n• Plan to land near the center of your half of the runway\n\n• Do not touchdown long and fast\n\n\n\nWingman:\n\n• Maintain a minimum of 10 feet lateral wingtip spacing\n\n• Stack level with lead as briefed when configured on final approach\n\n• Cross check the runway to ensure sufficient runway is available\n\n== Overhead Break ==\n• Used to split a formation in an orderly and expeditious manner to prepare for landing\n\n• Also called “360 overhead\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 10.10.2025 15 05 58.png|frameless|936x936px]]\n\n== Standard Formations ==\n\n=== Fingertip Formations ===\nUsage:\n\n• Weather penetration\n\n• Arfield arrivals / departures\n\n• Shoe formations\n\n\n\nAdvantages:\n\n• Lead aircraft is easy to see in bad weather\n\n• Close formation is used for formation takeoff / landing\n\n• Preferred before overhead break (360 overhead)\n\n\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n• Does not allow for much head down time\n\n• Limited maneuvering\n\n• Shallow turns (up to 30 deg AOB) only\n\n• Both aircraft are easily detected as one group by an adversary\n\n\n\nParameters:\n\n• Use fingertip reference points\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 10.10.2025 15 20 01.png|frameless|330x330px]]\n\n=== Finger four (Four Ship Fingertip) ===\nUsage:\n\n• Weather penetration\n\n• Show formations\n\n\nAdvantages:\n\n• Lead aircraft is easy to see in bad weather\n\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n• Does not allow for much heads down time\n\n• Limited maneuvering\n\n• Shallow turns (up to 30 deg AOB) only\n\n• All aircraft are easily detected as a group by an adversary\n\n\nParameters:\n\n• Same as fingertip\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 13 45 26.png|frameless|463x463px]]\n\n== Route Formations ==\nUsage:\n\n• Enroute\n\n\nAdvantages\n\n• Lead aircraft is easy to see in moderate weather\n\n• Easy to fly, allowing for a good amount of heads down time\n\n• Steeper turns up to 60 deg AOB possible\n\n• Easier to fly in a low altitude environment\n\n\nDisadvantages\n\n• All aircraft are easily detected as one group by an adversary\n\n• Limited engagement flexibility\n\n\nParameters:\n\n• Modified fingertip\n\n• Up to 500 feet between aircraft\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 13 54 38.png|frameless|583x583px]]\n\n=== Route Echelon ===\nUsage:\n\n• Enroute\n\n\nAdvantages:\n\n• Lead aircraft is easy to see in moderate weather\n\n• Easy to fly, allowing for a good amount of heads down time\n\n• Steeper turns up to 60 deg AOB possible\n\n• Easier to fly in a low altitude environment\n\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n• All aircraft are easily detected as one group by an adversary\n\n• Limited engagement flexibility\n\n• Turns into the echelon have to be shallow (max. 30 deg AOB)\n\n\nParameters:\n\n• Modified echelon\n\n• Up to 500 feet between aircraft\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 13 59 02.png|frameless|513x513px]]\n\n== Trail Formations ==\n\n=== Close Trail Formation ===\nUsage:\n\n• Tactical, Whenever necessary (e.g. Very low level)\n\n\nParameters:\n\n• One to two ship lengths behind lead aircraft\n\n• Below lead aircraft´s jetwash\n\n=== Trail Formations ===\nUsage:\n\n• Tactical, Whenever necessary (e.g. Very low level)\n\n\nParameters:\n\n• Flown in a cone 30 deg – 60 deg aft of lead\n\n• Range briefed by flight lead\n\n• Avoid flying at lead´s high six\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 14 06 03.png|frameless|503x503px]]\n\n== Non-Standard Formation ==\n\n=== Fighting Wings ===\nUsage:\n\n• Tactical\n\n\nAdvantages:\n\n• Helps to maintain flight integrity in bad weather or rough terrain\n\n• Allows for heads down time\n\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n• Poor 6 o´clock coverage\n\n• Easily detectable as bandit\n\n\nParameters:\n\n• 30 – 60 deg aft of 3/9 line\n\n• 30 – 60 deg above or below\n\n• 500 – 3000 ft spacing\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 14 11 11.png|frameless|666x666px]]\n\n=== Wedge ===\nUsage:\n\n• Tactical\n\n\nAdvantages:\n\n• Leader protected on 6 o´clock position\n\n• Leader free to maneuvering\n\n• Wingman may always switch sides\n\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n• No 6 o´clock protection for wingman\n\n• Lead changes difficult\n\n\nParameters:\n\n• 30 – 60 deg aft of 3/9 line\n\n• 4000 – 6000 ft spacing\n\n\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 14 18 22.png|frameless|417x417px]]\n\n=== Fluid Four ===\nUsage:\n\n• Tactical\n\n\nAdvantages:\n\n• Wingman kept close\n\n• Good maneuverability\n\n• Concentration of force\n\n• Easily converts to three ship if required\n\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n• Adversary can aquire all four aircraft\n\n• Defensive maneuvering confusing\n\n• Difficult in low altitude rough terrain\n\n\nParameters:\n\nTwo times fighting wing\n\n6000 – 9000 ft separated\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 14 58 09.png|frameless|620x620px]]\n\n=== Four Ship Wedge ===\nUsage:\n\n• Tactical\n\n\nAdvantages:\n\n• Very offensive for threats forward of 3/9 L\n\n• Easy to fly\n\n• Formation in bad weather / rough terrain\n\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n• Poor 6 o´clock lookout\n\n• Formation easily detected by bandit\n\n• Limited defensive maneuvering flexibility\n\n\nParameters:\n\n• Two times wedge\n\n• 1,5 – 3NM separated\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 15 04 22.png|frameless|419x419px]]\n\n=== Spread Four ===\nUsage:\n\n• Tactical\n\n\nAdvantages:\n\n• Difficult to aquire entire flight visually\n\n• Maximized fire power for BVR\n\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n• Maneuvering difficult\n\n• Very difficult to fly at low altitude\n\n\nParameters:\n\n• “1” and “2” 6000 – 9000 ft apart\n\n• “2” positioned 0 – 30 deg behind 3/9 of “1”\n\n• “1” and “3” 6000 – 9000 ft spread\n\n• “3” and “4” 6000 – 9000 ft apart\n\n• “4” positioned 0 – 30 deg behind 3/9 of “3”\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 15 13 03.png|frameless|804x804px]]\n\n=== Box Formation# ===\nUsage:\n\n• Tactical\n\n\nAdvantages:\n\n• Difficult to detect simultaneously by bandit\n\n• Easy support\n\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n• Difficult to maintain formation integrity\n\n\nParameters:\n\n• Two times spread\n\n• 1,5 – 3 NM separated\n\n• Variation: Four Ship Offset Box\n\n\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 15 18 55.png|frameless|397x397px]]\n\n=== Arrowhead Formation ===\nUsage:\n\n• Tactical\n\n\nAdvantages:\n\n• Same as box\n\n• Easier to fly as “2” allowing for more look out\n\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n• Same as box\n\n\nParameters:\n\n• Aircraft “3” and “4” fly spread 1,5 – 3 NM behind “1!”\n\n• Aircraft “2” 30 – 60 deg behind leader´s 3/9 line and 3000 ft spacing\n\n\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 16 14 37.png|frameless|589x589px]]\n\n=== Three Ship Vic ===\nUsage:\n\n• Tactical\n\n\nParameters:\n\n• Leader 1,5 – 3 NM in front of “2” and “3”\n\n• “2” and “3” 6000 – 9000 ft apart\n\n\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 16 18 26.png|frameless|419x419px]]\n\n== Line Abreast (Spread) ==\nUsage:\n\n• Tactical\n\n• Enroute\n\n\nAdvantages:\n\n• Minimizes the chances of simultaneous detection by bandit\n\n• Ordnance can be released quickly\n\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n• Hard to fly for unexperienced wingman\n\n\nParameters:\n\n• 0 – 20 deg aft\n\n• 4000 – 12000 ft spacing\n\n• Between 30 deg above and below\n\n• Optimum 0 deg and 6000 – 9000 ft\n\n• Or as briefed\n\n\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 14 25 43.png|frameless|616x616px]]\n\n\n== Line Abreast Turns ==\n\n=== Tac- and Hook Turns ===\nExecution TAC Turn:\n\nA TAC Turn is a delayed ninety 90 turn either to right or left. Depending on the direction of turn, the aircraft outside the turn turns first. The turn of the inner / second aircraft turning is delayed by a visual reference. Once the nose of the first turning aircraft is coming around to point on the second, the second aircraft starts its turn.\n\nThe TAC Turn is flown in full military Power with sustained altitude and speed.\n\n\nExecution Hook Turn:\n\nA Hook turn is a simultaneous 180 degree turn either to the left or rigt. It is flown in full military Power with sunstained altitude and speed.\n\n[[File:16V5.DOC - 16v5.pdf — Mozilla Firefox 08.07.2026 04 40 52.png|frameless|783x783px]]\n\n=== Delayed 45 Degree Turns, Crossturns and Check Turns ===\nExecution Delayed 45 Degree Turn:\n\nA Delayed 45 Degree turn is self explained by its name. It is flown in full military power with susatined speed and altitude.\n\nDepending on the direction of turn, the inner aircraft starts its turn when crossing the outer aircrafts flightpath.\n\n\nExecution Crossturn:\n\nIs a Hook turn with both aircrafts turning into each other. The same parameters as for a normal Hook turn are required.\n\n\nExecution Check Turn:\n\nCheck Turns are Turns for Heading adjustments that are smaller than 45 Degrees. They are flown with a desired power setting but with sustained altitude. A slight formation offset is required. The lead will adjust the heading by gently turning into the choosen direction.\n\n\n\n[[File:16V5.DOC - 16v5.pdf — Mozilla Firefox 08.07.2026 04 42 04.png|frameless|791x791px]]\n\n\n\nWho ever menages to come to this point, my congratulations. You are now a lot smarter and allowed to screenshot this and send it into \"genaral\"\n\nIceman','utf-8'),
(594,'= Available tankers (Source Training Theatre) =\n[[File:US Navy 030125-N-0905V-002 S-3B \'Viking assigned to the Screwbirds of Sea Control Squadron 33 (VS-33) .jpg|thumb|383x383px|Lockheed S-3B Viking]]\n[[File:Screen 260212 152743.png|thumb|380x380px|Boeing KC-135 Stratotanker (with MPRS Pods)]]\n{| class=\"wikitable\"\n|+TANKER INFORMATION\n|\n|CALLSIGN \n|FREQUENCY\n|TACAN\n|ALTITUDE\n|NOTES\n|-\n|S-3B VIKING\n|MAULER 6-1\n|311.5 MHz\n|25Y\n|6,000ft\n|CARRIER RECOVERY TANKER\n|-\n|KC-135 MPRS\n|SHELL 2-1\n|254.0 MHz\n|38X\n|24,000ft\n|TRA ADANA\n|}\n\'\'\'S-3B\'\'\' is a small, carrier capable tanker that has one drogue capable of refueling only one aircraft at a time. It is established in a left hand racetrack pattern around the carrier on the BRC.\n\n\'\'\'KC-135 MPRS\'\'\' is a large purpose built tanker with the Multi-Point Refueling System (MPRS) pods installed on each wingtip, which allows for two probe-style aircraft to connect and receive fuel at the same time. It is established in a left hand racetrack pattern within TRA ADANA.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n= F/A-18 Hornet — Air-to-Air Refueling (AAR) Standard Procedure =\n\'\'Note: This guide blends standard Navy probe-and-drogue technique with sim-community best practices. Lines marked (IRL) reflect real-world reasoning; lines marked (SIM) are conventions common in DCS/sim training rather than confirmed NATOPS text.\'\'\n----\n\n== 1. Inbound and Join-Up on Port (Left) Observation ==\n\'\'\'Tanker Intercept\'\'\'\n\n* Proceed to the tanker\'s position via the assigned rendezvous point, altitude, and heading (as briefed or per controlling agency instructions).\n* Call the tanker with \"Intent to refuel\" once on tanker frequency\n* You need to be at least 1,000ft (recommended 2,000ft) underneath the tanker while maneuvering for the join-up. Once you have visual with the tanker (not with onboard sensors, with your actual eyes), you may begin a slow climb to reach the tanker at co-altitude level)\n\n== Closure Rate vs. Range — Speed Management Table ==\n\'\'Use this as a decelerating \"ladder\" during the intercept: bleed closure down a step as each range gate is crossed, rather than holding one speed the whole way and cutting power hard at the end.\'\' \n{| class=\"wikitable\"\n!Range\n!Target Closure\n|-\n|2 NM\n|≤ 200 KIAS closure\n|-\n|1 NM\n|≤ 100 KIAS closure\n|-\n|0.5 NM\n|≤ 50 KIAS closure\n|-\n|0.4 NM\n|≤ 40 KIAS closure\n|-\n|0.3 NM\n|≤ 30 KIAS closure\n|-\n|0.2 NM\n|≤ 20 KIAS closure\n|-\n|0.1 NM\n|≤ 10 KIAS closure\n|}\n\'\'\'How to use this table in practice:\'\'\'\n\n* Easy way to memorize is to multiply your range by 100. This will give you your MAXIMUM CLOSURE VELOCITY (Indicated as \"Vc\" on the HUD). Do not treat it as a required speed checkpoint, if you have less than 400kts closure at 4nm this does not mean you need to accelerate. \n* This method works best during rear aspect join-ups (if you\'re intercepting from the front of the tanker you have a bigger problem)\n\n\'\'\'Port Observation\'\'\'\n\n* Port Observation is the holding area established in left echelon off the tanker, slightly aft and level with (or slightly below) the tanker\'s wing line.\n* This is where flight lead sorts jets in refueling order — lowest fuel state moves toward the basket first.\n* Maintain a disciplined and rigid formation here, no swaying around. this is a \"hold and wait your turn\" position, not a precision station-keeping position yet.\n\n----\n\n== 2. Transition to Pre-Contact and Connect ==\nWhen cleared and ready to move from Port Observation to Pre-Contact, complete the following memory items BEFORE leaving port observation:\n{| class=\"wikitable\"\n!Item\n!Setting\n|-\n|Refueling probe\n|EXTEND\n|-\n|Radar\n|SIL / STBY\n|-\n|Engine Bleed Air\n|RIGHT OFF (optional)\n|-\n|Throttle\n|Manual preferred; avoid ATC\n|}\n\'\'Right engine bleed air was switched off IRL to prevent drogue fuel leaks from entering the cabin via the right engine intake\'\'\n\n\'\'\'Throttle technique:\'\'\' Avoid relying on Automatic Throttle Control (ATC) whenever possible. While convenient, leaning on ATC keeps you from developing and maintaining the fine throttle control skills that are important throughout many other phases of flying the Hornet.\n\n\'\'\'Closing on the basket:\'\'\'\n\n* Move from Port Observation, cross behind the tanker, and stabilize in the Pre-Contact position — a few feet aft and slightly below the drogue, matching speed with zero rate of closure.\n* On clearance, close smoothly and steadily into the basket. Make small, early corrections rather than large, late ones.\n* Reference a fixed point on the tanker (e.g., the refueling pod itself) rather than staring at the moving basket, which tends to cause overcorrection.\n* Confirm a good connection (contact light/indication, fuel flow) before settling into position and monitoring fuel transfer.\n\n----\n\n== 3. Disconnect and Join Up on Starboard (Right) Observation ==\n\n* Once refueling is complete (full load or as briefed), smoothly reduce power slightly to back straight out of the basket — avoid abrupt throttle changes or lateral drift during disconnect.\n* Confirm disconnect indication and retract the refueling probe.\n* \'\'\'Engine Bleed Air\'\'\' — restore to NORM (if previously turned off for the right engine).\n* Clear from behind the tanker to the \'\'\'Starboard (Right) Observation\'\'\' position — the mirror of Port Observation, held in right echelon off the tanker.\n* This clears the pre-contact/contact area for the next aircraft in the flight and positions you to either re-enter the queue (if more fuel is needed later) or depart the tanker formation once the flight is complete.\n\n== Quick-Reference Memory Items (Pre-Contact) ==\n{| class=\"wikitable\"\n!Item\n!Setting\n|-\n|Refueling probe\n|EXTEND\n|-\n|Radar\n|SIL / STBY\n|-\n|Engine Bleed Air\n|RIGHT OFF (optional)\n|-\n|Throttle\n|Manual preferred; avoid ATC\n|}','utf-8'),
(595,'= Available tankers (Source Training Theatre) =\n[[File:US Navy 030125-N-0905V-002 S-3B \'Viking assigned to the Screwbirds of Sea Control Squadron 33 (VS-33) .jpg|thumb|383x383px|Lockheed S-3B Viking]]\n[[File:Screen 260212 152743.png|thumb|380x380px|Boeing KC-135 Stratotanker (with MPRS Pods)]]\n{| class=\"wikitable\"\n|+TANKER INFORMATION\n|\n|CALLSIGN \n|FREQUENCY\n|TACAN\n|ALTITUDE\n|NOTES\n|-\n|S-3B VIKING\n|MAULER 6-1\n|311.5 MHz\n|25Y\n|6,000ft\n|CARRIER RECOVERY TANKER\n|-\n|KC-135 MPRS\n|SHELL 2-1\n|254.0 MHz\n|38X\n|24,000ft\n|TRA ADANA\n|}\n\'\'\'S-3B\'\'\' is a small, carrier capable tanker that has one drogue capable of refueling only one aircraft at a time. It is established in a left hand racetrack pattern around the carrier on the BRC.\n\n\'\'\'KC-135 MPRS\'\'\' is a large purpose built tanker with the Multi-Point Refueling System (MPRS) pods installed on each wingtip, which allows for two probe-style aircraft to connect and receive fuel at the same time. It is established in a left hand racetrack pattern within TRA ADANA.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n= F/A-18C Hornet — Air-to-Air Refueling (AAR) Standard Procedure =\n\'\'Note: This guide blends standard Navy probe-and-drogue technique with sim-community best practices. Lines marked (IRL) reflect real-world reasoning; lines marked (SIM) are conventions common in DCS/sim training rather than confirmed NATOPS text.\'\'\n----\n\n== \'\'\'1. Inbound and Join-Up on Port (Left) Observation\'\'\' ==\n\'\'\'Tanker Intercept\'\'\'\n\n* Proceed to the tanker\'s position via the assigned rendezvous point, altitude, and heading (as briefed or per controlling agency instructions).\n* Call the tanker with \"Intent to refuel\" once on tanker frequency\n* You need to be at least 1,000ft (recommended 2,000ft) underneath the tanker while maneuvering for the join-up. Once you have visual with the tanker (not with onboard sensors, with your actual eyes), you may begin a slow climb to reach the tanker at co-altitude level)\n\n== Closure Rate vs. Range — Speed Management Table ==\n\'\'Use this as a decelerating \"ladder\" during the intercept: bleed closure down a step as each range gate is crossed, rather than holding one speed the whole way and cutting power hard at the end.\'\' \n{| class=\"wikitable\"\n!Range\n!Target Closure\n|-\n|2 NM\n|≤ 200 KIAS closure\n|-\n|1 NM\n|≤ 100 KIAS closure\n|-\n|0.5 NM\n|≤ 50 KIAS closure\n|-\n|0.4 NM\n|≤ 40 KIAS closure\n|-\n|0.3 NM\n|≤ 30 KIAS closure\n|-\n|0.2 NM\n|≤ 20 KIAS closure\n|-\n|0.1 NM\n|≤ 10 KIAS closure\n|}\n\'\'\'How to use this table in practice:\'\'\'\n\n* Easy way to memorize is to multiply your range by 100. This will give you your MAXIMUM CLOSURE VELOCITY (Indicated as \"Vc\" on the HUD). Do not treat it as a required speed checkpoint, if you have less than 400kts closure at 4nm this does not mean you need to accelerate. \n* This method works best during rear aspect join-ups (if you\'re intercepting from the front of the tanker you have a bigger problem)\n\nPort Observation\n\n* Port Observation is the holding area established in left echelon off the tanker, slightly aft and level with (or slightly below) the tanker\'s wing line.\n* This is where flight lead sorts flight members in refueling order — lowest fuel is closest to the basket.\n* Maintain a disciplined and rigid echelon here, no swaying around.\n\n----\n\n== 2. \'\'\'Transition to Pre-Contact and Connect\'\'\' ==\nWhen cleared and ready to move from Port Observation to Pre-Contact, complete the following memory items BEFORE leaving port observation:\n{| class=\"wikitable\"\n!Item\n!Setting\n|-\n|Refueling probe\n|EXTEND\n|-\n|Radar\n|SIL / STBY\n|-\n|Engine Bleed Air\n|RIGHT OFF (optional)\n|-\n|Throttle\n|Manual preferred; avoid ATC\n|}\n\'\'Right engine bleed air was switched off IRL to prevent drogue fuel leaks from entering the cabin via the right engine intake\'\'\n\n\'\'\'Throttle technique:\'\'\' Avoid relying on Automatic Throttle Control (ATC) whenever possible. While convenient, relying on ATC keeps you from developing and maintaining the fine throttle control skills that are important throughout many other phases of flying the Hornet.\n\n\'\'\'Closing on the basket:\'\'\'\n\n* Move from Port Observation, cross UNDER AND BEHIND the tanker and any potential aircraft that may already be connected, and stabilize in the Pre-Contact position.\n* On clearance, wait for the basket to fully extend before crawling the jet forward very very slowly. Make small, early corrections rather than large, late ones.\n* Once connected, shift your perspective to flying in formation with the aircraft. Do not tunnel vision on the basket. \n* Confirm a good connection (contact light/indication, fuel flow) before settling into position and monitoring fuel transfer. If the MPRS pod shows a steady light, you are in a good position and are taking fuel. If the light suddenly goes off while you are still connected, it means you are either too close or too far from the basket. If the light starts blinking, the refueling process will complete in about 5 seconds and automatically disconnect. \n\n----\n\n== 3. \'\'\'Disconnect and Join Up on Starboard (Right) Observation\'\'\' ==\n\n* Once refueling is complete (full load or as briefed), smoothly reduce power slightly to back straight out of the basket — avoid abrupt throttle changes or lateral drift during disconnect.\n* Confirm disconnected from basket and retract the refueling probe.\n* \'\'\'Engine Bleed Air\'\'\' — restore to NORM (if previously turned off for the right engine).\n* Clear from BEHIND and UNDER the tanker to the \'\'\'Starboard (Right) Observation\'\'\' position — the mirror of Port Observation, held in right echelon off the tanker. \n* ----','utf-8'),
(596,'== Formation ==\nA formation is defined as more than one aircraft that, by prior arrangement between the pilots, operates as a single aircraft with regard to navigation and position reporting. Separation between aircraft within the formation is the responsibility of the flight leader and the pilots of the other aircraft in the flight. This includes transition periods when aircraft within the formation are maneuvering to attain separation from each other to effect individual control and during join up or breakaway.\n\n# Standard Formation. A formation in which a proximity of no more than 1 mile laterally or longitudinally and within 100 feet vertically from the flight leader is maintained by each wingman.\n# Nonstandard Formation. A formation operating under any of the following conditions:\n## When the flight leader has requested and air traffic control has approved other than standard formation dimensions.\n## When operating within an authorized Altitude reservation or under the provisions of a letter of agreement.\n## When the operations are conducted in airspace specifically designed for a special activity.\n\n=== Formation Responsibilities ===\nThe flight lead assigns responsibilities for each flight member. Dividing responsibilities ensures each pilot has a manageable number of tasks to perform. Flight member normal responsibilities are:\n\n* \'\'\'Number One:\'\'\' Primary planner and decision maker, primary navigation and radar lookout, visual lookout for mutual support of #2, and primary engaged fighter, if practical.\n* \'\'\'Number Two:\'\'\' Maintain formation position, visual lookout, mutual support of number one. Navigation position awareness, and radar awareness as other responsibilities allow.\n* \'\'\'Number Three:\'\'\' Support number one. Secondary planner and alternate decision maker, maintain support position for lead element, secondary navigation and radar monitor, visual lookout for number four, mutual support of the entire flight, and secondary engaged fighter, if practical.\n* \'\'\'Number Four:\'\'\' Maintain formation position, visual lookout for the flight, mutual support of number three. Navigation position awareness, and radar awareness as other responsibilities allow.\n\n=== Time Sharing ===\nThe goal on every low level training mission should be to improve the development of this cross-check. Practice and discipline are essential to maximizing visual mutual support. On each mission, pilots must start with the basic NEAR ROCKS, FAR ROCKS, CHECK SIX pattern and build up the visual search arena as allowed by task saturation, threat and flight conditions. When encountering extremely rough terrain, defensive reactions, navigation turns, etc., drop the lowest priority sectors in order. There will be times, such as hard turns, when only NEAR ROCKS can be cross-checked. The key is to quickly re-establish the cross-check one sector at a time as tasks permit.\n\n[[File:16V5.DOC - 16v5.pdf — Mozilla Firefox 08.07.2026 04 34 19.png|frameless|737x737px]]\n\n== Formation Departure ==\nA formation departure consists of more than one aircraft at intervals of 1 minute or less which, by prior arrangement between the pilots, operate as a single aircraft with regard to navigation and position reporting. The departure portion of the flight may terminate at a preplanned breakup point which may be located up to, but not beyond, the planned initial level-off at cruise altitude.\n\n== Taxi Operations ==\n* Keep use of power to a minimum\n* Taxi with 20 – 25 knots at straight taxiway sections and turn with 10 – 15 knots\n* Avoid jet blast damage\n* Check nose wheel steering and brakes as you taxi out of the spot\n* Check the flight instrument in the turn onto the taxiway\n* Taxi only in authorized areas\n* Slow down and taxi on the centerline in congested areas\n* Do not taxi within 10 feet of any obstacle\n* Do not taxi within 25 feet without a wingwalker\n* If taxiing behind an aircraft, a minimum spacing of four ship lengths is necessary\n\n== Before Departure (procedure at LCC, EOR, BTI) ==\n\n=== Aircraft checks ===\n* Aircraft are checked, armed and dearmed on Arming / Dearming Pads (of LCC/EOR Pads)\n* Before departure, aircraft are checked and armed at the departure end EOR pad\n* After Arrival, aircraft are disarmed at the arrival end EOR pad\n\n\'\'\'LCC\'\'\' = Last Chance Check\n\n\'\'\'EOR\'\'\' = End of Runway\n\n\'\'\'BTI\'\'\' = Before Takeoff Inspection\n\n=== End of Runway (EOR) ===\n* After stopping EOR, confirm all weapons switches safe\n* Keep hands in full view during arming\n* Aircraft should not taxi in front of aircraft with forward firing ordnance being armed / dearmed\n* As soon as you stop in the arming area, check your GS (reads zero)\n* If GS is zero or one knot, the INS platform is good\n* If GS is two or more, accomplish another alignment if possible\n* Ensure Speed Brake Stowed\n* Review takeoff procedure and details as EP immediately after takeoff\n* Check other aircraft for leaks, loose panels, proper configuration, etc\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_21_05_47.png|alt=Example of EOR Lineup|border|frameless|691x691px]]\n\n== Departure Briefing ==\nIn the so-called “Departure Briefing”, which takes place on the EOR / LCC Pad prior to the lineup, the Flight Lead informs his Flight about all relevant information regarding departure. A Departure Briefing usually contains the following information:\n\n# Which type of Lineup\n# Type of Takeoff (Two Ship, Interval or Trail)\n# Speeds\n# Lost Wingmen Procedure\n# Clearence for visual join-up in case of interval and trail departure\n# Further / Additional information / structure\n\nExample of a Departure Briefing:\n\nWe´ll make a 3 ship echelon gear doors allignment for interval departure 5 seconds, (max Brake 140, min Go 150, Go 160 (are V speeds currently not used in SK), rotation speed 200, AB off at 300, contract speed 350, 5 nose up, in case of lost Wingmen turn right for 30 seconds unsing 30 AOB, inform Lead, transition to instruments, obtain seperate ATC clearence and attempt re-join, say when airborne, you´re cleared to join on the right, say when in position.\n\n== Runway Lineup ==\nGeneral:\n\n* Leader will direct the appropriate runway line up based on winds and runway width\n* Wingmen should line up ensuring wingtip clearance\n\nLineup Types:\n\n* Two Ship Lineup\n* Three Ship Echelon\n* Four Ship Echelon\n* Four Ship three in The Slot\n* Four Ship four in The Slot\n* Three Ship three in The Slot\n\nLineup Standards:\n\n* Runway lineup is determined by taking into account several factors:\n** Wind direction and speed\n** Direction of turn out of traffic\n** Weather\n* Minimum wingtip spacing is wingtip clearance but may be wider as desired or required\n\n=== Two Ship Lineup ===\n* Lead takes the center of it´s half of the runway\n* The wingmen will line up lead´s main gear doors and take wingtip spacing as briefed\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_19_39_19.png|alt=Example Two Ship Lineup|frameless|692x692px]]\n\n=== Three ship echelon ===\n* Three ship formations may line up in echelon, if the runway is wide enough\n* “2” and “3” will line up gear doors and take wingtip spacing as briefed\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_19_46_25.png|frameless|753x753px]]\n\n=== Four Ship Echelon ===\n* Four ship may line up in echelon, if the runway is wide enough\n* “2” will line up gear doors and take wingtip spacing as briefed\n* “3” and “4” should align helmets of preceding flight members\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_19_51_04.png|frameless|628x628px]]\n\n=== Four Ship Three in The Slot ===\n* Lead should line up as far to the side of the runway as practical\n* “2” will place the wingtip closest to lead on the centerline an line up gear doors\n* “3” will line up between lead and “2”, maintaining nose-tail clearance out to 500 ft\n* “4” will line up offset from “2”s jet blast while aligning gear doors of “3”\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_19_59_31.png|frameless|713x713px]]\n\n=== Four Ship Four in The Slot ===\n* Lead should line up as far to the side of the runway as practical\n* “2” will place the wingtip closest to lead on the center line and line up gear doors\n* “4” will pull in between lead and “2” while aligning gear doors\n* “3” will line up with wingtip clearance on “2” while aligning gear doors\n* “4” will pull in further to align tail fin or gear doors of “3”\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_20_06_54.png|frameless|651x651px]]\n\n=== Three Ship Three in The Slot ===\n* Lead should line up as far to the side of the runway as practical\n* “2” will place the wingtip closest to lead on the center line and line up gear doors\n* “3” will line up between lead and “2”, maintaining nose-tail clearance out to 500 ft\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_20_10_03.png|frameless|696x696px]]\n\n== Takeoff ==\n\n=== Takeoff Types: ===\n* Two Ship Takeoff\n** Formation Takeoff\n** Usable for two aircraft only\n** Not usable during periods of high crosswind, low visibility, wet runway\n* Interval Departure\n** Used whenever a two ship takeoff is not possible\n** Separation assured by timing\n** Rejoin required after departure\n* (Radar assisted) Trail Departure\n** Used whenever a two ship takeoff is not possible\n** A type of interval departure\n** Separation assured by on board radar\n\n=== Two Ship Takeoff ===\n* Signal for brake release is a head nod or radio call\n* Leader will make a normal takeoff with throttle slightly retarded (power advantage for Wingman)\n* Leader will pass a gear up and AB out signal, if appropriate\n* Wingman will maintain wingtip clearance throughout takeoff roll\n* If overrunning the leader, wingman will be directed to assume the lead while continuing takeoff\n* If either member or the element must abort, the other member should continue the takeoff\n* In either case, stay on your side of the runway!\n\n=== Trail Departure ===\n* Used when conditions won´t permit a formation takeoff\n* Examples: Wet runways, crosswind limits, weapon loads, configuration differences, visibility\n* Set up radar for easy acquisition\n** Select RWS Mode\n* Priority is to fly the aircraft, not operating the radar\n* Ignore the radar until all high priority tasks are completed\n* During climbout, establish and maintain briefed power setting and airspeed\n* Unless restricted by procedure or ATC, begin all climbs at the same time as the leader\n* Lead should call passing every 5000 ft increment until all flight members are tied/visual\n* Rule of thumb: For 30 AOB turns, allow preceding aircraft to drift 5 deg laterally for each mile of separation between aircraft, Example: to maintain a 2 NM trail position, allow the preceding aircraft to drift 10 deg before initiating your turn\n* Once a tied or visual call ha been made, advise the leader if either radar or visual contact is subsequently lost.\n\n=== Lost Wingman ===\nGeneral:\n\n* Transition to instruments\n* Notify Lead (“Lost Wingman”)\n* Execute lost wingman procedures\n* Obtain separate ATC clearance\n* Attempt rejoin\n* Flight lead: Acknowledge lost wingman, transmit altitude, speed, post, etc.\n\nWings level after departure:\n\n* Turn away using 15 deg angle of bank (AOB) for 15 seconds, ensure operation and obtain separate clearance\n\nWingman outside of the turn after departure:\n\n* Reverse the direction of the turn using 15 deg AOB for 15 seconds, ensure separation and obtain separate clearance\n\nWingman inside of the turn after departure:\n\n* Momentarily reduce power to ensure separation and inform lead to roll out, maintain bank to ensure lateral separation and obtain separate clearance\n\nFinal approach:\n\n* Momentarily turn away to ensure separation, commence the published missed approach procedure and obtain separate clearance\n\nMissed approach:\n\n* Momentarily turn away to ensure separation, continue the missed approach procedure while climbing 500 ft above missed approach altitude and get a separate clearance\n\nLocal procedures:\n\n* Sometimes local conditions require different lost wingman procedures\n\n== Rejoin ==\nPurpose:\n\n* The purpose of a rejoin is to get the flight back together safely and efficiently\n* Necessary after an interval / trail departure or lost wingman procedure\n\nBasics:\n\n* Lead should monitor wingmen during all rejoins\n* Airspeeds and bank angles are prebriefed or unit standard\n* Lead should make a radio call if flying different speed or bank angle\n\nRejoin Techniques:\n\n* A radar lock on may be used during the rejoin to provide range and overtake info\n* Maintaining 50 – 100 Knots of true overtake will provide a controlled, expeditious rejoin\n* “3” and “4” should be locked to the leader\n* At 3000 ft reduce power smoothly to control overtake\n* At 1500 ft, true overtake should be 50 knots\n* Use speed brakes if overtake is excessive\n* If overtake is excessive approaching the extended fingertip position, initiate a controlled overshoot\n\n== Crossunder ==\nGeneral:\n\n* Used to move wingman to the other side\n\nTwo Ship Crossunder:\n\n* On lead´s signal wingman will reduce power\n* The wingman will move back and down below lead´s Plane of Motion (POM) and add power again to avoid falling further behind\n* Next, wingman moves across and behind lead with a minimum of nose-tail clearance\n* Add power as required to avoid falling further behind\n* Once on the opposite side and with wingtip clearance, wingman will add power to move up and forward into position\n\nFour Ship Fingertip Crossunder:\n\n* If “2” is crossing to the side that “3” and “4” are on, “3” should move out to create room for “2”\n* “2” begins a normal crossunder (ensuring adequate spacing before crossing lead´s six o´clock)\n* If “3” and “4” are crossing simultaneously, “3” performs a normal crossunder on “2” and “1”\n* As “3” begins the crossunder, “4” performs a crossunder on “3”\n\nFour Ship Echelon Crossunder:\n\n* As “2” begins the crossunder, “3” performs a crossunder on “2”, “4” performs a crossunder on “3”\n\nVisual Signals:\n\n* Crossunder may be initiated using a wing dip to the side the wingman is requested to move or after instruction on radio\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 10.10.2025 13 59 01.png|frameless|663x663px]]\n\n== Lead Changes ==\n* Lead changes require an unmistakable transfer of responsibilities from one flight member to another\n* The flight member assuming lead will be no further aft than normal route/fingertip\n* Lead changes only change “Nav Lead” responsibilities\n* Flight lead responsibilities cannot be transferred\n\n== Formation Turns ==\n\n=== Fingertip Turns ===\n* Wingman will maintain fingertip position using fingertip spacing reference\n* Always stay slightly below preceding aircraft\n* Fingertip turns are limited to approximately 30 deg AOB\n* Lead will avoid sudden or abrupt changes in AOB or back stick pressure\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 10.10.2025 14 25 57.png|frameless|663x663px]]\n\n=== Echelon Turn Into the Echelon ===\n* Turns into the echelon will be avoided if at all possible\n* Wingman will maintain fingertip position using fingertip spacing references\n* Turns are limit to very shallow turns\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_10.10.2025_14_34_38.png|frameless|656x656px]]\n\n=== Echelon Turns Away From the Echelon ===\n* Plan to limit AOB to 60 degrees maximum\n* All aircraft will match lead´s roll rates while maintaining the same horizontal plane\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 10.10.2025 14 38 58.png|frameless|656x656px]]\n\n=== Route Turns ===\n* When turned away from the wingman, will stack level with the leader\n* When turned into, the wingman will only stack down enough to keep lead in sight and avoid his maneuvering plane\n\n=== Route Echelon Turns ===\n* If a turn is made into the echelon, each aircraft will only stack down enough to keep rest of the formation in sight and avoid their maneuvering plane\n* On turns away from the echelon, all aircrafts will maintain the same horizontal plane\n\n== Channel Changes ==\n* When a channel / frequency change is directed, wingman will normally move out from fingertip to route or from echelon to route echelon\n\n== Leaving Formation ==\nLeave Formation:\n\n* When directed to do so\n* When you loose sight of the aircraft ahead (lost wingman, blind)\n* When you are unable to join up or to stay in formation without crossing over, under or in front of the aircraft ahead\n* At any other time you feel that your presence in the formation constitutes a hazard\n* When leaving a formation, clear yourself in the direction of your turn and notify leader\n* If you have lost sight, comply with the appropriate lost wingman procedure\n* Rejoin only by directed to do so by the flight lead\n\n== Formation Landing ==\nLead:\n\n* Approach speed should be consistent with heavier aircraft\n* Wingmen should be positioned upwind if crosswind component exceeds 5 knots\n* Plan to land near the center of your half of the runway\n* Do not touchdown long and fast\n\nWingman:\n\n* Maintain a minimum of 10 feet lateral wingtip spacing\n* Stack level with lead as briefed when configured on final approach\n* Cross check the runway to ensure sufficient runway is available\n\n== Overhead Break ==\n* Used to split a formation in an orderly and expeditious manner to prepare for landing\n* Also called “360 overhead”\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 10.10.2025 15 05 58.png|frameless|936x936px]]\n\n== Standard Formations ==\n\n=== Fingertip Formations ===\nUsage:\n\n* Weather penetration\n* Arfield arrivals / departures\n* Shoe formations\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Lead aircraft is easy to see in bad weather\n* Close formation is used for formation takeoff / landing\n* Preferred before overhead break (360 overhead)\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* Does not allow for much head down time\n* Limited maneuvering\n* Shallow turns (up to 30 deg AOB) only\n* Both aircraft are easily detected as one group by an adversary\n\nParameters:\n\n* Use fingertip reference points\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 10.10.2025 15 20 01.png|frameless|330x330px]]\n\n=== Finger four (Four Ship Fingertip) ===\nUsage:\n\n* Weather penetration\n* Show formations\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Lead aircraft is easy to see in bad weather\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* Does not allow for much heads down time\n* Limited maneuvering\n* Shallow turns (up to 30 deg AOB) only\n* All aircraft are easily detected as a group by an adversary\n\nParameters:\n\n* Same as fingertip\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 13 45 26.png|frameless|463x463px]]\n\n== Route Formations ==\nUsage:\n\n* Enroute\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Lead aircraft is easy to see in moderate weather\n* Easy to fly, allowing for a good amount of heads down time\n* Steeper turns up to 60 deg AOB possible\n* Easier to fly in a low altitude environment\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* All aircraft are easily detected as one group by an adversary\n* Limited engagement flexibility\n\nParameters:\n\n* Modified fingertip\n* Up to 500 feet between aircraft\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 13 54 38.png|frameless|583x583px]]\n\n=== Route Echelon ===\nUsage:\n\n* Enroute\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Lead aircraft is easy to see in moderate weather\n* Easy to fly, allowing for a good amount of heads down time\n* Steeper turns up to 60 deg AOB possible\n* Easier to fly in a low altitude environment\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* All aircraft are easily detected as one group by an adversary\n* Limited engagement flexibility\n* Turns into the echelon have to be shallow (max. 30 deg AOB)\n\nParameters:\n\n* Modified echelon\n* Up to 500 feet between aircraft\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 13 59 02.png|frameless|513x513px]]\n\n== Trail Formations ==\n\n=== Close Trail Formation ===\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical, Whenever necessary (e.g. Very low level)\n\nParameters:\n\n* One to two ship lengths behind lead aircraft\n* Below lead aircraft´s jetwash\n\n=== Trail Formations ===\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical, Whenever necessary (e.g. Very low level)\n\nParameters:\n\n* Flown in a cone 30 deg – 60 deg aft of lead\n* Range briefed by flight lead\n* Avoid flying at lead´s high six\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 14 06 03.png|frameless|503x503px]]\n\n== Non-Standard Formation ==\n\n=== Fighting Wings ===\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Helps to maintain flight integrity in bad weather or rough terrain\n* Allows for heads down time\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* Poor 6 o´clock coverage\n* Easily detectable as bandit\n\nParameters:\n\n* 30 – 60 deg aft of 3/9 line\n* 30 – 60 deg above or below\n* 500 – 3000 ft spacing\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 14 11 11.png|frameless|666x666px]]\n\n=== Wedge ===\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Leader protected on 6 o´clock position\n* Leader free to maneuvering\n* Wingman may always switch sides\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* No 6 o´clock protection for wingman\n* Lead changes difficult\n\nParameters:\n\n* 30 – 60 deg aft of 3/9 line\n* 4000 – 6000 ft spacing\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 14 18 22.png|frameless|417x417px]]\n\n=== Fluid Four ===\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Wingman kept close\n* Good maneuverability\n* Concentration of force\n* Easily converts to three ship if required\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* Adversary can aquire all four aircraft\n* Defensive maneuvering confusing\n* Difficult in low altitude rough terrain\n\nParameters:\n\n* Two times fighting wing\n* 6000 – 9000 ft separated\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 14 58 09.png|frameless|620x620px]]\n\n=== Four Ship Wedge ===\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Very offensive for threats forward of 3/9 L\n* Easy to fly\n* Formation in bad weather / rough terrain\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* Poor 6 o´clock lookout\n* Formation easily detected by bandit\n* Limited defensive maneuvering flexibility\n\nParameters:\n\n* Two times wedge\n* 1,5 – 3NM separated\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 15 04 22.png|frameless|419x419px]]\n\n=== Spread Four ===\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Difficult to aquire entire flight visually\n* Maximized fire power for BVR\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* Maneuvering difficult\n* Very difficult to fly at low altitude\n\nParameters:\n\n* “1” and “2” 6000 – 9000 ft apart\n* “2” positioned 0 – 30 deg behind 3/9 of “1”\n* “1” and “3” 6000 – 9000 ft spread\n* “3” and “4” 6000 – 9000 ft apart\n* “4” positioned 0 – 30 deg behind 3/9 of “3”\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 15 13 03.png|frameless|804x804px]]\n\n=== Box Formation ===\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Difficult to detect simultaneously by bandit\n* Easy support\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* Difficult to maintain formation integrity\n\nParameters:\n\n* Two times spread\n* 1,5 – 3 NM separated\n* Variation: Four Ship Offset Box\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 15 18 55.png|frameless|397x397px]]\n\n=== Arrowhead Formation ===\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Same as box\n* Easier to fly as “2” allowing for more look out\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* Same as box\n\nParameters:\n\n* Aircraft “3” and “4” fly spread 1,5 – 3 NM behind “1!”\n* Aircraft “2” 30 – 60 deg behind leader´s 3/9 line and 3000 ft spacing\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 16 14 37.png|frameless|589x589px]]\n\n=== Three Ship Vic ===\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical\n\nParameters:\n\n* Leader 1,5 – 3 NM in front of “2” and “3”\n* “2” and “3” 6000 – 9000 ft apart\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 16 18 26.png|frameless|419x419px]]\n\n== Line Abreast (Spread) ==\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical\n* Enroute\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Minimizes the chances of simultaneous detection by bandit\n* Ordnance can be released quickly\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* Hard to fly for unexperienced wingman\n\nParameters:\n\n* 0 – 20 deg aft\n* 4000 – 12000 ft spacing\n* Between 30 deg above and below\n* Optimum 0 deg and 6000 – 9000 ft\n* Or as briefed\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 14 25 43.png|frameless|616x616px]]\n\n== Line Abreast Turns ==\n\n=== Tac- and Hook Turns ===\nExecution TAC Turn:\n\nA TAC Turn is a delayed ninety 90 turn either to right or left. Depending on the direction of turn, the aircraft outside the turn turns first. The turn of the inner / second aircraft turning is delayed by a visual reference. Once the nose of the first turning aircraft is coming around to point on the second, the second aircraft starts its turn.\n\nThe TAC Turn is flown in full military Power with sustained altitude and speed.\n\nExecution Hook Turn:\n\nA Hook turn is a simultaneous 180 degree turn either to the left or rigt. It is flown in full military Power with sunstained altitude and speed.\n\n[[File:16V5.DOC - 16v5.pdf — Mozilla Firefox 08.07.2026 04 40 52.png|frameless|783x783px]]\n\n=== Delayed 45 Degree Turns, Crossturns and Check Turns ===\nExecution Delayed 45 Degree Turn:\n\nA Delayed 45 Degree turn is self explained by its name. It is flown in full military power with susatined speed and altitude.\n\nDepending on the direction of turn, the inner aircraft starts its turn when crossing the outer aircrafts flightpath.\n\nExecution Crossturn:\n\nIs a Hook turn with both aircrafts turning into each other. The same parameters as for a normal Hook turn are required.\n\nExecution Check Turn:\n\nCheck Turns are Turns for Heading adjustments that are smaller than 45 Degrees. They are flown with a desired power setting but with sustained altitude. A slight formation offset is required. The lead will adjust the heading by gently turning into the choosen direction.\n\n[[File:16V5.DOC - 16v5.pdf — Mozilla Firefox 08.07.2026 04 42 04.png|frameless|791x791px]]\n\n----\n\nWho ever menages to come to this point, my congratulations. You are now a lot smarter and allowed to screenshot this and send it into \"genaral\"\n\n\'\'Iceman\'\'','utf-8'),
(597,'== Formation ==\nA formation is defined as more than one aircraft that, by prior arrangement between the pilots, operates as a single aircraft with regard to navigation and position reporting. Separation between aircraft within the formation is the responsibility of the flight leader and the pilots of the other aircraft in the flight. This includes transition periods when aircraft within the formation are maneuvering to attain separation from each other to effect individual control and during join up or breakaway.\n\n# Standard Formation. A formation in which a proximity of no more than 1 mile laterally or longitudinally and within 100 feet vertically from the flight leader is maintained by each wingman.\n# Nonstandard Formation. A formation operating under any of the following conditions:\n## When the flight leader has requested and air traffic control has approved other than standard formation dimensions.\n## When operating within an authorized Altitude reservation or under the provisions of a letter of agreement.\n## When the operations are conducted in airspace specifically designed for a special activity.\n\n=== Formation Responsibilities ===\nThe flight lead assigns responsibilities for each flight member. Dividing responsibilities ensures each pilot has a manageable number of tasks to perform. Flight member normal responsibilities are:\n\n* \'\'\'Number One:\'\'\' Primary planner and decision maker, primary navigation and radar lookout, visual lookout for mutual support of #2, and primary engaged fighter, if practical.\n* \'\'\'Number Two:\'\'\' Maintain formation position, visual lookout, mutual support of number one. Navigation position awareness, and radar awareness as other responsibilities allow.\n* \'\'\'Number Three:\'\'\' Support number one. Secondary planner and alternate decision maker, maintain support position for lead element, secondary navigation and radar monitor, visual lookout for number four, mutual support of the entire flight, and secondary engaged fighter, if practical.\n* \'\'\'Number Four:\'\'\' Maintain formation position, visual lookout for the flight, mutual support of number three. Navigation position awareness, and radar awareness as other responsibilities allow.\n\n=== Time Sharing ===\nThe goal on every low level training mission should be to improve the development of this cross-check. Practice and discipline are essential to maximizing visual mutual support. On each mission, pilots must start with the basic NEAR ROCKS, FAR ROCKS, CHECK SIX pattern and build up the visual search arena as allowed by task saturation, threat and flight conditions. When encountering extremely rough terrain, defensive reactions, navigation turns, etc., drop the lowest priority sectors in order. There will be times, such as hard turns, when only NEAR ROCKS can be cross-checked. The key is to quickly re-establish the cross-check one sector at a time as tasks permit.\n\n[[File:16V5.DOC - 16v5.pdf — Mozilla Firefox 08.07.2026 04 34 19.png|frameless|737x737px]]\n\n== Formation Departure ==\nA formation departure consists of more than one aircraft at intervals of 1 minute or less which, by prior arrangement between the pilots, operate as a single aircraft with regard to navigation and position reporting. The departure portion of the flight may terminate at a preplanned breakup point which may be located up to, but not beyond, the planned initial level-off at cruise altitude.\n\n== Taxi Operations ==\n* Keep use of power to a minimum\n* Taxi with 20 – 25 knots at straight taxiway sections and turn with 10 – 15 knots\n* Avoid jet blast damage\n* Check nose wheel steering and brakes as you taxi out of the spot\n* Check the flight instrument in the turn onto the taxiway\n* Taxi only in authorized areas\n* Slow down and taxi on the centerline in congested areas\n* Do not taxi within 10 feet of any obstacle\n* Do not taxi within 25 feet without a wingwalker\n* If taxiing behind an aircraft, a minimum spacing of four ship lengths is necessary\n\n== Before Departure (procedure at LCC, EOR, BTI) ==\n\n=== Aircraft checks ===\n* Aircraft are checked, armed and dearmed on Arming / Dearming Pads (of LCC/EOR Pads)\n* Before departure, aircraft are checked and armed at the departure end EOR pad\n* After Arrival, aircraft are disarmed at the arrival end EOR pad\n\n\'\'\'LCC\'\'\' = Last Chance Check\n\n\'\'\'EOR\'\'\' = End of Runway\n\n\'\'\'BTI\'\'\' = Before Takeoff Inspection\n\n=== End of Runway (EOR) ===\n* After stopping EOR, confirm all weapons switches safe\n* Keep hands in full view during arming\n* Aircraft should not taxi in front of aircraft with forward firing ordnance being armed / dearmed\n* As soon as you stop in the arming area, check your GS (reads zero)\n* If GS is zero or one knot, the INS platform is good\n* If GS is two or more, accomplish another alignment if possible\n* Ensure Speed Brake Stowed\n* Review takeoff procedure and details as EP immediately after takeoff\n* Check other aircraft for leaks, loose panels, proper configuration, etc\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_21_05_47.png|alt=Example of EOR Lineup|border|frameless|691x691px]]\n\n== Departure Briefing ==\nIn the so-called “Departure Briefing”, which takes place on the EOR / LCC Pad prior to the lineup, the Flight Lead informs his Flight about all relevant information regarding departure. A Departure Briefing usually contains the following information:\n\n# Which type of Lineup\n# Type of Takeoff (Two Ship, Interval or Trail)\n# Speeds\n# Lost Wingmen Procedure\n# Clearence for visual join-up in case of interval and trail departure\n# Further / Additional information / structure\n\nExample of a Departure Briefing:\n\nWe´ll make a 3 ship echelon gear doors allignment for interval departure 5 seconds, (max Brake 140, min Go 150, Go 160 (are V speeds currently not used in SK), rotation speed 200, AB off at 300, contract speed 350, 5 nose up, in case of lost Wingmen turn right for 30 seconds unsing 30 AOB, inform Lead, transition to instruments, obtain seperate ATC clearence and attempt re-join, say when airborne, you´re cleared to join on the right, say when in position.\n\n== Runway Lineup ==\nGeneral:\n\n* Leader will direct the appropriate runway line up based on winds and runway width\n* Wingmen should line up ensuring wingtip clearance\n\nLineup Types:\n\n* Two Ship Lineup\n* Three Ship Echelon\n* Four Ship Echelon\n* Four Ship three in The Slot\n* Four Ship four in The Slot\n* Three Ship three in The Slot\n\nLineup Standards:\n\n* Runway lineup is determined by taking into account several factors:\n** Wind direction and speed\n** Direction of turn out of traffic\n** Weather\n* Minimum wingtip spacing is wingtip clearance but may be wider as desired or required\n\n=== Two Ship Lineup ===\n* Lead takes the center of it´s half of the runway\n* The wingmen will line up lead´s main gear doors and take wingtip spacing as briefed\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_19_39_19.png|alt=Example Two Ship Lineup|frameless|692x692px]]\n\n=== Three ship echelon ===\n* Three ship formations may line up in echelon, if the runway is wide enough\n* “2” and “3” will line up gear doors and take wingtip spacing as briefed\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_19_46_25.png|frameless|753x753px]]\n\n=== Four Ship Echelon ===\n* Four ship may line up in echelon, if the runway is wide enough\n* “2” will line up gear doors and take wingtip spacing as briefed\n* “3” and “4” should align helmets of preceding flight members\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_19_51_04.png|frameless|628x628px]]\n\n=== Four Ship Three in The Slot ===\n* Lead should line up as far to the side of the runway as practical\n* “2” will place the wingtip closest to lead on the centerline an line up gear doors\n* “3” will line up between lead and “2”, maintaining nose-tail clearance out to 500 ft\n* “4” will line up offset from “2”s jet blast while aligning gear doors of “3”\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_19_59_31.png|frameless|713x713px]]\n\n=== Four Ship Four in The Slot ===\n* Lead should line up as far to the side of the runway as practical\n* “2” will place the wingtip closest to lead on the center line and line up gear doors\n* “4” will pull in between lead and “2” while aligning gear doors\n* “3” will line up with wingtip clearance on “2” while aligning gear doors\n* “4” will pull in further to align tail fin or gear doors of “3”\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_20_06_54.png|frameless|651x651px]]\n\n=== Three Ship Three in The Slot ===\n* Lead should line up as far to the side of the runway as practical\n* “2” will place the wingtip closest to lead on the center line and line up gear doors\n* “3” will line up between lead and “2”, maintaining nose-tail clearance out to 500 ft\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_20_10_03.png|frameless|696x696px]]\n\n== Takeoff ==\n\n=== Takeoff Types: ===\n* Two Ship Takeoff\n** Formation Takeoff\n** Usable for two aircraft only\n** Not usable during periods of high crosswind, low visibility, wet runway\n* Interval Departure\n** Used whenever a two ship takeoff is not possible\n** Separation assured by timing\n** Rejoin required after departure\n* (Radar assisted) Trail Departure\n** Used whenever a two ship takeoff is not possible\n** A type of interval departure\n** Separation assured by on board radar\n\n=== Two Ship Takeoff ===\n* Signal for brake release is a head nod or radio call\n* Leader will make a normal takeoff with throttle slightly retarded (power advantage for Wingman)\n* Leader will pass a gear up and AB out signal, if appropriate\n* Wingman will maintain wingtip clearance throughout takeoff roll\n* If overrunning the leader, wingman will be directed to assume the lead while continuing takeoff\n* If either member or the element must abort, the other member should continue the takeoff\n* In either case, stay on your side of the runway!\n\n=== Trail Departure ===\n* Used when conditions won´t permit a formation takeoff\n* Examples: Wet runways, crosswind limits, weapon loads, configuration differences, visibility\n* Set up radar for easy acquisition\n** Select RWS Mode\n* Priority is to fly the aircraft, not operating the radar\n* Ignore the radar until all high priority tasks are completed\n* During climbout, establish and maintain briefed power setting and airspeed\n* Unless restricted by procedure or ATC, begin all climbs at the same time as the leader\n* Lead should call passing every 5000 ft increment until all flight members are tied/visual\n* Rule of thumb: For 30 AOB turns, allow preceding aircraft to drift 5 deg laterally for each mile of separation between aircraft, Example: to maintain a 2 NM trail position, allow the preceding aircraft to drift 10 deg before initiating your turn\n* Once a tied or visual call ha been made, advise the leader if either radar or visual contact is subsequently lost.\n\n=== Lost Wingman ===\nGeneral:\n\n* Transition to instruments\n* Notify Lead (“Lost Wingman”)\n* Execute lost wingman procedures\n* Obtain separate ATC clearance\n* Attempt rejoin\n* Flight lead: Acknowledge lost wingman, transmit altitude, speed, post, etc.\n\nWings level after departure:\n\n* Turn away using 15 deg angle of bank (AOB) for 15 seconds, ensure operation and obtain separate clearance\n\nWingman outside of the turn after departure:\n\n* Reverse the direction of the turn using 15 deg AOB for 15 seconds, ensure separation and obtain separate clearance\n\nWingman inside of the turn after departure:\n\n* Momentarily reduce power to ensure separation and inform lead to roll out, maintain bank to ensure lateral separation and obtain separate clearance\n\nFinal approach:\n\n* Momentarily turn away to ensure separation, commence the published missed approach procedure and obtain separate clearance\n\nMissed approach:\n\n* Momentarily turn away to ensure separation, continue the missed approach procedure while climbing 500 ft above missed approach altitude and get a separate clearance\n\nLocal procedures:\n\n* Sometimes local conditions require different lost wingman procedures\n\n== Rejoin ==\nPurpose:\n\n* The purpose of a rejoin is to get the flight back together safely and efficiently\n* Necessary after an interval / trail departure or lost wingman procedure\n\nBasics:\n\n* Lead should monitor wingmen during all rejoins\n* Airspeeds and bank angles are prebriefed or unit standard\n* Lead should make a radio call if flying different speed or bank angle\n\nRejoin Techniques:\n\n* A radar lock on may be used during the rejoin to provide range and overtake info\n* Maintaining 50 – 100 Knots of true overtake will provide a controlled, expeditious rejoin\n* “3” and “4” should be locked to the leader\n* At 3000 ft reduce power smoothly to control overtake\n* At 1500 ft, true overtake should be 50 knots\n* Use speed brakes if overtake is excessive\n* If overtake is excessive approaching the extended fingertip position, initiate a controlled overshoot\n\n== Crossunder ==\nGeneral:\n\n* Used to move wingman to the other side\n\nTwo Ship Crossunder:\n\n* On lead´s signal wingman will reduce power\n* The wingman will move back and down below lead´s Plane of Motion (POM) and add power again to avoid falling further behind\n* Next, wingman moves across and behind lead with a minimum of nose-tail clearance\n* Add power as required to avoid falling further behind\n* Once on the opposite side and with wingtip clearance, wingman will add power to move up and forward into position\n\nFour Ship Fingertip Crossunder:\n\n* If “2” is crossing to the side that “3” and “4” are on, “3” should move out to create room for “2”\n* “2” begins a normal crossunder (ensuring adequate spacing before crossing lead´s six o´clock)\n* If “3” and “4” are crossing simultaneously, “3” performs a normal crossunder on “2” and “1”\n* As “3” begins the crossunder, “4” performs a crossunder on “3”\n\nFour Ship Echelon Crossunder:\n\n* As “2” begins the crossunder, “3” performs a crossunder on “2”, “4” performs a crossunder on “3”\n\nVisual Signals:\n\n* Crossunder may be initiated using a wing dip to the side the wingman is requested to move or after instruction on radio\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 10.10.2025 13 59 01.png|frameless|663x663px]]\n\n== Lead Changes ==\n* Lead changes require an unmistakable transfer of responsibilities from one flight member to another\n* The flight member assuming lead will be no further aft than normal route/fingertip\n* Lead changes only change “Nav Lead” responsibilities\n* Flight lead responsibilities cannot be transferred\n\n== Formation Turns ==\n\n=== Fingertip Turns ===\n* Wingman will maintain fingertip position using fingertip spacing reference\n* Always stay slightly below preceding aircraft\n* Fingertip turns are limited to approximately 30 deg AOB\n* Lead will avoid sudden or abrupt changes in AOB or back stick pressure\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 10.10.2025 14 25 57.png|frameless|663x663px]]\n\n=== Echelon Turn Into the Echelon ===\n* Turns into the echelon will be avoided if at all possible\n* Wingman will maintain fingertip position using fingertip spacing references\n* Turns are limit to very shallow turns\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_10.10.2025_14_34_38.png|frameless|656x656px]]\n\n=== Echelon Turns Away From the Echelon ===\n* Plan to limit AOB to 60 degrees maximum\n* All aircraft will match lead´s roll rates while maintaining the same horizontal plane\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 10.10.2025 14 38 58.png|frameless|656x656px]]\n\n=== Route Turns ===\n* When turned away from the wingman, will stack level with the leader\n* When turned into, the wingman will only stack down enough to keep lead in sight and avoid his maneuvering plane\n\n=== Route Echelon Turns ===\n* If a turn is made into the echelon, each aircraft will only stack down enough to keep rest of the formation in sight and avoid their maneuvering plane\n* On turns away from the echelon, all aircrafts will maintain the same horizontal plane\n\n== Channel Changes ==\n* When a channel / frequency change is directed, wingman will normally move out from fingertip to route or from echelon to route echelon\n\n== Leaving Formation ==\nLeave Formation:\n\n* When directed to do so\n* When you loose sight of the aircraft ahead (lost wingman, blind)\n* When you are unable to join up or to stay in formation without crossing over, under or in front of the aircraft ahead\n* At any other time you feel that your presence in the formation constitutes a hazard\n* When leaving a formation, clear yourself in the direction of your turn and notify leader\n* If you have lost sight, comply with the appropriate lost wingman procedure\n* Rejoin only by directed to do so by the flight lead\n\n== Formation Landing ==\nLead:\n\n* Approach speed should be consistent with heavier aircraft\n* Wingmen should be positioned upwind if crosswind component exceeds 5 knots\n* Plan to land near the center of your half of the runway\n* Do not touchdown long and fast\n\nWingman:\n\n* Maintain a minimum of 10 feet lateral wingtip spacing\n* Stack level with lead as briefed when configured on final approach\n* Cross check the runway to ensure sufficient runway is available\n\n== Overhead Break ==\n* Used to split a formation in an orderly and expeditious manner to prepare for landing\n* Also called “360 overhead”\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 10.10.2025 15 05 58.png|frameless|936x936px]]\n\n== Standard Formations ==\n\n=== Fingertip Formations ===\nUsage:\n\n* Weather penetration\n* Arfield arrivals / departures\n* Shoe formations\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Lead aircraft is easy to see in bad weather\n* Close formation is used for formation takeoff / landing\n* Preferred before overhead break (360 overhead)\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* Does not allow for much head down time\n* Limited maneuvering\n* Shallow turns (up to 30 deg AOB) only\n* Both aircraft are easily detected as one group by an adversary\n\nParameters:\n\n* Use fingertip reference points\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 10.10.2025 15 20 01.png|frameless|330x330px]]\n\n=== Finger four (Four Ship Fingertip) ===\nUsage:\n\n* Weather penetration\n* Show formations\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Lead aircraft is easy to see in bad weather\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* Does not allow for much heads down time\n* Limited maneuvering\n* Shallow turns (up to 30 deg AOB) only\n* All aircraft are easily detected as a group by an adversary\n\nParameters:\n\n* Same as fingertip\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 13 45 26.png|frameless|463x463px]]\n\n== Route Formations ==\nUsage:\n\n* Enroute\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Lead aircraft is easy to see in moderate weather\n* Easy to fly, allowing for a good amount of heads down time\n* Steeper turns up to 60 deg AOB possible\n* Easier to fly in a low altitude environment\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* All aircraft are easily detected as one group by an adversary\n* Limited engagement flexibility\n\nParameters:\n\n* Modified fingertip\n* Up to 500 feet between aircraft\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 13 54 38.png|frameless|583x583px]]\n\n=== Route Echelon ===\nUsage:\n\n* Enroute\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Lead aircraft is easy to see in moderate weather\n* Easy to fly, allowing for a good amount of heads down time\n* Steeper turns up to 60 deg AOB possible\n* Easier to fly in a low altitude environment\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* All aircraft are easily detected as one group by an adversary\n* Limited engagement flexibility\n* Turns into the echelon have to be shallow (max. 30 deg AOB)\n\nParameters:\n\n* Modified echelon\n* Up to 500 feet between aircraft\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 13 59 02.png|frameless|513x513px]]\n\n== Trail Formations ==\n\n=== Close Trail Formation ===\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical, Whenever necessary (e.g. Very low level)\n\nParameters:\n\n* One to two ship lengths behind lead aircraft\n* Below lead aircraft´s jetwash\n\n=== Trail Formations ===\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical, Whenever necessary (e.g. Very low level)\n\nParameters:\n\n* Flown in a cone 30 deg – 60 deg aft of lead\n* Range briefed by flight lead\n* Avoid flying at lead´s high six\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 14 06 03.png|frameless|503x503px]]\n\n== Non-Standard Formation ==\n\n=== Fighting Wings ===\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Helps to maintain flight integrity in bad weather or rough terrain\n* Allows for heads down time\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* Poor 6 o´clock coverage\n* Easily detectable as bandit\n\nParameters:\n\n* 30 – 60 deg aft of 3/9 line\n* 30 – 60 deg above or below\n* 500 – 3000 ft spacing\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 14 11 11.png|frameless|666x666px]]\n\n=== Wedge ===\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Leader protected on 6 o´clock position\n* Leader free to maneuvering\n* Wingman may always switch sides\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* No 6 o´clock protection for wingman\n* Lead changes difficult\n\nParameters:\n\n* 30 – 60 deg aft of 3/9 line\n* 4000 – 6000 ft spacing\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 14 18 22.png|frameless|417x417px]]\n\n=== Fluid Four ===\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Wingman kept close\n* Good maneuverability\n* Concentration of force\n* Easily converts to three ship if required\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* Adversary can aquire all four aircraft\n* Defensive maneuvering confusing\n* Difficult in low altitude rough terrain\n\nParameters:\n\n* Two times fighting wing\n* 6000 – 9000 ft separated\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 14 58 09.png|frameless|620x620px]]\n\n=== Four Ship Wedge ===\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Very offensive for threats forward of 3/9 L\n* Easy to fly\n* Formation in bad weather / rough terrain\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* Poor 6 o´clock lookout\n* Formation easily detected by bandit\n* Limited defensive maneuvering flexibility\n\nParameters:\n\n* Two times wedge\n* 1,5 – 3NM separated\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 15 04 22.png|frameless|419x419px]]\n\n=== Spread Four ===\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Difficult to aquire entire flight visually\n* Maximized fire power for BVR\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* Maneuvering difficult\n* Very difficult to fly at low altitude\n\nParameters:\n\n* “1” and “2” 6000 – 9000 ft apart\n* “2” positioned 0 – 30 deg behind 3/9 of “1”\n* “1” and “3” 6000 – 9000 ft spread\n* “3” and “4” 6000 – 9000 ft apart\n* “4” positioned 0 – 30 deg behind 3/9 of “3”\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 15 13 03.png|frameless|804x804px]]\n\n=== Box Formation ===\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Difficult to detect simultaneously by bandit\n* Easy support\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* Difficult to maintain formation integrity\n\nParameters:\n\n* Two times spread\n* 1,5 – 3 NM separated\n* Variation: Four Ship Offset Box\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 15 18 55.png|frameless|397x397px]]\n\n=== Arrowhead Formation ===\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Same as box\n* Easier to fly as “2” allowing for more look out\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* Same as box\n\nParameters:\n\n* Aircraft “3” and “4” fly spread 1,5 – 3 NM behind “1!”\n* Aircraft “2” 30 – 60 deg behind leader´s 3/9 line and 3000 ft spacing\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 16 14 37.png|frameless|589x589px]]\n\n=== Three Ship Vic ===\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical\n\nParameters:\n\n* Leader 1,5 – 3 NM in front of “2” and “3”\n* “2” and “3” 6000 – 9000 ft apart\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 16 18 26.png|frameless|419x419px]]\n\n== Line Abreast (Spread) ==\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical\n* Enroute\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Minimizes the chances of simultaneous detection by bandit\n* Ordnance can be released quickly\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* Hard to fly for unexperienced wingman\n\nParameters:\n\n* 0 – 20 deg aft\n* 4000 – 12000 ft spacing\n* Between 30 deg above and below\n* Optimum 0 deg and 6000 – 9000 ft\n* Or as briefed\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 14 25 43.png|frameless|616x616px]]\n\n== Line Abreast Turns ==\n\n=== Tac- and Hook Turns ===\nExecution TAC Turn:\n\nA TAC Turn is a delayed ninety 90 turn either to right or left. Depending on the direction of turn, the aircraft outside the turn turns first. The turn of the inner / second aircraft turning is delayed by a visual reference. Once the nose of the first turning aircraft is coming around to point on the second, the second aircraft starts its turn.\n\nThe TAC Turn is flown in full military Power with sustained altitude and speed.\n\nExecution Hook Turn:\n\nA Hook turn is a simultaneous 180 degree turn either to the left or rigt. It is flown in full military Power with sunstained altitude and speed.\n\n[[File:16V5.DOC - 16v5.pdf — Mozilla Firefox 08.07.2026 04 40 52.png|frameless|783x783px]]\n\n=== Delayed 45 Degree Turns, Crossturns and Check Turns ===\nExecution Delayed 45 Degree Turn:\n\nA Delayed 45 Degree turn is self explained by its name. It is flown in full military power with susatined speed and altitude.\n\nDepending on the direction of turn, the inner aircraft starts its turn when crossing the outer aircrafts flightpath.\n\nExecution Crossturn:\n\nIs a Hook turn with both aircrafts turning into each other. The same parameters as for a normal Hook turn are required.\n\nExecution Check Turn:\n\nCheck Turns are Turns for Heading adjustments that are smaller than 45 Degrees. They are flown with a desired power setting but with sustained altitude. A slight formation offset is required. The lead will adjust the heading by gently turning into the choosen direction.\n\n[[File:16V5.DOC - 16v5.pdf — Mozilla Firefox 08.07.2026 04 42 04.png|frameless|791x791px]]\n\n----\n\nTo whoever manages to reach this point, my congratulations. Screenshot this and send it into \"general\" ;)\n\n\'\'Iceman\'\'','utf-8'),
(598,'== Formation ==\nA formation is defined as more than one aircraft that, by prior arrangement between the pilots, operates as a single aircraft with regard to navigation and position reporting. Separation between aircraft within the formation is the responsibility of the flight leader and the pilots of the other aircraft in the flight. This includes transition periods when aircraft within the formation are maneuvering to attain separation from each other to effect individual control and during join up or breakaway.\n\n# Standard Formation. A formation in which a proximity of no more than 1 mile laterally or longitudinally and within 100 feet vertically from the flight leader is maintained by each wingman.\n# Nonstandard Formation. A formation operating under any of the following conditions:\n## When the flight leader has requested and air traffic control has approved other than standard formation dimensions.\n## When operating within an authorized Altitude reservation or under the provisions of a letter of agreement.\n## When the operations are conducted in airspace specifically designed for a special activity.\n\n=== Formation Responsibilities ===\nThe flight lead assigns responsibilities for each flight member. Dividing responsibilities ensures each pilot has a manageable number of tasks to perform. Flight member normal responsibilities are:\n\n* \'\'\'Number One:\'\'\' Primary planner and decision maker, primary navigation and radar lookout, visual lookout for mutual support of #2, and primary engaged fighter, if practical.\n* \'\'\'Number Two:\'\'\' Maintain formation position, visual lookout, mutual support of number one. Navigation position awareness, and radar awareness as other responsibilities allow.\n* \'\'\'Number Three:\'\'\' Support number one. Secondary planner and alternate decision maker, maintain support position for lead element, secondary navigation and radar monitor, visual lookout for number four, mutual support of the entire flight, and secondary engaged fighter, if practical.\n* \'\'\'Number Four:\'\'\' Maintain formation position, visual lookout for the flight, mutual support of number three. Navigation position awareness, and radar awareness as other responsibilities allow.\n\n=== Time Sharing ===\nThe goal on every low level training mission should be to improve the development of this cross-check. Practice and discipline are essential to maximizing visual mutual support. On each mission, pilots must start with the basic NEAR ROCKS, FAR ROCKS, CHECK SIX pattern and build up the visual search arena as allowed by task saturation, threat and flight conditions. When encountering extremely rough terrain, defensive reactions, navigation turns, etc., drop the lowest priority sectors in order. There will be times, such as hard turns, when only NEAR ROCKS can be cross-checked. The key is to quickly re-establish the cross-check one sector at a time as tasks permit.\n\n[[File:16V5.DOC - 16v5.pdf — Mozilla Firefox 08.07.2026 04 34 19.png|frameless|737x737px]]\n\n== Formation Departure ==\nA formation departure consists of more than one aircraft at intervals of 1 minute or less which, by prior arrangement between the pilots, operate as a single aircraft with regard to navigation and position reporting. The departure portion of the flight may terminate at a preplanned breakup point which may be located up to, but not beyond, the planned initial level-off at cruise altitude.\n\n== Taxi Operations ==\n* Keep use of power to a minimum\n* Taxi with 20 – 25 knots at straight taxiway sections and turn with 10 – 15 knots\n* Avoid jet blast damage\n* Check nose wheel steering and brakes as you taxi out of the spot\n* Check the flight instrument in the turn onto the taxiway\n* Taxi only in authorized areas\n* Slow down and taxi on the centerline in congested areas\n* Do not taxi within 10 feet of any obstacle\n* Do not taxi within 25 feet without a wingwalker\n* If taxiing behind an aircraft, a minimum spacing of four ship lengths is necessary\n\n== Before Departure (procedure at LCC, EOR, BTI) ==\n\n=== Aircraft checks ===\n* Aircraft are checked, armed and dearmed on Arming / Dearming Pads (of LCC/EOR Pads)\n* Before departure, aircraft are checked and armed at the departure end EOR pad\n* After Arrival, aircraft are disarmed at the arrival end EOR pad\n\n\'\'\'LCC\'\'\' = Last Chance Check\n\n\'\'\'EOR\'\'\' = End of Runway\n\n\'\'\'BTI\'\'\' = Before Takeoff Inspection\n\n=== End of Runway (EOR) ===\n* After stopping EOR, confirm all weapons switches safe\n* Keep hands in full view during arming\n* Aircraft should not taxi in front of aircraft with forward firing ordnance being armed / dearmed\n* As soon as you stop in the arming area, check your GS (reads zero)\n* If GS is zero or one knot, the INS platform is good\n* If GS is two or more, accomplish another alignment if possible\n* Ensure Speed Brake Stowed\n* Review takeoff procedure and details as EP immediately after takeoff\n* Check other aircraft for leaks, loose panels, proper configuration, etc\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_21_05_47.png|alt=Example of EOR Lineup|border|frameless|691x691px]]\n\n== Departure Briefing ==\nIn the so-called “Departure Briefing”, which takes place on the EOR / LCC Pad prior to the lineup, the Flight Lead informs his Flight about all relevant information regarding departure. A Departure Briefing usually contains the following information:\n\n# Which type of Lineup\n# Type of Takeoff (Two Ship, Interval or Trail)\n# Speeds\n# Lost Wingmen Procedure\n# Clearence for visual join-up in case of interval and trail departure\n# Further / Additional information / structure\n\nExample of a Departure Briefing:\n\nWe´ll make a 3 ship echelon gear doors allignment for interval departure 5 seconds, (max Brake 140, min Go 150, Go 160 (are V speeds currently not used in SK), rotation speed 200, AB off at 300, contract speed 350, 5 nose up, in case of lost Wingmen turn right for 30 seconds using 30 AOB, inform Lead, transition to instruments, obtain separate ATC clearance and attempt re-join, say when airborne, you´re cleared to join on the right, say when in position.\n\n== Runway Lineup ==\nGeneral:\n\n* Leader will direct the appropriate runway line up based on winds and runway width\n* Wingmen should line up ensuring wingtip clearance\n\nLineup Types:\n\n* Two Ship Lineup\n* Three Ship Echelon\n* Four Ship Echelon\n* Four Ship three in The Slot\n* Four Ship four in The Slot\n* Three Ship three in The Slot\n\nLineup Standards:\n\n* Runway lineup is determined by taking into account several factors:\n** Wind direction and speed\n** Direction of turn out of traffic\n** Weather\n* Minimum wingtip spacing is wingtip clearance but may be wider as desired or required\n\n=== Two Ship Lineup ===\n* Lead takes the center of it´s half of the runway\n* The wingmen will line up lead´s main gear doors and take wingtip spacing as briefed\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_19_39_19.png|alt=Example Two Ship Lineup|frameless|692x692px]]\n\n=== Three ship echelon ===\n* Three ship formations may line up in echelon, if the runway is wide enough\n* “2” and “3” will line up gear doors and take wingtip spacing as briefed\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_19_46_25.png|frameless|753x753px]]\n\n=== Four Ship Echelon ===\n* Four ship may line up in echelon, if the runway is wide enough\n* “2” will line up gear doors and take wingtip spacing as briefed\n* “3” and “4” should align helmets of preceding flight members\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_19_51_04.png|frameless|628x628px]]\n\n=== Four Ship Three in The Slot ===\n* Lead should line up as far to the side of the runway as practical\n* “2” will place the wingtip closest to lead on the centerline an line up gear doors\n* “3” will line up between lead and “2”, maintaining nose-tail clearance out to 500 ft\n* “4” will line up offset from “2”s jet blast while aligning gear doors of “3”\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_19_59_31.png|frameless|713x713px]]\n\n=== Four Ship Four in The Slot ===\n* Lead should line up as far to the side of the runway as practical\n* “2” will place the wingtip closest to lead on the center line and line up gear doors\n* “4” will pull in between lead and “2” while aligning gear doors\n* “3” will line up with wingtip clearance on “2” while aligning gear doors\n* “4” will pull in further to align tail fin or gear doors of “3”\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_20_06_54.png|frameless|651x651px]]\n\n=== Three Ship Three in The Slot ===\n* Lead should line up as far to the side of the runway as practical\n* “2” will place the wingtip closest to lead on the center line and line up gear doors\n* “3” will line up between lead and “2”, maintaining nose-tail clearance out to 500 ft\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_20_10_03.png|frameless|696x696px]]\n\n== Takeoff ==\n\n=== Takeoff Types: ===\n* Two Ship Takeoff\n** Formation Takeoff\n** Usable for two aircraft only\n** Not usable during periods of high crosswind, low visibility, wet runway\n* Interval Departure\n** Used whenever a two ship takeoff is not possible\n** Separation assured by timing\n** Rejoin required after departure\n* (Radar assisted) Trail Departure\n** Used whenever a two ship takeoff is not possible\n** A type of interval departure\n** Separation assured by on board radar\n\n=== Two Ship Takeoff ===\n* Signal for brake release is a head nod or radio call\n* Leader will make a normal takeoff with throttle slightly retarded (power advantage for Wingman)\n* Leader will pass a gear up and AB out signal, if appropriate\n* Wingman will maintain wingtip clearance throughout takeoff roll\n* If overrunning the leader, wingman will be directed to assume the lead while continuing takeoff\n* If either member or the element must abort, the other member should continue the takeoff\n* In either case, stay on your side of the runway!\n\n=== Trail Departure ===\n* Used when conditions won´t permit a formation takeoff\n* Examples: Wet runways, crosswind limits, weapon loads, configuration differences, visibility\n* Set up radar for easy acquisition\n** Select RWS Mode\n* Priority is to fly the aircraft, not operating the radar\n* Ignore the radar until all high priority tasks are completed\n* During climbout, establish and maintain briefed power setting and airspeed\n* Unless restricted by procedure or ATC, begin all climbs at the same time as the leader\n* Lead should call passing every 5000 ft increment until all flight members are tied/visual\n* Rule of thumb: For 30 AOB turns, allow preceding aircraft to drift 5 deg laterally for each mile of separation between aircraft, Example: to maintain a 2 NM trail position, allow the preceding aircraft to drift 10 deg before initiating your turn\n* Once a tied or visual call has been made, advise the leader if either radar or visual contact is subsequently lost.\n\n=== Lost Wingman ===\nGeneral:\n\n* Transition to instruments\n* Notify Lead (“Lost Wingman”)\n* Execute lost wingman procedures\n* Obtain separate ATC clearance\n* Attempt rejoin\n* Flight lead: Acknowledge lost wingman, transmit altitude, speed, post, etc.\n\nWings level after departure:\n\n* Turn away using 15 deg angle of bank (AOB) for 15 seconds, ensure operation and obtain separate clearance\n\nWingman outside of the turn after departure:\n\n* Reverse the direction of the turn using 15 deg AOB for 15 seconds, ensure separation and obtain separate clearance\n\nWingman inside of the turn after departure:\n\n* Momentarily reduce power to ensure separation and inform lead to roll out, maintain bank to ensure lateral separation and obtain separate clearance\n\nFinal approach:\n\n* Momentarily turn away to ensure separation, commence the published missed approach procedure and obtain separate clearance\n\nMissed approach:\n\n* Momentarily turn away to ensure separation, continue the missed approach procedure while climbing 500 ft above missed approach altitude and get a separate clearance\n\nLocal procedures:\n\n* Sometimes local conditions require different lost wingman procedures\n\n== Rejoin ==\nPurpose:\n\n* The purpose of a rejoin is to get the flight back together safely and efficiently\n* Necessary after an interval / trail departure or lost wingman procedure\n\nBasics:\n\n* Lead should monitor wingmen during all rejoins\n* Airspeeds and bank angles are prebriefed or unit standard\n* Lead should make a radio call if flying different speed or bank angle\n\nRejoin Techniques:\n\n* A radar lock on may be used during the rejoin to provide range and overtake info\n* Maintaining 50 – 100 Knots of true overtake will provide a controlled, expeditious rejoin\n* “3” and “4” should be locked to the leader\n* At 3000 ft reduce power smoothly to control overtake\n* At 1500 ft, true overtake should be 50 knots\n* Use speed brakes if overtake is excessive\n* If overtake is excessive approaching the extended fingertip position, initiate a controlled overshoot\n\n== Crossunder ==\nGeneral:\n\n* Used to move wingman to the other side\n\nTwo Ship Crossunder:\n\n* On lead´s signal wingman will reduce power\n* The wingman will move back and down below lead´s Plane of Motion (POM) and add power again to avoid falling further behind\n* Next, wingman moves across and behind lead with a minimum of nose-tail clearance\n* Add power as required to avoid falling further behind\n* Once on the opposite side and with wingtip clearance, wingman will add power to move up and forward into position\n\nFour Ship Fingertip Crossunder:\n\n* If “2” is crossing to the side that “3” and “4” are on, “3” should move out to create room for “2”\n* “2” begins a normal crossunder (ensuring adequate spacing before crossing lead´s six o´clock)\n* If “3” and “4” are crossing simultaneously, “3” performs a normal crossunder on “2” and “1”\n* As “3” begins the crossunder, “4” performs a crossunder on “3”\n\nFour Ship Echelon Crossunder:\n\n* As “2” begins the crossunder, “3” performs a crossunder on “2”, “4” performs a crossunder on “3”\n\nVisual Signals:\n\n* Crossunder may be initiated using a wing dip to the side the wingman is requested to move or after instruction on radio\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 10.10.2025 13 59 01.png|frameless|663x663px]]\n\n== Lead Changes ==\n* Lead changes require an unmistakable transfer of responsibilities from one flight member to another\n* The flight member assuming lead will be no further aft than normal route/fingertip\n* Lead changes only change “Nav Lead” responsibilities\n* Flight lead responsibilities cannot be transferred\n\n== Formation Turns ==\n\n=== Fingertip Turns ===\n* Wingman will maintain fingertip position using fingertip spacing reference\n* Always stay slightly below preceding aircraft\n* Fingertip turns are limited to approximately 30 deg AOB\n* Lead will avoid sudden or abrupt changes in AOB or back stick pressure\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 10.10.2025 14 25 57.png|frameless|663x663px]]\n\n=== Echelon Turn Into the Echelon ===\n* Turns into the echelon will be avoided if at all possible\n* Wingman will maintain fingertip position using fingertip spacing references\n* Turns are limit to very shallow turns\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_10.10.2025_14_34_38.png|frameless|656x656px]]\n\n=== Echelon Turns Away From the Echelon ===\n* Plan to limit AOB to 60 degrees maximum\n* All aircraft will match lead´s roll rates while maintaining the same horizontal plane\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 10.10.2025 14 38 58.png|frameless|656x656px]]\n\n=== Route Turns ===\n* When turned away from the wingman, will stack level with the leader\n* When turned into, the wingman will only stack down enough to keep lead in sight and avoid his maneuvering plane\n\n=== Route Echelon Turns ===\n* If a turn is made into the echelon, each aircraft will only stack down enough to keep rest of the formation in sight and avoid their maneuvering plane\n* On turns away from the echelon, all aircrafts will maintain the same horizontal plane\n\n== Channel Changes ==\n* When a channel / frequency change is directed, wingman will normally move out from fingertip to route or from echelon to route echelon\n\n== Leaving Formation ==\nLeave Formation:\n\n* When directed to do so\n* When you lose sight of the aircraft ahead (lost wingman, blind)\n* When you are unable to join up or to stay in formation without crossing over, under or in front of the aircraft ahead\n* At any other time you feel that your presence in the formation constitutes a hazard\n* When leaving a formation, clear yourself in the direction of your turn and notify leader\n* If you have lost sight, comply with the appropriate lost wingman procedure\n* Rejoin only by directed to do so by the flight lead\n\n== Formation Landing ==\nLead:\n\n* Approach speed should be consistent with heavier aircraft\n* Wingmen should be positioned upwind if crosswind component exceeds 5 knots\n* Plan to land near the center of your half of the runway\n* Do not touchdown long and fast\n\nWingman:\n\n* Maintain a minimum of 10 feet lateral wingtip spacing\n* Stack level with lead as briefed when configured on final approach\n* Cross check the runway to ensure sufficient runway is available\n\n== Overhead Break ==\n* Used to split a formation in an orderly and expeditious manner to prepare for landing\n* Also called “360 overhead”\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 10.10.2025 15 05 58.png|frameless|936x936px]]\n\n== Standard Formations ==\n\n=== Fingertip Formations ===\nUsage:\n\n* Weather penetration\n* Airfield arrivals / departures\n* Show formations\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Lead aircraft is easy to see in bad weather\n* Close formation is used for formation takeoff / landing\n* Preferred before overhead break (360 overhead)\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* Does not allow for much head down time\n* Limited maneuvering\n* Shallow turns (up to 30 deg AOB) only\n* Both aircraft are easily detected as one group by an adversary\n\nParameters:\n\n* Use fingertip reference points\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 10.10.2025 15 20 01.png|frameless|330x330px]]\n\n=== Finger four (Four Ship Fingertip) ===\nUsage:\n\n* Weather penetration\n* Show formations\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Lead aircraft is easy to see in bad weather\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* Does not allow for much heads down time\n* Limited maneuvering\n* Shallow turns (up to 30 deg AOB) only\n* All aircraft are easily detected as a group by an adversary\n\nParameters:\n\n* Same as fingertip\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 13 45 26.png|frameless|463x463px]]\n\n== Route Formations ==\nUsage:\n\n* Enroute\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Lead aircraft is easy to see in moderate weather\n* Easy to fly, allowing for a good amount of heads down time\n* Steeper turns up to 60 deg AOB possible\n* Easier to fly in a low altitude environment\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* All aircraft are easily detected as one group by an adversary\n* Limited engagement flexibility\n\nParameters:\n\n* Modified fingertip\n* Up to 500 feet between aircraft\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 13 54 38.png|frameless|583x583px]]\n\n=== Route Echelon ===\nUsage:\n\n* Enroute\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Lead aircraft is easy to see in moderate weather\n* Easy to fly, allowing for a good amount of heads down time\n* Steeper turns up to 60 deg AOB possible\n* Easier to fly in a low altitude environment\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* All aircraft are easily detected as one group by an adversary\n* Limited engagement flexibility\n* Turns into the echelon have to be shallow (max. 30 deg AOB)\n\nParameters:\n\n* Modified echelon\n* Up to 500 feet between aircraft\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 13 59 02.png|frameless|513x513px]]\n\n== Trail Formations ==\n\n=== Close Trail Formation ===\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical, Whenever necessary (e.g. Very low level)\n\nParameters:\n\n* One to two ship lengths behind lead aircraft\n* Below lead aircraft´s jetwash\n\n=== Trail Formations ===\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical, Whenever necessary (e.g. Very low level)\n\nParameters:\n\n* Flown in a cone 30 deg – 60 deg aft of lead\n* Range briefed by flight lead\n* Avoid flying at lead´s high six\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 14 06 03.png|frameless|503x503px]]\n\n== Non-Standard Formation ==\n\n=== Fighting Wings ===\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Helps to maintain flight integrity in bad weather or rough terrain\n* Allows for heads down time\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* Poor 6 o´clock coverage\n* Easily detectable as bandit\n\nParameters:\n\n* 30 – 60 deg aft of 3/9 line\n* 30 – 60 deg above or below\n* 500 – 3000 ft spacing\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 14 11 11.png|frameless|666x666px]]\n\n=== Wedge ===\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Leader protected on 6 o´clock position\n* Leader free to maneuvering\n* Wingman may always switch sides\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* No 6 o´clock protection for wingman\n* Lead changes difficult\n\nParameters:\n\n* 30 – 60 deg aft of 3/9 line\n* 4000 – 6000 ft spacing\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 14 18 22.png|frameless|417x417px]]\n\n=== Fluid Four ===\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Wingman kept close\n* Good maneuverability\n* Concentration of force\n* Easily converts to three ship if required\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* Adversary can acquire all four aircraft\n* Defensive maneuvering confusing\n* Difficult in low altitude rough terrain\n\nParameters:\n\n* Two times fighting wing\n* 6000 – 9000 ft separated\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 14 58 09.png|frameless|620x620px]]\n\n=== Four Ship Wedge ===\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Very offensive for threats forward of 3/9 L\n* Easy to fly\n* Formation in bad weather / rough terrain\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* Poor 6 o´clock lookout\n* Formation easily detected by bandit\n* Limited defensive maneuvering flexibility\n\nParameters:\n\n* Two times wedge\n* 1,5 – 3NM separated\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 15 04 22.png|frameless|419x419px]]\n\n=== Spread Four ===\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Difficult to acquire entire flight visually\n* Maximized fire power for BVR\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* Maneuvering difficult\n* Very difficult to fly at low altitude\n\nParameters:\n\n* “1” and “2” 6000 – 9000 ft apart\n* “2” positioned 0 – 30 deg behind 3/9 of “1”\n* “1” and “3” 6000 – 9000 ft spread\n* “3” and “4” 6000 – 9000 ft apart\n* “4” positioned 0 – 30 deg behind 3/9 of “3”\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 15 13 03.png|frameless|804x804px]]\n\n=== Box Formation ===\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Difficult to detect simultaneously by bandit\n* Easy support\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* Difficult to maintain formation integrity\n\nParameters:\n\n* Two times spread\n* 1,5 – 3 NM separated\n* Variation: Four Ship Offset Box\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 15 18 55.png|frameless|397x397px]]\n\n=== Arrowhead Formation ===\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Same as box\n* Easier to fly as “2” allowing for more look out\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* Same as box\n\nParameters:\n\n* Aircraft “3” and “4” fly spread 1,5 – 3 NM behind “1!”\n* Aircraft “2” 30 – 60 deg behind leader´s 3/9 line and 3000 ft spacing\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 16 14 37.png|frameless|589x589px]]\n\n=== Three Ship Vic ===\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical\n\nParameters:\n\n* Leader 1,5 – 3 NM in front of “2” and “3”\n* “2” and “3” 6000 – 9000 ft apart\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 16 18 26.png|frameless|419x419px]]\n\n== Line Abreast (Spread) ==\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical\n* Enroute\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Minimizes the chances of simultaneous detection by bandit\n* Ordnance can be released quickly\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* Hard to fly for inexperienced wingman\n\nParameters:\n\n* 0 – 20 deg aft\n* 4000 – 12000 ft spacing\n* Between 30 deg above and below\n* Optimum 0 deg and 6000 – 9000 ft\n* Or as briefed\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 14 25 43.png|frameless|616x616px]]\n\n== Line Abreast Turns ==\n\n=== Tac- and Hook Turns ===\nExecution TAC Turn:\n\nA TAC Turn is a delayed ninety 90 turn either to right or left. Depending on the direction of turn, the aircraft outside the turn turns first. The turn of the inner / second aircraft turning is delayed by a visual reference. Once the nose of the first turning aircraft is coming around to point on the second, the second aircraft starts its turn.\n\nThe TAC Turn is flown in full military Power with sustained altitude and speed.\n\nExecution Hook Turn:\n\nA Hook turn is a simultaneous 180 degree turn either to the left or right. It is flown in full military Power with sustained altitude and speed.\n\n[[File:16V5.DOC - 16v5.pdf — Mozilla Firefox 08.07.2026 04 40 52.png|frameless|783x783px]]\n\n=== Delayed 45 Degree Turns, Crossturns and Check Turns ===\nExecution Delayed 45 Degree Turn:\n\nA Delayed 45 Degree turn is self explained by its name. It is flown in full military power with sustained speed and altitude.\n\nDepending on the direction of turn, the inner aircraft starts its turn when crossing the outer aircrafts flightpath.\n\nExecution Crossturn:\n\nIs a Hook turn with both aircrafts turning into each other. The same parameters as for a normal Hook turn are required.\n\nExecution Check Turn:\n\nCheck Turns are Turns for Heading adjustments that are smaller than 45 Degrees. They are flown with a desired power setting but with sustained altitude. A slight formation offset is required. The lead will adjust the heading by gently turning into the chosen direction.\n\n[[File:16V5.DOC - 16v5.pdf — Mozilla Firefox 08.07.2026 04 42 04.png|frameless|791x791px]]\n\n----\n\nTo whoever manages to reach this point, my congratulations. Screenshot this and send it into \"general\" ;)\n\n\'\'Iceman\'\'','utf-8'),
(599,'== Formation ==\nA formation is defined as more than one aircraft that, by prior arrangement between the pilots, operates as a single aircraft with regard to navigation and position reporting. Separation between aircraft within the formation is the responsibility of the flight leader and the pilots of the other aircraft in the flight. This includes transition periods when aircraft within the formation are maneuvering to attain separation from each other to effect individual control and during join up or breakaway.\n\n# Standard Formation. A formation in which a proximity of no more than 1 mile laterally or longitudinally and within 100 feet vertically from the flight leader is maintained by each wingman.\n# Nonstandard Formation. A formation operating under any of the following conditions:\n## When the flight leader has requested and air traffic control has approved other than standard formation dimensions.\n## When operating within an authorized Altitude reservation or under the provisions of a letter of agreement.\n## When the operations are conducted in airspace specifically designed for a special activity.\n\n=== Formation Responsibilities ===\nThe flight lead assigns responsibilities for each flight member. Dividing responsibilities ensures each pilot has a manageable number of tasks to perform. Flight member normal responsibilities are:\n\n* \'\'\'Number One:\'\'\' Primary planner and decision maker, primary navigation and radar lookout, visual lookout for mutual support of #2, and primary engaged fighter, if practical.\n* \'\'\'Number Two:\'\'\' Maintain formation position, visual lookout, mutual support of number one. Navigation position awareness, and radar awareness as other responsibilities allow.\n* \'\'\'Number Three:\'\'\' Support number one. Secondary planner and alternate decision maker, maintain support position for lead element, secondary navigation and radar monitor, visual lookout for number four, mutual support of the entire flight, and secondary engaged fighter, if practical.\n* \'\'\'Number Four:\'\'\' Maintain formation position, visual lookout for the flight, mutual support of number three. Navigation position awareness, and radar awareness as other responsibilities allow.\n\n=== Time Sharing ===\nThe goal on every low level training mission should be to improve the development of this cross-check. Practice and discipline are essential to maximizing visual mutual support. On each mission, pilots must start with the basic NEAR ROCKS, FAR ROCKS, CHECK SIX pattern and build up the visual search arena as allowed by task saturation, threat and flight conditions. When encountering extremely rough terrain, defensive reactions, navigation turns, etc., drop the lowest priority sectors in order. There will be times, such as hard turns, when only NEAR ROCKS can be cross-checked. The key is to quickly re-establish the cross-check one sector at a time as tasks permit.\n\n[[File:16V5.DOC - 16v5.pdf — Mozilla Firefox 08.07.2026 04 34 19.png|frameless|737x737px]]\n\n== Formation Departure ==\nA formation departure consists of more than one aircraft at intervals of 1 minute or less which, by prior arrangement between the pilots, operate as a single aircraft with regard to navigation and position reporting. The departure portion of the flight may terminate at a preplanned breakup point which may be located up to, but not beyond, the planned initial level-off at cruise altitude.\n\n== Taxi Operations ==\n* Keep use of power to a minimum\n* Taxi with 20 – 25 knots at straight taxiway sections and turn with 10 – 15 knots\n* Avoid jet blast damage\n* Check nose wheel steering and brakes as you taxi out of the spot\n* Check the flight instrument in the turn onto the taxiway\n* Taxi only in authorized areas\n* Slow down and taxi on the centerline in congested areas\n* Do not taxi within 10 feet of any obstacle\n* Do not taxi within 25 feet without a wingwalker\n* If taxiing behind an aircraft, a minimum spacing of four ship lengths is necessary\n\n== Before Departure (procedure at LCC, EOR, BTI) ==\n\n=== Aircraft checks ===\n* Aircraft are checked, armed and dearmed on Arming / Dearming Pads (of LCC/EOR Pads)\n* Before departure, aircraft are checked and armed at the departure end EOR pad\n* After Arrival, aircraft are disarmed at the arrival end EOR pad\n\n\'\'\'LCC\'\'\' = Last Chance Check\n\n\'\'\'EOR\'\'\' = End of Runway\n\n\'\'\'BTI\'\'\' = Before Takeoff Inspection\n\n=== End of Runway (EOR) ===\n* After stopping EOR, confirm all weapons switches safe\n* Keep hands in full view during arming\n* Aircraft should not taxi in front of aircraft with forward firing ordnance being armed / dearmed\n* As soon as you stop in the arming area, check your GS (reads zero)\n* If GS is zero or one knot, the INS platform is good\n* If GS is two or more, accomplish another alignment if possible\n* Ensure Speed Brake Stowed\n* Review takeoff procedure and details as EP immediately after takeoff\n* Check other aircraft for leaks, loose panels, proper configuration, etc\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_21_05_47.png|alt=Example of EOR Lineup|border|frameless|691x691px]]\n\n== Departure Briefing ==\nIn the so-called “Departure Briefing”, which takes place on the EOR / LCC Pad prior to the lineup, the Flight Lead informs his Flight about all relevant information regarding departure. A Departure Briefing usually contains the following information:\n\n# Which type of Lineup\n# Type of Takeoff (Two Ship, Interval or Trail)\n# Speeds\n# Lost Wingmen Procedure\n# Clearence for visual join-up in case of interval and trail departure\n# Further / Additional information / structure\n\nExample of a Departure Briefing:\n\nWe´ll make a 3 ship echelon gear doors allignment for interval departure 5 seconds, (max Brake 140, min Go 150, Go 160 (are V speeds currently not used in SK), rotation speed 200, AB off at 300, contract speed 350, 5 nose up, in case of lost Wingmen turn right for 30 seconds using 30 AOB, inform Lead, transition to instruments, obtain separate ATC clearance and attempt re-join, say when airborne, you´re cleared to join on the right, say when in position.\n\n== Runway Lineup ==\nGeneral:\n\n* Leader will direct the appropriate runway line up based on winds and runway width\n* Wingmen should line up ensuring wingtip clearance\n\nLineup Types:\n\n* Two Ship Lineup\n* Three Ship Echelon\n* Four Ship Echelon\n* Four Ship three in The Slot\n* Four Ship four in The Slot\n* Three Ship three in The Slot\n\nLineup Standards:\n\n* Runway lineup is determined by taking into account several factors:\n** Wind direction and speed\n** Direction of turn out of traffic\n** Weather\n* Minimum wingtip spacing is wingtip clearance but may be wider as desired or required\n\n=== Two Ship Lineup ===\n* Lead takes the center of it´s half of the runway\n* The wingmen will line up lead´s main gear doors and take wingtip spacing as briefed\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_19_39_19.png|alt=Example Two Ship Lineup|frameless|692x692px]]\n\n=== Three ship echelon ===\n* Three ship formations may line up in echelon, if the runway is wide enough\n* “2” and “3” will line up gear doors and take wingtip spacing as briefed\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_19_46_25.png|frameless|753x753px]]\n\n=== Four Ship Echelon ===\n* Four ship may line up in echelon, if the runway is wide enough\n* “2” will line up gear doors and take wingtip spacing as briefed\n* “3” and “4” should align helmets of preceding flight members\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_19_51_04.png|frameless|628x628px]]\n\n=== Four Ship Three in The Slot ===\n* Lead should line up as far to the side of the runway as practical\n* “2” will place the wingtip closest to lead on the centerline an line up gear doors\n* “3” will line up between lead and “2”, maintaining nose-tail clearance out to 500 ft\n* “4” will line up offset from “2”s jet blast while aligning gear doors of “3”\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_19_59_31.png|frameless|713x713px]]\n\n=== Four Ship Four in The Slot ===\n* Lead should line up as far to the side of the runway as practical\n* “2” will place the wingtip closest to lead on the center line and line up gear doors\n* “4” will pull in between lead and “2” while aligning gear doors\n* “3” will line up with wingtip clearance on “2” while aligning gear doors\n* “4” will pull in further to align tail fin or gear doors of “3”\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_20_06_54.png|frameless|651x651px]]\n\n=== Three Ship Three in The Slot ===\n* Lead should line up as far to the side of the runway as practical\n* “2” will place the wingtip closest to lead on the center line and line up gear doors\n* “3” will line up between lead and “2”, maintaining nose-tail clearance out to 500 ft\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_20_10_03.png|frameless|696x696px]]\n\n== Takeoff ==\n\n=== Takeoff Types: ===\n* Two Ship Takeoff\n** Formation Takeoff\n** Usable for two aircraft only\n** Not usable during periods of high crosswind, low visibility, wet runway\n* Interval Departure\n** Used whenever a two ship takeoff is not possible\n** Separation assured by timing\n** Rejoin required after departure\n* (Radar assisted) Trail Departure\n** Used whenever a two ship takeoff is not possible\n** A type of interval departure\n** Separation assured by on board radar\n\n=== Two Ship Takeoff ===\n* Signal for brake release is a head nod or radio call\n* Leader will make a normal takeoff with throttle slightly retarded (power advantage for Wingman)\n* Leader will pass a gear up and AB out signal, if appropriate\n* Wingman will maintain wingtip clearance throughout takeoff roll\n* If overrunning the leader, wingman will be directed to assume the lead while continuing takeoff\n* If either member or the element must abort, the other member should continue the takeoff\n* In either case, stay on your side of the runway!\n\n=== Trail Departure ===\n* Used when conditions won´t permit a formation takeoff\n* Examples: Wet runways, crosswind limits, weapon loads, configuration differences, visibility\n* Set up radar for easy acquisition\n** Select RWS Mode\n* Priority is to fly the aircraft, not operating the radar\n* Ignore the radar until all high priority tasks are completed\n* During climbout, establish and maintain briefed power setting and airspeed\n* Unless restricted by procedure or ATC, begin all climbs at the same time as the leader\n* Lead should call passing every 5000 ft increment until all flight members are tied/visual\n* Rule of thumb: For 30 AOB turns, allow preceding aircraft to drift 5 deg laterally for each mile of separation between aircraft, Example: to maintain a 2 NM trail position, allow the preceding aircraft to drift 10 deg before initiating your turn\n* Once a tied or visual call has been made, advise the leader if either radar or visual contact is subsequently lost.\n\n=== Lost Wingman ===\nGeneral:\n\n* Transition to instruments\n* Notify Lead (“Lost Wingman”)\n* Execute lost wingman procedures\n* Obtain separate ATC clearance\n* Attempt rejoin\n* Flight lead: Acknowledge lost wingman, transmit altitude, speed, post, etc.\n\nWings level after departure:\n\n* Turn away using 15 deg angle of bank (AOB) for 15 seconds, ensure operation and obtain separate clearance\n\nWingman outside of the turn after departure:\n\n* Reverse the direction of the turn using 15 deg AOB for 15 seconds, ensure separation and obtain separate clearance\n\nWingman inside of the turn after departure:\n\n* Momentarily reduce power to ensure separation and inform lead to roll out, maintain bank to ensure lateral separation and obtain separate clearance\n\nFinal approach:\n\n* Momentarily turn away to ensure separation, commence the published missed approach procedure and obtain separate clearance\n\nMissed approach:\n\n* Momentarily turn away to ensure separation, continue the missed approach procedure while climbing 500 ft above missed approach altitude and get a separate clearance\n\nLocal procedures:\n\n* Sometimes local conditions require different lost wingman procedures\n\n== Rejoin ==\nPurpose:\n\n* The purpose of a rejoin is to get the flight back together safely and efficiently\n* Necessary after an interval / trail departure or lost wingman procedure\n\nBasics:\n\n* Lead should monitor wingmen during all rejoins\n* Airspeeds and bank angles are prebriefed or unit standard\n* Lead should make a radio call if flying different speed or bank angle\n\nRejoin Techniques:\n\n* A radar lock on may be used during the rejoin to provide range and overtake info\n* Maintaining 50 – 100 Knots of true overtake will provide a controlled, expeditious rejoin\n* “3” and “4” should be locked to the leader\n* At 3000 ft reduce power smoothly to control overtake\n* At 1500 ft, true overtake should be 50 knots\n* Use speed brakes if overtake is excessive\n* If overtake is excessive approaching the extended fingertip position, initiate a controlled overshoot\n\n== Crossunder ==\nGeneral:\n\n* Used to move wingman to the other side\n\nTwo Ship Crossunder:\n\n* On lead´s signal wingman will reduce power\n* The wingman will move back and down below lead´s Plane of Motion (POM) and add power again to avoid falling further behind\n* Next, wingman moves across and behind lead with a minimum of nose-tail clearance\n* Add power as required to avoid falling further behind\n* Once on the opposite side and with wingtip clearance, wingman will add power to move up and forward into position\n\nFour Ship Fingertip Crossunder:\n\n* If “2” is crossing to the side that “3” and “4” are on, “3” should move out to create room for “2”\n* “2” begins a normal crossunder (ensuring adequate spacing before crossing lead´s six o´clock)\n* If “3” and “4” are crossing simultaneously, “3” performs a normal crossunder on “2” and “1”\n* As “3” begins the crossunder, “4” performs a crossunder on “3”\n\nFour Ship Echelon Crossunder:\n\n* As “2” begins the crossunder, “3” performs a crossunder on “2”, “4” performs a crossunder on “3”\n\nVisual Signals:\n\n* Crossunder may be initiated using a wing dip to the side the wingman is requested to move or after instruction on radio\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 10.10.2025 13 59 01.png|frameless|663x663px]]\n\n== Lead Changes ==\n* Lead changes require an unmistakable transfer of responsibilities from one flight member to another\n* The flight member assuming lead will be no further aft than normal route/fingertip\n* Lead changes only change “Nav Lead” responsibilities\n* Flight lead responsibilities cannot be transferred\n\n== Formation Turns ==\n\n=== Fingertip Turns ===\n* Wingman will maintain fingertip position using fingertip spacing reference\n* Always stay slightly below preceding aircraft\n* Fingertip turns are limited to approximately 30 deg AOB\n* Lead will avoid sudden or abrupt changes in AOB or back stick pressure\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 10.10.2025 14 25 57.png|frameless|663x663px]]\n\n=== Echelon Turn Into the Echelon ===\n* Turns into the echelon will be avoided if at all possible\n* Wingman will maintain fingertip position using fingertip spacing references\n* Turns are limit to very shallow turns\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_10.10.2025_14_34_38.png|frameless|656x656px]]\n\n=== Echelon Turns Away From the Echelon ===\n* Plan to limit AOB to 60 degrees maximum\n* All aircraft will match lead´s roll rates while maintaining the same horizontal plane\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 10.10.2025 14 38 58.png|frameless|656x656px]]\n\n=== Route Turns ===\n* When turned away from the wingman, will stack level with the leader\n* When turned into, the wingman will only stack down enough to keep lead in sight and avoid his maneuvering plane\n\n=== Route Echelon Turns ===\n* If a turn is made into the echelon, each aircraft will only stack down enough to keep rest of the formation in sight and avoid their maneuvering plane\n* On turns away from the echelon, all aircrafts will maintain the same horizontal plane\n\n== Channel Changes ==\n* When a channel / frequency change is directed, wingman will normally move out from fingertip to route or from echelon to route echelon\n\n== Leaving Formation ==\nLeave Formation:\n\n* When directed to do so\n* When you lose sight of the aircraft ahead (lost wingman, blind)\n* When you are unable to join up or to stay in formation without crossing over, under or in front of the aircraft ahead\n* At any other time you feel that your presence in the formation constitutes a hazard\n* When leaving a formation, clear yourself in the direction of your turn and notify leader\n* If you have lost sight, comply with the appropriate lost wingman procedure\n* Rejoin only by directed to do so by the flight lead\n\n== Formation Landing ==\nLead:\n\n* Approach speed should be consistent with heavier aircraft\n* Wingmen should be positioned upwind if crosswind component exceeds 5 knots\n* Plan to land near the center of your half of the runway\n* Do not touchdown long and fast\n\nWingman:\n\n* Maintain a minimum of 10 feet lateral wingtip spacing\n* Stack level with lead as briefed when configured on final approach\n* Cross check the runway to ensure sufficient runway is available\n\n== Overhead Break ==\n* Used to split a formation in an orderly and expeditious manner to prepare for landing\n* Also called “360 overhead”\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 10.10.2025 15 05 58.png|frameless|936x936px]]\n\n== Standard Formations ==\n\n=== Fingertip Formations ===\nUsage:\n\n* Weather penetration\n* Airfield arrivals / departures\n* Show formations\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Lead aircraft is easy to see in bad weather\n* Close formation is used for formation takeoff / landing\n* Preferred before overhead break (360 overhead)\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* Does not allow for much head down time\n* Limited maneuvering\n* Shallow turns (up to 30 deg AOB) only\n* Both aircraft are easily detected as one group by an adversary\n\nParameters:\n\n* Use fingertip reference points\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 10.10.2025 15 20 01.png|frameless|330x330px]]\n\n=== Finger four (Four Ship Fingertip) ===\nUsage:\n\n* Weather penetration\n* Show formations\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Lead aircraft is easy to see in bad weather\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* Does not allow for much heads down time\n* Limited maneuvering\n* Shallow turns (up to 30 deg AOB) only\n* All aircraft are easily detected as a group by an adversary\n\nParameters:\n\n* Same as fingertip\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 13 45 26.png|frameless|463x463px]]\n\n== Route Formations ==\nUsage:\n\n* Enroute\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Lead aircraft is easy to see in moderate weather\n* Easy to fly, allowing for a good amount of heads down time\n* Steeper turns up to 60 deg AOB possible\n* Easier to fly in a low altitude environment\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* All aircraft are easily detected as one group by an adversary\n* Limited engagement flexibility\n\nParameters:\n\n* Modified fingertip\n* Up to 500 feet between aircraft\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 13 54 38.png|frameless|583x583px]]\n\n=== Route Echelon ===\nUsage:\n\n* Enroute\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Lead aircraft is easy to see in moderate weather\n* Easy to fly, allowing for a good amount of heads down time\n* Steeper turns up to 60 deg AOB possible\n* Easier to fly in a low altitude environment\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* All aircraft are easily detected as one group by an adversary\n* Limited engagement flexibility\n* Turns into the echelon have to be shallow (max. 30 deg AOB)\n\nParameters:\n\n* Modified echelon\n* Up to 500 feet between aircraft\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 13 59 02.png|frameless|513x513px]]\n\n== Trail Formations ==\n\n=== Close Trail Formation ===\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical, Whenever necessary (e.g. Very low level)\n\nParameters:\n\n* One to two ship lengths behind lead aircraft\n* Below lead aircraft´s jetwash\n\n=== Trail Formations ===\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical, Whenever necessary (e.g. Very low level)\n\nParameters:\n\n* Flown in a cone 30 deg – 60 deg aft of lead\n* Range briefed by flight lead\n* Avoid flying at lead´s high six\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 14 06 03.png|frameless|503x503px]]\n\n== Non-Standard Formation ==\n\n=== Fighting Wings ===\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Helps to maintain flight integrity in bad weather or rough terrain\n* Allows for heads down time\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* Poor 6 o´clock coverage\n* Easily detectable as bandit\n\nParameters:\n\n* 30 – 60 deg aft of 3/9 line\n* 30 – 60 deg above or below\n* 500 – 3000 ft spacing\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 14 11 11.png|frameless|666x666px]]\n\n=== Wedge ===\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Leader protected on 6 o´clock position\n* Leader free to maneuvering\n* Wingman may always switch sides\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* No 6 o´clock protection for wingman\n* Lead changes difficult\n\nParameters:\n\n* 30 – 60 deg aft of 3/9 line\n* 4000 – 6000 ft spacing\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 14 18 22.png|frameless|417x417px]]\n\n=== Fluid Four ===\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Wingman kept close\n* Good maneuverability\n* Concentration of force\n* Easily converts to three ship if required\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* Adversary can acquire all four aircraft\n* Defensive maneuvering confusing\n* Difficult in low altitude rough terrain\n\nParameters:\n\n* Two times fighting wing\n* 6000 – 9000 ft separated\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 14 58 09.png|frameless|620x620px]]\n\n=== Four Ship Wedge ===\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Very offensive for threats forward of 3/9 L\n* Easy to fly\n* Formation in bad weather / rough terrain\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* Poor 6 o´clock lookout\n* Formation easily detected by bandit\n* Limited defensive maneuvering flexibility\n\nParameters:\n\n* Two times wedge\n* 1,5 – 3NM separated\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 15 04 22.png|frameless|419x419px]]\n\n=== Spread Four ===\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Difficult to acquire entire flight visually\n* Maximized fire power for BVR\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* Maneuvering difficult\n* Very difficult to fly at low altitude\n\nParameters:\n\n* “1” and “2” 6000 – 9000 ft apart\n* “2” positioned 0 – 30 deg behind 3/9 of “1”\n* “1” and “3” 6000 – 9000 ft spread\n* “3” and “4” 6000 – 9000 ft apart\n* “4” positioned 0 – 30 deg behind 3/9 of “3”\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 15 13 03.png|frameless|804x804px]]\n\n=== Box Formation ===\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Difficult to detect simultaneously by bandit\n* Easy support\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* Difficult to maintain formation integrity\n\nParameters:\n\n* Two times spread\n* 1,5 – 3 NM separated\n* Variation: Four Ship Offset Box\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 15 18 55.png|frameless|397x397px]]\n\n=== Arrowhead Formation ===\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Same as box\n* Easier to fly as “2” allowing for more look out\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* Same as box\n\nParameters:\n\n* Aircraft “3” and “4” fly spread 1,5 – 3 NM behind “1!”\n* Aircraft “2” 30 – 60 deg behind leader´s 3/9 line and 3000 ft spacing\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 16 14 37.png|frameless|589x589px]]\n\n=== Three Ship Vic ===\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical\n\nParameters:\n\n* Leader 1,5 – 3 NM in front of “2” and “3”\n* “2” and “3” 6000 – 9000 ft apart\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 16 18 26.png|frameless|419x419px]]\n\n== Line Abreast (Spread) ==\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical\n* Enroute\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Minimizes the chances of simultaneous detection by bandit\n* Ordnance can be released quickly\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* Hard to fly for inexperienced wingman\n\nParameters:\n\n* 0 – 20 deg aft\n* 4000 – 12000 ft spacing\n* Between 30 deg above and below\n* Optimum 0 deg and 6000 – 9000 ft\n* Or as briefed\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 14 25 43.png|frameless|616x616px]]\n\n== Line Abreast Turns ==\n\n=== Tactical TURNS ===\nExecution TAC Turn:\n\nA TAC Turn is a delayed ninety 90 turn either to right or left. Depending on the direction of turn, the aircraft outside the turn turns first. The turn of the inner / second aircraft turning is delayed by a visual reference. Once the nose of the first turning aircraft is coming around to point on the second, the second aircraft starts its turn.\n\nThe TAC Turn is flown in full military Power with sustained altitude and speed.\n\nExecution Hook Turn:\n\nA Hook turn is a simultaneous 180 degree turn either to the left or right. It is flown in full military Power with sustained altitude and speed.\n\n[[File:16V5.DOC - 16v5.pdf — Mozilla Firefox 08.07.2026 04 40 52.png|frameless|783x783px]]\n\n=== Delayed 45 Degree Turns, Crossturns and Check Turns ===\nExecution Delayed 45 Degree Turn:\n\nA Delayed 45 Degree turn is self explained by its name. It is flown in full military power with sustained speed and altitude.\n\nDepending on the direction of turn, the inner aircraft starts its turn when crossing the outer aircrafts flightpath.\n\nExecution Crossturn:\n\nIs a Hook turn with both aircrafts turning into each other. The same parameters as for a normal Hook turn are required.\n\nExecution Check Turn:\n\nCheck Turns are Turns for Heading adjustments that are smaller than 45 Degrees. They are flown with a desired power setting but with sustained altitude. A slight formation offset is required. The lead will adjust the heading by gently turning into the chosen direction.\n\n[[File:16V5.DOC - 16v5.pdf — Mozilla Firefox 08.07.2026 04 42 04.png|frameless|791x791px]]\n\n----\n\nTo whoever manages to reach this point, my congratulations. Screenshot this and send it into \"general\" ;)\n\n\'\'Iceman\'\'','utf-8'),
(600,'__NOTOC__\n__NOEDITSECTION__\n\n<!-- HERO -->\n<div style=\"border:1px solid #c8c5bf; padding:48px 32px 36px 32px; margin-bottom:32px; text-align:center;\">\n<div style=\"font-size:0.6rem; letter-spacing:5px; color:#9a9690; margin-bottom:20px;\">&#9632;&#9632;&#9632; UNCLASSIFIED — FOR SOURCE MEMBERS ONLY &#9632;&#9632;&#9632;</div>\n<div style=\"border-top:1px solid #c8c5bf; max-width:200px; margin:0 auto 24px auto;\"></div>\n[[File:Sourcelogosmall.png|100px|center|link=]]\n<div style=\"font-size:1.6rem; font-weight:600; letter-spacing:8px; margin:20px 0 8px 0;\">SOURCE DCS</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:0.72rem; letter-spacing:4px; color:#4a4845; margin-bottom:20px;\">SHADOW OPERATIONS - UNDERSTANDING REAL COMBAT ENVIRONMENTS</div>\n<div style=\"border-top:1px solid #c8c5bf; max-width:200px; margin:0 auto 16px auto;\"></div>\n<div style=\"font-size:0.62rem; letter-spacing:2px; color:#9a9690;\">\nCURRENT THEATER: SYRIA - \n[[Server Info/Airfields|AIRFIELDS]] - [[Theater/DTC-INFO|DTCINFO]]\n</div>\n</div>\n\n----\n\n== 01 / Personnel Onboarding ==\n\nNew to SOURCE? Complete all onboarding steps before your first operation. Install required software, read the rules of engagement, and familiarize yourself with communications procedures.\n\n[[Guides/New Member Guide|New Member Guide]] · [[Guides/SRS Setup|SRS Setup]] · [[Guides/Mod Installation|Mod Installation]]\n\n== 02 / General Knowledge ==\nContains all the Knowledge about the threats we could Face along with tactics to evade an neutralize.\n\n[[SAM Surface to Air Missile]] · [[How To Read Charts]] · [[GENERAL/Airspace Classifications|Airspace Classes]] \n\n----\n\n== 03 / multi-Service tactics, techniques, and procedures (MTTP) ==\n\nAll personnel are expected to operate within SOURCE standard procedures.\n\n=== Airspace COMMAND and CONTROL ===\n\'\'\'IN CASE OF DOUBT USE PLAIN LANGUAGE\'\'\'\n\n[[General/Tactical Communication Revision 1|Tactical Communication]] · [[General/Air Traffic Communication|Air Traffic Communication]] · [[DDFORM1801|Filing Flight Plans]]  · [[MTTP/ACC/Training Range Communications|Training Range Communication]] \n\n=== CLOSE AIR SUPPORT: ===\nTO BE ADDED\n\n=== Formation Flying ===\n[[Formation Flying]]\n\nFor Unit-Specific Procedures reference SQUADRON STANDARD OPERATING PROCEDURES below\n\n----\n\n== 04 / Squadron STANDARD OPERATING PROCEDURES ==\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! Squadron !! Aircraft\n|-\n| [[Squadrons/78th VMRS Shadow Knights|78th VMRS Shadow Knights]] || F-16C Viper\n|-\n| [[14th VSFS Top Hatters]]|| F/A-18C Hornet\n|-\n| [[Squadrons/509th VSFS Black Anvils|509th VSFS Black Anvils]] || F-15E Strike Eagle\n|-\n|[[41st VSFS Black Aces]]\n|F-14B(U) Tomcat\n|}\n\n----\n\n== 05 / Active Operations ==\n\nCurrent campaign briefings, mission assignments, and sortie records are maintained in the operations section. All flight leads are responsible for keeping mission pages up to date following each operation.\n\n[[Campaigns|View All Campaigns]]\n\n== 06 / IRL RESOURCE DATABASE ==\nComming Soon(TM)','utf-8');
INSERT INTO `text` VALUES
(601,'\n== Introduction ==\nThe F-14 is not a single-seat fighter with a passenger in the back. It is a two-crew combat aircraft, and its effectiveness depends on disciplined cooperation between the Pilot and the Radar Intercept Officer, or RIO.\n\nIn the F-14B(U), the workload is split by design. The Pilot flies, fights the aircraft, manages formation, maintains visual lookout, controls energy, and executes weapons employment. The RIO manages the tactical picture, sensors, datalink, navigation support, radios, and crew coordination. When both crew members try to do everything, communication becomes messy and important information is missed. When duties are divided clearly, the aircraft becomes far more effective.\n\nThis SOP defines how Pilot–RIO communication should work inside the squadron. Its purpose is not to create unnecessary radio discipline for its own sake. Its purpose is to make sure that the Pilot always has the information needed to fly and fight the aircraft, while the RIO stays ahead of the situation and turns raw information into useful decisions.\n\nThe golden principle is simple: the Pilot should not need to ask basic tactical questions. The RIO should already be answering them.\n\n== General Crew Philosophy ==\nWhenever possible, the RIO owns external communication. This includes communication with AWACS, ATC, carrier marshal, approach, tower, tankers, other flights, JTAC/FAC, GCI controllers, and package-level frequencies.\n\nThe Pilot should normally only transmit externally when required for aircraft safety, weapons employment, immediate tactical need, or when the RIO is task-saturated or unavailable.\n\nThe Pilot’s primary focus is flying the aircraft. This includes aircraft control, formation keeping, visual lookout, fuel awareness, defensive manoeuvring, and weapons employment.\n\nThe RIO’s primary focus is managing information. The RIO acts as the aircraft’s tactical coordinator, radar operator, datalink manager, navigation assistant, communications manager, and second set of eyes.\n\nThe RIO should not only report what is happening. The RIO should help decide what should happen next.\n\n== Internal Communication Principles ==\nCrew communication must be short, clear, and useful. The goal is not to talk constantly. The goal is to pass the right information early enough for it to matter.\n\nThe RIO should keep the Pilot informed, speak early rather than late, prioritise threats, and \'\'\'avoid unnecessary chatter\'\'\'. The RIO should give recommendations instead of only giving raw data.\n\nThe Pilot should acknowledge important calls, state intentions clearly, confirm tactical decisions, and immediately report visual contacts, missiles, friendlies, aircraft problems, or loss of sight.\n\nBoth crew members should avoid vague language during tactical situations. Calls such as “over there”, “I think something is close”, or “maybe turn” are not good enough. Use direction, range, altitude, aspect, and clear recommendations whenever possible.\n\n== Standard Tactical Picture Updates ==\nThe RIO is responsible for keeping the Pilot updated whenever the tactical picture changes. A picture update should be given whenever a new group appears, a group changes heading, a group turns hot, a group splits, a group merges, a group enters threat range, an enemy locks the flight, the friendly picture changes, or the picture becomes unclear.\n\nA good picture update should tell the Pilot what exists, where it is, what it is doing, and what matters most.\n\nExample:\n\nRIO: “Picture update. Two groups. Group one BRAA 340 for 55, angels 28, hot. Group two BRAA 015 for 70, angels 22, flanking east. Group one remains primary threat.”\n\nPilot: “Copy.”\n\nThe RIO should avoid flooding the Pilot with every radar contact. The important part is threat priority. The Pilot needs to know what matters first.\n\n== BRAA CallS ==\nBRAA calls are the standard way to describe an air contact relative to the aircraft or fighter group. BRAA means bearing, range, altitude, and aspect.\n\nBRAA calls should be made when a new contact is detected, when a target is designated, when a target turns hot, when range changes significantly, when the merge is approaching, or when visual acquisition is expected.\n\nStandard format:\n\n“Bandit BRAA [bearing], [range], [altitude], [aspect].”\n\nExample:\n\nRIO: “Bandit BRAA 345, 42 miles, angels 24, hot.”\n\nPilot: “Copy.”\n\nThe RIO should use BRAA calls to build the Pilot’s mental picture. Do not assume the Pilot sees what the RIO sees on the scope.\n\n== Priority Threat Calls ==\nSome calls require immediate attention and should interrupt normal communication.\n\n=== Spike ===\nA spike means the aircraft is being tracked or targeted by enemy radar.\n\nExamples:\n\nRIO: “Spike twelve o’clock.”\n\nRIO: “Spike SA-10, three o’clock.”\n\nPilot: “Copy.”\n\n=== Launch ===\nA launch call means a missile launch has been detected or is strongly suspected.\n\nExamples:\n\nRIO: “Launch! Twelve o’clock!”\n\nRIO: “Fox One inbound, Break Right!\"\n\nPilot: “Defending.”\n\n=== Active Missile ===\nAn active missile call means an active radar-guided missile is a direct threat.\n\nExample:\n\nRIO: “Missile Active, 6 o clock, drop low!”\n\nPilot: “Defending.”\n\n=== Merge ===\nA merge call is used when hostile aircraft are entering visual merge range.\n\nExample:\n\nRIO: “Merge”\n\nPilot: “Visual.”\n\nor:\n\nPilot: “Blind.”\n\nThreat calls must be direct. This is not the time for long explanations.\n\n== Intercept Timeline Communication ==\nThe RIO should adjust communication based on range and timeline. Long-range intercepts need picture management. Short-range intercepts need fast, precise, tactical calls.\n\n=== Long Range: More than 40 nautical miles ===\nAt long range, the RIO should manage the tactical picture, datalink, AWACS communication, target sorting, and intercept recommendations.\n\nExample:\n\nRIO: “Primary group BRAA 330 for 58, angels 30, hot. Recommend continue heading.”\n\n=== Medium Range: 40 to 20 nautical miles ===\nAt medium range, the RIO should increase update frequency. The RIO should monitor closure, aspect, altitude, and weapon opportunities.\n\nExample:\n\nRIO: “Bandit 330 for 28, angels 29, hot. Closure high. Recommend Phoenix.”\n\n=== Commit Range: 20 to 10 nautical miles ===\nAt commit range, the RIO should provide frequent updates on target aspect, range, altitude, and immediate threat level. The Pilot needs simple and useful information.\n\nExample:\n\nRIO: “Bandit 335 for 15, angels 28, hot. Fast closure.”\n\n=== Merge Range: Less than 10 nautical miles ===\nInside 10 nautical miles, the RIO should help with visual acquisition, clock references, second bandit calls, and defensive awareness. So \'\'\'look out the Window\'\'\'!!!\n\nExamples:\n\nRIO: “Bandit left 10 o’clock low.”\n\nRIO: “Second bandit right 2 o’clock high.”\n\nAt this range, long radar explanations are usually useless. The Pilot needs direction, position, and threat priority.\n\n== RIO Recommendations ==\nThe RIO should provide recommendations, not only information. Raw data forces the Pilot to interpret everything alone. A recommendation helps the crew act faster.\n\nExamples:\n\nNavigation: “Recommend right turn twenty degrees.”\n\nIntercept: “Recommend commit.”\n\nDefence: “Recommend crank right.”\n\nFuel: “Recommend tanker after engagement.”\n\nWeapons: “Recommend Sparrow.”\n\nA recommendation is not a command. The Pilot still flies the aircraft and makes the final aircraft-control decision. But the RIO should always try to stay one step ahead.\n\n== Standard Pilot Responses ==\nPilot responses should be short. The purpose is to confirm that the information was received and understood.\n\nStandard responses:\n\n“Copy” means the information was received.\n\n“Roger” means the information was understood.\n\n“Executing” means the Pilot is following the recommendation.\n\n“Defending” means the Pilot is performing defensive manoeuvring.\n\n“Visual” means the Pilot sees the referenced aircraft or object.\n\n“Blind” means the Pilot does not see the referenced friendly aircraft.\n\n“Tally” means the Pilot sees the bandit or hostile aircraft.\n\n“No joy” means the Pilot does not see the bandit or hostile aircraft.\n\n“Locked” means the Pilot or aircraft has a valid lock.\n\n“Fox One”, “Fox Two”, or “Fox Three” means a missile has been fired.\n\nThe Pilot should not give long replies during high workload unless clarification is needed.\n\n== Visual Communication ==\nThe Pilot must immediately report important visual information. This includes visual bandits, visual missiles, visual friendlies, loss of sight, aircraft damage, engine issues, smoke, fire, or anything that affects survival.\n\nExamples:\n\nPilot: “Visual bandit, twelve o’clock.”\n\nPilot: “Tally one.”\n\nPilot: “No joy.”\n\nPilot: “Lost visual.”\n\nPilot: “Missile launch, eleven o’clock low.”\n\nThe RIO should support the Pilot with clock-code references when the fight becomes visual. Once visual, the RIO’s role shifts from radar picture management to helping the Pilot survive and maintain awareness.\n\n== Defensive Communication ==\nDuring defensive situations, communication must be short and exact.\n\nThe RIO should state the threat type, direction, priority, and recommendation.\n\nExample:\n\nRIO: “Missile six o’clock low. Recommend break left.”\n\nPilot: “Defending left.”\n\nIf there are multiple threats, the RIO must prioritise the most immediate threat first. Do not describe every contact while a missile is about to hit the aircraft.\n\nBad call:\n\n“Possible missile somewhere behind us, maybe low, also there is another group north.”\n\nGood call:\n\n“Missile six o’clock low. Break left now.”\n\n== Multi-Group Sorting ==\nWhen multiple groups are present, the RIO should help sort targets clearly. The Pilot should not be forced to guess which group is primary.\n\nExamples:\n\nRIO: “Primary group north. Secondary group south.”\n\nRIO: “Lead target west group. Trail target east group.”\n\nRIO: “We take the hot group. Friendly flight has the flanking group.”\n\nSorting must be simple. Avoid changing target assignments without saying so clearly.\n\n== Fuel State Updates ==\nThe RIO should help monitor fuel state, especially during complex intercepts, carrier operations, tanker operations, and extended engagements.\n\nThe RIO should call approaching Joker, Joker, Bingo, and recovery fuel state when relevant.\n\nExamples:\n\nRIO: “Approaching Joker.”\n\nRIO: “Joker fuel.”\n\nRIO: “Bingo fuel.”\n\nRIO: “Recommend tanker after engagement.”\n\nFuel calls must be early. A fuel problem discovered late is usually a crew coordination failure.\n\n== Carrier Operations ==\nDuring carrier operations, the RIO normally handles communication with Marshal, Approach, and Tower. This allows the Pilot to focus on aircraft control, descent, lineup, on-speed condition, and landing.\n\nThe RIO should support the Pilot with short internal calls during recovery.\n\nExamples:\n\nRIO: “On-speed.”\n\nRIO: “Lineup good.”\n\nRIO: “High.”\n\nRIO: “Low.”\n\nRIO: “Start your turn.”\n\nThe RIO should avoid distracting the Pilot during the final phase unless the call improves safety or landing performance.\n\n== Critical Information Checklist ==\nThe RIO should always keep the Pilot updated on the following areas.\n\n=== Tactical ===\nThe Pilot needs to know the picture, BRAA, target aspect, altitude, closure, threat priority, supporting friendlies, missile launches, and radar locks.\n\n=== Navigation ===\nThe Pilot needs waypoint information, steering corrections, distance to target, recovery routing, marshal instructions, and tanker routing when relevant.\n\n=== Aircraft and Systems ===\nThe Pilot should be informed about fuel state, weapon inventory, radar status, datalink status, system failures, and any limitation affecting the fight.\n\n=== Air Combat Decisions ===\nThe RIO should support decisions such as commit, crank, notch, defend, recommit, merge, extend, disengage, and recover.\n\n== Golden Rule ==\nThe Pilot should never have to ask:\n\n“Where is the threat?”\n\n“What is the picture?”\n\n“Who is the primary target?”\n\n“How much fuel do we have?”\n\n“What should we do next?”\n\nThe RIO’s job is to answer these questions before they are asked.\n\nA strong F-14 crew is not defined by how much they talk. It is defined by how early, clearly, and usefully they communicate. The Pilot flies the aircraft. The RIO manages the fight. Together, they make the Tomcat dangerous.','utf-8'),
(602,'{{DISPLAYTITLE:Squadrons/41st Black Aces}} {{DISPLAYTITLE:41st VSFS Black Aces}} {{DISPLAYTITLE:41st VSFS Black Aces}}\n\n= 41st VSFS special operating procedures =\n\n== Overview ==\n[[File:Screen_260330_155302_4.png|thumb|387x387px]]\nThe 41st Virtual Strike Fighter Squadron (VSFS) \"Black Aces\" is a carrier-based F-14B(U) strike fighter squadron deployed aboard the USS Harry S. Truman (CVN-75). The squadron takes its name, identity, and doctrine from VFA-41 Black Aces: A distinguished US Navy strike fighter squadron established in 1950 and renowned for its combat record, operational excellence, and carrier-based fighter tradition.\n\n41st VSFS strives to honour that legacy by embracing the professionalism, discipline, and combat effectiveness of the real Black Aces, integrating authentic US Navy carrier procedures, tactical doctrine, and mission planning into every virtual operation.\n\n== Carrier Operations and Procedures ==\n[[File:Screen_260405_152707.png|left|thumb|429x429px]]\nThe USS Harry S. Truman is the beating heart of our operations. All mission-ready pilots in our squadron are expected to be comfortable operating to, on, and from the carrier. Below you will find useful guides and resources to conduct safe and efficient carrier operations.\n\n[[41st VSFS/Case I Procedures|Case I Procedures (work in progress)]]\n\n[[41st VSFS/Case III Procedures|Case III Procedures (work in progress)]]\n\n[[General/CATC Communications Guide|Carrier Communications and Phraseology (CATCC)]]\n\n[[Landing Signal Officer (LSO) Guide]]\n\n== Tactical Procedures (WIP) ==\n[[File:Screen_260330_155406.png|thumb|510x510px]]\n\'\'\'<u>Tactical Procedures will cover how we fight together.</u>\'\'\'\n\nAir to Ground Tactics\n\n* [[14th VSFS/Close Air Support (CAS)|Close Air Support (CAS)]]\n* [[Strike/Air Interdiction]]\n* [[SEAD/DEAD]]\n* [[Anti-Ship]]\n\nAir to Air Tactics\n\n* [[Tactical Turns]]\n* [[Beyond Visual Range (BVR)]]\n* [[Basic Fighter Maneuvers (BFM)]]\n* [[Combat Air Patrol (CAP)]]\n\n== General Flight Procedures (WIP) ==\n[[File:Screen_260327_220735.png|left|thumb|464x464px]]\n\'\'\'<u>General Flight Procedures will cover how we fly together.</u>\'\'\'\n\n[[Airfield operations (Shore-based)]]\n\n[[Formation Flying]]\n\n[[14th VSFS/Air to Air Refueling|Air to Air Refueling]]\n\n[[Advanced Fuel Management]]\n\n[[Night Flying]]\n\n[[Pilot & RIO Communications]]\n\n== Resources and Files ==\nTIP: CTRL+CLICK on any link to open it in a new tab instead of replacing this one\n\n[Link to livery, when livery]\n\n[https://polybox.ethz.ch/index.php/s/S8Es6KCzBao5WYm/download?path=%2FKneeboards&files=FA-18C_hornet.rar <u>14th VSFS Kneeboard (Checklists + Quick Reference Guides + LTAG Aerodrome and Tacan Approach Charts)</u>]\n\n[https://info.publicintelligence.net/F14AAP-1B.pdf F-14B Emergency Procedures]\n\n[https://chucksguides.com/aircraft/dcs/f-14b/#&#x5B;1,%22XYZ%22,0,540,1&#x5D; Chucks Guides - F-14B] \n\n[https://server.3rd-wing.net/public/Bureau_VF31/Docs%20F-14%20r%C3%A9el/NAVAIR%2001-F14AAP-1%20-%20NATOPS%20Flight%20Manual%20-%20F-14B.pdf NATOPS FLIGHT MANUAL 01-F14AAP-1] (Aug, 2001)\n\n[https://f14.manuals.heatblur.se/ Heatblur F-14 Manual] (June, 2026)','utf-8'),
