(604,'== Formation ==\nA formation is defined as more than one aircraft that, by prior arrangement between the pilots, operates as a single aircraft with regard to navigation and position reporting. Separation between aircraft within the formation is the responsibility of the flight leader and the pilots of the other aircraft in the flight. This includes transition periods when aircraft within the formation are maneuvering to attain separation from each other to effect individual control and during join up or breakaway.\n\n# Standard Formation. A formation in which a proximity of no more than 1 mile laterally or longitudinally and within 100 feet vertically from the flight leader is maintained by each wingman.\n# Nonstandard Formation. A formation operating under any of the following conditions:\n## When the flight leader has requested and air traffic control has approved other than standard formation dimensions.\n## When operating within an authorized Altitude reservation or under the provisions of a letter of agreement.\n## When the operations are conducted in airspace specifically designed for a special activity.\n\n=== Formation Responsibilities ===\nThe flight lead assigns responsibilities for each flight member. Dividing responsibilities ensures each pilot has a manageable number of tasks to perform. Flight member normal responsibilities are:\n\n* \'\'\'Number One:\'\'\' Primary planner and decision maker, primary navigation and radar lookout, visual lookout for mutual support of #2, and primary engaged fighter, if practical.\n* \'\'\'Number Two:\'\'\' Maintain formation position, visual lookout, mutual support of number one. Navigation position awareness, and radar awareness as other responsibilities allow.\n* \'\'\'Number Three:\'\'\' Support number one. Secondary planner and alternate decision maker, maintain support position for lead element, secondary navigation and radar monitor, visual lookout for number four, mutual support of the entire flight, and secondary engaged fighter, if practical.\n* \'\'\'Number Four:\'\'\' Maintain formation position, visual lookout for the flight, mutual support of number three. Navigation position awareness, and radar awareness as other responsibilities allow.\n\n=== Time Sharing ===\nThe goal on every low level training mission should be to improve the development of this cross-check. Practice and discipline are essential to maximizing visual mutual support. On each mission, pilots must start with the basic NEAR ROCKS, FAR ROCKS, CHECK SIX pattern and build up the visual search arena as allowed by task saturation, threat and flight conditions. When encountering extremely rough terrain, defensive reactions, navigation turns, etc., drop the lowest priority sectors in order. There will be times, such as hard turns, when only NEAR ROCKS can be cross-checked. The key is to quickly re-establish the cross-check one sector at a time as tasks permit.\n\n[[File:16V5.DOC - 16v5.pdf — Mozilla Firefox 08.07.2026 04 34 19.png|frameless|737x737px]]\n\n=== Visual References IN Cockpit (F-16) ===\n[[File:16V5.DOC - 16v5.pdf — Mozilla Firefox 08.07.2026 21 23 29.png|frameless|739x739px]]\n\n== Formation Departure ==\nA formation departure consists of more than one aircraft at intervals of 1 minute or less which, by prior arrangement between the pilots, operate as a single aircraft with regard to navigation and position reporting. The departure portion of the flight may terminate at a preplanned breakup point which may be located up to, but not beyond, the planned initial level-off at cruise altitude.\n\n== Taxi Operations ==\n* Keep use of power to a minimum\n* Taxi with 20 – 25 knots at straight taxiway sections and turn with 10 – 15 knots\n* Avoid jet blast damage\n* Check nose wheel steering and brakes as you taxi out of the spot\n* Check the flight instrument in the turn onto the taxiway\n* Taxi only in authorized areas\n* Slow down and taxi on the centerline in congested areas\n* Do not taxi within 10 feet of any obstacle\n* Do not taxi within 25 feet without a wingwalker\n* If taxiing behind an aircraft, a minimum spacing of four ship lengths is necessary\n\n== Before Departure (procedure at LCC, EOR, BTI) ==\n\n=== Aircraft checks ===\n* Aircraft are checked, armed and dearmed on Arming / Dearming Pads (of LCC/EOR Pads)\n* Before departure, aircraft are checked and armed at the departure end EOR pad\n* After Arrival, aircraft are disarmed at the arrival end EOR pad\n\n\'\'\'LCC\'\'\' = Last Chance Check\n\n\'\'\'EOR\'\'\' = End of Runway\n\n\'\'\'BTI\'\'\' = Before Takeoff Inspection\n\n=== End of Runway (EOR) ===\n* After stopping EOR, confirm all weapons switches safe\n* Keep hands in full view during arming\n* Aircraft should not taxi in front of aircraft with forward firing ordnance being armed / dearmed\n* As soon as you stop in the arming area, check your GS (reads zero)\n* If GS is zero or one knot, the INS platform is good\n* If GS is two or more, accomplish another alignment if possible\n* Ensure Speed Brake Stowed\n* Review takeoff procedure and details as EP immediately after takeoff\n* Check other aircraft for leaks, loose panels, proper configuration, etc\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_21_05_47.png|alt=Example of EOR Lineup|border|frameless|691x691px]]\n\n== Departure Briefing ==\nIn the so-called “Departure Briefing”, which takes place on the EOR / LCC Pad prior to the lineup, the Flight Lead informs his Flight about all relevant information regarding departure. A Departure Briefing usually contains the following information:\n\n# Which type of Lineup\n# Type of Takeoff (Two Ship, Interval or Trail)\n# Speeds\n# Lost Wingmen Procedure\n# Clearence for visual join-up in case of interval and trail departure\n# Further / Additional information / structure\n\nExample of a Departure Briefing:\n\nWe´ll make a 3 ship echelon gear doors allignment for interval departure 5 seconds, (max Brake 140, min Go 150, Go 160 (are V speeds currently not used in SK), rotation speed 200, AB off at 300, contract speed 350, 5 nose up, in case of lost Wingmen turn right for 30 seconds using 30 AOB, inform Lead, transition to instruments, obtain separate ATC clearance and attempt re-join, say when airborne, you´re cleared to join on the right, say when in position.\n\n== Runway Lineup ==\nGeneral:\n\n* Leader will direct the appropriate runway line up based on winds and runway width\n* Wingmen should line up ensuring wingtip clearance\n\nLineup Types:\n\n* Two Ship Lineup\n* Three Ship Echelon\n* Four Ship Echelon\n* Four Ship three in The Slot\n* Four Ship four in The Slot\n* Three Ship three in The Slot\n\nLineup Standards:\n\n* Runway lineup is determined by taking into account several factors:\n** Wind direction and speed\n** Direction of turn out of traffic\n** Weather\n* Minimum wingtip spacing is wingtip clearance but may be wider as desired or required\n\n=== Two Ship Lineup ===\n* Lead takes the center of it´s half of the runway\n* The wingmen will line up lead´s main gear doors and take wingtip spacing as briefed\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_19_39_19.png|alt=Example Two Ship Lineup|frameless|692x692px]]\n\n=== Three ship echelon ===\n* Three ship formations may line up in echelon, if the runway is wide enough\n* “2” and “3” will line up gear doors and take wingtip spacing as briefed\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_19_46_25.png|frameless|753x753px]]\n\n=== Four Ship Echelon ===\n* Four ship may line up in echelon, if the runway is wide enough\n* “2” will line up gear doors and take wingtip spacing as briefed\n* “3” and “4” should align helmets of preceding flight members\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_19_51_04.png|frameless|628x628px]]\n\n=== Four Ship Three in The Slot ===\n* Lead should line up as far to the side of the runway as practical\n* “2” will place the wingtip closest to lead on the centerline an line up gear doors\n* “3” will line up between lead and “2”, maintaining nose-tail clearance out to 500 ft\n* “4” will line up offset from “2”s jet blast while aligning gear doors of “3”\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_19_59_31.png|frameless|713x713px]]\n\n=== Four Ship Four in The Slot ===\n* Lead should line up as far to the side of the runway as practical\n* “2” will place the wingtip closest to lead on the center line and line up gear doors\n* “4” will pull in between lead and “2” while aligning gear doors\n* “3” will line up with wingtip clearance on “2” while aligning gear doors\n* “4” will pull in further to align tail fin or gear doors of “3”\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_20_06_54.png|frameless|651x651px]]\n\n=== Three Ship Three in The Slot ===\n* Lead should line up as far to the side of the runway as practical\n* “2” will place the wingtip closest to lead on the center line and line up gear doors\n* “3” will line up between lead and “2”, maintaining nose-tail clearance out to 500 ft\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_05.10.2025_20_10_03.png|frameless|696x696px]]\n\n== Takeoff ==\n\n=== Takeoff Types: ===\n* Two Ship Takeoff\n** Formation Takeoff\n** Usable for two aircraft only\n** Not usable during periods of high crosswind, low visibility, wet runway\n* Interval Departure\n** Used whenever a two ship takeoff is not possible\n** Separation assured by timing\n** Rejoin required after departure\n* (Radar assisted) Trail Departure\n** Used whenever a two ship takeoff is not possible\n** A type of interval departure\n** Separation assured by on board radar\n\n=== Two Ship Takeoff ===\n* Signal for brake release is a head nod or radio call\n* Leader will make a normal takeoff with throttle slightly retarded (power advantage for Wingman)\n* Leader will pass a gear up and AB out signal, if appropriate\n* Wingman will maintain wingtip clearance throughout takeoff roll\n* If overrunning the leader, wingman will be directed to assume the lead while continuing takeoff\n* If either member or the element must abort, the other member should continue the takeoff\n* In either case, stay on your side of the runway!\n\n=== Trail Departure ===\n* Used when conditions won´t permit a formation takeoff\n* Examples: Wet runways, crosswind limits, weapon loads, configuration differences, visibility\n* Set up radar for easy acquisition\n** Select RWS Mode\n* Priority is to fly the aircraft, not operating the radar\n* Ignore the radar until all high priority tasks are completed\n* During climbout, establish and maintain briefed power setting and airspeed\n* Unless restricted by procedure or ATC, begin all climbs at the same time as the leader\n* Lead should call passing every 5000 ft increment until all flight members are tied/visual\n* Rule of thumb: For 30 AOB turns, allow preceding aircraft to drift 5 deg laterally for each mile of separation between aircraft, Example: to maintain a 2 NM trail position, allow the preceding aircraft to drift 10 deg before initiating your turn\n* Once a tied or visual call has been made, advise the leader if either radar or visual contact is subsequently lost.\n\n=== Lost Wingman ===\nGeneral:\n\n* Transition to instruments\n* Notify Lead (“Lost Wingman”)\n* Execute lost wingman procedures\n* Obtain separate ATC clearance\n* Attempt rejoin\n* Flight lead: Acknowledge lost wingman, transmit altitude, speed, post, etc.\n\nWings level after departure:\n\n* Turn away using 15 deg angle of bank (AOB) for 15 seconds, ensure operation and obtain separate clearance\n\nWingman outside of the turn after departure:\n\n* Reverse the direction of the turn using 15 deg AOB for 15 seconds, ensure separation and obtain separate clearance\n\nWingman inside of the turn after departure:\n\n* Momentarily reduce power to ensure separation and inform lead to roll out, maintain bank to ensure lateral separation and obtain separate clearance\n\nFinal approach:\n\n* Momentarily turn away to ensure separation, commence the published missed approach procedure and obtain separate clearance\n\nMissed approach:\n\n* Momentarily turn away to ensure separation, continue the missed approach procedure while climbing 500 ft above missed approach altitude and get a separate clearance\n\nLocal procedures:\n\n* Sometimes local conditions require different lost wingman procedures\n\n== Rejoin ==\nPurpose:\n\n* The purpose of a rejoin is to get the flight back together safely and efficiently\n* Necessary after an interval / trail departure or lost wingman procedure\n\nBasics:\n\n* Lead should monitor wingmen during all rejoins\n* Airspeeds and bank angles are prebriefed or unit standard\n* Lead should make a radio call if flying different speed or bank angle\n\nRejoin Techniques:\n\n* A radar lock on may be used during the rejoin to provide range and overtake info\n* Maintaining 50 – 100 Knots of true overtake will provide a controlled, expeditious rejoin\n* “3” and “4” should be locked to the leader\n* At 3000 ft reduce power smoothly to control overtake\n* At 1500 ft, true overtake should be 50 knots\n* Use speed brakes if overtake is excessive\n* If overtake is excessive approaching the extended fingertip position, initiate a controlled overshoot\n\n== Crossunder ==\nGeneral:\n\n* Used to move wingman to the other side\n\nTwo Ship Crossunder:\n\n* On lead´s signal wingman will reduce power\n* The wingman will move back and down below lead´s Plane of Motion (POM) and add power again to avoid falling further behind\n* Next, wingman moves across and behind lead with a minimum of nose-tail clearance\n* Add power as required to avoid falling further behind\n* Once on the opposite side and with wingtip clearance, wingman will add power to move up and forward into position\n\nFour Ship Fingertip Crossunder:\n\n* If “2” is crossing to the side that “3” and “4” are on, “3” should move out to create room for “2”\n* “2” begins a normal crossunder (ensuring adequate spacing before crossing lead´s six o´clock)\n* If “3” and “4” are crossing simultaneously, “3” performs a normal crossunder on “2” and “1”\n* As “3” begins the crossunder, “4” performs a crossunder on “3”\n\nFour Ship Echelon Crossunder:\n\n* As “2” begins the crossunder, “3” performs a crossunder on “2”, “4” performs a crossunder on “3”\n\nVisual Signals:\n\n* Crossunder may be initiated using a wing dip to the side the wingman is requested to move or after instruction on radio\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 10.10.2025 13 59 01.png|frameless|663x663px]]\n\n== Lead Changes ==\n* Lead changes require an unmistakable transfer of responsibilities from one flight member to another\n* The flight member assuming lead will be no further aft than normal route/fingertip\n* Lead changes only change “Nav Lead” responsibilities\n* Flight lead responsibilities cannot be transferred\n\n== Formation Turns ==\n\n=== Fingertip Turns ===\n* Wingman will maintain fingertip position using fingertip spacing reference\n* Always stay slightly below preceding aircraft\n* Fingertip turns are limited to approximately 30 deg AOB\n* Lead will avoid sudden or abrupt changes in AOB or back stick pressure\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 10.10.2025 14 25 57.png|frameless|663x663px]]\n\n=== Echelon Turn Into the Echelon ===\n* Turns into the echelon will be avoided if at all possible\n* Wingman will maintain fingertip position using fingertip spacing references\n* Turns are limit to very shallow turns\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf_–_Mozilla_Firefox_10.10.2025_14_34_38.png|frameless|656x656px]]\n\n=== Echelon Turns Away From the Echelon ===\n* Plan to limit AOB to 60 degrees maximum\n* All aircraft will match lead´s roll rates while maintaining the same horizontal plane\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 10.10.2025 14 38 58.png|frameless|656x656px]]\n\n=== Route Turns ===\n* When turned away from the wingman, will stack level with the leader\n* When turned into, the wingman will only stack down enough to keep lead in sight and avoid his maneuvering plane\n\n=== Route Echelon Turns ===\n* If a turn is made into the echelon, each aircraft will only stack down enough to keep rest of the formation in sight and avoid their maneuvering plane\n* On turns away from the echelon, all aircrafts will maintain the same horizontal plane\n\n== Channel Changes ==\n* When a channel / frequency change is directed, wingman will normally move out from fingertip to route or from echelon to route echelon\n\n== Leaving Formation ==\nLeave Formation:\n\n* When directed to do so\n* When you lose sight of the aircraft ahead (lost wingman, blind)\n* When you are unable to join up or to stay in formation without crossing over, under or in front of the aircraft ahead\n* At any other time you feel that your presence in the formation constitutes a hazard\n* When leaving a formation, clear yourself in the direction of your turn and notify leader\n* If you have lost sight, comply with the appropriate lost wingman procedure\n* Rejoin only by directed to do so by the flight lead\n\n== Formation Landing ==\nLead:\n\n* Approach speed should be consistent with heavier aircraft\n* Wingmen should be positioned upwind if crosswind component exceeds 5 knots\n* Plan to land near the center of your half of the runway\n* Do not touchdown long and fast\n\nWingman:\n\n* Maintain a minimum of 10 feet lateral wingtip spacing\n* Stack level with lead as briefed when configured on final approach\n* Cross check the runway to ensure sufficient runway is available\n\n== Overhead Break ==\n* Used to split a formation in an orderly and expeditious manner to prepare for landing\n* Also called “360 overhead”\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 10.10.2025 15 05 58.png|frameless|936x936px]]\n\n== Standard Formations ==\n\n=== Fingertip Formations ===\nUsage:\n\n* Weather penetration\n* Airfield arrivals / departures\n* Show formations\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Lead aircraft is easy to see in bad weather\n* Close formation is used for formation takeoff / landing\n* Preferred before overhead break (360 overhead)\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* Does not allow for much head down time\n* Limited maneuvering\n* Shallow turns (up to 30 deg AOB) only\n* Both aircraft are easily detected as one group by an adversary\n\nParameters:\n\n* Use fingertip reference points\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 10.10.2025 15 20 01.png|frameless|330x330px]]\n\n=== Finger four (Four Ship Fingertip) ===\nUsage:\n\n* Weather penetration\n* Show formations\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Lead aircraft is easy to see in bad weather\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* Does not allow for much heads down time\n* Limited maneuvering\n* Shallow turns (up to 30 deg AOB) only\n* All aircraft are easily detected as a group by an adversary\n\nParameters:\n\n* Same as fingertip\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 13 45 26.png|frameless|463x463px]]\n\n== Route Formations ==\nUsage:\n\n* Enroute\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Lead aircraft is easy to see in moderate weather\n* Easy to fly, allowing for a good amount of heads down time\n* Steeper turns up to 60 deg AOB possible\n* Easier to fly in a low altitude environment\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* All aircraft are easily detected as one group by an adversary\n* Limited engagement flexibility\n\nParameters:\n\n* Modified fingertip\n* Up to 500 feet between aircraft\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 13 54 38.png|frameless|583x583px]]\n\n=== Route Echelon ===\nUsage:\n\n* Enroute\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Lead aircraft is easy to see in moderate weather\n* Easy to fly, allowing for a good amount of heads down time\n* Steeper turns up to 60 deg AOB possible\n* Easier to fly in a low altitude environment\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* All aircraft are easily detected as one group by an adversary\n* Limited engagement flexibility\n* Turns into the echelon have to be shallow (max. 30 deg AOB)\n\nParameters:\n\n* Modified echelon\n* Up to 500 feet between aircraft\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 13 59 02.png|frameless|513x513px]]\n\n== Trail Formations ==\n\n=== Close Trail Formation ===\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical, Whenever necessary (e.g. Very low level)\n\nParameters:\n\n* One to two ship lengths behind lead aircraft\n* Below lead aircraft´s jetwash\n\n=== Trail Formations ===\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical, Whenever necessary (e.g. Very low level)\n\nParameters:\n\n* Flown in a cone 30 deg – 60 deg aft of lead\n* Range briefed by flight lead\n* Avoid flying at lead´s high six\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 14 06 03.png|frameless|503x503px]]\n\n== Non-Standard Formation ==\n\n=== Fighting Wings ===\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Helps to maintain flight integrity in bad weather or rough terrain\n* Allows for heads down time\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* Poor 6 o´clock coverage\n* Easily detectable as bandit\n\nParameters:\n\n* 30 – 60 deg aft of 3/9 line\n* 30 – 60 deg above or below\n* 500 – 3000 ft spacing\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 14 11 11.png|frameless|666x666px]]\n\n=== Wedge ===\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Leader protected on 6 o´clock position\n* Leader free to maneuvering\n* Wingman may always switch sides\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* No 6 o´clock protection for wingman\n* Lead changes difficult\n\nParameters:\n\n* 30 – 60 deg aft of 3/9 line\n* 4000 – 6000 ft spacing\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 14 18 22.png|frameless|417x417px]]\n\n=== Fluid Four ===\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Wingman kept close\n* Good maneuverability\n* Concentration of force\n* Easily converts to three ship if required\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* Adversary can acquire all four aircraft\n* Defensive maneuvering confusing\n* Difficult in low altitude rough terrain\n\nParameters:\n\n* Two times fighting wing\n* 6000 – 9000 ft separated\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 14 58 09.png|frameless|620x620px]]\n\n=== Four Ship Wedge ===\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Very offensive for threats forward of 3/9 L\n* Easy to fly\n* Formation in bad weather / rough terrain\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* Poor 6 o´clock lookout\n* Formation easily detected by bandit\n* Limited defensive maneuvering flexibility\n\nParameters:\n\n* Two times wedge\n* 1,5 – 3NM separated\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 15 04 22.png|frameless|419x419px]]\n\n=== Spread Four ===\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Difficult to acquire entire flight visually\n* Maximized fire power for BVR\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* Maneuvering difficult\n* Very difficult to fly at low altitude\n\nParameters:\n\n* “1” and “2” 6000 – 9000 ft apart\n* “2” positioned 0 – 30 deg behind 3/9 of “1”\n* “1” and “3” 6000 – 9000 ft spread\n* “3” and “4” 6000 – 9000 ft apart\n* “4” positioned 0 – 30 deg behind 3/9 of “3”\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 15 13 03.png|frameless|804x804px]]\n\n=== Box Formation ===\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Difficult to detect simultaneously by bandit\n* Easy support\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* Difficult to maintain formation integrity\n\nParameters:\n\n* Two times spread\n* 1,5 – 3 NM separated\n* Variation: Four Ship Offset Box\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 15 18 55.png|frameless|397x397px]]\n\n=== Arrowhead Formation ===\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Same as box\n* Easier to fly as “2” allowing for more look out\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* Same as box\n\nParameters:\n\n* Aircraft “3” and “4” fly spread 1,5 – 3 NM behind “1!”\n* Aircraft “2” 30 – 60 deg behind leader´s 3/9 line and 3000 ft spacing\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 16 14 37.png|frameless|589x589px]]\n\n=== Three Ship Vic ===\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical\n\nParameters:\n\n* Leader 1,5 – 3 NM in front of “2” and “3”\n* “2” and “3” 6000 – 9000 ft apart\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 16 18 26.png|frameless|419x419px]]\n\n== Line Abreast (Spread) ==\nUsage:\n\n* Tactical\n* Enroute\n\nAdvantages:\n\n* Minimizes the chances of simultaneous detection by bandit\n* Ordnance can be released quickly\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n* Hard to fly for inexperienced wingman\n\nParameters:\n\n* 0 – 20 deg aft\n* 4000 – 12000 ft spacing\n* Between 30 deg above and below\n* Optimum 0 deg and 6000 – 9000 ft\n* Or as briefed\n\n[[File:STANAG7001FormationFlying.pdf – Mozilla Firefox 11.10.2025 14 25 43.png|frameless|616x616px]]\n\n== Line Abreast Turns ==\n\n=== Tactical TURNS ===\nExecution TAC Turn:\n\nA TAC Turn is a delayed ninety 90 turn either to right or left. Depending on the direction of turn, the aircraft outside the turn turns first. The turn of the inner / second aircraft turning is delayed by a visual reference. Once the nose of the first turning aircraft is coming around to point on the second, the second aircraft starts its turn.\n\nThe TAC Turn is flown in full military Power with sustained altitude and speed.\n\nExecution Hook Turn:\n\nA Hook turn is a simultaneous 180 degree turn either to the left or right. It is flown in full military Power with sustained altitude and speed.\n\n[[File:16V5.DOC - 16v5.pdf — Mozilla Firefox 08.07.2026 04 40 52.png|frameless|783x783px]]\n\n=== Delayed 45 Degree Turns, Crossturns and Check Turns ===\nExecution Delayed 45 Degree Turn:\n\nA Delayed 45 Degree turn is self explained by its name. It is flown in full military power with sustained speed and altitude.\n\nDepending on the direction of turn, the inner aircraft starts its turn when crossing the outer aircrafts flightpath.\n\nExecution Crossturn:\n\nIs a Hook turn with both aircrafts turning into each other. The same parameters as for a normal Hook turn are required.\n\nExecution Check Turn:\n\nCheck Turns are Turns for Heading adjustments that are smaller than 45 Degrees. They are flown with a desired power setting but with sustained altitude. A slight formation offset is required. The lead will adjust the heading by gently turning into the chosen direction.\n\n[[File:16V5.DOC - 16v5.pdf — Mozilla Firefox 08.07.2026 04 42 04.png|frameless|791x791px]]\n\n----\n\nTo whoever manages to reach this point, my congratulations. Screenshot this and send it into \"general\" ;)\n\n\'\'Iceman\'\'','utf-8'),
(605,'\n== Intercept (ID) ==\nAn Identification (ID) intercept establishes the type, registration, colour, and armament of an unknown contact by radar and/or visual means before follow-on tasking (shadow, intervention, radio interrogation, obey-check, or friendly termination) is assigned.\n\n=== ID Result Format ===\nThe ID result is reported as: \'\'\'Number of Acft, Type, Registration, Colour, Armament\'\'\'\n\n=== Task: ID ===\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n! style=\"width:25%;\" |Station\n!Phrase\n|-\n|Controller\n|{CS} confirm ready to copy ID(-result)\n|-\n|FL\n|{Controller-CS} ready\n|-\n|Controller\n|Bogey is {Number} {Colour} {Acft Type}, registration {REGISTRATION}, {Armament}, at {Altitude}, Heading {HDG}, speed {Speed}\n|-\n|FL\n|{Controller-CS} copied, ({ID result}) new task {Task}\n|-\n|Controller\n|{CS} roger task {Task}\n|}\n\'\'This concludes Task ID; the controller can then assign one of the following follow-on tasks: Shadow, Obey-Check, Radio Interrogation, Intervention, or Friendly/Terminate.\'\'\n\n=== Task: Shadow ===\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n! style=\"width:25%;\" |Station\n!Phrase\n|-\n|Controller\n|{CS} new task shadow\n|-\n|FL\n|{Controller-CS} roger task shadow\n|}\n\'\'Task Shadow means following the aircraft at a distance of approximately 1–2 NM without further closure.\'\'\n\n=== Task: Obey-Check ===\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n! style=\"width:25%;\" |Station\n!Phrase\n|-\n|Controller\n|{CS} new task obey-check, fly HDG {HDG}\n|-\n|FL\n|{Controller-CS} task obey-check HDG {HDG}\n|-\n|FL\n|{Controller-CS} bogey is playmate \'\'(or: aircraft is not obeying)\'\'\n|-\n|Controller\n|{CS} copied\n|}\n\'\'Obey-Check means the fighter rocks its wings and then flies a turn in the specified direction. While doing so, it observes the target and reports whether it also rocks its wings and follows (\"playmate\") or does not (\"aircraft is not obeying\").\'\'\n\n=== Task: Radio Interrogation ===\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n! style=\"width:25%;\" |Station\n!Phrase\n|-\n|Controller\n|{CS} new task radio interrogation (on simulated guard {Frequency})\n|-\n|FL\n|{Controller-CS} roger, task radio interrogation\n|-\n|FL (on guard)\n|{Colour} {Acft Type} {Position}, with registration {REGISTRATION} (squawking {Squawk}) this is the eurofighter on your left wing calling on (simulated) guard ({Frequency}) you have been intercepted by the german air force. For purpose of identification state: Registration, Callsign, aerodrome of departure and aerodrome of destination on (simulated) guard ({Frequency})\n|-\n|Intercepted Acft\n|(German Interceptor) this is {Callsign} registration {REG} on guard, departure is {ICAO}, destination {ICAO}\n|-\n|Controller\n|Roger, standby\n|-\n|Controller\n|{CS} did you copy (the answer) on guard?\n|-\n|FL\n|Affirmative / Negative\n|}\n\'\'If the controller did not copy the response on guard, the fighter relays the answer. After this, termination is possible, or further tasks may follow.\'\'\n\n=== Task: Intervention ===\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n! style=\"width:25%;\" |Station\n!Phrase\n|-\n|Controller\n|{CS} new task intervention to {ICAO}\n|-\n|FL\n|{Controller-CS} roger, intervention to {ICAO}\n|-\n| colspan=\"2\" |\'\'Further radio follows the Intercept Call, kneeboard side (see below)\'\'\n|}\n\'\'During Interrogation and Intervention, the fighter flies to the left of the target at cockpit height (the pilot in command usually sits on the left).\'\'\n\n\'\'\'Intercept Call (Kneeboard):\'\'\'\n\nINTERCEPTED AIRCRAFT this is german INTERCEPTOR callsign {Callsign}, you will be intervented to {Airport} \'\'\'FOLLOW, FOLLOW\'\'\'\n\n* What is your Callsign\n* Descent to altitude {ALT} ft, QNH {HPA}\n* What is your preferred approach speed?\n* What is your gear down speed?\n\nThis is your airport, after split up join right/left hand pattern of RWY {NR} at {Altitude} feet, QNH {HPA}; \'\'\'YOU LAND, YOU LAND\'\'\'\n\n\'\'\'Phrases used by the intercepted aircraft (ICAO standard responses):\'\'\'\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n! style=\"width:25%;\" |Phrase\n!Meaning\n|-\n|CALL SIGN (callsign)\n|My callsign is....\n|-\n|WILCO\n|Understood. I will perform\n|-\n|CAN NOT\n|Unable to perform\n|-\n|REPEAT\n|Repeat the instructions\n|-\n|AM LOST\n|Unknown location\n|-\n|MAYDAY\n|They are in danger\n|-\n|HIJACK\n|I was hijacked\n|-\n|LAND (location)\n|I ask to land at...\n|-\n|DESCEND\n|I ask to descend\n|}\n\n=== Termination ===\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n! style=\"width:25%;\" |Station\n!Phrase\n|-\n|Controller\n|{CS}, ID friendly / ID {Result} (per task result)\n|-\n|FL\n|{Controller-CS} roger friendly, terminate\n|-\n|FL2 (Wingman)\n|{Controller-CS} terminate\n|-\n|Controller\n|{CS} terminate\n|}\n\'\'Termination always occurs in the order Blue – Red – Controller (flight lead first, then wingman, with the controller confirming last). The formation then splits and a new setup begins.\'\'\n----\n\n== 07 / Quick Reaction Alert (QRA) ==\nQuick Reaction Alert (QRA), termed \"Airspace Control Alert\" in US usage, is the standing peacetime posture NATO air forces maintain to protect sovereign and Allied airspace. Armed fighters and crews sit at high readiness so they can launch within minutes to identify, shadow, and if necessary escort or engage an aircraft that has entered, or is approaching, protected airspace without authorisation or without maintaining required communications.\n\n=== Purpose ===\nQRA(I) — the Interceptor role of QRA — is one of the peacetime activities carried out under NATO Integrated Air and Missile Defence (NIAMD), alongside the Air Surveillance and Control System (ASACS) and the Air Command and Control (Air C2) structure. Typical triggers for a scramble include: an aircraft entering national/Allied airspace without clearance, loss of radio contact with a civil aircraft, an aircraft deviating from its filed flight plan without notification, or an unidentified/unresponsive military contact approaching the border of Allied airspace.\n\n=== Force Structure ===\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n! style=\"width:30%;\" |Element\n!Role\n|-\n|ASACS / CRC\n|Ground- and air-based sensors build and maintain the Recognised Air Picture (RAP); a Control and Reporting Centre (CRC) tracks all air traffic within its assigned airspace 24/7/365.\n|-\n|Air C2 (CAOC)\n|A Combined Air Operations Centre (for NATO, Uedem or Torrejón) authorises and directs the scramble, tasks the QRA(I) flight, and applies the current Rules of Engagement (ROE).\n|-\n|QRA(I) Flight\n|Armed fighters (2-ship standard) held at cockpit- or runway-alert, ready to launch within a short, nationally-defined reaction time once tasked.\n|}\n\n=== Readiness & Scramble ===\nReaction timelines and exact alert postures are nationally classified; publicly reported figures (e.g. UK QRA crews reported at readiness to be airborne within about 15 minutes) can be used as an unclassified planning reference for training purposes, but should not be presented as a fixed NATO standard.\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n! style=\"width:25%;\" |Station\n!Phrase\n|-\n|CAOC/Controller\n|{CS} SCRAMBLE SCRAMBLE SCRAMBLE, {Group Label} BULLSEYE {Bearing/Range} {Altitude} {Declaration}\n|-\n|FL\n|{CS} SCRAMBLE, copied\n|-\n|FL\n|{Controller-CS} {CS} AIRBORNE\n|-\n|Controller\n|{CS} roger airborne, {further vectors / handoff to intercept controller}\n|}\n\'\'After the scramble, the sequence transitions seamlessly into the intercept procedure — depending on the tactical picture, either as an ID intercept (see above) or a VID intercept (see Chapter 08), including Commit/Guidance/JUDY/Tally/Identification.\'\'\n\n=== Civil ATC Interface (Airspace Coordination) ===\n\'\'The following describes how a scramble is coordinated with civil air traffic control before and during the intercept. This section is modelled on the publicly released US FAA/NORAD procedure (FAA Order 7610.4J, \"Special Military Operations,\" 1998) as an unclassified, structurally representative reference — the equivalent German/Luftwaffe–DFS coordination procedure is not publicly published, but the underlying logic (retain-until-requested handoff, filed scramble/airborne orders, defined transfer point) is broadly representative of how any NATO nation integrates a QRA launch into controlled civil airspace.\'\'\n\n==== Who the fighters are actually talking to ====\nOnce airborne, an interceptor does not go straight to a dedicated military-only frequency. It is worked by ordinary civil Air Traffic Control (ATC) like any other IFR flight, with priority/expedited handling, until tactical control is formally handed to the military radar unit directing the intercept — in the US model, the \'\'\'Air Defence Control Facility (ADCF)\'\'\', a military radar unit (Air Defence Sector / AWACS) that is the only type of unit authorised to direct interceptors. The CRC/CAOC in the NATO force structure above fills the equivalent role.\n\n==== Transfer of control: when and how ====\nThe handoff from civil ATC to the military controller is governed by a small set of rules, not a fixed altitude or distance:\n\n* The interceptor is retained under civil ATC control for as long as possible. ATC does not initiate the handoff on its own.\n* The military controller (ADCF) must request the transfer; it is not allowed to request control until the aircraft actually reaches the point where control must be assumed to carry out the intercept — i.e. it cannot take the aircraft \"early, just in case.\"\n* When requested, ATC must expedite the transfer.\n* The transfer point (a time, fix, or altitude) is chosen so that no conflict remains with any other traffic still under the transferring facility\'s control.\n* After the intercept phase is complete and the fighter is clear of all other traffic under military control, control reverts back to civil ATC for the return-to-base/recovery phase.\n\n==== Scramble order & flight plan (filed by the military controller) ====\nWhen a scramble is ordered, the ADCF/CRC notifies the relevant terminal ATC facility and identifies the mission as an active air-defence mission. It also files a short flight plan containing:\n\n* Call sign and aircraft type/equipment\n* Number of aircraft\n* Departure point\n* Proposed departure time — or simply the word \'\'\'\"immediate\"\'\'\'\n* Heading, altitude, and duration of flight\n* Remarks: frequency, type of climb, controlling agency, and desired transfer point\n\n==== Airborne orders (filed once the flight is up) ====\nAs early as possible after launch, the military controller separately files \"airborne orders\" with the relevant en-route/terminal ATC facilities, containing:\n\n* Call sign, aircraft type/number\n* Departure point and time\n* Heading and altitude\n* Route of flight, broken into three legs: departure route → route to a fix serving the operating/tasked area → route from that fix to the intercept/destination\n* Frequency, and MARSA (Military Authority Assumes Responsibility for Separation) status if applicable\n\n==== Standing authorisation & squawk ====\nFor missions that cannot be conducted within normal ATC procedures, a standing pre-authorisation (in the US model, the FAA Authorization for Interceptor Operations, AFIO) allows active air-defence missions to proceed outside the normal clearance process. Aircraft operating under this authorisation squawk a dedicated identification code (Mode 3/A \'\'\'7777\'\'\' in the US model) so ATC and the military controller can positively track them even while outside the standard clearance framework.\n\n==== Beyond controlled airspace: \"due regard\" ====\nWhen an intercept and its supporting aircraft operate in controlled airspace beyond national territorial limits and a normal ATC clearance is impractical, the operation shifts to the \"due regard\" concept (from DOD FLIP, used for operations over the high seas): the military controlling agency itself assumes responsibility for separating its aircraft from all observed traffic, in place of ATC. This only applies under specific conditions — e.g. the aircraft is in visual meteorological conditions, is within surveillance/radio contact of a radar facility, or carries its own separation-capable radar — and separation becomes the aircraft commander\'s own responsibility once outside the controlling agency\'s radar coverage.\n\n=== Post-Intercept Options ===\nFollowing identification, the QRA(I) flight is normally tasked to shadow or escort the contact out of the airspace, hand it to a follow-on national asset, or — in the case of a genuine emergency (lost comms, distress) — assist it to a safe landing per the Intervention procedure above. Engagement is authorised only as an absolute last resort and strictly IAW the current ROE issued by Air C2.\n----\n\n== 08 / Intercept (VID) ==\nA Visual Identification (VID) intercept is conducted when a contact cannot be declared HOSTILE or FRIENDLY by electronic means alone, and visual confirmation of aircraft type, markings, or behavior is required before engagement authority can be granted or withheld.\n\nVID intercepts follow a structured sequence: the controller guides the fighter to a position from which a visual identification pass can be made, then the fighter reports the result and awaits further direction.\n\n=== Intercept Flow ===\n\n==== Commit ====\nThe controller or flight lead initiates the intercept against the unidentified contact.\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n! style=\"width:25%;\" |Station\n!Phrase\n|-\n|Controller\n|{CS} COMMIT {Group Label} BULLSEYE {Bearing/Range} {Altitude} {Declaration} {Nr of Contacts}\n|-\n|FL\n|{CS} COMMIT\n|}\n\n==== Guidance ====\nThe controller provides vectors to position the fighter for a VID pass. This is the only phase where the controller takes positive close control of the intercept geometry. BRAA format is used throughout.\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n! style=\"width:25%;\" |Station\n!Phrase\n|-\n|Controller\n|{CS} BRAA {Bearing/Range} {Altitude} {Aspect} {Declaration}\n|-\n|FL\n|{CS}\n|-\n| colspan=\"2\" style=\"text-align:center;\" |\'\'Controller continues to provide updated vectors until fighter calls JUDY\'\'\n|}\n\n==== JUDY ====\nThe fighter calls JUDY when radar contact is established and no further controller guidance is required. The controller minimizes transmissions from this point.\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n! style=\"width:25%;\" |Station\n!Phrase\n|-\n|FL\n|{Controller-CS} {CS} JUDY\n|-\n|Controller\n|\'\'Minimizes transmissions. Continues to provide SA information only.\'\'\n|}\n\n==== Tally ====\nThe fighter calls TALLY when visual contact is established with the target.\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n! style=\"width:25%;\" |Station\n!Phrase\n|-\n|FL\n|{CS} TALLY {Position}\n|}\n\n==== Identification ====\nThe fighter conducts the VID pass and reports the result to the controller. The report includes aircraft type, markings, and behavior observed.\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n! style=\"width:25%;\" |Station\n!Phrase\n|-\n|FL\n|{Controller-CS} {CS} IDENTIFIED {Aircraft Type} {Markings / Behavior}\n|-\n|Controller\n|{CS} {Further Instructions / Declaration Update}\n|}\nIf the contact is identified as HOSTILE following the VID pass, the controller issues the updated declaration and the flight may proceed to engagement IAW ROE. If the contact is FRIENDLY or NEUTRAL, the flight disengages and returns to its prebriefed position.\n----\n\n== Sources / Further Reading ==\nPlease review and approve before I link footnotes in the wiki:\n\n\'\'\'Intercept (ID) / VID:\'\'\'\n\n* ICAO Annex 2 to the Convention on International Civil Aviation, Appendix 1 & 2 (Interception of Civil Aircraft; Signals) — https://www.bazl.admin.ch/dam/bazl/it/dokumente/Fachleute/Regulationen_und_Grundlagen/icao-annex/icao_annex_2_rulesoftheair.pdf.download.pdf/icao_annex_2_rulesoftheair.pdf\n* ICAO Doc 9433 — Manual concerning Interception of Civil Aircraft (Appendix D: example flashcards) — https://news.ncac.mn/uploads/bookSubject/2022-11/637c784cde8a2.pdf\n* SKYbrary — \"Military Interception Signalling\" (not official, but a good summary of interceptor positioning) — https://skybrary.aero/articles/military-interception-signalling\n* ATP 1-02.1/MCRP 3-30B.1/NTTP 6-02.1/AFTTP 3-2.5/STTP 3-9002 — Multi-Service Brevity Codes, Signature Draft, 01 Jan 2025 — https://www.alssa.mil/Portals/9/Documents/mttps/sd_brevity_2025.pdf\n* ATP 3-52.4/MCRP 3-20F.10/NTTP 6-02.9/AFTTP 3-2.8 — Multi-Service Tactics, Techniques, and Procedures for Air Control (ACC MTTP), 2024 — https://www.alssa.mil/Portals/9/Documents/mttps/acc_2024.pdf\n\n\'\'\'Quick Reaction Alert (QRA) — NATO:\'\'\'\n\n* AJP-3.3 — Allied Joint Doctrine for Air and Space Operations (official NATO standardisation document; references QRA and points to MC 0613, the overarching NIAMD concept) — https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/698ef9fb7da91680ad7f4389/AJP_3_3_EdC_V1.pdf\n* NATO.int — \"NATO Air Policing\" (official, unclassified overview) — https://www.nato.int/cps/en/natohq/topics_132685.htm\n* JAPCC (Joint Air Power Competence Centre) — \"NATO Air Policing Against Unmanned Aircraft\" (NATO-accredited Centre of Excellence; describes the ASACS/Air C2/CAOC/QRA(I) structure; not a doctrine document itself) — https://www.japcc.org/articles/nato-air-policing-against-unmanned-aircraft/\n\n\'\'\'Civil ATC Interface (US/NORAD model, used illustratively):\'\'\'\n\n* FAA Order 7610.4J — \"Special Military Operations,\" 3 November 1998, Distribution Statement A (public release). Chapter 4: \"FAA/NORAD/PACAF Procedures for Control of Air Defense Aircraft\" — covers ADCF control instructions, transfer-of-control rules, scramble/airborne order format, AFIO, and \"due regard\" operations. Superseded internally by the current, FOUO-restricted JO 7610.4 (sensitive) / JO 7610.14 (non-sensitive) editions; this 1998 edition remains the last fully public release and is archived via DTIC, accession no. ADA355579. — https://apps.dtic.mil/sti/tr/pdf/ADA355579.pdf\n* FAA Order JO 7110.65 — Air Traffic Control (current edition), §9-2-7 \"Interceptor Operations\" — baseline ATC separation/priority handling for active air-defence scrambles — https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/publications/atpubs/atc_html/chap9_section_2.html\n\n\'\'Note: Exact QRA reaction times and alert levels are nationally classified; the ~15 minutes mentioned in this chapter is a publicly known, unclassified reference figure (RAF), not an official NATO standard. Likewise, the ATC-interface section above reflects the publicly available US/NORAD procedural model; the exact German/Luftwaffe scramble-to-ATC coordination procedure is not publicly published.\'\'','utf-8'),
(606,'== 01 / Overview ==\nAir combat is primarily carried out by CAP aircraft. The two main mission types are \'\'\'DCA\'\'\' (Defensive Counter Air) and \'\'\'OCA\'\'\' (Offensive Counter Air).\n\n=== Groups and Contacts ===\nA \'\'\'GROUP\'\'\' is any number of air contacts within 3 nm in azimuth and range of each other. A \'\'\'CONTACT\'\'\' is an individual radar return within a GROUP. Single contact is the assumed strength for all GROUPs unless otherwise specified. A GROUP with three or more contacts is called \'\'\'HEAVY\'\'\'.\n\nThe \'\'\'PICTURE\'\'\' establishes a common tactical air image and describes the spatial relationship of GROUPs. It is always communicated in BULLSEYE format unless briefed otherwise. Only the Three GROUPs with highest Priority are Conveyed\n\n=== Contact Declaration ===\nBefore a fighter can engage, a contact must be declared. Declaration is performed by the controller in response to a DECLARE request.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! Declaration !! Meaning !! Engagement Authority\n|-\n| \'\'\'BOGEY\'\'\' || Identity unknown || Not cleared to engage\n|-\n| \'\'\'OUTLAW\'\'\' || Originates from known or suspected hostile area || Not cleared to engage; escalation criteria apply\n|-\n| \'\'\'SPADES\'\'\' || No valid IFF response on interrogation || Not cleared to engage; escalation criteria apply\n|-\n| \'\'\'BANDIT\'\'\' || Positively identified as enemy IAW theater ID criteria || Not cleared to engage; does not imply authority to fire\n|-\n| \'\'\'HOSTILE\'\'\' || Confirmed enemy; clearance to fire authorized IAW ROE || Cleared to engage\n|-\n| \'\'\'NEUTRAL\'\'\' || Positively identified as neutral || Not cleared to engage\n|-\n| \'\'\'FRIENDLY\'\'\' || Positively identified as friendly || Not cleared to engage\n|-\n| \'\'\'FURBALL\'\'\' || Friendly and non-friendly aircraft within 5 nm || Employment not authorized until resolved\n|}\n\nA BOGEY, BANDIT, OUTLAW, or SPADES classification does not authorize engagement. HOSTILE is the only declaration that authorizes weapons employment IAW ROE.\n\n== 02 / Important Concepts ==\n\n=== Communication Cadence by Phase ===\nCommunication priority shifts between the controller and fighters depending on the phase of the intercept.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! Phase !! Priority Communicators\n|-\n| Pre-COMMIT / Marshal || 1. Controller · 2. Fighters\n|-\n| Post-COMMIT / Push || 1. Controller · 2. Fighter\n|-\n| Targeting / Weapons Employment || 1. Fighters · 2. Controller\n|-\n| Merge || 1. Engaged Fighter · 2. Supporting Fighters · 3. Controller\n|-\n| Post-Merge || 1. Fighter Clearing Merge · 2. Supporting Fighters · 3. Controller\n|}\n\n=== TAC Range ===\nCalled at 60 nm (default) by the first asset to recognize the separation. Signals all players to prepare for targeting. Pre-commit SA building continues until this point.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Controller or Fighter || {CS} {Group Label} TAC RANGE\n|}\n\n=== Airspace Sanitization ===\nA Section consists of two aircraft, each equipped with its own radar. Regardless of the number of bars or maximum azimuth a single radar supports, no single system can cover the entire sky. To compensate, both radars are stacked and assigned to different portions of the airspace — each portion is called an \'\'\'Area of Responsibility (AOR)\'\'\'. This technique, known as \'\'\'Radar Mating\'\'\', vastly increases the total volume scanned and ensures that no unknown contacts are operating in the area beyond already-tracked groups. The exact parameters depend on the aircraft type: the US Navy recommends RWS, 140°, 6 bars, end scale 80 nm, with radars mated at 25 nm: placing the flight lead between 40,000 and 15,000 ft, and the wingman from ground level to 25,000 ft.\n\nThe process of actively scanning assigned AORs to confirm no unknown contacts are present is called \'\'\'Sanitisation\'\'\'.\n\nBecause both Radar Mating parameters and Sanitisation responsibilities directly affect how an engagement develops, all of this must be \'\'\'pre-briefed\'\'\' and set as a \'\'\'contract\'\'\' before the mission. Time pressure may not always permit a thorough sanitisation pass, and how to handle that contingency should likewise be agreed upon in the brief.\n\n=== Targeting ===\nThe flight assigns GROUP responsibility and confirms classification.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| FL || {Controller-CS} {CS} DECLARE {Group Label or Bullseye Position}\n|-\n| Controller || {Group Label} DECLARED {HOSTILE / BOGEY / FRIENDLY / UNABLE / FURBALL}\n|-\n| FL || {CS} TARGET {Group Label} {Tactic}\n|-\n| colspan=\"2\" style=\"text-align:center;\" | \'\'Once group appears on radar\'\'\n|-\n| FL || {CS} TARGETED {Group Label} BULLSEYE {Bearing/Range}\n|-\n| Controller || \'\'No response if information correlates. Comparative response if correction is required.\'\'\n|}\n\n== 03 / Common Calls ==\n\n=== DECLARE ===\nInquiry as to the identity of a specified GROUP. Full positional data in BULLSEYE format must accompany all responses.\n  MIKE, EAGLE 11, DECLARE NORTH GROUP.\n  EAGLE 11, NORTH GROUP BULLSEYE 2-7-3/27, TWENTY-TWO THOUSAND, TRACK EAST, BOGEY SPADES.\n\n=== BOGEY DOPE ===\nRequest for BRAA information on the indicated GROUP or the closest GROUP if not specified. Does not imply fighter targeting.\n  MIKE, EAGLE 11, BOGEY DOPE NORTH GROUP.\n  EAGLE 11, NORTH GROUP BRAA 2-1-0/28, THIRTY-EIGHT THOUSAND, HOT, HOSTILE.\n\n=== NAILS ===\nFighter\'s radar warning receiver is detecting an airborne interceptor radar in search mode. Called with bearing or cardinal direction.\n  EAGLE 1, NAILS 2-7-0.\n\n=== SPIKED ===\nFighter is being actively tracked by an airborne radar. Called with bearing or cardinal direction. Controller correlates to a GROUP within ±30 degrees and responds with range, altitude, aspect, declaration, and GROUP name.\n  EAGLE 1, SPIKED 2-7-0.\n  EAGLE 1, SPIKE RANGE 17, THIRTY THOUSAND, HOSTILE EAST GROUP, TWO CONTACTS.\n\n=== THREAT Call ===\nIf a fighter will pierce THREAT range to an untargeted GROUP, the controller makes a THREAT call in BRAA format no later than THREAT range.\n  HEAT 31, ADDITIONAL GROUP THREAT, BRAA 1-2-5/35, THIRTY-FIVE THOUSAND, HOT, HOSTILE.\n\nIf the THREAT is within 5 nm of friendly fighters:\n  BOLT 1, THREAT NORTH THREE, TEN THOUSAND, TRACK SOUTH, HOSTILE.\n\n=== FADED and VANISHED ===\n\'\'\'FADED\'\'\' — a previously tracked GROUP not updated by sensors for 30 seconds. The controller reports the GROUP as FADED with last known BULLSEYE position. Fighters maintain MONITOR responsibility if they can maintain correlation. FADED is not used for friendly aircraft — controllers use NEGATIVE CONTACT for friendlies not held on sensors.\n\n\'\'\'VANISHED\'\'\' — a special case of FADED used when the GROUP is not in a known blind zone and is correlated to a friendly shot that meets parameters for successful termination. \n\n=== Unexpected Contacts ===\nThree distinct call types exist for contacts not part of the established picture:\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! Call !! Criteria !! Format\n|-\n| \'\'\'ADDITIONAL GROUP\'\'\' || Newly detected outside targeting range, or does not fit traditional label || BULLSEYE\n|-\n| \'\'\'POP-UP GROUP\'\'\' || Previously undetected; appears inside targeting range but outside THREAT range || BULLSEYE\n|-\n| \'\'\'THREAT GROUP\'\'\' || Undetected or unreported GROUP meeting briefed THREAT criteria || BRAA to the closest aircraft\n|}\n\n  DARKSTAR, POP-UP GROUP BULLSEYE 2-7-0/15, FIVE THOUSAND, TRACK WEST, BOGEY SPADES.\n  RAMBO 2, THREAT GROUP BRAA 2-7-0/13, ONE THOUSAND, HOT, HOSTILE.\n\n== 04 / Engagement ==\nThe engagement loop is the core sequence executed by all fighter flights once committed and targeting is established. It repeats until the GROUP is splashed, leaves the area, or the flight resets.\n\n=== MELD ===\nThe flight lead directs all flight members to shift their radars from wide sanitization to focused SA on the assigned GROUP. This transition narrows the radar from maximum azimuth and altitude coverage to a tighter volume centered on the target. Sanitization of the surrounding airspace ceases until DROPPING is called.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| FL || {Controller-CS} {CS} MELD\n|-\n| colspan=\"2\" style=\"text-align:center;\" | \'\'All flight members shift radar from sanitization to the assigned GROUP\'\'\n|}\n\n=== SORT ===\nThe flight lead assigns individual contacts within the GROUP to specific flight members. Each member acknowledges with SORTED when they have confirmed their assigned contact on radar and are prepared to engage it. If a member cannot confirm their assigned contact, SORTED is withheld until resolution.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| FL || \'\'(intraflight) FLIGHT SORT {Criteria}\'\'\n|-\n| #2 || \'\'(intraflight) #2 SORTED\'\'\n|-\n| #3 || \'\'(intraflight) #3 SORTED\'\'\n|-\n| #4 || \'\'(intraflight) #4 SORTED\'\'\n|}\n\n=== JUDY ===\nCalled by the flight lead when the flight no longer requires controller guidance to prosecute the intercept. From this point, the controller minimizes transmissions and provides situational awareness information only. JUDY is not always called.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| FL || {Controller-CS} {CS} JUDY\n|-\n| Controller || \'\'Minimizes transmissions. Provides SA and THREAT calls only.\'\'\n|}\n\n=== ENGAGE ===\nDirective call from the flight lead to a specific wingman to employ weapons. The flight lead may also direct simultaneous employment by multiple members.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| FL || {CS} ENGAGE\n|-\n| Wingman || {CS}\n|}\n\n=== Weapon Employment ===\nThe firing aircraft calls the shot on TACC2 NET immediately after employment. The call includes shot type, GROUP label, contact description, BULLSEYE position, and altitude. The controller acknowledges with COPY SHOT.\n\nIf a fighter determines a GROUP HOSTILE and ROE has been met, the fighter may employ autonomously and include HOSTILE in the shot transmission.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Firing Aircraft || {TACC2-CS} {CS} FOX THREE {Group Label} {Contact Description} BULLSEYE {Bearing/Range} {Altitude}\n|-\n| TACC2 || {TACC2-CS} COPY SHOT\n|-\n| colspan=\"2\" style=\"text-align:center;\" | \'\'Autonomous HOSTILE employment\'\'\n|-\n| Firing Aircraft || {Controller-CS} {CS} FOX THREE {Group Label} BULLSEYE {Bearing/Range} {Altitude} HOSTILE\n|-\n| TACC2 || {TACC2-CS} COPY SHOT\n|}\n\nMultiple shots in one transmission are acceptable:\n  SHADOW12 FOX THREE NORTH GROUP LEAD CONTACT BULLSEYE 2-7-0/20 TWENTY THOUSAND; SHADOW11 FOX THREE NORTH GROUP TRAIL CONTACT.\n\n=== OUT / DEFENDING ===\nFollowing weapon employment the flight turns away from the threat. OUT is a planned maneuver executed at a prebriefed range. DEFENDING indicates a reactive maneuver against an incoming weapon and is called with the cardinal direction of the defensive turn.\n\nOUT cancels targeting responsibility\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| FL || {Controller-CS} {CS} OUT {Direction}\n|-\n| colspan=\"2\" style=\"text-align:center;\" | \'\'or, if reacting to an incoming weapon\'\'\n|-\n| Aircraft || {CS} DEFENDING {Direction}\n|}\n\n=== DROPPING ===\nMay be called in conjunction with OUT. Signals the controller that the flight has ceased monitoring the GROUP and is returning to sanitization radar settings. Monitoring responsibility transfers back to the controller. Targeting responsibility is cancelled \n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| FL || {Controller-CS} {CS} OUT {Direction} DROPPING {Group Label}\n|-\n| colspan=\"2\" style=\"text-align:center;\" | \'\'Controller resumes monitoring of the dropped GROUP\'\'\n|-\n| Controller || \'\'Resumes monitoring.\'\'\n|}\n\n=== Assessment ===\nThe flight assesses weapon effects from sensor data or controller reports. Three outcomes are possible:\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! Outcome !! Action\n|-\n| GROUP splashed || Call SPLASH\n|-\n| GROUP remains a factor, follow-up shot required || Turn IN, return to MELD\n|-\n| GROUP has left the area or is no longer a factor || Proceed with Tasking\n|}\n\n=== IN (Follow-up Engagement) ===\nIf the GROUP remains a factor and the tactic permits a follow-up shot, the flight turns back toward the GROUP. The IN call triggers a new cycle beginning at MELD.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| FL || {Controller-CS} {CS} IN {Direction}\n|-\n| FL || {Controller-CS} {CS} MELD\n|-\n| colspan=\"2\" style=\"text-align:center;\" | \'\'Re-sort as required, then return to ENGAGE\'\'\n|}\n\n=== SPLASH ===\nCalled when weapon effects confirm the GROUP or contact is destroyed.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Firing Aircraft or Controller || {CS} SPLASH {Group Label or Contact Description}\n|}\n\n----\n\n== 05 / Defensive Counter Air (DCA) ==\nDCA is the use of fighter aircraft to defend a ground asset or airspace against hostile air threats. It uses prebriefed lanes and defined ranges.\n\n=== DCA Range Structure ===\nAll ranges are prebriefed per sortie. The following are the standard range lines used in DCA:\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! Range Line !! Definition\n|-\n| \'\'\'Mission Fail Line\'\'\' || The line the enemy must not cross for the mission to succeed. Defines the maximum depth of the defensive problem.\n|-\n| \'\'\'Commit Line (CL)\'\'\' || The line at which a bogey is declared HOSTILE IAW theater ROE and the flight is authorized to commit. Prebriefed for all DCA sorties.\n|-\n| \'\'\'Targeting Range\'\'\' || The prebriefed distance at which all contacts must be intercepted. A GROUP inside this range with no assigned fighter is UNTARGETED. Prebriefed per sortie.\n|-\n| \'\'\'TAC Range\'\'\' || Default 60 nm from the closest fighter to the closest GROUP. Signals all players to prepare for targeting.\n|-\n| \'\'\'Threat Range\'\'\' || The prebriefed range at which an untargeted GROUP meeting THREAT criteria triggers a THREAT call. Prebriefed per sortie.\n|}\n\n=== COMMIT Authority ===\nCOMMIT authority is derived from the combatant commander and delegated through the chain of command. Any of the following may hold or recommend COMMIT authority depending on what is delegated:\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! Role !! Abbreviation !! Authority\n|-\n| Flight Lead || FL || Usually holds COMMIT authority unless delegated\n|-\n| Team Lead || TL || May hold COMMIT authority if delegated\n|-\n| Mission Commander || MC || May hold COMMIT authority if delegated\n|-\n| TACC2 || — || May hold COMMIT authority if delegated\n|}\n\nIf only the MC, TL, or FL holds COMMIT authority, the controller may only recommend COMMIT. If the controller holds COMMIT authority, the MC and TL may only recommend.\n\n=== DCA Flow ===\n\n==== Lane Handover ====\nWhen an outgoing flight is relieved on station by an incoming flight, a positive lane handover is required before the outgoing flight departs.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Off-going Lane Commander || {CS} MOVE FORWARD\n|-\n| On-coming Lane Commander || {CS}\n|-\n| colspan=\"2\" style=\"text-align:center;\" | \'\'On-coming aircraft may enter the lane. Lane commander authority has not yet transferred.\'\'\n|-\n| Off-going Lane Commander || {CS} YOU HAVE THE LANE\n|-\n| On-coming Lane Commander || {CS} HAS THE LANE\n|}\n\nThe off-going flight passes critical mission information to the controller before departing. The controller passes big-picture information to the on-coming flight before pushing them to the tactical frequency.\n\n==== Pre-Commit ====\nThe flight arrives at its prebriefed CAP position with FENCE IN complete and radars in sanitization configuration. The controller holds picture responsibility.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| FL || {Controller-CS} {CS} PICTURE\n|-\n| Controller || {CS} {Nr of Groups} {Group Label} BULLSEYE {Bearing/Range} {Altitude} TRACK {Direction} {Declaration} {Nr of Contacts}\n|}\n\nThe flight evaluates commit criteria continuously. Pictures are requested as needed to maintain SA.\n\n==== Commit ====\n\n===== FIGHTER AUTHORITY COMMIT =====\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| TACC2 || {TACC2-CS} {BULLSEYE CALL} Recommend {CS} COMMIT \n|-\n| FL || {CS} COMMIT\n|}\n\n===== CONTROLLER AUTHORITY COMMIT =====\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| FL || {CS} Recommend Commit\n|-\n| TACC2 || {TACC2-CS} {BULLSEYE CALL} {CS} COMMIT \n|-\n|FL\n|{CS}\n|}\n\nAfter COMMIT, fighters hold radio priority.\n\n=== Engagement Loop ===\nThe flight executes the standard engagement loop as defined above The loop repeats until the GROUP is splashed, leaves the lane, or:\n\nOnce the Engagement is Complete the flight \'\'\'RESETS\'\'\' back to the briefed Position\n\n==== RESET ====\nDirects the flight to return to its prebriefed position or area of operations. RESET implies DROPPING and returns all targeting responsibility to the controller.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| FL || {Controller-CS} {CS} RESET\n|-\n| colspan=\"2\" style=\"text-align:center;\" | \'\'Flight returns to prebriefed CAP position. Controller resumes full picture responsibility\'\'\n|}\n\n----\n\n== 06 / Offensive Counter Air (OCA) ==\nOCA is the use of fighter aircraft to suppress, destroy, or neutralize enemy air assets before they can threaten friendly forces. Unlike DCA, OCA is not lane-based and does not require a commit line. The flight pushes into the threat environment rather than waiting for the threat to approach.\n\n=== OCA Range Structure ===\nThe same range lines apply as in DCA — Threat Range, Targeting Range, and TAC Range — but all are prebriefed based on the specific mission geometry and threat environment. There is no commit line or mission fail line in OCA.\n\n=== OCA Flow ===\nOCA does not have a lane handover or a commit phase. The flight checks in, receives the picture, establishes targeting, and executes the engagement loop.\n\n==== FULL COMMUNICATIONS EXAMPLE ====\nIn this Scenario, SHADOW11 four-ship F16C on OCA. TACC2 Agency CS THUMPER executing Positive Control. The Example begins after the Handover. Check In was done on the previous Frequency. TAC RANGE 60, THREAT RANGE 30  \n THUMPER SHADOW11 PICTURE\n\n THUMPER PICTURE TEN GROUPS GROUP BULLSEYE 0-2-0/25, TWENTY-SEVEN THOUSAND, THOUSAND, TRACK EAST, HOSTILE, HEAVY, THREE CONTACTS. GROUP BULLSEYE 2-7-0/25, FIFTEEN THOUSAND, TRACK WEST, HOSTILE. GROUP BULLSEYE 2-9-0/35, TEN THOUSAND, TRACK WEST, HOSTILE.\n\n SHADOW11 TARGETED GROUP BULLSEYE 0-2-0/25 \n\n SHADOW11 GROUP BULLSEYE 0-2-0/25 TAC RANGE\n\n SHADOW11 SKATE, MELD\n\n SHADOW11 SORTED\n\n SHADOW11 FOX3 BULLSEYE 0-2-0/25\n\n THUMPER COPY SHOT\n\n SHADOW11 OUT SOUTH DROPPING\n\n THUMPER\n\n THUMPER SHADOW11 BOGEYDOPE\n\n SHADOW11 EAST GROUP BRAA 2-1-0/28, FIFTEEN-THOUSAND, COLD, HOSTILE\n\n SHADOW11 IN, MELD\n\n SHADOW11 SORTED\n\n THUMPER SHADOW11 SPLASH EAST GROUP\n\n THUMPER\nIn the Following Example additional Adversarys Pop Up within Threat Range of SHADOW11\n SHADOW11 THUMPER THREAT GROUP, BRAA 1-2-5/28, FIVE-THOUSAND, HOT, HOSTILE, 2 CONTACTS\n\n SHADOW11 TARGETED SORTED LEAD, FOX 3 LEADER, FOX3 TRAILER, BULLSEYE 0-5-0/45, FIVE-THOUSAND\n\n THUMPER COPY SHOTS \n\n SHADOW11 DEFENDING SOUTH, DROPPING\n\n THUMPER\n\n SHADOW11 THUMPER THREAT GROUP, BRAA 0-6-0/15, TWO-THOUSAND, COLD, HOSTILE\n\n SHADOW11\n\n SHADOW11 IN \n\n SHADOW11 FOX3 BULLSEYE 0-6-0/15, TWO-THOUSAND\n\n THUMPER COPY SHOT\n\n SHADOW11 THUMPER THREAT GROUP VANISHED\n----\n\n== 07 / QRA / INTERCEPT / ID ==\n[[MTTP/A2A/INTERCEPT]]\n\n== 07 / Merge ==\nA merge occurs when friendly and target aircraft arrive in the visual arena, within 3–5 nm of each other. At the merge, radar resolution becomes limiting and the engagement transitions from BVR to Within Visual Range (WVR). The engagement is now managed through ACM contracts and direct communication between flight members.\n\n=== Authority and Radio Priority ===\nTwo distinct concepts govern merge communications: \'\'\'authority\'\'\' and \'\'\'radio priority\'\'\'.\n\n\'\'\'Authority\'\'\' remains with the Flight Lead throughout the engagement. The FL can override, redirect, or terminate the engagement at any time regardless of who is engaged.\n\n\'\'\'Radio priority\'\'\' belongs to the engaged fighter. Other flight members subordinate their transmissions to avoid stepping on critical engaged fighter calls. The controller minimizes transmissions entirely and provides only THREAT calls, SA updates, and responses to direct requests.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! Role !! Authority !! Radio Priority\n|-\n| \'\'\'Flight Lead\'\'\' || Full authority over the engagement at all times || Secondary — subordinates to engaged fighter\n|-\n| \'\'\'Engaged Fighter\'\'\' || Manages own engagement IAW FL direction || Primary — holds radio priority\n|-\n| \'\'\'Supporting Fighter\'\'\' || Executes IAW engaged fighter and FL direction || Tertiary — transmits only when necessary\n|-\n| \'\'\'Controller\'\'\' || Provides SA only || Minimal — THREAT calls and direct requests only\n|}\n\n=== MERGED ===\nThe controller calls MERGED when radar tracks come together within 3–5 nm, or the engaged fighter calls it when visual contact is made at the merge.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Controller or Engaged Fighter || {CS} MERGED {Group Label}\n|}\n\n=== Role Assignment ===\nAt or before the merge, engaged and supporting roles must be clearly established. By default the flight lead is engaged. Roles can be assigned or transferred in two ways:\n\n==== Wingman-Initiated Transfer ====\nThe wingman identifies he is the engaged fighter based on the bandit\'s plane of motion and lift vector orientation, and calls it.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Wingman || #2 ENGAGED\n|-\n| Flight Lead || PRESS\n|}\n\n==== Flight Lead-Initiated Transfer ====\nThe flight lead recognizes the wingman is the engaged fighter and assigns the role.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Flight Lead || #2 PRESS\n|-\n| Wingman || #2 ENGAGED\n|}\n\n==== Bandit Switches ====\nIf the bandit maneuvers to engage the other fighter, the currently engaged fighter communicates the change.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Engaged Fighter || BANDIT SWITCHED\n|-\n| colspan=\"2\" style=\"text-align:center;\" | \'\'Roles are reassessed and reassigned as required\'\'\n|}\n\n=== Engaged Fighter SA Calls ===\nThe engaged fighter is responsible for narrating the fight to maintain the supporting fighter\'s situational awareness. This enables the supporting fighter to position correctly and employ at the right moment. SA calls include the fight geometry, altitude, and direction.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Engaged Fighter || {CS} ENGAGED {ONE / TWO CIRCLE} {Direction} {Altitude}\n|}\n\n  VIPER 1, ENGAGED ONE CIRCLE CLIMBING THROUGH FIFTEEN THOUSAND.\n  VIPER 1, ENGAGED TWO CIRCLE NORTHBOUND BANDITS LOW TWO\'S HIGH.\n\nThese calls are made continuously as the fight develops, not just once at the merge. The supporting fighter cannot position or employ safely without them.\n\n=== STATUS Calls ===\nSTATUS is a request from the supporting fighter for a specific situational update from the engaged fighter. It is the primary tool for IFF at the merge. A generic STATUS call returns a general update. A specific STATUS call asks a targeted question and returns a more useful answer.\n\nThe supporting fighter should tailor the STATUS call to the specific information needed to resolve the IFF situation. Generic STATUS calls are acceptable but specific calls are faster and more effective.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! Call !! Question Being Asked !! Example Response\n|-\n| \'\'\'STATUS\'\'\' || General situation update || ENGAGED ONE CIRCLE NORTHBOUND BANDITS LOW TWO\'S HIGH\n|-\n| \'\'\'STATUS HIGH/LOW\'\'\' || Which aircraft is higher || BANDITS LOW TWO\'S HIGH\n|-\n| \'\'\'STATUS NOSE HIGH/NOSE LOW\'\'\' || Which aircraft has nose above or below horizon || TWO\'S NOSE HIGH BANDIT NOSE LOW\n|-\n| \'\'\'STATUS LEFT/RIGHT\'\'\' || Which aircraft is left or right || TWO\'S LEFT BANDIT RIGHT\n|-\n| \'\'\'STATUS NORTH/SOUTH/EAST/WEST\'\'\' || Cardinal orientation of aircraft in the fight || TWO\'S NORTH BANDIT SOUTH\n|}\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Supporting Fighter || {CS} STATUS {Qualifier if applicable}\n|-\n| Engaged Fighter || {Specific SA answer to the STATUS question}\n|}\n\n  Supporting Fighter: TWO STATUS HIGH LOW.\n  Engaged Fighter: BANDITS LOW TWO\'S HIGH.\n\nIf a STATUS call returns an ambiguous response, rephrase with a more specific qualifier before resorting to a FLASH call.\n\nAs a last resort, a FLASH call can be used to add physical confirmation to IFF — for example FLASH FLARES or FLASH BURNER. This directs the wingman to briefly activate the specified system so the supporting fighter can visually confirm identity. Exercise extreme caution — the bandit may also be employing flares, and a mistaken identification cannot be undone.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Supporting Fighter || FLASH {FLARES / BURNER}\n|-\n| Wingman || \'\'(executes flash of specified system briefly)\'\'\n|}\n\n=== IFF Brevity ===\nStandard brevity calls for visual identification status:\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! Term !! Meaning\n|-\n| \'\'\'TALLY\'\'\' || Visual contact established on the target or bandit\n|-\n| \'\'\'NO JOY\'\'\' || No visual contact with the target or bandit\n|-\n| \'\'\'VISUAL\'\'\' || Visual contact established on a friendly aircraft\n|-\n| \'\'\'BLIND\'\'\' || No visual contact with a friendly aircraft\n|}\n\nIFF must be confirmed before employment. Do not rely on aircraft visuals alone — use STATUS calls to confirm orientation before taking a shot.\n\n=== Talk-On ===\nIf the supporting fighter loses visual on the engaged fighter (BLIND) after the merge, the engaged fighter or flight lead can provide a talk-on to re-establish visual.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Engaged Fighter or FL || {CS} {Clock Position} {Range} {Altitude Qualifier}\n|}\n\n  VIPER 1, THREE O\'CLOCK ONE AND A HALF MILES LOW.\n\nOnce visual is re-established:\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Supporting Fighter || {CS} VISUAL\n|}\n\n=== Employment ===\nOnce IFF is confirmed and a valid shot opportunity exists, the supporting fighter employs. The call includes weapon type and target description.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Supporting or Engaged Fighter || {Controller-CS} {CS} FOX TWO {Target Description}\n|-\n| Controller || COPY SHOT\n|}\n\nGun employment:\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Supporting or Engaged Fighter || {Controller-CS} {CS} GUNS {Target Description}\n|}\n\nThe flight lead retains authority to call NEGATIVE if employment is unsafe or premature, regardless of who holds radio priority.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Flight Lead || NEGATIVE {CS} {Reason if required}\n|}\n\n=== SPLASH ===\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Engaged or Supporting Fighter || {Controller-CS} {CS} SPLASH {Target Description}\n|-\n| Controller || {CS} COPY SPLASH\n|}\n\n=== Bug Out ===\nFollowing SPLASH, or when the flight lead determines the engagement should end, the supporting fighter directs the egress. The supporting fighter holds the best big-picture SA at this point and is best placed to select a safe egress direction.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Supporting Fighter || {CS} BUG OUT {Direction}\n|-\n| colspan=\"2\" style=\"text-align:center;\" | \'\'All flight members flow in the stated direction\'\'\n|}\n\n  VIPER\'S BUG OUT 0-9-0.\n\nIf the flight lead was the supporting fighter and the wingman is now visual after the bug out, conventional flight roles are re-established:\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Wingman || {CS} VISUAL PRESS\n|-\n| Flight Lead || ENGAGED\n|}\n\nFollowing the bug out the flight calls RESET on the TAC C2 NET and returns to the prebriefed position unless directed otherwise.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Flight Lead || {Controller-CS} {CS} RESET\n|}','utf-8'),
(607,'== 01 / Overview ==\nAir combat is primarily carried out by CAP aircraft. The two main mission types are \'\'\'DCA\'\'\' (Defensive Counter Air) and \'\'\'OCA\'\'\' (Offensive Counter Air).\n\n=== Groups and Contacts ===\nA \'\'\'GROUP\'\'\' is any number of air contacts within 3 nm in azimuth and range of each other. A \'\'\'CONTACT\'\'\' is an individual radar return within a GROUP. Single contact is the assumed strength for all GROUPs unless otherwise specified. A GROUP with three or more contacts is called \'\'\'HEAVY\'\'\'.\n\nThe \'\'\'PICTURE\'\'\' establishes a common tactical air image and describes the spatial relationship of GROUPs. It is always communicated in BULLSEYE format unless briefed otherwise. Only the Three GROUPs with highest Priority are Conveyed\n\n=== Contact Declaration ===\nBefore a fighter can engage, a contact must be declared. Declaration is performed by the controller in response to a DECLARE request.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! Declaration !! Meaning !! Engagement Authority\n|-\n| \'\'\'BOGEY\'\'\' || Identity unknown || Not cleared to engage\n|-\n| \'\'\'OUTLAW\'\'\' || Originates from known or suspected hostile area || Not cleared to engage; escalation criteria apply\n|-\n| \'\'\'SPADES\'\'\' || No valid IFF response on interrogation || Not cleared to engage; escalation criteria apply\n|-\n| \'\'\'BANDIT\'\'\' || Positively identified as enemy IAW theater ID criteria || Not cleared to engage; does not imply authority to fire\n|-\n| \'\'\'HOSTILE\'\'\' || Confirmed enemy; clearance to fire authorized IAW ROE || Cleared to engage\n|-\n| \'\'\'NEUTRAL\'\'\' || Positively identified as neutral || Not cleared to engage\n|-\n| \'\'\'FRIENDLY\'\'\' || Positively identified as friendly || Not cleared to engage\n|-\n| \'\'\'FURBALL\'\'\' || Friendly and non-friendly aircraft within 5 nm || Employment not authorized until resolved\n|}\n\nA BOGEY, BANDIT, OUTLAW, or SPADES classification does not authorize engagement. HOSTILE is the only declaration that authorizes weapons employment IAW ROE.\n\n== 02 / Important Concepts ==\n\n=== Communication Cadence by Phase ===\nCommunication priority shifts between the controller and fighters depending on the phase of the intercept.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! Phase !! Priority Communicators\n|-\n| Pre-COMMIT / Marshal || 1. Controller · 2. Fighters\n|-\n| Post-COMMIT / Push || 1. Controller · 2. Fighter\n|-\n| Targeting / Weapons Employment || 1. Fighters · 2. Controller\n|-\n| Merge || 1. Engaged Fighter · 2. Supporting Fighters · 3. Controller\n|-\n| Post-Merge || 1. Fighter Clearing Merge · 2. Supporting Fighters · 3. Controller\n|}\n\n=== TAC Range ===\nCalled at 60 nm (default) by the first asset to recognize the separation. Signals all players to prepare for targeting. Pre-commit SA building continues until this point.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Controller or Fighter || {CS} {Group Label} TAC RANGE\n|}\n\n=== Airspace Sanitization ===\nA Section consists of two aircraft, each equipped with its own radar. Regardless of the number of bars or maximum azimuth a single radar supports, no single system can cover the entire sky. To compensate, both radars are stacked and assigned to different portions of the airspace — each portion is called an \'\'\'Area of Responsibility (AOR)\'\'\'. This technique, known as \'\'\'Radar Mating\'\'\', vastly increases the total volume scanned and ensures that no unknown contacts are operating in the area beyond already-tracked groups. The exact parameters depend on the aircraft type: the US Navy recommends RWS, 140°, 6 bars, end scale 80 nm, with radars mated at 25 nm: placing the flight lead between 40,000 and 15,000 ft, and the wingman from ground level to 25,000 ft.\n\nThe process of actively scanning assigned AORs to confirm no unknown contacts are present is called \'\'\'Sanitisation\'\'\'.\n\nBecause both Radar Mating parameters and Sanitisation responsibilities directly affect how an engagement develops, all of this must be \'\'\'pre-briefed\'\'\' and set as a \'\'\'contract\'\'\' before the mission. Time pressure may not always permit a thorough sanitisation pass, and how to handle that contingency should likewise be agreed upon in the brief.\n\n=== Targeting ===\nThe flight assigns GROUP responsibility and confirms classification.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| FL || {Controller-CS} {CS} DECLARE {Group Label or Bullseye Position}\n|-\n| Controller || {Group Label} DECLARED {HOSTILE / BOGEY / FRIENDLY / UNABLE / FURBALL}\n|-\n| FL || {CS} TARGET {Group Label} {Tactic}\n|-\n| colspan=\"2\" style=\"text-align:center;\" | \'\'Once group appears on radar\'\'\n|-\n| FL || {CS} TARGETED {Group Label} BULLSEYE {Bearing/Range}\n|-\n| Controller || \'\'No response if information correlates. Comparative response if correction is required.\'\'\n|}\n\n== 03 / Common Calls ==\n\n=== DECLARE ===\nInquiry as to the identity of a specified GROUP. Full positional data in BULLSEYE format must accompany all responses.\n  MIKE, EAGLE 11, DECLARE NORTH GROUP.\n  EAGLE 11, NORTH GROUP BULLSEYE 2-7-3/27, TWENTY-TWO THOUSAND, TRACK EAST, BOGEY SPADES.\n\n=== BOGEY DOPE ===\nRequest for BRAA information on the indicated GROUP or the closest GROUP if not specified. Does not imply fighter targeting.\n  MIKE, EAGLE 11, BOGEY DOPE NORTH GROUP.\n  EAGLE 11, NORTH GROUP BRAA 2-1-0/28, THIRTY-EIGHT THOUSAND, HOT, HOSTILE.\n\n=== NAILS ===\nFighter\'s radar warning receiver is detecting an airborne interceptor radar in search mode. Called with bearing or cardinal direction.\n  EAGLE 1, NAILS 2-7-0.\n\n=== SPIKED ===\nFighter is being actively tracked by an airborne radar. Called with bearing or cardinal direction. Controller correlates to a GROUP within ±30 degrees and responds with range, altitude, aspect, declaration, and GROUP name.\n  EAGLE 1, SPIKED 2-7-0.\n  EAGLE 1, SPIKE RANGE 17, THIRTY THOUSAND, HOSTILE EAST GROUP, TWO CONTACTS.\n\n=== THREAT Call ===\nIf a fighter will pierce THREAT range to an untargeted GROUP, the controller makes a THREAT call in BRAA format no later than THREAT range.\n  HEAT 31, ADDITIONAL GROUP THREAT, BRAA 1-2-5/35, THIRTY-FIVE THOUSAND, HOT, HOSTILE.\n\nIf the THREAT is within 5 nm of friendly fighters:\n  BOLT 1, THREAT NORTH THREE, TEN THOUSAND, TRACK SOUTH, HOSTILE.\n\n=== FADED and VANISHED ===\n\'\'\'FADED\'\'\' — a previously tracked GROUP not updated by sensors for 30 seconds. The controller reports the GROUP as FADED with last known BULLSEYE position. Fighters maintain MONITOR responsibility if they can maintain correlation. FADED is not used for friendly aircraft — controllers use NEGATIVE CONTACT for friendlies not held on sensors.\n\n\'\'\'VANISHED\'\'\' — a special case of FADED used when the GROUP is not in a known blind zone and is correlated to a friendly shot that meets parameters for successful termination. \n\n=== Unexpected Contacts ===\nThree distinct call types exist for contacts not part of the established picture:\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! Call !! Criteria !! Format\n|-\n| \'\'\'ADDITIONAL GROUP\'\'\' || Newly detected outside targeting range, or does not fit traditional label || BULLSEYE\n|-\n| \'\'\'POP-UP GROUP\'\'\' || Previously undetected; appears inside targeting range but outside THREAT range || BULLSEYE\n|-\n| \'\'\'THREAT GROUP\'\'\' || Undetected or unreported GROUP meeting briefed THREAT criteria || BRAA to the closest aircraft\n|}\n\n  DARKSTAR, POP-UP GROUP BULLSEYE 2-7-0/15, FIVE THOUSAND, TRACK WEST, BOGEY SPADES.\n  RAMBO 2, THREAT GROUP BRAA 2-7-0/13, ONE THOUSAND, HOT, HOSTILE.\n\n== 04 / Engagement ==\nThe engagement loop is the core sequence executed by all fighter flights once committed and targeting is established. It repeats until the GROUP is splashed, leaves the area, or the flight resets.\n\n=== MELD ===\nThe flight lead directs all flight members to shift their radars from wide sanitization to focused SA on the assigned GROUP. This transition narrows the radar from maximum azimuth and altitude coverage to a tighter volume centered on the target. Sanitization of the surrounding airspace ceases until DROPPING is called.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| FL || {Controller-CS} {CS} MELD\n|-\n| colspan=\"2\" style=\"text-align:center;\" | \'\'All flight members shift radar from sanitization to the assigned GROUP\'\'\n|}\n\n=== SORT ===\nThe flight lead assigns individual contacts within the GROUP to specific flight members. Each member acknowledges with SORTED when they have confirmed their assigned contact on radar and are prepared to engage it. If a member cannot confirm their assigned contact, SORTED is withheld until resolution.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| FL || \'\'(intraflight) FLIGHT SORT {Criteria}\'\'\n|-\n| #2 || \'\'(intraflight) #2 SORTED\'\'\n|-\n| #3 || \'\'(intraflight) #3 SORTED\'\'\n|-\n| #4 || \'\'(intraflight) #4 SORTED\'\'\n|}\n\n=== JUDY ===\nCalled by the flight lead when the flight no longer requires controller guidance to prosecute the intercept. From this point, the controller minimizes transmissions and provides situational awareness information only. JUDY is not always called.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| FL || {Controller-CS} {CS} JUDY\n|-\n| Controller || \'\'Minimizes transmissions. Provides SA and THREAT calls only.\'\'\n|}\n\n=== ENGAGE ===\nDirective call from the flight lead to a specific wingman to employ weapons. The flight lead may also direct simultaneous employment by multiple members.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| FL || {CS} ENGAGE\n|-\n| Wingman || {CS}\n|}\n\n=== Weapon Employment ===\nThe firing aircraft calls the shot on TACC2 NET immediately after employment. The call includes shot type, GROUP label, contact description, BULLSEYE position, and altitude. The controller acknowledges with COPY SHOT.\n\nIf a fighter determines a GROUP HOSTILE and ROE has been met, the fighter may employ autonomously and include HOSTILE in the shot transmission.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Firing Aircraft || {TACC2-CS} {CS} FOX THREE {Group Label} {Contact Description} BULLSEYE {Bearing/Range} {Altitude}\n|-\n| TACC2 || {TACC2-CS} COPY SHOT\n|-\n| colspan=\"2\" style=\"text-align:center;\" | \'\'Autonomous HOSTILE employment\'\'\n|-\n| Firing Aircraft || {Controller-CS} {CS} FOX THREE {Group Label} BULLSEYE {Bearing/Range} {Altitude} HOSTILE\n|-\n| TACC2 || {TACC2-CS} COPY SHOT\n|}\n\nMultiple shots in one transmission are acceptable:\n  SHADOW12 FOX THREE NORTH GROUP LEAD CONTACT BULLSEYE 2-7-0/20 TWENTY THOUSAND; SHADOW11 FOX THREE NORTH GROUP TRAIL CONTACT.\n\n=== OUT / DEFENDING ===\nFollowing weapon employment the flight turns away from the threat. OUT is a planned maneuver executed at a prebriefed range. DEFENDING indicates a reactive maneuver against an incoming weapon and is called with the cardinal direction of the defensive turn.\n\nOUT cancels targeting responsibility\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| FL || {Controller-CS} {CS} OUT {Direction}\n|-\n| colspan=\"2\" style=\"text-align:center;\" | \'\'or, if reacting to an incoming weapon\'\'\n|-\n| Aircraft || {CS} DEFENDING {Direction}\n|}\n\n=== DROPPING ===\nMay be called in conjunction with OUT. Signals the controller that the flight has ceased monitoring the GROUP and is returning to sanitization radar settings. Monitoring responsibility transfers back to the controller. Targeting responsibility is cancelled \n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| FL || {Controller-CS} {CS} OUT {Direction} DROPPING {Group Label}\n|-\n| colspan=\"2\" style=\"text-align:center;\" | \'\'Controller resumes monitoring of the dropped GROUP\'\'\n|-\n| Controller || \'\'Resumes monitoring.\'\'\n|}\n\n=== Assessment ===\nThe flight assesses weapon effects from sensor data or controller reports. Three outcomes are possible:\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! Outcome !! Action\n|-\n| GROUP splashed || Call SPLASH\n|-\n| GROUP remains a factor, follow-up shot required || Turn IN, return to MELD\n|-\n| GROUP has left the area or is no longer a factor || Proceed with Tasking\n|}\n\n=== IN (Follow-up Engagement) ===\nIf the GROUP remains a factor and the tactic permits a follow-up shot, the flight turns back toward the GROUP. The IN call triggers a new cycle beginning at MELD.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| FL || {Controller-CS} {CS} IN {Direction}\n|-\n| FL || {Controller-CS} {CS} MELD\n|-\n| colspan=\"2\" style=\"text-align:center;\" | \'\'Re-sort as required, then return to ENGAGE\'\'\n|}\n\n=== SPLASH ===\nCalled when weapon effects confirm the GROUP or contact is destroyed.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Firing Aircraft or Controller || {CS} SPLASH {Group Label or Contact Description}\n|}\n\n----\n\n== 05 / Defensive Counter Air (DCA) ==\nDCA is the use of fighter aircraft to defend a ground asset or airspace against hostile air threats. It uses prebriefed lanes and defined ranges.\n\n=== DCA Range Structure ===\nAll ranges are prebriefed per sortie. The following are the standard range lines used in DCA:\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! Range Line !! Definition\n|-\n| \'\'\'Mission Fail Line\'\'\' || The line the enemy must not cross for the mission to succeed. Defines the maximum depth of the defensive problem.\n|-\n| \'\'\'Commit Line (CL)\'\'\' || The line at which a bogey is declared HOSTILE IAW theater ROE and the flight is authorized to commit. Prebriefed for all DCA sorties.\n|-\n| \'\'\'Targeting Range\'\'\' || The prebriefed distance at which all contacts must be intercepted. A GROUP inside this range with no assigned fighter is UNTARGETED. Prebriefed per sortie.\n|-\n| \'\'\'TAC Range\'\'\' || Default 60 nm from the closest fighter to the closest GROUP. Signals all players to prepare for targeting.\n|-\n| \'\'\'Threat Range\'\'\' || The prebriefed range at which an untargeted GROUP meeting THREAT criteria triggers a THREAT call. Prebriefed per sortie.\n|}\n\n=== COMMIT Authority ===\nCOMMIT authority is derived from the combatant commander and delegated through the chain of command. Any of the following may hold or recommend COMMIT authority depending on what is delegated:\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! Role !! Abbreviation !! Authority\n|-\n| Flight Lead || FL || Usually holds COMMIT authority unless delegated\n|-\n| Team Lead || TL || May hold COMMIT authority if delegated\n|-\n| Mission Commander || MC || May hold COMMIT authority if delegated\n|-\n| TACC2 || — || May hold COMMIT authority if delegated\n|}\n\nIf only the MC, TL, or FL holds COMMIT authority, the controller may only recommend COMMIT. If the controller holds COMMIT authority, the MC and TL may only recommend.\n\n=== DCA Flow ===\n\n==== Lane Handover ====\nWhen an outgoing flight is relieved on station by an incoming flight, a positive lane handover is required before the outgoing flight departs.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Off-going Lane Commander || {CS} MOVE FORWARD\n|-\n| On-coming Lane Commander || {CS}\n|-\n| colspan=\"2\" style=\"text-align:center;\" | \'\'On-coming aircraft may enter the lane. Lane commander authority has not yet transferred.\'\'\n|-\n| Off-going Lane Commander || {CS} YOU HAVE THE LANE\n|-\n| On-coming Lane Commander || {CS} HAS THE LANE\n|}\n\nThe off-going flight passes critical mission information to the controller before departing. The controller passes big-picture information to the on-coming flight before pushing them to the tactical frequency.\n\n==== Pre-Commit ====\nThe flight arrives at its prebriefed CAP position with FENCE IN complete and radars in sanitization configuration. The controller holds picture responsibility.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| FL || {Controller-CS} {CS} PICTURE\n|-\n| Controller || {CS} {Nr of Groups} {Group Label} BULLSEYE {Bearing/Range} {Altitude} TRACK {Direction} {Declaration} {Nr of Contacts}\n|}\n\nThe flight evaluates commit criteria continuously. Pictures are requested as needed to maintain SA.\n\n==== Commit ====\n\n===== FIGHTER AUTHORITY COMMIT =====\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| TACC2 || {TACC2-CS} {BULLSEYE CALL} Recommend {CS} COMMIT \n|-\n| FL || {CS} COMMIT\n|}\n\n===== CONTROLLER AUTHORITY COMMIT =====\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| FL || {CS} Recommend Commit\n|-\n| TACC2 || {TACC2-CS} {BULLSEYE CALL} {CS} COMMIT \n|-\n|FL\n|{CS}\n|}\n\nAfter COMMIT, fighters hold radio priority.\n\n=== Engagement Loop ===\nThe flight executes the standard engagement loop as defined above The loop repeats until the GROUP is splashed, leaves the lane, or:\n\nOnce the Engagement is Complete the flight \'\'\'RESETS\'\'\' back to the briefed Position\n\n==== RESET ====\nDirects the flight to return to its prebriefed position or area of operations. RESET implies DROPPING and returns all targeting responsibility to the controller.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| FL || {Controller-CS} {CS} RESET\n|-\n| colspan=\"2\" style=\"text-align:center;\" | \'\'Flight returns to prebriefed CAP position. Controller resumes full picture responsibility\'\'\n|}\n\n----\n\n== 06 / Offensive Counter Air (OCA) ==\nOCA is the use of fighter aircraft to suppress, destroy, or neutralize enemy air assets before they can threaten friendly forces. Unlike DCA, OCA is not lane-based and does not require a commit line. The flight pushes into the threat environment rather than waiting for the threat to approach.\n\n=== OCA Range Structure ===\nThe same range lines apply as in DCA — Threat Range, Targeting Range, and TAC Range — but all are prebriefed based on the specific mission geometry and threat environment. There is no commit line or mission fail line in OCA.\n\n=== OCA Flow ===\nOCA does not have a lane handover or a commit phase. The flight checks in, receives the picture, establishes targeting, and executes the engagement loop.\n\n==== FULL COMMUNICATIONS EXAMPLE ====\nIn this Scenario, SHADOW11 four-ship F16C on OCA. TACC2 Agency CS THUMPER executing Positive Control. The Example begins after the Handover. Check In was done on the previous Frequency. TAC RANGE 60, THREAT RANGE 30  \n THUMPER SHADOW11 PICTURE\n\n THUMPER PICTURE TEN GROUPS GROUP BULLSEYE 0-2-0/25, TWENTY-SEVEN THOUSAND, THOUSAND, TRACK EAST, HOSTILE, HEAVY, THREE CONTACTS. GROUP BULLSEYE 2-7-0/25, FIFTEEN THOUSAND, TRACK WEST, HOSTILE. GROUP BULLSEYE 2-9-0/35, TEN THOUSAND, TRACK WEST, HOSTILE.\n\n SHADOW11 TARGETED GROUP BULLSEYE 0-2-0/25 \n\n SHADOW11 GROUP BULLSEYE 0-2-0/25 TAC RANGE\n\n SHADOW11 SKATE, MELD\n\n SHADOW11 SORTED\n\n SHADOW11 FOX3 BULLSEYE 0-2-0/25\n\n THUMPER COPY SHOT\n\n SHADOW11 OUT SOUTH DROPPING\n\n THUMPER\n\n THUMPER SHADOW11 BOGEYDOPE\n\n SHADOW11 EAST GROUP BRAA 2-1-0/28, FIFTEEN-THOUSAND, COLD, HOSTILE\n\n SHADOW11 IN, MELD\n\n SHADOW11 SORTED\n\n THUMPER SHADOW11 SPLASH EAST GROUP\n\n THUMPER\nIn the Following Example additional Adversarys Pop Up within Threat Range of SHADOW11\n SHADOW11 THUMPER THREAT GROUP, BRAA 1-2-5/28, FIVE-THOUSAND, HOT, HOSTILE, 2 CONTACTS\n\n SHADOW11 TARGETED SORTED LEAD, FOX 3 LEADER, FOX3 TRAILER, BULLSEYE 0-5-0/45, FIVE-THOUSAND\n\n THUMPER COPY SHOTS \n\n SHADOW11 DEFENDING SOUTH, DROPPING\n\n THUMPER\n\n SHADOW11 THUMPER THREAT GROUP, BRAA 0-6-0/15, TWO-THOUSAND, COLD, HOSTILE\n\n SHADOW11\n\n SHADOW11 IN \n\n SHADOW11 FOX3 BULLSEYE 0-6-0/15, TWO-THOUSAND\n\n THUMPER COPY SHOT\n\n SHADOW11 THUMPER THREAT GROUP VANISHED\n----\n\n== 07 / QRA / INTERCEPT / ID ==\n[[MTTP/A2A/INTERCEPT|QRA / INTERCEPT / ID]]\n\n== 07 / Merge ==\nA merge occurs when friendly and target aircraft arrive in the visual arena, within 3–5 nm of each other. At the merge, radar resolution becomes limiting and the engagement transitions from BVR to Within Visual Range (WVR). The engagement is now managed through ACM contracts and direct communication between flight members.\n\n=== Authority and Radio Priority ===\nTwo distinct concepts govern merge communications: \'\'\'authority\'\'\' and \'\'\'radio priority\'\'\'.\n\n\'\'\'Authority\'\'\' remains with the Flight Lead throughout the engagement. The FL can override, redirect, or terminate the engagement at any time regardless of who is engaged.\n\n\'\'\'Radio priority\'\'\' belongs to the engaged fighter. Other flight members subordinate their transmissions to avoid stepping on critical engaged fighter calls. The controller minimizes transmissions entirely and provides only THREAT calls, SA updates, and responses to direct requests.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! Role !! Authority !! Radio Priority\n|-\n| \'\'\'Flight Lead\'\'\' || Full authority over the engagement at all times || Secondary — subordinates to engaged fighter\n|-\n| \'\'\'Engaged Fighter\'\'\' || Manages own engagement IAW FL direction || Primary — holds radio priority\n|-\n| \'\'\'Supporting Fighter\'\'\' || Executes IAW engaged fighter and FL direction || Tertiary — transmits only when necessary\n|-\n| \'\'\'Controller\'\'\' || Provides SA only || Minimal — THREAT calls and direct requests only\n|}\n\n=== MERGED ===\nThe controller calls MERGED when radar tracks come together within 3–5 nm, or the engaged fighter calls it when visual contact is made at the merge.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Controller or Engaged Fighter || {CS} MERGED {Group Label}\n|}\n\n=== Role Assignment ===\nAt or before the merge, engaged and supporting roles must be clearly established. By default the flight lead is engaged. Roles can be assigned or transferred in two ways:\n\n==== Wingman-Initiated Transfer ====\nThe wingman identifies he is the engaged fighter based on the bandit\'s plane of motion and lift vector orientation, and calls it.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Wingman || #2 ENGAGED\n|-\n| Flight Lead || PRESS\n|}\n\n==== Flight Lead-Initiated Transfer ====\nThe flight lead recognizes the wingman is the engaged fighter and assigns the role.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Flight Lead || #2 PRESS\n|-\n| Wingman || #2 ENGAGED\n|}\n\n==== Bandit Switches ====\nIf the bandit maneuvers to engage the other fighter, the currently engaged fighter communicates the change.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Engaged Fighter || BANDIT SWITCHED\n|-\n| colspan=\"2\" style=\"text-align:center;\" | \'\'Roles are reassessed and reassigned as required\'\'\n|}\n\n=== Engaged Fighter SA Calls ===\nThe engaged fighter is responsible for narrating the fight to maintain the supporting fighter\'s situational awareness. This enables the supporting fighter to position correctly and employ at the right moment. SA calls include the fight geometry, altitude, and direction.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Engaged Fighter || {CS} ENGAGED {ONE / TWO CIRCLE} {Direction} {Altitude}\n|}\n\n  VIPER 1, ENGAGED ONE CIRCLE CLIMBING THROUGH FIFTEEN THOUSAND.\n  VIPER 1, ENGAGED TWO CIRCLE NORTHBOUND BANDITS LOW TWO\'S HIGH.\n\nThese calls are made continuously as the fight develops, not just once at the merge. The supporting fighter cannot position or employ safely without them.\n\n=== STATUS Calls ===\nSTATUS is a request from the supporting fighter for a specific situational update from the engaged fighter. It is the primary tool for IFF at the merge. A generic STATUS call returns a general update. A specific STATUS call asks a targeted question and returns a more useful answer.\n\nThe supporting fighter should tailor the STATUS call to the specific information needed to resolve the IFF situation. Generic STATUS calls are acceptable but specific calls are faster and more effective.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! Call !! Question Being Asked !! Example Response\n|-\n| \'\'\'STATUS\'\'\' || General situation update || ENGAGED ONE CIRCLE NORTHBOUND BANDITS LOW TWO\'S HIGH\n|-\n| \'\'\'STATUS HIGH/LOW\'\'\' || Which aircraft is higher || BANDITS LOW TWO\'S HIGH\n|-\n| \'\'\'STATUS NOSE HIGH/NOSE LOW\'\'\' || Which aircraft has nose above or below horizon || TWO\'S NOSE HIGH BANDIT NOSE LOW\n|-\n| \'\'\'STATUS LEFT/RIGHT\'\'\' || Which aircraft is left or right || TWO\'S LEFT BANDIT RIGHT\n|-\n| \'\'\'STATUS NORTH/SOUTH/EAST/WEST\'\'\' || Cardinal orientation of aircraft in the fight || TWO\'S NORTH BANDIT SOUTH\n|}\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Supporting Fighter || {CS} STATUS {Qualifier if applicable}\n|-\n| Engaged Fighter || {Specific SA answer to the STATUS question}\n|}\n\n  Supporting Fighter: TWO STATUS HIGH LOW.\n  Engaged Fighter: BANDITS LOW TWO\'S HIGH.\n\nIf a STATUS call returns an ambiguous response, rephrase with a more specific qualifier before resorting to a FLASH call.\n\nAs a last resort, a FLASH call can be used to add physical confirmation to IFF — for example FLASH FLARES or FLASH BURNER. This directs the wingman to briefly activate the specified system so the supporting fighter can visually confirm identity. Exercise extreme caution — the bandit may also be employing flares, and a mistaken identification cannot be undone.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Supporting Fighter || FLASH {FLARES / BURNER}\n|-\n| Wingman || \'\'(executes flash of specified system briefly)\'\'\n|}\n\n=== IFF Brevity ===\nStandard brevity calls for visual identification status:\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! Term !! Meaning\n|-\n| \'\'\'TALLY\'\'\' || Visual contact established on the target or bandit\n|-\n| \'\'\'NO JOY\'\'\' || No visual contact with the target or bandit\n|-\n| \'\'\'VISUAL\'\'\' || Visual contact established on a friendly aircraft\n|-\n| \'\'\'BLIND\'\'\' || No visual contact with a friendly aircraft\n|}\n\nIFF must be confirmed before employment. Do not rely on aircraft visuals alone — use STATUS calls to confirm orientation before taking a shot.\n\n=== Talk-On ===\nIf the supporting fighter loses visual on the engaged fighter (BLIND) after the merge, the engaged fighter or flight lead can provide a talk-on to re-establish visual.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Engaged Fighter or FL || {CS} {Clock Position} {Range} {Altitude Qualifier}\n|}\n\n  VIPER 1, THREE O\'CLOCK ONE AND A HALF MILES LOW.\n\nOnce visual is re-established:\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Supporting Fighter || {CS} VISUAL\n|}\n\n=== Employment ===\nOnce IFF is confirmed and a valid shot opportunity exists, the supporting fighter employs. The call includes weapon type and target description.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Supporting or Engaged Fighter || {Controller-CS} {CS} FOX TWO {Target Description}\n|-\n| Controller || COPY SHOT\n|}\n\nGun employment:\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Supporting or Engaged Fighter || {Controller-CS} {CS} GUNS {Target Description}\n|}\n\nThe flight lead retains authority to call NEGATIVE if employment is unsafe or premature, regardless of who holds radio priority.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Flight Lead || NEGATIVE {CS} {Reason if required}\n|}\n\n=== SPLASH ===\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Engaged or Supporting Fighter || {Controller-CS} {CS} SPLASH {Target Description}\n|-\n| Controller || {CS} COPY SPLASH\n|}\n\n=== Bug Out ===\nFollowing SPLASH, or when the flight lead determines the engagement should end, the supporting fighter directs the egress. The supporting fighter holds the best big-picture SA at this point and is best placed to select a safe egress direction.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Supporting Fighter || {CS} BUG OUT {Direction}\n|-\n| colspan=\"2\" style=\"text-align:center;\" | \'\'All flight members flow in the stated direction\'\'\n|}\n\n  VIPER\'S BUG OUT 0-9-0.\n\nIf the flight lead was the supporting fighter and the wingman is now visual after the bug out, conventional flight roles are re-established:\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Wingman || {CS} VISUAL PRESS\n|-\n| Flight Lead || ENGAGED\n|}\n\nFollowing the bug out the flight calls RESET on the TAC C2 NET and returns to the prebriefed position unless directed otherwise.\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Flight Lead || {Controller-CS} {CS} RESET\n|}','utf-8'),
(608,'\n== 07 / Quick Reaction Alert (QRA) — Overview ==\nQuick Reaction Alert (QRA), termed \"Airspace Control Alert\" in US usage, is the standing peacetime posture NATO air forces maintain to protect sovereign and Allied airspace. Armed fighters and crews sit at high readiness so they can launch within minutes to identify, shadow, and if necessary escort or engage an aircraft that has entered, or is approaching, protected airspace without authorisation or without maintaining required communications.\n\n=== Purpose ===\nQRA(I) — the Interceptor role of QRA — is one of the peacetime activities carried out under NATO Integrated Air and Missile Defence (NIAMD), alongside the Air Surveillance and Control System (ASACS) and the Air Command and Control (Air C2) structure. Typical triggers for a scramble include: an aircraft entering national/Allied airspace without clearance, loss of radio contact with a civil aircraft, an aircraft deviating from its filed flight plan without notification, or an unidentified/unresponsive military contact approaching the border of Allied airspace.\n\n=== Force Structure ===\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n! style=\"width:30%;\" |Element\n!Role\n|-\n|ASACS / CRC\n|Ground- and air-based sensors build and maintain the Recognised Air Picture (RAP); a Control and Reporting Centre (CRC) tracks all air traffic within its assigned airspace 24/7/365.\n|-\n|Air C2 (CAOC)\n|A Combined Air Operations Centre (for NATO, Uedem or Torrejón) authorises and directs the scramble, tasks the QRA(I) flight, and applies the current Rules of Engagement (ROE). This is also the agency that takes tactical control of the interceptor once handover occurs — see Step 4, Handover, below.\n|-\n|QRA(I) Flight\n|Armed fighters (2-ship standard) held at cockpit- or runway-alert, ready to launch within a short, nationally-defined reaction time once tasked.\n|}\nThe remainder of this chapter follows a single QRA sortie in order: \'\'\'1. Alert & Scramble Order → 2. Group-Up & Departure → 3. ATC Guidance (Transit) → 4. Handover → 5. Intercept & Tasking → 6. Recovery.\'\'\'\n----\n\n== 07.1 / Alert & Scramble Order ==\nReaction timelines and exact alert postures are nationally classified; publicly reported figures (e.g. UK QRA crews reported at readiness to be airborne within about 15 minutes) can be used as an unclassified planning reference for training purposes, but should not be presented as a fixed NATO standard.\n\n=== Scramble Call ===\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n! style=\"width:25%;\" |Station\n!Phrase\n|-\n|CAOC/Controller\n|{CS} SCRAMBLE SCRAMBLE SCRAMBLE, {Group Label} BULLSEYE {Bearing/Range} {Altitude} {Declaration}\n|-\n|FL\n|{CS} SCRAMBLE, copied\n|-\n|FL\n|{Controller-CS} {CS} AIRBORNE\n|-\n|Controller\n|{CS} roger airborne, {further vectors / handoff to intercept controller}\n|}\n\n=== Scramble Order (filed by CAOC/ADCF) ===\n\'\'Sourced from the publicly released FAA Order 7610.4J model — see Sources.\'\' Behind the scramble call above, the controlling agency (CAOC in the NATO structure; ADCF in the US/NORAD model this section is based on) identifies the mission to civil ATC as an active air-defence mission and files a short flight plan containing:\n\n* Call sign and aircraft type/equipment\n* Number of aircraft\n* Departure point\n* Proposed departure time — or simply the word \'\'\'\"immediate\"\'\'\'\n* Heading, altitude, and duration of flight\n* Remarks: frequency, type of climb, controlling agency, and desired transfer point\n\n----\n\n== 07.2 / Group-Up & Departure ==\n\'\'Placeholder — squadron-specific formation join-up and departure check-in brevity (post-AIRBORNE-call sequencing) not yet documented here.\'\'\n----\n\n== 07.3 / ATC Guidance (Transit) ==\n\'\'The following describes how a scramble is coordinated with civil air traffic control between launch and handover. This section is modelled on the publicly released US FAA/NORAD procedure (FAA Order 7610.4J, \"Special Military Operations,\" 1998) as an unclassified, structurally representative reference — the equivalent German/Luftwaffe–DFS coordination procedure is not publicly published, but the underlying logic is broadly representative of how any NATO nation integrates a QRA launch into controlled civil airspace.\'\'\n\nOnce airborne, an interceptor does not go straight to a dedicated military-only frequency. It is worked by ordinary civil Air Traffic Control (ATC) like any other IFR flight, with priority/expedited handling, until tactical control is formally handed to the military radar unit directing the intercept — in the US model, the \'\'\'Air Defence Control Facility (ADCF)\'\'\', a military radar unit (Air Defence Sector / AWACS) that is the only type of unit authorised to direct interceptors. The CRC/CAOC in the NATO force structure above fills the equivalent role.\n\n=== Airborne Orders (filed once the flight is up) ===\nAs early as possible after launch, the controlling agency separately files \"airborne orders\" with the relevant en-route/terminal ATC facilities, containing:\n\n* Call sign, aircraft type/number\n* Departure point and time\n* Heading and altitude\n* Route of flight, broken into three legs: departure route → route to a fix serving the operating/tasked area → route from that fix to the intercept/destination\n* Frequency, and MARSA (Military Authority Assumes Responsibility for Separation) status if applicable\n\n=== Standing Authorisation & Squawk ===\nFor missions that cannot be conducted within normal ATC procedures, a standing pre-authorisation (in the US model, the FAA Authorization for Interceptor Operations, AFIO) allows active air-defence missions to proceed outside the normal clearance process. Aircraft operating under this authorisation squawk a dedicated identification code (Mode 3/A \'\'\'7777\'\'\' in the US model) so ATC and the controlling agency can positively track them even while outside the standard clearance framework.\n\n=== Beyond Controlled Airspace: \"Due Regard\" ===\nWhen an intercept and its supporting aircraft operate in controlled airspace beyond national territorial limits and a normal ATC clearance is impractical, the operation shifts to the \"due regard\" concept (from DOD FLIP, used for operations over the high seas): the controlling agency itself assumes responsibility for separating its aircraft from all observed traffic, in place of ATC. This only applies under specific conditions — e.g. the aircraft is in visual meteorological conditions, is within surveillance/radio contact of a radar facility, or carries its own separation-capable radar — and separation becomes the aircraft commander\'s own responsibility once outside the controlling agency\'s radar coverage.\n----\n\n== 07.4 / Handover (Transfer of Control) ==\nThe handoff from civil ATC to the controlling agency (CRC/CAOC, ADCF in the US model) is governed by a small set of rules, not a fixed altitude or distance:\n\n* The interceptor is retained under civil ATC control for as long as possible. ATC does not initiate the handoff on its own.\n* The controlling agency must request the transfer; it is not allowed to request control until the aircraft actually reaches the point where control must be assumed to carry out the intercept — i.e. it cannot take the aircraft \"early, just in case.\"\n* When requested, ATC must expedite the transfer.\n* The transfer point (a time, fix, or altitude) is chosen so that no conflict remains with any other traffic still under the transferring facility\'s control.\n\nOnce handover is complete, the sequence transitions seamlessly into the intercept procedure — see Step 5, below.\n----\n\n== 07.5 / Intercept & Tasking ==\nFollowing handover, the intercept controller runs the flight through one of two intercept types depending on the tactical picture: an \'\'\'ID intercept\'\'\' (radar/visual identification with follow-on tasking) or a \'\'\'VID intercept\'\'\' (visual identification required before a HOSTILE/FRIENDLY declaration can be made).\n\n=== ID Intercept ===\nAn Identification (ID) intercept establishes the type, registration, colour, and armament of an unknown contact by radar and/or visual means before follow-on tasking (shadow, intervention, radio interrogation, obey-check, or friendly termination) is assigned.\n\n\'\'\'ID Result Format:\'\'\' \'\'\'Number of Acft, Type, Registration, Colour, Armament\'\'\'\n\n==== Task: ID ====\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n! style=\"width:25%;\" |Station\n!Phrase\n|-\n|Controller\n|{CS} confirm ready to copy ID(-result)\n|-\n|FL\n|{Controller-CS} ready\n|-\n|Controller\n|Bogey is {Number} {Colour} {Acft Type}, registration {REGISTRATION}, {Armament}, at {Altitude}, Heading {HDG}, speed {Speed}\n|-\n|FL\n|{Controller-CS} copied, ({ID result}) new task {Task}\n|-\n|Controller\n|{CS} roger task {Task}\n|}\n\'\'This concludes Task ID; the controller can then assign one of the following follow-on tasks: Shadow, Obey-Check, Radio Interrogation, Intervention, or Friendly/Terminate.\'\'\n\n==== Task: Shadow ====\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n! style=\"width:25%;\" |Station\n!Phrase\n|-\n|Controller\n|{CS} new task shadow\n|-\n|FL\n|{Controller-CS} roger task shadow\n|}\n\'\'Task Shadow means following the aircraft at a distance of approximately 1–2 NM without further closure.\'\'\n\n==== Task: Obey-Check ====\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n! style=\"width:25%;\" |Station\n!Phrase\n|-\n|Controller\n|{CS} new task obey-check, fly HDG {HDG}\n|-\n|FL\n|{Controller-CS} task obey-check HDG {HDG}\n|-\n|FL\n|{Controller-CS} bogey is playmate \'\'(or: aircraft is not obeying)\'\'\n|-\n|Controller\n|{CS} copied\n|}\n\'\'Obey-Check means the fighter rocks its wings and then flies a turn in the specified direction. While doing so, it observes the target and reports whether it also rocks its wings and follows (\"playmate\") or does not (\"aircraft is not obeying\").\'\'\n\n==== Task: Radio Interrogation ====\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n! style=\"width:25%;\" |Station\n!Phrase\n|-\n|Controller\n|{CS} new task radio interrogation (on simulated guard {Frequency})\n|-\n|FL\n|{Controller-CS} roger, task radio interrogation\n|-\n|FL (on guard)\n|{Colour} {Acft Type} {Position}, with registration {REGISTRATION} (squawking {Squawk}) this is the eurofighter on your left wing calling on (simulated) guard ({Frequency}) you have been intercepted by the german air force. For purpose of identification state: Registration, Callsign, aerodrome of departure and aerodrome of destination on (simulated) guard ({Frequency})\n|-\n|Intercepted Acft\n|(German Interceptor) this is {Callsign} registration {REG} on guard, departure is {ICAO}, destination {ICAO}\n|-\n|Controller\n|Roger, standby\n|-\n|Controller\n|{CS} did you copy (the answer) on guard?\n|-\n|FL\n|Affirmative / Negative\n|}\n\'\'If the controller did not copy the response on guard, the fighter relays the answer. After this, termination is possible, or further tasks may follow.\'\'\n\n==== Task: Intervention ====\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n! style=\"width:25%;\" |Station\n!Phrase\n|-\n|Controller\n|{CS} new task intervention to {ICAO}\n|-\n|FL\n|{Controller-CS} roger, intervention to {ICAO}\n|-\n| colspan=\"2\" |\'\'Further radio follows the Intercept Call, kneeboard side (see below)\'\'\n|}\n\'\'During Interrogation and Intervention, the fighter flies to the left of the target at cockpit height (the pilot in command usually sits on the left).\'\'\n\n\'\'\'Intercept Call (Kneeboard):\'\'\'\n\nINTERCEPTED AIRCRAFT this is german INTERCEPTOR callsign {Callsign}, you will be intervented to {Airport} \'\'\'FOLLOW, FOLLOW\'\'\'\n\n* What is your Callsign\n* Descent to altitude {ALT} ft, QNH {HPA}\n* What is your preferred approach speed?\n* What is your gear down speed?\n\nThis is your airport, after split up join right/left hand pattern of RWY {NR} at {Altitude} feet, QNH {HPA}; \'\'\'YOU LAND, YOU LAND\'\'\'\n\n\'\'\'Phrases used by the intercepted aircraft (ICAO standard responses):\'\'\'\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n! style=\"width:25%;\" |Phrase\n!Meaning\n|-\n|CALL SIGN (callsign)\n|My callsign is....\n|-\n|WILCO\n|Understood. I will perform\n|-\n|CAN NOT\n|Unable to perform\n|-\n|REPEAT\n|Repeat the instructions\n|-\n|AM LOST\n|Unknown location\n|-\n|MAYDAY\n|They are in danger\n|-\n|HIJACK\n|I was hijacked\n|-\n|LAND (location)\n|I ask to land at...\n|-\n|DESCEND\n|I ask to descend\n|}\n\n==== Termination ====\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n! style=\"width:25%;\" |Station\n!Phrase\n|-\n|Controller\n|{CS}, ID friendly / ID {Result} (per task result)\n|-\n|FL\n|{Controller-CS} roger friendly, terminate\n|-\n|FL2 (Wingman)\n|{Controller-CS} terminate\n|-\n|Controller\n|{CS} terminate\n|}\n\'\'Termination always occurs in the order Blue – Red – Controller (flight lead first, then wingman, with the controller confirming last). The formation then splits and a new setup begins.\'\'\n\n=== VID Intercept ===\nA Visual Identification (VID) intercept is conducted when a contact cannot be declared HOSTILE or FRIENDLY by electronic means alone, and visual confirmation of aircraft type, markings, or behavior is required before engagement authority can be granted or withheld.\n\nVID intercepts follow a structured sequence: the controller guides the fighter to a position from which a visual identification pass can be made, then the fighter reports the result and awaits further direction.\n\n==== Commit ====\nThe controller or flight lead initiates the intercept against the unidentified contact.\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n! style=\"width:25%;\" |Station\n!Phrase\n|-\n|Controller\n|{CS} COMMIT {Group Label} BULLSEYE {Bearing/Range} {Altitude} {Declaration} {Nr of Contacts}\n|-\n|FL\n|{CS} COMMIT\n|}\n\n==== Guidance ====\nThe controller provides vectors to position the fighter for a VID pass. This is the only phase where the controller takes positive close control of the intercept geometry. BRAA format is used throughout.\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n! style=\"width:25%;\" |Station\n!Phrase\n|-\n|Controller\n|{CS} BRAA {Bearing/Range} {Altitude} {Aspect} {Declaration}\n|-\n|FL\n|{CS}\n|-\n| colspan=\"2\" style=\"text-align:center;\" |\'\'Controller continues to provide updated vectors until fighter calls JUDY\'\'\n|}\n\n==== JUDY ====\nThe fighter calls JUDY when radar contact is established and no further controller guidance is required. The controller minimizes transmissions from this point.\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n! style=\"width:25%;\" |Station\n!Phrase\n|-\n|FL\n|{Controller-CS} {CS} JUDY\n|-\n|Controller\n|\'\'Minimizes transmissions. Continues to provide SA information only.\'\'\n|}\n\n==== Tally ====\nThe fighter calls TALLY when visual contact is established with the target.\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n! style=\"width:25%;\" |Station\n!Phrase\n|-\n|FL\n|{CS} TALLY {Position}\n|}\n\n==== Identification ====\nThe fighter conducts the VID pass and reports the result to the controller. The report includes aircraft type, markings, and behavior observed.\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n! style=\"width:25%;\" |Station\n!Phrase\n|-\n|FL\n|{Controller-CS} {CS} IDENTIFIED {Aircraft Type} {Markings / Behavior}\n|-\n|Controller\n|{CS} {Further Instructions / Declaration Update}\n|}\nIf the contact is identified as HOSTILE following the VID pass, the controller issues the updated declaration and the flight may proceed to engagement IAW ROE. If the contact is FRIENDLY or NEUTRAL, the flight disengages and returns to its prebriefed position.\n\n=== Post-Intercept Options ===\nFollowing identification (ID or VID), the QRA(I) flight is normally tasked to shadow or escort the contact out of the airspace, hand it to a follow-on national asset, or — in the case of a genuine emergency (lost comms, distress) — assist it to a safe landing per the Intervention procedure above. Engagement is authorised only as an absolute last resort and strictly IAW the current ROE issued by Air C2.\n----\n\n== 07.6 / Recovery ==\nOnce tasking is complete and the flight is terminated (see Termination, above), control jurisdiction reverts from the intercept controller (CRC/ADCF) back to civil ATC for the return-to-base and recovery phase — this happens once the flight is clear of any other traffic still under the controlling agency\'s control.\n\nFrom this handback point onward, the sortie is flown as normal IFR traffic under standard ATC procedures; see [SOURCE ATC/Recovery document — link TBD] for departure/arrival handling, approach, and landing sequencing. Not duplicated here.\n----\n\n== Sources / Further Reading ==\nPlease review and approve before I link footnotes in the wiki:\n\n\'\'\'Quick Reaction Alert (QRA) — NATO:\'\'\'\n\n* AJP-3.3 — Allied Joint Doctrine for Air and Space Operations (official NATO standardisation document; references QRA and points to MC 0613, the overarching NIAMD concept) — https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/698ef9fb7da91680ad7f4389/AJP_3_3_EdC_V1.pdf\n* NATO.int — \"NATO Air Policing\" (official, unclassified overview) — https://www.nato.int/cps/en/natohq/topics_132685.htm\n* JAPCC (Joint Air Power Competence Centre) — \"NATO Air Policing Against Unmanned Aircraft\" (NATO-accredited Centre of Excellence; describes the ASACS/Air C2/CAOC/QRA(I) structure; not a doctrine document itself) — https://www.japcc.org/articles/nato-air-policing-against-unmanned-aircraft/\n\n\'\'\'Civil ATC Interface — Scramble Order, Transit, Handover (US/NORAD model, used illustratively):\'\'\'\n\n* FAA Order 7610.4J — \"Special Military Operations,\" 3 November 1998, Distribution Statement A (public release). Chapter 4: \"FAA/NORAD/PACAF Procedures for Control of Air Defense Aircraft\" — covers ADCF control instructions, transfer-of-control rules, scramble/airborne order format, AFIO, and \"due regard\" operations. Superseded internally by the current, FOUO-restricted JO 7610.4 (sensitive) / JO 7610.14 (non-sensitive) editions; this 1998 edition remains the last fully public release and is archived via DTIC, accession no. ADA355579. — https://apps.dtic.mil/sti/tr/pdf/ADA355579.pdf\n* FAA Order JO 7110.65 — Air Traffic Control (current edition), §9-2-7 \"Interceptor Operations\" — baseline ATC separation/priority handling for active air-defence scrambles — https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/publications/atpubs/atc_html/chap9_section_2.html\n\n\'\'\'Intercept & Tasking (ID / VID):\'\'\'\n\n* ICAO Annex 2 to the Convention on International Civil Aviation, Appendix 1 & 2 (Interception of Civil Aircraft; Signals) — https://www.bazl.admin.ch/dam/bazl/it/dokumente/Fachleute/Regulationen_und_Grundlagen/icao-annex/icao_annex_2_rulesoftheair.pdf.download.pdf/icao_annex_2_rulesoftheair.pdf\n* ICAO Doc 9433 — Manual concerning Interception of Civil Aircraft (Appendix D: example flashcards) — https://news.ncac.mn/uploads/bookSubject/2022-11/637c784cde8a2.pdf\n* SKYbrary — \"Military Interception Signalling\" (not official, but a good summary of interceptor positioning) — https://skybrary.aero/articles/military-interception-signalling\n* ATP 1-02.1/MCRP 3-30B.1/NTTP 6-02.1/AFTTP 3-2.5/STTP 3-9002 — Multi-Service Brevity Codes, Signature Draft, 01 Jan 2025 — https://www.alssa.mil/Portals/9/Documents/mttps/sd_brevity_2025.pdf\n* ATP 3-52.4/MCRP 3-20F.10/NTTP 6-02.9/AFTTP 3-2.8 — Multi-Service Tactics, Techniques, and Procedures for Air Control (ACC MTTP), 2024 — https://www.alssa.mil/Portals/9/Documents/mttps/acc_2024.pdf\n\n\'\'Note: Exact QRA reaction times and alert levels are nationally classified; the ~15 minutes mentioned in Step 1 is a publicly known, unclassified reference figure (RAF), not an official NATO standard. Likewise, Steps 3–4 (ATC Guidance/Handover) reflect the publicly available US/NORAD procedural model; the exact German/Luftwaffe scramble-to-ATC coordination procedure is not publicly published.\'\'','utf-8'),
(609,'== 07 / Quick Reaction Alert (QRA) — Overview ==\nQuick Reaction Alert (QRA), termed \"Airspace Control Alert\" in US usage, is the standing peacetime posture NATO air forces maintain to protect sovereign and Allied airspace. Armed fighters and crews sit at high readiness so they can launch within minutes to identify, shadow, and if necessary escort or engage an aircraft that has entered, or is approaching, protected airspace without authorisation or without maintaining required communications.\n\n=== Purpose ===\nQRA(I) — the Interceptor role of QRA — is one of the peacetime activities carried out under NATO Integrated Air and Missile Defence (NIAMD), alongside the Air Surveillance and Control System (ASACS) and the Air Command and Control (Air C2) structure. Typical triggers for a scramble include: an aircraft entering national/Allied airspace without clearance, loss of radio contact with a civil aircraft, an aircraft deviating from its filed flight plan without notification, or an unidentified/unresponsive military contact approaching the border of Allied airspace.\n\n=== Force Structure ===\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:30%;\" | Element !! Role\n|-\n| ASACS / CRC || Ground- and air-based sensors build and maintain the Recognised Air Picture (RAP); a Control and Reporting Centre (CRC) tracks all air traffic within its assigned airspace 24/7/365.\n|-\n| Air C2 (CAOC) || A Combined Air Operations Centre (for NATO, Uedem or Torrejón) authorises and directs the scramble, tasks the QRA(I) flight, and applies the current Rules of Engagement (ROE). This is also the agency that takes tactical control of the interceptor once handover occurs — see Step 4, Handover, below.\n|-\n| QRA(I) Flight || Armed fighters (2-ship standard) held at cockpit- or runway-alert, ready to launch within a short, nationally-defined reaction time once tasked.\n|}\n\nThe remainder of this chapter follows a single QRA sortie in order: \'\'\'1. Alert & Scramble Order → 2. Group-Up & Departure → 3. ATC Guidance (Transit) → 4. Handover → 5. Intercept & Tasking → 6. Recovery.\'\'\'\n\n----\n\n== 07.1 / Alert & Scramble Order ==\nReaction timelines and exact alert postures are nationally classified; publicly reported figures (e.g. UK QRA crews reported at readiness to be airborne within about 15 minutes) can be used as an unclassified planning reference for training purposes.\n\n=== Scramble Notification ===\nThe scramble order is passed via the base\'s scramble phone (or equivalent dedicated alert line) direct from CAOC/CRC to the alert shed/squadron ops, triggering the launch sequence. Normal base, ground, tower, and departure operations from that point onward are covered in [SOURCE base/departure ops document — link TBD] TODO\n\n=== Scramble Order (filed by CAOC/ADCF) ===\n\'\'Sourced from the publicly released FAA Order 7610.4J model — see Sources.\'\' Behind the scramble call above, the controlling agency (CAOC in the NATO structure; ADCF in the US/NORAD model this section is based on) identifies the mission to civil ATC as an active air-defence mission and files a short flight plan containing:\n* Call sign and aircraft type/equipment\n* Number of aircraft\n* Departure point\n* Proposed departure time — or simply the word \'\'\'\"immediate\"\'\'\'\n* Heading, altitude, and duration of flight\n* Remarks: frequency, type of climb, controlling agency, and desired transfer point\n\n\n----\n\n== 07.3 / ATC Guidance (Transit) ==\n\'\'The following describes how a scramble is coordinated with civil air traffic control between launch and handover. This section is modelled on the publicly released US FAA/NORAD procedure (FAA Order 7610.4J, \"Special Military Operations,\" 1998) as an unclassified, structurally representative reference — the equivalent German/Luftwaffe–DFS coordination procedure is not publicly published, but the underlying logic is broadly representative of how any NATO nation integrates a QRA launch into controlled civil airspace.\'\'\n\nOnce airborne, an interceptor does not go straight to a dedicated military-only frequency. It is worked by ordinary civil Air Traffic Control (ATC) like any other IFR flight, with priority/expedited handling, until tactical control is formally handed to the military radar unit directing the intercept — in the US model, the \'\'\'Air Defence Control Facility (ADCF)\'\'\', a military radar unit (Air Defence Sector / AWACS) that is the only type of unit authorised to direct interceptors. The CRC/CAOC in the NATO force structure above fills the equivalent role.\n\n=== Airborne Orders (filed once the flight is up) ===\nAs early as possible after launch, the controlling agency separately files \"airborne orders\" with the relevant en-route/terminal ATC facilities, containing:\n* Call sign, aircraft type/number\n* Departure point and time\n* Heading and altitude\n* Route of flight, broken into three legs: departure route → route to a fix serving the operating/tasked area → route from that fix to the intercept/destination\n* Frequency, and MARSA (Military Authority Assumes Responsibility for Separation) status if applicable\n\n=== Standing Authorisation & Squawk ===\nFor missions that cannot be conducted within normal ATC procedures, a standing pre-authorisation (in the US model, the FAA Authorization for Interceptor Operations, AFIO) allows active air-defence missions to proceed outside the normal clearance process. Aircraft operating under this authorisation squawk a dedicated identification code (Mode 3/A \'\'\'7777\'\'\' in the US model) so ATC and the controlling agency can positively track them even while outside the standard clearance framework.\n\n=== Beyond Controlled Airspace: \"Due Regard\" ===\nWhen an intercept and its supporting aircraft operate in controlled airspace beyond national territorial limits and a normal ATC clearance is impractical, the operation shifts to the \"due regard\" concept (from DOD FLIP, used for operations over the high seas): the controlling agency itself assumes responsibility for separating its aircraft from all observed traffic, in place of ATC. This only applies under specific conditions — e.g. the aircraft is in visual meteorological conditions, is within surveillance/radio contact of a radar facility, or carries its own separation-capable radar — and separation becomes the aircraft commander\'s own responsibility once outside the controlling agency\'s radar coverage.\n\n----\n\n== 07.4 / Handover (Transfer of Control) ==\nThe handoff from civil ATC to the controlling agency (CRC/CAOC, ADCF in the US model) is governed by a small set of rules, not a fixed altitude or distance:\n* The interceptor is retained under civil ATC control for as long as possible. ATC does not initiate the handoff on its own.\n* The controlling agency must request the transfer; it is not allowed to request control until the aircraft actually reaches the point where control must be assumed to carry out the intercept — i.e. it cannot take the aircraft \"early, just in case.\"\n* When requested, ATC must expedite the transfer.\n* The transfer point (a time, fix, or altitude) is chosen so that no conflict remains with any other traffic still under the transferring facility\'s control.\n\nOnce handover is complete, the sequence transitions seamlessly into the intercept procedure — see Step 5, below.\n\n----\n\n== 07.5 / Intercept & Tasking ==\nFollowing handover, the intercept controller runs the flight through one of two intercept types depending on the tactical picture: an \'\'\'ID intercept\'\'\' (radar/visual identification with follow-on tasking) or a \'\'\'VID intercept\'\'\' (visual identification required before a HOSTILE/FRIENDLY declaration can be made).\n\n=== ID Intercept ===\nAn Identification (ID) intercept establishes the type, registration, colour, and armament of an unknown contact by radar and/or visual means before follow-on tasking (shadow, intervention, radio interrogation, obey-check, or friendly termination) is assigned.\n\n\'\'\'ID Result Format:\'\'\' \'\'\'Number of Acft, Type, Registration, Colour, Armament\'\'\'\n\n==== Task: ID ====\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Controller || {CS} confirm ready to copy ID(-result)\n|-\n| FL || {Controller-CS} ready\n|-\n| Controller || Bogey is {Number} {Colour} {Acft Type}, registration {REGISTRATION}, {Armament}, at {Altitude}, Heading {HDG}, speed {Speed}\n|-\n| FL || {Controller-CS} copied, ({ID result}) new task {Task}\n|-\n| Controller || {CS} roger task {Task}\n|}\n\'\'This concludes Task ID; the controller can then assign one of the following follow-on tasks: Shadow, Obey-Check, Radio Interrogation, Intervention, or Friendly/Terminate.\'\'\n\n==== Task: Shadow ====\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Controller || {CS} new task shadow\n|-\n| FL || {Controller-CS} roger task shadow\n|}\n\'\'Task Shadow means following the aircraft at a distance of approximately 1–2 NM without further closure.\'\'\n\n==== Task: Obey-Check ====\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Controller || {CS} new task obey-check, fly HDG {HDG}\n|-\n| FL || {Controller-CS} task obey-check HDG {HDG}\n|-\n| FL || {Controller-CS} bogey is playmate \'\'(or: aircraft is not obeying)\'\'\n|-\n| Controller || {CS} copied\n|}\n\'\'Obey-Check means the fighter rocks its wings and then flies a turn in the specified direction. While doing so, it observes the target and reports whether it also rocks its wings and follows (\"playmate\") or does not (\"aircraft is not obeying\").\'\'\n\n==== Task: Radio Interrogation ====\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Controller || {CS} new task radio interrogation (on simulated guard {Frequency})\n|-\n| FL || {Controller-CS} roger, task radio interrogation\n|-\n| FL (on guard) || {Colour} {Acft Type} {Position}, with registration {REGISTRATION} (squawking {Squawk}) this is the eurofighter on your left wing calling on (simulated) guard ({Frequency}) you have been intercepted by the german air force. For purpose of identification state: Registration, Callsign, aerodrome of departure and aerodrome of destination on (simulated) guard ({Frequency})\n|-\n| Intercepted Acft || (German Interceptor) this is {Callsign} registration {REG} on guard, departure is {ICAO}, destination {ICAO}\n|-\n| Controller || Roger, standby\n|-\n| Controller || {CS} did you copy (the answer) on guard?\n|-\n| FL || Affirmative / Negative\n|}\n\'\'If the controller did not copy the response on guard, the fighter relays the answer. After this, termination is possible, or further tasks may follow.\'\'\n\n==== Task: Intervention ====\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Controller || {CS} new task intervention to {ICAO}\n|-\n| FL || {Controller-CS} roger, intervention to {ICAO}\n|-\n| colspan=\"2\" | \'\'Further radio follows the Intercept Call, kneeboard side (see below)\'\'\n|}\n\'\'During Interrogation and Intervention, the fighter flies to the left of the target at cockpit height (the pilot in command usually sits on the left).\'\'\n\n\'\'\'Intercept Call (Kneeboard):\'\'\'\n\nINTERCEPTED AIRCRAFT this is german INTERCEPTOR callsign {Callsign}, you will be intervented to {Airport} \'\'\'FOLLOW, FOLLOW\'\'\'\n* What is your Callsign\n* Descent to altitude {ALT} ft, QNH {HPA}\n* What is your preferred approach speed?\n* What is your gear down speed?\n\nThis is your airport, after split up join right/left hand pattern of RWY {NR} at {Altitude} feet, QNH {HPA}; \'\'\'YOU LAND, YOU LAND\'\'\'\n\n\'\'\'Phrases used by the intercepted aircraft (ICAO standard responses):\'\'\'\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Phrase !! Meaning\n|-\n| CALL SIGN (callsign) || My callsign is....\n|-\n| WILCO || Understood. I will perform\n|-\n| CAN NOT || Unable to perform\n|-\n| REPEAT || Repeat the instructions\n|-\n| AM LOST || Unknown location\n|-\n| MAYDAY || They are in danger\n|-\n| HIJACK || I was hijacked\n|-\n| LAND (location) || I ask to land at...\n|-\n| DESCEND || I ask to descend\n|}\n\n==== Termination ====\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Controller || {CS}, ID friendly / ID {Result} (per task result)\n|-\n| FL || {Controller-CS} roger friendly, terminate\n|-\n| FL2 (Wingman) || {Controller-CS} terminate\n|-\n| Controller || {CS} terminate\n|}\n\'\'Termination always occurs in the order Blue – Red – Controller (flight lead first, then wingman, with the controller confirming last). The formation then splits and a new setup begins.\'\'\n\n=== VID Intercept ===\nA Visual Identification (VID) intercept is conducted when a contact cannot be declared HOSTILE or FRIENDLY by electronic means alone, and visual confirmation of aircraft type, markings, or behavior is required before engagement authority can be granted or withheld.\n\nVID intercepts follow a structured sequence: the controller guides the fighter to a position from which a visual identification pass can be made, then the fighter reports the result and awaits further direction.\n\n==== Commit ====\nThe controller or flight lead initiates the intercept against the unidentified contact.\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Controller || {CS} COMMIT {Group Label} BULLSEYE {Bearing/Range} {Altitude} {Declaration} {Nr of Contacts}\n|-\n| FL || {CS} COMMIT\n|}\n\n==== Guidance ====\nThe controller provides vectors to position the fighter for a VID pass. This is the only phase where the controller takes positive close control of the intercept geometry. BRAA format is used throughout.\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Controller || {CS} BRAA {Bearing/Range} {Altitude} {Aspect} {Declaration}\n|-\n| FL || {CS}\n|-\n| colspan=\"2\" style=\"text-align:center;\" | \'\'Controller continues to provide updated vectors until fighter calls JUDY\'\'\n|}\n\n==== JUDY ====\nThe fighter calls JUDY when radar contact is established and no further controller guidance is required. The controller minimizes transmissions from this point.\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| FL || {Controller-CS} {CS} JUDY\n|-\n| Controller || \'\'Minimizes transmissions. Continues to provide SA information only.\'\'\n|}\n\n==== Tally ====\nThe fighter calls TALLY when visual contact is established with the target.\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| FL || {CS} TALLY {Position}\n|}\n\n==== Identification ====\nThe fighter conducts the VID pass and reports the result to the controller. The report includes aircraft type, markings, and behavior observed.\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| FL || {Controller-CS} {CS} IDENTIFIED {Aircraft Type} {Markings / Behavior}\n|-\n| Controller || {CS} {Further Instructions / Declaration Update}\n|}\n\nIf the contact is identified as HOSTILE following the VID pass, the controller issues the updated declaration and the flight may proceed to engagement IAW ROE. If the contact is FRIENDLY or NEUTRAL, the flight disengages and returns to its prebriefed position.\n\n=== Post-Intercept Options ===\nFollowing identification (ID or VID), the QRA(I) flight is normally tasked to shadow or escort the contact out of the airspace, hand it to a follow-on national asset, or — in the case of a genuine emergency (lost comms, distress) — assist it to a safe landing per the Intervention procedure above. Engagement is authorised only as an absolute last resort and strictly IAW the current ROE issued by Air C2.\n\n----\n\n== 07.6 / Recovery ==\nOnce tasking is complete and the flight is terminated (see Termination, above), control jurisdiction reverts from the intercept controller (CRC/ADCF) back to civil ATC for the return-to-base and recovery phase — this happens once the flight is clear of any other traffic still under the controlling agency\'s control.\n\nFrom this handback point onward, the sortie is flown as normal IFR traffic under standard ATC procedures; see [SOURCE ATC/Recovery document — link TBD] for departure/arrival handling, approach, and landing sequencing. Not duplicated here.\n\n----\n\n== Sources / Further Reading ==\nPlease review and approve before I link footnotes in the wiki:\n\n\'\'\'Quick Reaction Alert (QRA) — NATO:\'\'\'\n* AJP-3.3 — Allied Joint Doctrine for Air and Space Operations (official NATO standardisation document; references QRA and points to MC 0613, the overarching NIAMD concept) — https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/698ef9fb7da91680ad7f4389/AJP_3_3_EdC_V1.pdf\n* NATO.int — \"NATO Air Policing\" (official, unclassified overview) — https://www.nato.int/cps/en/natohq/topics_132685.htm\n* JAPCC (Joint Air Power Competence Centre) — \"NATO Air Policing Against Unmanned Aircraft\" (NATO-accredited Centre of Excellence; describes the ASACS/Air C2/CAOC/QRA(I) structure; not a doctrine document itself) — https://www.japcc.org/articles/nato-air-policing-against-unmanned-aircraft/\n\n\'\'\'Civil ATC Interface — Scramble Order, Transit, Handover (US/NORAD model, used illustratively):\'\'\'\n* FAA Order 7610.4J — \"Special Military Operations,\" 3 November 1998, Distribution Statement A (public release). Chapter 4: \"FAA/NORAD/PACAF Procedures for Control of Air Defense Aircraft\" — covers ADCF control instructions, transfer-of-control rules, scramble/airborne order format, AFIO, and \"due regard\" operations. Superseded internally by the current, FOUO-restricted JO 7610.4 (sensitive) / JO 7610.14 (non-sensitive) editions; this 1998 edition remains the last fully public release and is archived via DTIC, accession no. ADA355579. — https://apps.dtic.mil/sti/tr/pdf/ADA355579.pdf\n* FAA Order JO 7110.65 — Air Traffic Control (current edition), §9-2-7 \"Interceptor Operations\" — baseline ATC separation/priority handling for active air-defence scrambles — https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/publications/atpubs/atc_html/chap9_section_2.html\n\n\'\'\'Intercept & Tasking (ID / VID):\'\'\'\n* ICAO Annex 2 to the Convention on International Civil Aviation, Appendix 1 & 2 (Interception of Civil Aircraft; Signals) — https://www.bazl.admin.ch/dam/bazl/it/dokumente/Fachleute/Regulationen_und_Grundlagen/icao-annex/icao_annex_2_rulesoftheair.pdf.download.pdf/icao_annex_2_rulesoftheair.pdf\n* ICAO Doc 9433 — Manual concerning Interception of Civil Aircraft (Appendix D: example flashcards) — https://news.ncac.mn/uploads/bookSubject/2022-11/637c784cde8a2.pdf\n* SKYbrary — \"Military Interception Signalling\" (not official, but a good summary of interceptor positioning) — https://skybrary.aero/articles/military-interception-signalling\n* ATP 1-02.1/MCRP 3-30B.1/NTTP 6-02.1/AFTTP 3-2.5/STTP 3-9002 — Multi-Service Brevity Codes, Signature Draft, 01 Jan 2025 — https://www.alssa.mil/Portals/9/Documents/mttps/sd_brevity_2025.pdf\n* ATP 3-52.4/MCRP 3-20F.10/NTTP 6-02.9/AFTTP 3-2.8 — Multi-Service Tactics, Techniques, and Procedures for Air Control (ACC MTTP), 2024 — https://www.alssa.mil/Portals/9/Documents/mttps/acc_2024.pdf\n\n\'\'Note: Exact QRA reaction times and alert levels are nationally classified; the ~15 minutes mentioned in Step 1 is a publicly known, unclassified reference figure (RAF), not an official NATO standard. Likewise, Steps 3–4 (ATC Guidance/Handover) reflect the publicly available US/NORAD procedural model; the exact German/Luftwaffe scramble-to-ATC coordination procedure is not publicly published.\'\'','utf-8'),
(610,'== 07 / Quick Reaction Alert (QRA) — Overview ==\nQuick Reaction Alert (QRA), termed \"Airspace Control Alert\" in US usage, is the standing peacetime posture NATO air forces maintain to protect sovereign and Allied airspace. Armed fighters and crews sit at high readiness so they can launch within minutes to identify, shadow, and if necessary escort or engage an aircraft that has entered, or is approaching, protected airspace without authorisation or without maintaining required communications.\n\n=== Purpose ===\nQRA(I) — the Interceptor role of QRA — is one of the peacetime activities carried out under NATO Integrated Air and Missile Defence (NIAMD), alongside the Air Surveillance and Control System (ASACS) and the Air Command and Control (Air C2) structure. Typical triggers for a scramble include: an aircraft entering national/Allied airspace without clearance, loss of radio contact with a civil aircraft, an aircraft deviating from its filed flight plan without notification, or an unidentified/unresponsive military contact approaching the border of Allied airspace.\n\n=== Force Structure ===\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:30%;\" | Element !! Role\n|-\n| ASACS / CRC || Ground- and air-based sensors build and maintain the Recognised Air Picture (RAP); a Control and Reporting Centre (CRC) tracks all air traffic within its assigned airspace 24/7/365.\n|-\n| Air C2 (CAOC) || A Combined Air Operations Centre (for NATO, Uedem or Torrejón) authorises and directs the scramble, tasks the QRA(I) flight, and applies the current Rules of Engagement (ROE). This is also the agency that takes tactical control of the interceptor once handover occurs — see Step 4, Handover, below.\n|-\n| QRA(I) Flight || Armed fighters (2-ship standard) held at cockpit- or runway-alert, ready to launch within a short, nationally-defined reaction time once tasked.\n|}\n\nThe remainder of this chapter follows a single QRA sortie in order: \'\'\'1. Alert & Scramble Order → 2. Group-Up & Departure → 3. ATC Guidance (Transit) → 4. Handover → 5. Intercept & Tasking → 6. Recovery.\'\'\'\n\n----\n\n== 07.1 / Alert & Scramble Order ==\nReaction timelines and exact alert postures are nationally classified; publicly reported figures (e.g. UK QRA crews reported at readiness to be airborne within about 15 minutes) can be used as an unclassified planning reference for training purposes.\n\n=== Scramble Notification ===\nThe scramble order is passed via the base\'s scramble phone (or equivalent dedicated alert line) direct from CAOC/CRC to the alert shed/squadron ops, triggering the launch sequence. Normal base, ground, tower, and departure operations from that point onward are covered in [SOURCE base/departure ops document — link TBD] TODO\n\n=== Scramble Order (filed by CAOC/ADCF) ===\n\'\'Sourced from the publicly released FAA Order 7610.4J model — see Sources.\'\' Behind the scramble call above, the controlling agency (CAOC in the NATO structure; ADCF in the US/NORAD model this section is based on) identifies the mission to civil ATC as an active air-defence mission and files a short flight plan containing:\n* Call sign and aircraft type/equipment\n* Number of aircraft\n* Departure point\n* Proposed departure time — or simply the word \'\'\'\"immediate\"\'\'\'\n* Heading, altitude, and duration of flight\n* Remarks: frequency, type of climb, controlling agency, and desired transfer point\n\n----\n\n== 07.3 / ATC Guidance (Transit) ==\n\'\'The following describes how a scramble is coordinated with civil air traffic control between launch and handover. This section is modelled on the publicly released US FAA/NORAD procedure (FAA Order 7610.4J, \"Special Military Operations,\" 1998) as an unclassified, structurally representative reference — the equivalent German/Luftwaffe–DFS coordination procedure is not publicly published, but the underlying logic is broadly representative of how any NATO nation integrates a QRA launch into controlled civil airspace.\'\'\n\nOnce airborne, an interceptor does not go straight to a dedicated military-only frequency. It is worked by ordinary civil Air Traffic Control (ATC) like any other IFR flight, with priority/expedited handling, until tactical control is formally handed to the military radar unit directing the intercept — in the US model, the \'\'\'Air Defence Control Facility (ADCF)\'\'\', a military radar unit (Air Defence Sector / AWACS) that is the only type of unit authorised to direct interceptors. The CRC/CAOC in the NATO force structure above fills the equivalent role.\n\n=== Airborne Orders (filed once the flight is up) ===\nAs early as possible after launch, the controlling agency separately files \"airborne orders\" with the relevant en-route/terminal ATC facilities, containing:\n* Call sign, aircraft type/number\n* Departure point and time\n* Heading and altitude\n* Route of flight, broken into three legs: departure route → route to a fix serving the operating/tasked area → route from that fix to the intercept/destination\n* Frequency, and MARSA (Military Authority Assumes Responsibility for Separation) status if applicable\n\n=== Standing Authorisation & Squawk ===\nFor missions that cannot be conducted within normal ATC procedures, a standing pre-authorisation (in the US model, the FAA Authorization for Interceptor Operations, AFIO) allows active air-defence missions to proceed outside the normal clearance process. Aircraft operating under this authorisation squawk a dedicated identification code (Mode 3/A \'\'\'7777\'\'\' in the US model) so ATC and the controlling agency can positively track them even while outside the standard clearance framework.\n\n=== Beyond Controlled Airspace: \"Due Regard\" ===\nWhen an intercept and its supporting aircraft operate in controlled airspace beyond national territorial limits and a normal ATC clearance is impractical, the operation shifts to the \"due regard\" concept (from DOD FLIP, used for operations over the high seas): the controlling agency itself assumes responsibility for separating its aircraft from all observed traffic, in place of ATC. This only applies under specific conditions — e.g. the aircraft is in visual meteorological conditions, is within surveillance/radio contact of a radar facility, or carries its own separation-capable radar — and separation becomes the aircraft commander\'s own responsibility once outside the controlling agency\'s radar coverage.\n\n----\n\n== 07.4 / Handover (Transfer of Control) ==\nThe handoff from civil ATC to the controlling agency (CRC/CAOC, ADCF in the US model) is governed by a small set of rules, not a fixed altitude or distance:\n* The interceptor is retained under civil ATC control for as long as possible. ATC does not initiate the handoff on its own.\n* The controlling agency must request the transfer; it is not allowed to request control until the aircraft actually reaches the point where control must be assumed to carry out the intercept — i.e. it cannot take the aircraft \"early, just in case.\"\n* When requested, ATC must expedite the transfer.\n* The transfer point (a time, fix, or altitude) is chosen so that no conflict remains with any other traffic still under the transferring facility\'s control.\n\nOnce handover is complete, the sequence transitions seamlessly into the intercept procedure — see Step 5, below.\n\n----\n\n== 07.5 / Intercept & Tasking ==\nFollowing handover, the intercept controller runs the flight through one of two intercept types depending on the tactical picture: an \'\'\'ID intercept\'\'\' (radar/visual identification with follow-on tasking) or a \'\'\'VID intercept\'\'\' (visual identification required before a HOSTILE/FRIENDLY declaration can be made).\n\n=== ID Intercept ===\nAn Identification (ID) intercept establishes the type, registration, colour, and armament of an unknown contact by radar and/or visual means before follow-on tasking (shadow, intervention, radio interrogation, obey-check, or friendly termination) is assigned.\n\n\'\'\'ID Result Format:\'\'\' \'\'\'Number of Acft, Type, Registration, Colour, Armament\'\'\'\n\n==== Task: ID ====\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Controller || {CS} confirm ready to copy ID(-result)\n|-\n| FL || {Controller-CS} ready\n|-\n| Controller || Bogey is {Number} {Colour} {Acft Type}, registration {REGISTRATION}, {Armament}, at {Altitude}, Heading {HDG}, speed {Speed}\n|-\n| FL || {Controller-CS} copied, ({ID result}) new task {Task}\n|-\n| Controller || {CS} roger task {Task}\n|}\n\'\'This concludes Task ID; the controller can then assign one of the following follow-on tasks: Shadow, Obey-Check, Radio Interrogation, Intervention, or Friendly/Terminate.\'\'\n\n==== Task: Shadow ====\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Controller || {CS} new task shadow\n|-\n| FL || {Controller-CS} roger task shadow\n|}\n\'\'Task Shadow means following the aircraft at a distance of approximately 1–2 NM without further closure.\'\'\n\n==== Task: Obey-Check ====\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Controller || {CS} new task obey-check, fly HDG {HDG}\n|-\n| FL || {Controller-CS} task obey-check HDG {HDG}\n|-\n| FL || {Controller-CS} bogey is playmate \'\'(or: aircraft is not obeying)\'\'\n|-\n| Controller || {CS} copied\n|}\n\'\'Obey-Check means the fighter rocks its wings and then flies a turn in the specified direction. While doing so, it observes the target and reports whether it also rocks its wings and follows (\"playmate\") or does not (\"aircraft is not obeying\").\'\'\n\n==== Task: Radio Interrogation ====\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Controller || {CS} new task radio interrogation (on simulated guard {Frequency})\n|-\n| FL || {Controller-CS} roger, task radio interrogation\n|-\n| FL (on guard) || {Colour} {Acft Type} {Position}, with registration {REGISTRATION} (squawking {Squawk}) this is the eurofighter on your left wing calling on (simulated) guard ({Frequency}) you have been intercepted by the german air force. For purpose of identification state: Registration, Callsign, aerodrome of departure and aerodrome of destination on (simulated) guard ({Frequency})\n|-\n| Intercepted Acft || (German Interceptor) this is {Callsign} registration {REG} on guard, departure is {ICAO}, destination {ICAO}\n|-\n| Controller || Roger, standby\n|-\n| FL || {Controller-CS} did you copy (the answer) on guard?\n|-\n| Controller || Affirmative / Negative\n|}\n\'\'If the controller did not copy the response on guard, the fighter relays the answer. After this, termination is possible, or further tasks may follow.\'\'\n\n==== Task: Intervention ====\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Controller || {CS} new task intervention to {ICAO}\n|-\n| FL || {Controller-CS} roger, intervention to {ICAO}\n|-\n| colspan=\"2\" | \'\'Further radio follows the Intercept Call, (see below)\'\'\n|}\n\'\'During Interrogation and Intervention, the fighter flies to the left of the target at cockpit height (the pilot in command usually sits on the left).\'\'\n\n\'\'\'Intercept Call:\'\'\'\n INTERCEPTED AIRCRAFT this is german INTERCEPTOR callsign {Callsign}, you will be intervented to {Airport} \'\'\'FOLLOW, FOLLOW\'\'\'\n* What is your Callsign\n* Descent to altitude {ALT} ft, QNH {HPA}\n* What is your preferred approach speed?\n* What is your gear down speed?\n This is your airport, after split up join right/left hand pattern of RWY {NR} at {Altitude} feet, QNH {HPA}; \'\'\'YOU LAND, YOU LAND\'\'\'\n\'\'\'Phrases used by the intercepted aircraft (ICAO standard responses):\'\'\'\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Phrase !! Meaning\n|-\n| CALL SIGN (callsign) || My callsign is....\n|-\n| WILCO || Understood. I will perform\n|-\n| CAN NOT || Unable to perform\n|-\n| REPEAT || Repeat the instructions\n|-\n| AM LOST || Unknown location\n|-\n| MAYDAY || They are in danger\n|-\n| HIJACK || I was hijacked\n|-\n| LAND (location) || I ask to land at...\n|-\n| DESCEND || I ask to descend\n|}\n\n==== Post-Intercept Options ====\nFollowing identification (ID or VID), the QRA(I) flight is normally tasked to shadow or escort the contact out of the airspace, hand it to a follow-on national asset, or — in the case of a genuine emergency (lost comms, distress) — assist it to a safe landing per the Intervention procedure above. Engagement is authorised only as an absolute last resort and strictly IAW the current ROE issued by Air C2.\n\n----\n\n== 07.6 / Recovery ==\nOnce tasking is complete and the flight is terminated (see Termination, above), control jurisdiction reverts from the intercept controller (CRC/ADCF) back to civil ATC for the return-to-base and recovery phase — this happens once the flight is clear of any other traffic still under the controlling agency\'s control.\n\nFrom this handback point onward, the sortie is flown as normal IFR traffic under standard ATC procedures; see [SOURCE ATC/Recovery document — link TBD] for departure/arrival handling, approach, and landing sequencing. Not duplicated here.\n\n----\n\n== Sources / Further Reading ==\nPlease review and approve before I link footnotes in the wiki:\n\n\'\'\'Quick Reaction Alert (QRA) — NATO:\'\'\'\n* AJP-3.3 — Allied Joint Doctrine for Air and Space Operations (official NATO standardisation document; references QRA and points to MC 0613, the overarching NIAMD concept) — https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/698ef9fb7da91680ad7f4389/AJP_3_3_EdC_V1.pdf\n* NATO.int — \"NATO Air Policing\" (official, unclassified overview) — https://www.nato.int/cps/en/natohq/topics_132685.htm\n* JAPCC (Joint Air Power Competence Centre) — \"NATO Air Policing Against Unmanned Aircraft\" (NATO-accredited Centre of Excellence; describes the ASACS/Air C2/CAOC/QRA(I) structure; not a doctrine document itself) — https://www.japcc.org/articles/nato-air-policing-against-unmanned-aircraft/\n\n\'\'\'Civil ATC Interface — Scramble Order, Transit, Handover (US/NORAD model, used illustratively):\'\'\'\n* FAA Order 7610.4J — \"Special Military Operations,\" 3 November 1998, Distribution Statement A (public release). Chapter 4: \"FAA/NORAD/PACAF Procedures for Control of Air Defense Aircraft\" — covers ADCF control instructions, transfer-of-control rules, scramble/airborne order format, AFIO, and \"due regard\" operations. Superseded internally by the current, FOUO-restricted JO 7610.4 (sensitive) / JO 7610.14 (non-sensitive) editions; this 1998 edition remains the last fully public release and is archived via DTIC, accession no. ADA355579. — https://apps.dtic.mil/sti/tr/pdf/ADA355579.pdf\n* FAA Order JO 7110.65 — Air Traffic Control (current edition), §9-2-7 \"Interceptor Operations\" — baseline ATC separation/priority handling for active air-defence scrambles — https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/publications/atpubs/atc_html/chap9_section_2.html\n\n\'\'\'Intercept & Tasking (ID / VID):\'\'\'\n* ICAO Annex 2 to the Convention on International Civil Aviation, Appendix 1 & 2 (Interception of Civil Aircraft; Signals) — https://www.bazl.admin.ch/dam/bazl/it/dokumente/Fachleute/Regulationen_und_Grundlagen/icao-annex/icao_annex_2_rulesoftheair.pdf.download.pdf/icao_annex_2_rulesoftheair.pdf\n* ICAO Doc 9433 — Manual concerning Interception of Civil Aircraft (Appendix D: example flashcards) — https://news.ncac.mn/uploads/bookSubject/2022-11/637c784cde8a2.pdf\n* SKYbrary — \"Military Interception Signalling\" (not official, but a good summary of interceptor positioning) — https://skybrary.aero/articles/military-interception-signalling\n* ATP 1-02.1/MCRP 3-30B.1/NTTP 6-02.1/AFTTP 3-2.5/STTP 3-9002 — Multi-Service Brevity Codes, Signature Draft, 01 Jan 2025 — https://www.alssa.mil/Portals/9/Documents/mttps/sd_brevity_2025.pdf\n* ATP 3-52.4/MCRP 3-20F.10/NTTP 6-02.9/AFTTP 3-2.8 — Multi-Service Tactics, Techniques, and Procedures for Air Control (ACC MTTP), 2024 — https://www.alssa.mil/Portals/9/Documents/mttps/acc_2024.pdf\n\n\'\'Note: Exact QRA reaction times and alert levels are nationally classified; the ~15 minutes mentioned in Step 1 is a publicly known, unclassified reference figure (RAF), not an official NATO standard. Likewise, Steps 3–4 (ATC Guidance/Handover) reflect the publicly available US/NORAD procedural model; the exact German/Luftwaffe scramble-to-ATC coordination procedure is not publicly published.\'\'','utf-8'),
