 over the high seas): the controlling agency itself assumes responsibility for separating its aircraft from all observed traffic, in place of ATC. This only applies under specific conditions — e.g. the aircraft is in visual meteorological conditions, is within surveillance/radio contact of a radar facility, or carries its own separation-capable radar — and separation becomes the aircraft commander\'s own responsibility once outside the controlling agency\'s radar coverage.\n\n----\n\n== 07.4 / Handover (Transfer of Control) ==\nThe handoff from civil ATC to the controlling agency (CRC/CAOC, ADCF in the US model) is governed by a small set of rules, not a fixed altitude or distance:\n* The interceptor is retained under civil ATC control for as long as possible. ATC does not initiate the handoff on its own.\n* The controlling agency must request the transfer; it is not allowed to request control until the aircraft actually reaches the point where control must be assumed to carry out the intercept — i.e. it cannot take the aircraft \"early, just in case.\"\n* When requested, ATC must expedite the transfer.\n* The transfer point (a time, fix, or altitude) is chosen so that no conflict remains with any other traffic still under the transferring facility\'s control.\n\nOnce handover is complete, the sequence transitions seamlessly into the intercept procedure — see Step 5, below.\n\n----\n\n== 07.5 / Intercept & Tasking ==\nFollowing handover, the intercept controller runs the flight through one of two intercept types depending on the tactical picture: an \'\'\'ID intercept\'\'\' (radar/visual identification with follow-on tasking) or a \'\'\'VID intercept\'\'\' (visual identification required before a HOSTILE/FRIENDLY declaration can be made).\n\n=== ID Intercept ===\nAn Identification (ID) intercept establishes the type, registration, colour, and armament of an unknown contact by radar and/or visual means before follow-on tasking (shadow, intervention, radio interrogation, obey-check, or friendly termination) is assigned.\n\n\'\'\'ID Result Format:\'\'\' \'\'\'Number of Acft, Type, Registration, Colour, Armament\'\'\'\n\n==== Task: ID ====\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Controller || {CS} confirm ready to copy ID(-result)\n|-\n| FL || {Controller-CS} ready\n|-\n| Controller || Bogey is {Number} {Colour} {Acft Type}, registration {REGISTRATION}, {Armament}, at {Altitude}, Heading {HDG}, speed {Speed}\n|-\n| FL || {Controller-CS} copied, ({ID result}) new task {Task}\n|-\n| Controller || {CS} roger task {Task}\n|}\n\'\'This concludes Task ID; the controller can then assign one of the following follow-on tasks: Shadow, Obey-Check, Radio Interrogation, Intervention, or Friendly/Terminate.\'\'\n\n==== Task: Shadow ====\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Controller || {CS} new task shadow\n|-\n| FL || {Controller-CS} roger task shadow\n|}\n\'\'Task Shadow means following the aircraft at a distance of approximately 1–2 NM without further closure.\'\'\n\n==== Task: Obey-Check ====\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Controller || {CS} new task obey-check, fly HDG {HDG}\n|-\n| FL || {Controller-CS} task obey-check HDG {HDG}\n|-\n| FL || {Controller-CS} bogey is playmate \'\'(or: aircraft is not obeying)\'\'\n|-\n| Controller || {CS} copied\n|}\n\'\'Obey-Check means the fighter rocks its wings and then flies a turn in the specified direction. While doing so, it observes the target and reports whether it also rocks its wings and follows (\"playmate\") or does not (\"aircraft is not obeying\").\'\'\n\n==== Task: Radio Interrogation ====\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Controller || {CS} new task radio interrogation (on simulated guard {Frequency})\n|-\n| FL || {Controller-CS} roger, task radio interrogation\n|-\n| FL (on guard) || {Colour} {Acft Type} {Position}, with registration {REGISTRATION} (squawking {Squawk}) this is the eurofighter on your left wing calling on (simulated) guard ({Frequency}) you have been intercepted by the german air force. For purpose of identification state: Registration, Callsign, aerodrome of departure and aerodrome of destination on (simulated) guard ({Frequency})\n|-\n| Intercepted Acft || (German Interceptor) this is {Callsign} registration {REG} on guard, departure is {ICAO}, destination {ICAO}\n|-\n| Controller || Roger, standby\n|-\n| FL || {Controller-CS} did you copy (the answer) on guard?\n|-\n| Controller || Affirmative / Negative\n|}\n\'\'If the controller did not copy the response on guard, the fighter relays the answer. After this, termination is possible, or further tasks may follow.\'\'\n\n==== Task: Intervention ====\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Controller || {CS} new task intervention to {ICAO}\n|-\n| FL || {Controller-CS} roger, intervention to {ICAO}\n|-\n| colspan=\"2\" | \'\'Further radio follows the Intercept Call, kneeboard side (see below)\'\'\n|}\n\'\'During Interrogation and Intervention, the fighter flies to the left of the target at cockpit height (the pilot in command usually sits on the left).\'\'\n\n\'\'\'Intercept Call:\'\'\'\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Interceptor || INTERCEPTED AIRCRAFT this is german INTERCEPTOR callsign {Callsign}, you will be intervented to {Airport} \'\'\'FOLLOW, FOLLOW\'\'\'\n|-\n| Intercepted Acft || WILCO / CAN NOT\n|-\n| Interceptor || What is your callsign?\n|-\n| Intercepted Acft || CALL SIGN {Callsign}\n|-\n| Interceptor || Descend to altitude {ALT} ft, QNH {HPA}\n|-\n| Intercepted Acft || WILCO / CAN NOT\n|-\n| Interceptor || What is your preferred approach speed?\n|-\n| Intercepted Acft || {Speed}\n|-\n| Interceptor || What is your gear down speed?\n|-\n| Intercepted Acft || {Speed}\n|-\n| Interceptor || This is your airport, after split up join right/left hand pattern of RWY {NR} at {Altitude} feet, QNH {HPA}; \'\'\'YOU LAND, YOU LAND\'\'\'\n|-\n| Intercepted Acft || WILCO\n|}\n\n\'\'\'Phrases used by the intercepted aircraft (ICAO standard responses):\'\'\'\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Phrase !! Meaning\n|-\n| CALL SIGN (callsign) || My callsign is....\n|-\n| WILCO || Understood. I will perform\n|-\n| CAN NOT || Unable to perform\n|-\n| REPEAT || Repeat the instructions\n|-\n| AM LOST || Unknown location\n|-\n| MAYDAY || They are in danger\n|-\n| HIJACK || I was hijacked\n|-\n| LAND (location) || I ask to land at...\n|-\n| DESCEND || I ask to descend\n|}\n\n==== Post-Intercept Options ====\nFollowing identification (ID or VID), the QRA(I) flight is normally tasked to shadow or escort the contact out of the airspace, hand it to a follow-on national asset, or — in the case of a genuine emergency (lost comms, distress) — assist it to a safe landing per the Intervention procedure above. Engagement is authorised only as an absolute last resort and strictly IAW the current ROE issued by Air C2.\n\n----\n\n== 07.6 / Recovery ==\nOnce tasking is complete and the flight is terminated (see Termination, above), control jurisdiction reverts from the intercept controller (CRC/ADCF) back to civil ATC for the return-to-base and recovery phase — this happens once the flight is clear of any other traffic still under the controlling agency\'s control.\n\nFrom this handback point onward, the sortie is flown as normal IFR traffic under standard ATC procedures; see [SOURCE ATC/Recovery document — link TBD] for departure/arrival handling, approach, and landing sequencing. Not duplicated here.\n\n----\n\n== Sources / Further Reading ==\nPlease review and approve before I link footnotes in the wiki:\n\n\'\'\'Quick Reaction Alert (QRA) — NATO:\'\'\'\n* AJP-3.3 — Allied Joint Doctrine for Air and Space Operations (official NATO standardisation document; references QRA and points to MC 0613, the overarching NIAMD concept) — https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/698ef9fb7da91680ad7f4389/AJP_3_3_EdC_V1.pdf\n* NATO.int — \"NATO Air Policing\" (official, unclassified overview) — https://www.nato.int/cps/en/natohq/topics_132685.htm\n* JAPCC (Joint Air Power Competence Centre) — \"NATO Air Policing Against Unmanned Aircraft\" (NATO-accredited Centre of Excellence; describes the ASACS/Air C2/CAOC/QRA(I) structure; not a doctrine document itself) — https://www.japcc.org/articles/nato-air-policing-against-unmanned-aircraft/\n\n\'\'\'Civil ATC Interface — Scramble Order, Transit, Handover (US/NORAD model, used illustratively):\'\'\'\n* FAA Order 7610.4J — \"Special Military Operations,\" 3 November 1998, Distribution Statement A (public release). Chapter 4: \"FAA/NORAD/PACAF Procedures for Control of Air Defense Aircraft\" — covers ADCF control instructions, transfer-of-control rules, scramble/airborne order format, AFIO, and \"due regard\" operations. Superseded internally by the current, FOUO-restricted JO 7610.4 (sensitive) / JO 7610.14 (non-sensitive) editions; this 1998 edition remains the last fully public release and is archived via DTIC, accession no. ADA355579. — https://apps.dtic.mil/sti/tr/pdf/ADA355579.pdf\n* FAA Order JO 7110.65 — Air Traffic Control (current edition), §9-2-7 \"Interceptor Operations\" — baseline ATC separation/priority handling for active air-defence scrambles — https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/publications/atpubs/atc_html/chap9_section_2.html\n\n\'\'\'Intercept & Tasking (ID / VID):\'\'\'\n* ICAO Annex 2 to the Convention on International Civil Aviation, Appendix 1 & 2 (Interception of Civil Aircraft; Signals) — https://www.bazl.admin.ch/dam/bazl/it/dokumente/Fachleute/Regulationen_und_Grundlagen/icao-annex/icao_annex_2_rulesoftheair.pdf.download.pdf/icao_annex_2_rulesoftheair.pdf\n* ICAO Doc 9433 — Manual concerning Interception of Civil Aircraft (Appendix D: example flashcards) — https://news.ncac.mn/uploads/bookSubject/2022-11/637c784cde8a2.pdf\n* SKYbrary — \"Military Interception Signalling\" (not official, but a good summary of interceptor positioning) — https://skybrary.aero/articles/military-interception-signalling\n* ATP 1-02.1/MCRP 3-30B.1/NTTP 6-02.1/AFTTP 3-2.5/STTP 3-9002 — Multi-Service Brevity Codes, Signature Draft, 01 Jan 2025 — https://www.alssa.mil/Portals/9/Documents/mttps/sd_brevity_2025.pdf\n* ATP 3-52.4/MCRP 3-20F.10/NTTP 6-02.9/AFTTP 3-2.8 — Multi-Service Tactics, Techniques, and Procedures for Air Control (ACC MTTP), 2024 — https://www.alssa.mil/Portals/9/Documents/mttps/acc_2024.pdf\n\n\'\'Note: Exact QRA reaction times and alert levels are nationally classified; the ~15 minutes mentioned in Step 1 is a publicly known, unclassified reference figure (RAF), not an official NATO standard. Likewise, Steps 3–4 (ATC Guidance/Handover) reflect the publicly available US/NORAD procedural model; the exact German/Luftwaffe scramble-to-ATC coordination procedure is not publicly published.\'\'','utf-8'),
(612,'== 07 / Quick Reaction Alert (QRA) — Overview ==\nQuick Reaction Alert (QRA), termed \"Airspace Control Alert\" in US usage, is the standing peacetime posture NATO air forces maintain to protect sovereign and Allied airspace. Armed fighters and crews sit at high readiness so they can launch within minutes to identify, shadow, and if necessary escort or engage an aircraft that has entered, or is approaching, protected airspace without authorisation or without maintaining required communications.\n\n=== Purpose ===\nQRA(I) — the Interceptor role of QRA — is one of the peacetime activities carried out under NATO Integrated Air and Missile Defence (NIAMD), alongside the Air Surveillance and Control System (ASACS) and the Air Command and Control (Air C2) structure. Typical triggers for a scramble include: an aircraft entering national/Allied airspace without clearance, loss of radio contact with a civil aircraft, an aircraft deviating from its filed flight plan without notification, or an unidentified/unresponsive military contact approaching the border of Allied airspace.\n\n=== Force Structure ===\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:30%;\" | Element !! Role\n|-\n| ASACS / CRC || Ground- and air-based sensors build and maintain the Recognised Air Picture (RAP); a Control and Reporting Centre (CRC) tracks all air traffic within its assigned airspace 24/7/365.\n|-\n| Air C2 (CAOC) || A Combined Air Operations Centre (for NATO, Uedem or Torrejón) authorises and directs the scramble, tasks the QRA(I) flight, and applies the current Rules of Engagement (ROE). This is also the agency that takes tactical control of the interceptor once handover occurs — see Step 4, Handover, below.\n|-\n| QRA(I) Flight || Armed fighters (2-ship standard) held at cockpit- or runway-alert, ready to launch within a short, nationally-defined reaction time once tasked.\n|}\n\nThe remainder of this chapter follows a single QRA sortie in order: \'\'\'1. Alert & Scramble Order → 2. Group-Up & Departure → 3. ATC Guidance (Transit) → 4. Handover → 5. Intercept & Tasking → 6. Recovery.\'\'\'\n\n----\n\n== 07.1 / Alert & Scramble Order ==\nReaction timelines and exact alert postures are nationally classified; publicly reported figures (e.g. UK QRA crews reported at readiness to be airborne within about 15 minutes) can be used as an unclassified planning reference for training purposes.\n\n=== Scramble Notification ===\nThe scramble order is passed via the base\'s scramble phone (or equivalent dedicated alert line) direct from CAOC/CRC to the alert shed/squadron ops, triggering the launch sequence. Normal base, ground, tower, and departure operations from that point onward are covered in [[MTTP/ACC/Air Traffic|Base / Civil Operations]] \n\n=== Scramble Order (filed by CAOC/ADCF) ===\n\'\'Sourced from the publicly released FAA Order 7610.4J model — see Sources.\'\' Behind the scramble call above, the controlling agency (CAOC in the NATO structure; ADCF in the US/NORAD model this section is based on) identifies the mission to civil ATC as an active air-defence mission and files a short flight plan containing:\n* Call sign and aircraft type/equipment\n* Number of aircraft\n* Departure point\n* Proposed departure time — or simply the word \'\'\'\"immediate\"\'\'\'\n* Heading, altitude, and duration of flight\n* Remarks: frequency, type of climb, controlling agency, and desired transfer point\n\n----\n\n== 07.3 / ATC Guidance (Transit) ==\n\'\'The following describes how a scramble is coordinated with civil air traffic control between launch and handover. This section is modelled on the publicly released US FAA/NORAD procedure (FAA Order 7610.4J, \"Special Military Operations,\" 1998) as an unclassified, structurally representative reference — the equivalent German/Luftwaffe–DFS coordination procedure is not publicly published, but the underlying logic is broadly representative of how any NATO nation integrates a QRA launch into controlled civil airspace.\'\'\n\nOnce airborne, an interceptor does not go straight to a dedicated military-only frequency. It is worked by ordinary civil Air Traffic Control (ATC) like any other IFR flight, with priority/expedited handling, until tactical control is formally handed to the military radar unit directing the intercept — in the US model, the \'\'\'Air Defence Control Facility (ADCF)\'\'\', a military radar unit (Air Defence Sector / AWACS) that is the only type of unit authorised to direct interceptors. The CRC/CAOC in the NATO force structure above fills the equivalent role.\n\n=== Airborne Orders (filed once the flight is up) ===\nAs early as possible after launch, the controlling agency separately files \"airborne orders\" with the relevant en-route/terminal ATC facilities, containing:\n* Call sign, aircraft type/number\n* Departure point and time\n* Heading and altitude\n* Route of flight, broken into three legs: departure route → route to a fix serving the operating/tasked area → route from that fix to the intercept/destination\n* Frequency, and MARSA (Military Authority Assumes Responsibility for Separation) status if applicable\n\n=== Standing Authorisation & Squawk ===\nFor missions that cannot be conducted within normal ATC procedures, a standing pre-authorisation (in the US model, the FAA Authorization for Interceptor Operations, AFIO) allows active air-defence missions to proceed outside the normal clearance process. Aircraft operating under this authorisation squawk a dedicated identification code (Mode 3/A \'\'\'7777\'\'\' in the US model) so ATC and the controlling agency can positively track them even while outside the standard clearance framework.\n\n=== Beyond Controlled Airspace: \"Due Regard\" ===\nWhen an intercept and its supporting aircraft operate in controlled airspace beyond national territorial limits and a normal ATC clearance is impractical, the operation shifts to the \"due regard\" concept (from DOD FLIP, used for operations over the high seas): the controlling agency itself assumes responsibility for separating its aircraft from all observed traffic, in place of ATC. This only applies under specific conditions — e.g. the aircraft is in visual meteorological conditions, is within surveillance/radio contact of a radar facility, or carries its own separation-capable radar — and separation becomes the aircraft commander\'s own responsibility once outside the controlling agency\'s radar coverage.\n\n----\n\n== 07.4 / Handover (Transfer of Control) ==\nThe handoff from civil ATC to the controlling agency (CRC/CAOC, ADCF in the US model) is governed by a small set of rules, not a fixed altitude or distance:\n* The interceptor is retained under civil ATC control for as long as possible. ATC does not initiate the handoff on its own.\n* The controlling agency must request the transfer; it is not allowed to request control until the aircraft actually reaches the point where control must be assumed to carry out the intercept — i.e. it cannot take the aircraft \"early, just in case.\"\n* When requested, ATC must expedite the transfer.\n* The transfer point (a time, fix, or altitude) is chosen so that no conflict remains with any other traffic still under the transferring facility\'s control.\n\nOnce handover is complete, the sequence transitions seamlessly into the intercept procedure — see Step 5, below.\n\n----\n\n== 07.5 / Intercept & Tasking ==\nFollowing handover, the intercept controller runs the flight through one of two intercept types depending on the tactical picture: an \'\'\'ID intercept\'\'\' (radar/visual identification with follow-on tasking) or a \'\'\'VID intercept\'\'\' (visual identification required before a HOSTILE/FRIENDLY declaration can be made).\n\n=== ID Intercept ===\nAn Identification (ID) intercept establishes the type, registration, colour, and armament of an unknown contact by radar and/or visual means before follow-on tasking (shadow, intervention, radio interrogation, obey-check, or friendly termination) is assigned.\n\n\'\'\'ID Result Format:\'\'\' \'\'\'Number of Acft, Type, Registration, Colour, Armament\'\'\'\n\n==== Task: ID ====\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Controller || {CS} confirm ready to copy ID(-result)\n|-\n| FL || {Controller-CS} ready\n|-\n| Controller || Bogey is {Number} {Colour} {Acft Type}, registration {REGISTRATION}, {Armament}, at {Altitude}, Heading {HDG}, speed {Speed}\n|-\n| FL || {Controller-CS} copied, ({ID result}) new task {Task}\n|-\n| Controller || {CS} roger task {Task}\n|}\n\'\'This concludes Task ID; the controller can then assign one of the following follow-on tasks: Shadow, Obey-Check, Radio Interrogation, Intervention, or Friendly/Terminate.\'\'\n\n==== Task: Shadow ====\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Controller || {CS} new task shadow\n|-\n| FL || {Controller-CS} roger task shadow\n|}\n\'\'Task Shadow means following the aircraft at a distance of approximately 1–2 NM without further closure.\'\'\n\n==== Task: Obey-Check ====\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Controller || {CS} new task obey-check, fly HDG {HDG}\n|-\n| FL || {Controller-CS} task obey-check HDG {HDG}\n|-\n| FL || {Controller-CS} bogey is playmate \'\'(or: aircraft is not obeying)\'\'\n|-\n| Controller || {CS} copied\n|}\n\'\'Obey-Check means the fighter rocks its wings and then flies a turn in the specified direction. While doing so, it observes the target and reports whether it also rocks its wings and follows (\"playmate\") or does not (\"aircraft is not obeying\").\'\'\n\n==== Task: Radio Interrogation ====\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Controller || {CS} new task radio interrogation (on simulated guard {Frequency})\n|-\n| FL || {Controller-CS} roger, task radio interrogation\n|-\n| FL (on guard) || {Colour} {Acft Type} {Position}, with registration {REGISTRATION} (squawking {Squawk}) this is the eurofighter on your left wing calling on (simulated) guard ({Frequency}) you have been intercepted by the german air force. For purpose of identification state: Registration, Callsign, aerodrome of departure and aerodrome of destination on (simulated) guard ({Frequency})\n|-\n| Intercepted Acft || (German Interceptor) this is {Callsign} registration {REG} on guard, departure is {ICAO}, destination {ICAO}\n|-\n| Controller || Roger, standby\n|-\n| FL || {Controller-CS} did you copy (the answer) on guard?\n|-\n| Controller || Affirmative / Negative\n|}\n\'\'If the controller did not copy the response on guard, the fighter relays the answer. After this, termination is possible, or further tasks may follow.\'\'\n\n==== Task: Intervention ====\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Controller || {CS} new task intervention to {ICAO}\n|-\n| FL || {Controller-CS} roger, intervention to {ICAO}\n|-\n| colspan=\"2\" | \'\'Further radio follows the Intercept Call, kneeboard side (see below)\'\'\n|}\n\'\'During Interrogation and Intervention, the fighter flies to the left of the target at cockpit height (the pilot in command usually sits on the left).\'\'\n\n\'\'\'Intercept Call:\'\'\'\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Station !! Phrase\n|-\n| Interceptor || INTERCEPTED AIRCRAFT this is german INTERCEPTOR callsign {Callsign}, you will be intervented to {Airport} \'\'\'FOLLOW, FOLLOW\'\'\'\n|-\n| Intercepted Acft || WILCO / CAN NOT\n|-\n| Interceptor || What is your callsign?\n|-\n| Intercepted Acft || CALL SIGN {Callsign}\n|-\n| Interceptor || Descend to altitude {ALT} ft, QNH {HPA}\n|-\n| Intercepted Acft || WILCO / CAN NOT\n|-\n| Interceptor || What is your preferred approach speed?\n|-\n| Intercepted Acft || {Speed}\n|-\n| Interceptor || What is your gear down speed?\n|-\n| Intercepted Acft || {Speed}\n|-\n| Interceptor || This is your airport, after split up join right/left hand pattern of RWY {NR} at {Altitude} feet, QNH {HPA}; \'\'\'YOU LAND, YOU LAND\'\'\'\n|-\n| Intercepted Acft || WILCO\n|}\n\n\'\'\'Phrases used by the intercepted aircraft (ICAO standard responses):\'\'\'\n{| class=\"wikitable\" style=\"width:100%;\"\n|-\n! style=\"width:25%;\" | Phrase !! Meaning\n|-\n| CALL SIGN (callsign) || My callsign is....\n|-\n| WILCO || Understood. I will perform\n|-\n| CAN NOT || Unable to perform\n|-\n| REPEAT || Repeat the instructions\n|-\n| AM LOST || Unknown location\n|-\n| MAYDAY || They are in danger\n|-\n| HIJACK || I was hijacked\n|-\n| LAND (location) || I ask to land at...\n|-\n| DESCEND || I ask to descend\n|}\n\n==== Post-Intercept Options ====\nFollowing identification (ID or VID), the QRA(I) flight is normally tasked to shadow or escort the contact out of the airspace, hand it to a follow-on national asset, or — in the case of a genuine emergency (lost comms, distress) — assist it to a safe landing per the Intervention procedure above. Engagement is authorised only as an absolute last resort and strictly IAW the current ROE issued by Air C2.\n\n----\n\n== 07.6 / Recovery ==\nOnce tasking is complete and the flight is terminated (see Termination, above), control jurisdiction reverts from the intercept controller (CRC/ADCF) back to civil ATC for the return-to-base and recovery phase — this happens once the flight is clear of any other traffic still under the controlling agency\'s control.\n\nFrom this handback point onward, the sortie is flown as normal IFR traffic under standard ATC procedures; see [SOURCE ATC/Recovery document — link TBD] for departure/arrival handling, approach, and landing sequencing. Not duplicated here.\n\n----\n\n== Sources / Further Reading ==\nPlease review and approve before I link footnotes in the wiki:\n\n\'\'\'Quick Reaction Alert (QRA) — NATO:\'\'\'\n* AJP-3.3 — Allied Joint Doctrine for Air and Space Operations (official NATO standardisation document; references QRA and points to MC 0613, the overarching NIAMD concept) — https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/698ef9fb7da91680ad7f4389/AJP_3_3_EdC_V1.pdf\n* NATO.int — \"NATO Air Policing\" (official, unclassified overview) — https://www.nato.int/cps/en/natohq/topics_132685.htm\n* JAPCC (Joint Air Power Competence Centre) — \"NATO Air Policing Against Unmanned Aircraft\" (NATO-accredited Centre of Excellence; describes the ASACS/Air C2/CAOC/QRA(I) structure; not a doctrine document itself) — https://www.japcc.org/articles/nato-air-policing-against-unmanned-aircraft/\n\n\'\'\'Civil ATC Interface — Scramble Order, Transit, Handover (US/NORAD model, used illustratively):\'\'\'\n* FAA Order 7610.4J — \"Special Military Operations,\" 3 November 1998, Distribution Statement A (public release). Chapter 4: \"FAA/NORAD/PACAF Procedures for Control of Air Defense Aircraft\" — covers ADCF control instructions, transfer-of-control rules, scramble/airborne order format, AFIO, and \"due regard\" operations. Superseded internally by the current, FOUO-restricted JO 7610.4 (sensitive) / JO 7610.14 (non-sensitive) editions; this 1998 edition remains the last fully public release and is archived via DTIC, accession no. ADA355579. — https://apps.dtic.mil/sti/tr/pdf/ADA355579.pdf\n* FAA Order JO 7110.65 — Air Traffic Control (current edition), §9-2-7 \"Interceptor Operations\" — baseline ATC separation/priority handling for active air-defence scrambles — https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/publications/atpubs/atc_html/chap9_section_2.html\n\n\'\'\'Intercept & Tasking (ID / VID):\'\'\'\n* ICAO Annex 2 to the Convention on International Civil Aviation, Appendix 1 & 2 (Interception of Civil Aircraft; Signals) — https://www.bazl.admin.ch/dam/bazl/it/dokumente/Fachleute/Regulationen_und_Grundlagen/icao-annex/icao_annex_2_rulesoftheair.pdf.download.pdf/icao_annex_2_rulesoftheair.pdf\n* ICAO Doc 9433 — Manual concerning Interception of Civil Aircraft (Appendix D: example flashcards) — https://news.ncac.mn/uploads/bookSubject/2022-11/637c784cde8a2.pdf\n* SKYbrary — \"Military Interception Signalling\" (not official, but a good summary of interceptor positioning) — https://skybrary.aero/articles/military-interception-signalling\n* ATP 1-02.1/MCRP 3-30B.1/NTTP 6-02.1/AFTTP 3-2.5/STTP 3-9002 — Multi-Service Brevity Codes, Signature Draft, 01 Jan 2025 — https://www.alssa.mil/Portals/9/Documents/mttps/sd_brevity_2025.pdf\n* ATP 3-52.4/MCRP 3-20F.10/NTTP 6-02.9/AFTTP 3-2.8 — Multi-Service Tactics, Techniques, and Procedures for Air Control (ACC MTTP), 2024 — https://www.alssa.mil/Portals/9/Documents/mttps/acc_2024.pdf\n\n\'\'Note: Exact QRA reaction times and alert levels are nationally classified; the ~15 minutes mentioned in Step 1 is a publicly known, unclassified reference figure (RAF), not an official NATO standard. Likewise, Steps 3–4 (ATC Guidance/Handover) reflect the publicly available US/NORAD procedural model; the exact German/Luftwaffe scramble-to-ATC coordination procedure is not publicly published.\'\'','utf-8'),
(613,'== Introduction ==\nTactical formation turns are a core part of section-level fighter employment. In the F-14, they are not just formation manoeuvres. They are tools used to preserve mutual support, maintain radar and visual coverage, manage spacing, and reposition the section efficiently in response to threats or tasking.\n\nMany crews know the names of these turns, but fewer understand the geometry behind them. That is a problem, because these manoeuvres only work when both aircraft understand not only what turn is being called, but also why it is being used and how the geometry is supposed to develop. A section that performs the wrong turn, or performs the correct turn poorly, will lose spacing, lose visual support, reduce sensor coverage, and may even create a mid-air risk.\n\nThis page defines the four most important tactical formation turns for squadron use: the Check Turn, Tac Turn, Hook Turn, and Cross Turn. It also explains when each should be used and what F-14 crews should be trying to achieve with them.\n\n== General Purpose of Tactical Turns ==\nTactical turns are used to change the heading of a two-ship formation without giving up tactical effectiveness. In practice, this means the section should try to maintain:\n\n* Mutual support between both aircraft\n* Visual contact where possible\n* Useful radar coverage\n* Safe formation spacing\n* Good geometry relative to the threat\n\nThese turns are normally flown from \'\'\'Combat Spread\'\'\', usually around \'\'\'0.8 to 1.5 NM\'\'\', depending on the tactical situation, weather, threat, and crew proficiency.\n\nThe correct turn depends on three main factors:\n\n# \'\'\'How large the heading change is\'\'\'\n# \'\'\'Where the threat is relative to the formation\'\'\'\n# \'\'\'What type of coverage or posture the section wants to preserve\'\'\'\n\n== \'\'\'Check Turn\'\'\' ==\n\n=== \'\'\'Purpose\'\'\' ===\nThe Check Turn is used for small heading corrections without significantly changing formation geometry.\n\n=== \'\'\'When to Use It\'\'\' ===\nA Check Turn is appropriate for minor changes in direction, such as CAP station corrections, small navigation changes, or slight intercept adjustments. As a rule, it is best used for heading changes of about 30° or less.\n\n=== \'\'\'Geometry\'\'\' ===\nIn a Check Turn, both aircraft turn in the same direction at the same time. It is the simplest of the tactical turns and requires the least coordination. Because both aircraft turn together, the formation remains easy to manage during small heading changes.\n\n=== \'\'\'Advantages\'\'\' ===\nThe Check Turn is simple, quick, and causes minimal disruption when only a small correction is needed.\n\n=== \'\'\'Disadvantages\'\'\' ===\nIf used for larger heading changes, spacing and geometry will begin to degrade. It also does less to preserve full threat coverage during the turn than more formal tactical manoeuvres\'\'\'.\'\'\'\n\n=== \'\'\'F-14 Notes\'\'\' ===\nFor Tomcat crews, the Check Turn is most useful for routine CAP management, small AWG-9 search adjustments, and minor repositioning when a full tactical turn would be unnecessary.\n\n== Tac Turn ==\n\n=== Purpose ===\nThe Tac Turn is used to reposition the section through \'\'\'90°\'\'\' while maintaining mutual support and tactical usefulness.\n\n=== When to Use It ===\nA Tac Turn is commonly used when the threat, target, or required new heading lies roughly abeam of the formation. It is a standard manoeuvre for BVR repositioning, heading changes during intercepts, and sector reorientation.\n\n=== Geometry ===\nIn a Tac Turn, the \'\'\'outside aircraft turns first\'\'\'. The \'\'\'inside aircraft delays its turn\'\'\' until the outside aircraft approaches a \'\'\'nose-on\'\'\' position relative to it. This timing is what preserves tactical geometry.\n\nDuring the manoeuvre, the aircraft will \'\'\'swap sides\'\'\'. That is normal and should be expected.\n\n=== Advantages ===\nThe Tac Turn allows a fast repositioning while preserving offensive posture and mutual support. It is especially useful when the section needs to react to a tactical picture without losing structure.\n\n=== Disadvantages ===\nIt requires practice and good timing. If airspeeds differ, or if one aircraft turns too early or too late, spacing can degrade quickly.\n\n=== F-14 Notes ===\nFor F-14 operations, the Tac Turn is especially useful for:\n\n* BVR commits\n* Repositioning towards a BRAA call\n* Sector changes during CAP or BARCAP\n* Tactical heading changes during intercept timelines\n\nTomcat crews should be disciplined with timing here. The AWG-9 gives the section strong long-range capability, but poor Tac Turn execution will still damage formation support and complicate the intercept.\n\n== Hook Turn ==\n\n=== Purpose ===\nThe Hook Turn is used for large formation heading changes or controlled 180° reversals while keeping the section together.\n\n=== When to Use It ===\nUse a Hook Turn for CAP reversals, BARCAP flow, BVR repositioning, or any situation where the section must turn together without crossing flight paths.\n\n=== Geometry ===\nIn a Hook Turn, both aircraft turn \'\'\'to the same side at the same time\'\'\'.\n\nBoth aircraft select \'\'\'MIL power\'\'\' and fly a tight, controlled turn. The aim is to turn efficiently without gaining or losing airspeed. Do not pull so hard that the aircraft bleeds energy, but do not make the turn so wide that the section becomes slow to reposition.\n\n=== Advantages ===\nThe Hook Turn is simple, predictable, and useful for controlled reversals.\n\n=== Disadvantages ===\nPoor power or turn management can cause spacing problems. If the turn is too soft, it wastes airspace. If it is too hard, the aircraft loses airspeed and becomes vulnerable.\n\n=== F-14 Notes ===\nFor the F-14, fly the Hook Turn in \'\'\'MIL power\'\'\' with an energy-sustaining turn. \n\n== \'\'\'F-14 Employment Considerations\'\'\' ==\nIn the F-14, tactical turns must always be considered together with radar employment, timeline management, and crew coordination.\n\nThe aircraft’s long-range intercept capability is only useful if the section keeps good tactical geometry. A poor turn may temporarily blind part of the formation, degrade mutual support, or force one aircraft out of position during an intercept.\n\nTomcat crews should therefore think about tactical turns in the following way:\n\n* Check Turn for small corrections with minimal disruption\n* Tac Turn for efficient section repositioning during BVR or intercept work\n* Hook Turn for controlled reversals while maintaining radar and visual coverage\n\nRIOs should understand the expected geometry just as well as Pilots. Even though the Pilot flies the turn, the RIO should help maintain tactical awareness, call spacing concerns, and support the lead’s plan.','utf-8'),
(614,'\n== Purpose ==\nAir-to-air refuelling extends mission endurance, increases time on station, and allows the F-14 to continue operations beyond internal fuel limits. Good refuelling is not aggressive. It is stable formation flying with careful closure control.\n\n== Pre-Contact Setup ==\nBefore joining the tanker, configure the aircraft:\n\n* Wing Sweep: \'\'\'AUTO (Official BOMB)\'\'\'\n* Autopilot: \'\'\'OFF\'\'\'\n* Probe: \'\'\'OUT\'\'\'\n* Exterior lights: \'\'\'As briefed\'\'\'\n* Tanker frequency/TACAN: \'\'\'Confirmed\'\'\'\n\nThe aircraft must be \'\'\'nose cold\'\'\' around the tanker:\n\n* Master Arm: \'\'\'SAFE\'\'\'\n* ACM Cover: \'\'\'CLOSED\'\'\'\n* No air-to-air lock\n* No air-to-ground delivery mode\n\n== Join-Up ==\nJoin from \'\'\'below and behind\'\'\' the tanker. Do not approach from above.\n\nMove to the observation position, normally on the \'\'\'left side of the tanker\'\'\', slightly aft and stable. For KC-130 operations, stay clear of prop wash until cleared in.\n\n== Closure Control ==\nMost failed tankings start with too much closure. Use the rule:\n\n\'\'\'Closure rate ≈ distance × 100\'\'\'\n\nExamples:\n\n* 1.0 NM → 100 knots closure\n* 0.5 NM → 50 knots closure\n* 0.2 NM → 20 knots closure\n* 0.1 NM → 10 knots closure\n\nThe closer you are, the slower you close. Inside the basket area, closure should be only \'\'\'2–5 knots\'\'\'.\n\n== Contact Technique ==\nWhen cleared pre-contact, move forward slowly and stabilise.\n\nUse the controls correctly:\n\n* \'\'\'Throttle controls closure\'\'\'\n* \'\'\'Stick controls position\'\'\'\n* \'\'\'Trim reduces workload\'\'\'\n\nFly the aircraft into the basket. Do not chase the basket. The drogue moves; the tanker is the stable reference. The RIO usualy helps with alignement with the basket. Look at the \'\'\'tanker, basket, and reference points\'\'\'. Do not stare at the probe. Staring at the probe usually causes overcontrolling. Do not stab the basket. Use small corrections and gentle forward movement.\n\n== In Contact ==\nOnce connected, stop trying to “push” into the basket. Fly formation on the tanker.\n\nMake only small corrections:\n\n* Small power change\n* Wait\n* Observe\n* Correct again if needed\n\nThe F-14 is heavy and slow to respond. Large throttle or stick inputs usually create oscillations and disconnects.\n\n== Disconnect ==\nFor a normal disconnect, reduce power slightly, let the basket separate naturally, then move down and away from the tanker onto the waiting area.\n\nIf unstable, ordered to disconnect, or losing situational awareness:\n\n# Disconnect\n# Move down\n# Move away from the tanker\n# Regain stable flight\n# Rejoin safely\n\nDo not pull aggressively away unless required for safety.\n\n== F-14 Specific Notes ==\nThe Tomcat has significant inertia, especially when heavy with fuel. Think ahead of the aircraft and avoid late corrections. So \'\'\'use that Trim!!!\'\'\'\n\nThe probe is far forward of the pilot’s seating position. Don\'t overcorrect lateral alignment. Small inputs are usually enough.\n\nDon\'t input more than one dimension of movement. Fix one dimension at a time.\n\n== Rules of Thumb ==\n\n* Nose cold\n* Probe out\n* Join from below and behind\n* Closure = distance × 100\n* Throttle controls closure\n* Stick controls position\n* Trim is your friend\n* Fly the tanker, not the basket\n* Small corrections only','utf-8'),
(615,'{{DISPLAYTITLE:Squadrons/41st Black Aces}} {{DISPLAYTITLE:41st VSFS Black Aces}} {{DISPLAYTITLE:41st VSFS Black Aces}}\n\n= 41st VSFS special operating procedures =\n\n== Overview ==\n[[File:Screen_260330_155302_4.png|thumb|387x387px]]\nThe 41st Virtual Strike Fighter Squadron (VSFS) \"Black Aces\" is a carrier-based F-14B(U) strike fighter squadron deployed aboard the USS Harry S. Truman (CVN-75). The squadron takes its name, identity, and doctrine from VFA-41 Black Aces: A distinguished US Navy strike fighter squadron established in 1950 and renowned for its combat record, operational excellence, and carrier-based fighter tradition.\n\n41st VSFS strives to honour that legacy by embracing the professionalism, discipline, and combat effectiveness of the real Black Aces, integrating authentic US Navy carrier procedures, tactical doctrine, and mission planning into every virtual operation.\n\n== Carrier Operations and Procedures ==\n[[File:Screen_260405_152707.png|left|thumb|429x429px]]\nThe USS Harry S. Truman is the beating heart of our operations. All mission-ready pilots in our squadron are expected to be comfortable operating to, on, and from the carrier. Below you will find useful guides and resources to conduct safe and efficient carrier operations.\n\n[[41st VSFS/Case I Procedures|Case I Procedures (work in progress)]]\n\n[[41st VSFS/Case III Procedures|Case III Procedures (work in progress)]]\n\n[[General/CATC Communications Guide|Carrier Communications and Phraseology (CATCC)]]\n\n[[Landing Signal Officer (LSO) Guide]]\n\n== Tactical Procedures (WIP) ==\n[[File:Screen_260330_155406.png|thumb|510x510px]]\n\'\'\'<u>Tactical Procedures will cover how we fight together.</u>\'\'\'\n\nAir to Ground Tactics\n\n* [[14th VSFS/Close Air Support (CAS)|Close Air Support (CAS)]]\n* [[Strike/Air Interdiction]]\n* [[SEAD/DEAD]]\n* [[Anti-Ship]]\n\nAir to Air Tactics\n\n* [[Tactical Turns]]\n* [[Beyond Visual Range (BVR)]]\n* [[Basic Fighter Maneuvers (BFM)]]\n* [[Combat Air Patrol (CAP)]]\n\n== General Flight Procedures (WIP) ==\n[[File:Screen_260327_220735.png|left|thumb|464x464px]]\n\'\'\'<u>General Flight Procedures will cover how we fly together.</u>\'\'\'\n\n[[Airfield operations (Shore-based)]]\n\n[[Formation Flying]]\n\n[[VSFS 41st Air to Air Refueling|Air to Air Refueling]]\n\n[[Advanced Fuel Management]]\n\n[[Night Flying]]\n\n[[Pilot & RIO Communications]]\n\n== Resources and Files ==\nTIP: CTRL+CLICK on any link to open it in a new tab instead of replacing this one\n\n[Link to livery, when livery]\n\n[https://polybox.ethz.ch/index.php/s/S8Es6KCzBao5WYm/download?path=%2FKneeboards&files=FA-18C_hornet.rar <u>14th VSFS Kneeboard (Checklists + Quick Reference Guides + LTAG Aerodrome and Tacan Approach Charts)</u>]\n\n[https://info.publicintelligence.net/F14AAP-1B.pdf F-14B Emergency Procedures]\n\n[https://chucksguides.com/aircraft/dcs/f-14b/#&#x5B;1,%22XYZ%22,0,540,1&#x5D; Chucks Guides - F-14B] \n\n[https://server.3rd-wing.net/public/Bureau_VF31/Docs%20F-14%20r%C3%A9el/NAVAIR%2001-F14AAP-1%20-%20NATOPS%20Flight%20Manual%20-%20F-14B.pdf NATOPS FLIGHT MANUAL 01-F14AAP-1] (Aug, 2001)\n\n[https://f14.manuals.heatblur.se/ Heatblur F-14 Manual] (June, 2026)','utf-8'),
(616,'= Available tankers (Source Training Theatre) =\n[[File:US Navy 030125-N-0905V-002 S-3B \'Viking assigned to the Screwbirds of Sea Control Squadron 33 (VS-33) .jpg|thumb|383x383px|Lockheed S-3B Viking]]\n[[File:Screen 260212 152743.png|thumb|380x380px|Boeing KC-135 Stratotanker (with MPRS Pods)]]\n{| class=\"wikitable\"\n|+TANKER INFORMATION\n|\n|CALLSIGN \n|FREQUENCY\n|TACAN\n|ALTITUDE\n|NOTES\n|-\n|S-3B VIKING\n|MAULER 6-1\n|311.5 MHz\n|25Y\n|6,000ft\n|CARRIER RECOVERY TANKER\n|-\n|KC-135 MPRS\n|SHELL 2-1\n|254.0 MHz\n|38X\n|24,000ft\n|TRA ADANA\n|}\n\'\'\'S-3B\'\'\' is a small, carrier capable tanker that has one drogue capable of refueling only one aircraft at a time. It is established in a left hand racetrack pattern around the carrier on the BRC.\n\n\'\'\'KC-135 MPRS\'\'\' is a large purpose built tanker with the Multi-Point Refueling System (MPRS) pods installed on each wingtip, which allows for two probe-style aircraft to connect and receive fuel at the same time. It is established in a left hand racetrack pattern within TRA ADANA.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n= F/A-18C Hornet — Air-to-Air Refueling (AAR) Standard Procedure =\n\'\'Note: This guide blends standard Navy probe-and-drogue technique with sim-community best practices. Lines marked (IRL) reflect real-world reasoning; lines marked (SIM) are conventions common in DCS/sim training rather than confirmed NATOPS text.\'\'\n----\n\n== \'\'\'1. Inbound and Join-Up on Port (Left) Observation\'\'\' ==\n\'\'\'Tanker Intercept\'\'\'\n\n* Proceed to the tanker\'s position via the assigned rendezvous point, altitude, and heading (as briefed or per controlling agency instructions).\n* Call the tanker with \"Intent to refuel\" once on tanker frequency\n* You need to be at least 1,000ft (recommended 2,000ft) underneath the tanker while maneuvering for the join-up. Once you have visual with the tanker (not with onboard sensors, with your actual eyes), you may begin a slow climb to reach the tanker at co-altitude level)\n\n== Closure Rate vs. Range — Speed Management Table ==\n{| class=\"wikitable\"\n!Range\n!Target Closure\n|-\n|2 NM\n|≤ 200 KIAS closure\n|-\n|1 NM\n|≤ 100 KIAS closure\n|-\n|0.5 NM\n|≤ 50 KIAS closure\n|-\n|0.4 NM\n|≤ 40 KIAS closure\n|-\n|0.3 NM\n|≤ 30 KIAS closure\n|-\n|0.2 NM\n|≤ 20 KIAS closure\n|-\n|0.1 NM\n|≤ 10 KIAS closure\n|}\n\'\'\'How to use this table in practice:\'\'\'\n\n* Easy way to memorize is to multiply your range by 100. This will give you your MAXIMUM CLOSURE VELOCITY (Indicated as \"Vc\" on the HUD). Do not treat it as a required speed checkpoint, if you have less than 400kts closure at 4nm this does not mean you need to accelerate. \n* This method works best during rear aspect join-ups (if you\'re intercepting from the front of the tanker you have a bigger problem)\n\nPort Observation\n\n* Port Observation is the holding area established in left echelon off the tanker, slightly aft and level with (or slightly below) the tanker\'s wing line.\n* This is where flight lead sorts flight members in refueling order — lowest fuel is closest to the basket.\n* Maintain a disciplined and rigid echelon here, no swaying around.\n\n----\n\n== 2. \'\'\'Transition to Pre-Contact and Connect\'\'\' ==\nWhen cleared and ready to move from Port Observation to Pre-Contact, complete the following memory items BEFORE leaving port observation:\n{| class=\"wikitable\"\n!Item\n!Setting\n|-\n|Refueling probe\n|EXTEND\n|-\n|Radar\n|SIL / STBY\n|-\n|Engine Bleed Air\n|RIGHT OFF (optional)\n|-\n|Throttle\n|Manual preferred; avoid ATC\n|}\n\'\'Right engine bleed air was switched off IRL to prevent drogue fuel leaks from entering the cabin via the right engine intake\'\'\n\n\'\'\'Throttle technique:\'\'\' Avoid relying on Automatic Throttle Control (ATC) whenever possible. While convenient, relying on ATC keeps you from developing and maintaining the fine throttle control skills that are important throughout many other phases of flying the Hornet.\n\n\'\'\'Closing on the basket:\'\'\'\n\n* Move from Port Observation, cross UNDER AND BEHIND the tanker and any potential aircraft that may already be connected, and stabilize in the Pre-Contact position.\n* On clearance, wait for the basket to fully extend before crawling the jet forward very very slowly. Make small, early corrections rather than large, late ones.\n* Once connected, shift your perspective to flying in formation with the aircraft. Do not tunnel vision on the basket. \n* Confirm a good connection (contact light/indication, fuel flow) before settling into position and monitoring fuel transfer. If the MPRS pod shows a steady light, you are in a good position and are taking fuel. If the light suddenly goes off while you are still connected, it means you are either too close or too far from the basket. If the light starts blinking, the refueling process will complete in about 5 seconds and automatically disconnect. \n\n----\n\n== 3. \'\'\'Disconnect and Join Up on Starboard (Right) Observation\'\'\' ==\n\n* Once refueling is complete (full load or as briefed), smoothly reduce power slightly to back straight out of the basket — avoid abrupt throttle changes or lateral drift during disconnect.\n* Confirm disconnected from basket and retract the refueling probe.\n* \'\'\'Engine Bleed Air\'\'\' — restore to NORM (if previously turned off for the right engine).\n* Clear from BEHIND and UNDER the tanker to the \'\'\'Starboard (Right) Observation\'\'\' position — the mirror of Port Observation, held in right echelon off the tanker. \n* ----','utf-8'),
